window.SUBJECT_DATA=window.SUBJECT_DATA||{};
window.SUBJECT_DATA["mhn"]=[{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The term \"psychiatry\" was coined in 1808 by which German physician, anatomist and physiologist?","options":["Philippe Pinel","Johann Christian Reil","Sigmund Freud","Benjamin Rush"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the term psychiatry was coined in 1808 by Johann Christian Reil, a German physician, anatomist and physiologist, meaning \"the soul as doctor.\"  Why A wrong: Philippe Pinel is the father of modern psychiatry, not the coiner of the term.  Why C wrong: Sigmund Freud is the father of psychiatry / psychoanalysis, not the one who coined the term.  Why D wrong: Benjamin (Benzamine) Rush wrote the first psychiatry textbook (Father of American Psychiatry)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The word \"psychiatry\" literally means:","options":["Study of the mind and behaviour","Treatment by medicine","The soul as doctor — treating the patient by using his psyche (soul) as the therapeutic agent","Science of abnormal behaviour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines psychiatry as \"the soul as doctor, a way of treating the patient by using his psyche (meaning the soul) as the therapeutic agent.\"  Why A wrong: That is the definition of psychology.  Why B wrong: Treatment by medicine is not the literal meaning given.  Why D wrong: Study of abnormal behaviour defines mental illness/psychiatric study, not the meaning of the word."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Who is regarded as the \"Father of Psychiatry\"?","options":["Philippe Pinel","Linda Richards","Dr. Sigmund Freud","Johann Christian Reil"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book lists Father of psychiatry as Dr. Sigmund Freud.  Why A wrong: Pinel is the father of modern psychiatry.  Why B wrong: Linda Richards is the first psychiatry nurse.  Why D wrong: Reil coined the term psychiatry."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The \"Father of Modern Psychiatry,\" who introduced the concept of moral and humane treatment of the mentally ill, is:","options":["Sigmund Freud","Philippe Pinel","Benjamin Rush","Johann Christian Reil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Philippe Pinel is named the father of modern psychiatry and introduced the concept of moral and humane treatment of the mentally ill.  Why A wrong: Freud is father of psychiatry and psychoanalysis.  Why C wrong: Rush is father of American psychiatry.  Why D wrong: Reil coined the term psychiatry."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Who is called the \"Father of Psychoanalysis\"?","options":["Philippe Pinel","Gockel","Sigmund Freud","Linda Richards"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book states father of psychoanalysis is Sigmund Freud.  Why A wrong: Pinel is father of modern psychiatry.  Why B wrong: Gockel coined the word psychology.  Why D wrong: Linda Richards is the first psychiatry nurse."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The first anti-psychotic drug is:","options":["Lithium","Chlorpromazine","Haloperidol","Diazepam"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text identifies chlorpromazine as the first anti-psychotic drug.  Why A wrong: Lithium is a mood stabiliser, not the first antipsychotic.  Why C wrong: Haloperidol is a later typical antipsychotic.  Why D wrong: Diazepam is a benzodiazepine (anxiolytic)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Who is recognized as the first psychiatry nurse?","options":["Florence Nightingale","Hildegard Peplau","Linda Richards","Dorothea Dix"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the first psychiatry nurse is Linda Richards (first psychiatric nurse, 1873).  Why A wrong: Nightingale is founder of modern nursing, not the first psychiatric nurse.  Why B wrong: Peplau is known for interpersonal theory but is not named here.  Why D wrong: Dorothea Dix is a reformer, not named here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the text, a psychiatrist is NOT posted for duties at which health facilities?","options":["District hospital and state hospital","Sub-Centre (SC) and Primary Health Centre (PHC)","Community Health Centre (CHC) only","Medical college hospital"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book explicitly states \"Psychiatrist is not posted for duties at SC and PHC.\"  Why A wrong: Higher facilities are not mentioned as excluded.  Why C wrong: Only SC and PHC are specified, not CHC alone.  Why D wrong: Medical college hospital is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In which year did the Indian Nursing Council (INC) include psychiatric nursing as a compulsory subject/course in B.Sc. Nursing?","options":["1956","1965","1976","1986"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 1965 the INC included psychiatric nursing as a compulsory subject in B.Sc. Nursing (syllabus added at RAK, New Delhi).  Why A wrong: 1956 was the 1-year diploma in psychiatric at NIMHANS.  Why C wrong: 1976 was M.Sc. Nursing in psychiatry starting.  Why D wrong: 1986 was syllabus added in GNM."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In 1976, M.Sc. Nursing in psychiatry was started at which institution (NIMHANS-2022)?","options":["RAK College of Nursing, New Delhi","RAC College of Nursing, New Delhi","NIMHANS, Bangalore","AIIMS, New Delhi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in 1976, M.Sc. Nursing in psychiatric started at RAC College of Nursing, New Delhi (tagged NIMHANS-2022).  Why A wrong: RAK New Delhi is linked with the 1965 B.Sc. syllabus addition.  Why C wrong: NIMHANS started the 1-year diploma (1956), not the M.Sc.  Why D wrong: AIIMS is not mentioned for this milestone."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The first psychiatry textbook was written by Benjamin Rush, who is also known as the:","options":["Father of psychoanalysis","Father of modern psychiatry","Father of American Psychiatry","Father of psychiatry"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the first psychiatry textbook was written by Benzamine (Benjamin) Rush, also known as the Father of American Psychiatry.  Why A wrong: Father of psychoanalysis is Freud.  Why B wrong: Father of modern psychiatry is Pinel.  Why D wrong: Father of psychiatry is Freud."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The first psychiatric hospital (1773) was established at:","options":["Williamsburg, Virginia","New Delhi","Bangalore","London"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The developmental milestone states the first psychiatric hospital in 1773 was at Williamsburg, Virginia.  Why B wrong: New Delhi relates to nursing course milestones, not the first hospital.  Why C wrong: Bangalore (NIMHANS) relates to the 1956 diploma.  Why D wrong: London is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In which year was a 1-year diploma in psychiatry started at NIMHANS?","options":["1773","1873","1956","1982"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone list states 1956 — 1-year diploma in psychiatric started in NIMHANS.  Why A wrong: 1773 is the first psychiatric hospital.  Why B wrong: 1873 is the first psychiatric nurse.  Why D wrong: 1982 is the National Mental Health Programme."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the developmental milestones, the National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) started in India in:","options":["1965","1976","1982","1987"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone list states 1982 — National Mental Health Program started in India.  Why A wrong: 1965 was B.Sc. syllabus addition.  Why B wrong: 1976 was M.Sc. Nursing start.  Why D wrong: 1987 was the National Mental Health Act."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The NDPSA Act was passed in India in which year?","options":["1982","1985","1986","1987"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone list states 1985 — NDPSA Act passed in India.  Why A wrong: 1982 was NMHP.  Why C wrong: 1986 was GNM syllabus addition.  Why D wrong: 1987 was National Mental Health Act."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The psychiatric nursing syllabus was added to the GNM course in which year?","options":["1965","1976","1986","2017"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone list states 1986 — Syllabus added in GNM.  Why A wrong: 1965 was the B.Sc. addition.  Why B wrong: 1976 was M.Sc. Nursing.  Why D wrong: 2017 was the India Mental Health Care Act."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The India Mental Health Care Act (MHA-2017) was passed in India in which year?","options":["1987","2012","2017","1993"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The milestone list states 2017 — India Mental Health Care Act (MHA-2017) passed in India.  Why A wrong: 1987 was the National Mental Health Act.  Why B wrong: 2012 is not mentioned.  Why D wrong: 1993 was when the 1987 Act came into force."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The word \"psychology\" was coined by:","options":["Freud","Gockel","Pinel","Reil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the word psychology was coined by Gockel.  Why A wrong: Freud is father of psychoanalysis.  Why C wrong: Pinel is father of modern psychiatry.  Why D wrong: Reil coined psychiatry."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Psychology is composed of two words: \"psycho\" and \"logos.\" What does \"logos\" mean?","options":["Mind","Soul","Study","Behaviour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The book states psycho means mind/spirit/soul and logos means study.  Why A wrong: Mind is the meaning of psycho.  Why B wrong: Soul is included under psycho.  Why D wrong: Behaviour is what psychology studies, not the meaning of logos."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Mind vs Brain comparison, which statement about the Mind is correct?","options":["It is concrete and can be seen and touched","It is the structural part of the nervous system","It is abstract (philosophical/imaginary), cannot be seen or touched, and is the functional part","It is the organic component of the nervous system"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table describes Mind as abstract (philosophical, imaginary), cannot see and touch, functional part.  Why A wrong: Concrete and can see/touch describes the Brain.  Why B wrong: Structural part describes the Brain.  Why D wrong: The Brain is the organic component; mind is the functional part."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Tri-Component Attitude Model of Mind, the component related to thinking, belief and knowledge associated with an object is:","options":["Affection","Conation","Cognition","Perception"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cognition is defined as related to thinking, belief and knowledge, associated with an object.  Why A wrong: Affection relates to emotions and feelings.  Why B wrong: Conation/behaviour relates to act or action.  Why D wrong: Perception is not a component of this model."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Tri-Component Attitude Model, the component related to emotions and feelings about a particular thing is:","options":["Cognition","Affection","Conation","Behaviour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Affection is defined as related to emotions and feelings — a consumer's feelings about particular things.  Why A wrong: Cognition is thinking/belief/knowledge.  Why C wrong: Conation is act or action.  Why D wrong: Behaviour is the same as conation, dealing with action tendency."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The component of the attitude model concerning the likelihood or tendency to undertake a specific action is called:","options":["Cognition","Affection","Conation (behaviour)","Validation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Conation or behaviour is defined as related to act or action — the likelihood/tendency that an individual will undertake a specific action.  Why A wrong: Cognition is thinking/knowledge.  Why B wrong: Affection is emotions/feelings.  Why D wrong: Validation is a communication technique, not an attitude component."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The Topographical Model (theory of level of mind) was given by:","options":["Philippe Pinel","Sigmund Freud","Linda Richards","Johann Reil"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Topographical Model of Mind is given by Sigmund Freud in psychoanalysis, also known as theory of level of mind.  Why A wrong: Pinel is father of modern psychiatry.  Why C wrong: Richards is the first psychiatric nurse.  Why D wrong: Reil coined the term psychiatry."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the Topographical Model, the mental apparatus is divided into which three systems?","options":["Id, Ego and Superego","Cognition, Affection and Conation","Conscious, Preconscious and Unconscious","Mind, Brain and Spirit"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The theory divides the mental apparatus into conscious, preconscious (sub/semi/pre-conscious) and unconscious systems.  Why A wrong: Id/Ego/Superego is the structural model, not the topographical one described here.  Why B wrong: Those are attitude model components.  Why D wrong: These are separate concepts, not the topographical levels."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the Topographical Model, the systems (conscious, preconscious, unconscious) represent:","options":["Anatomical structures of the brain","Mental processes, not anatomical structures","Lobes of the cerebral cortex","Neurotransmitter pathways"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text explicitly states these systems are not anatomical structures of the brain but rather mental processes.  Why A wrong: The book states they are NOT anatomical structures.  Why C wrong: They are not cortical lobes.  Why D wrong: They are not neurotransmitter pathways."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Iceberg theory, which of the following is stored in the \"unconscious\" mind?","options":["Thoughts and perceptions","Memories and stored knowledge","Fears, unacceptable desires, violent motives and traumatic experiences","Only current sensory information"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The iceberg figure lists the unconscious as holding fears, unacceptable desires, violent motives, immoral urges, selfish needs, shameful and traumatic experiences.  Why A wrong: Thoughts and perceptions belong to the conscious mind.  Why B wrong: Memories and stored knowledge belong to the preconscious.  Why D wrong: Sensory information relates to conscious awareness."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Iceberg theory, the \"preconscious\" mind contains:","options":["Thoughts and perceptions we currently know about","Memories and stored knowledge we could be aware of if we tried","Violent motives and immoral urges we cannot become aware of","Reflex actions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The preconscious is described as things we could be aware of if we wanted or tried — memories and stored knowledge.  Why A wrong: Thoughts and perceptions are conscious.  Why C wrong: Violent motives/immoral urges are unconscious.  Why D wrong: Reflex actions are not listed."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"As per the Note on page, all covert and overt activities (from womb to tomb) are collectively known as:","options":["Cognition","Behaviour","Perception","Instinct"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Note states all covert and overt activities are known as behaviour (activities from womb to tomb).  Why A wrong: Cognition is a specific attitude component.  Why C wrong: Perception is a conscious content.  Why D wrong: Instinct is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The study of disorders of the mind is known as:","options":["Psychology","Psychiatric","Neurology","Sociology"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the study of disorders of the mind is known as psychiatric.  Why A wrong: Psychology is the study of mind and behaviour in general.  Why C wrong: Neurology deals with the nervous system disorders structurally.  Why D wrong: Sociology studies society."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Mental illness is generally classified into which two major groups?","options":["Neurosis and psychosis","Organic mental disorders and functional mental disorders","Acute and chronic disorders","Mood and anxiety disorders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book states mental illness is classified into two major groups — organic mental disorders and functional mental disorders.  Why A wrong: Neurosis and psychosis are subtypes of functional disorders.  Why C wrong: Acute/chronic is not the primary classification given.  Why D wrong: Mood and anxiety are specific disorder categories, not the two major groups."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"As per the genetic risk data, what is the concordance/risk percentage for identical (identification) twins?","options":["15%","20%","50%","35%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The genetic factor list gives identification (identical) twins - 50%.  Why A wrong: 15% is for fraternal twins and one parent.  Why B wrong: 20% is for brother & sister.  Why D wrong: 35% is when both parents are affected."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the genetic risk figures, the risk when BOTH parents are affected is:","options":["15%","20%","35%","0.5 to 1%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list states both parents - 35%.  Why A wrong: 15% is fraternal twins / one parent.  Why B wrong: 20% is brother & sister.  Why D wrong: 0.5 to 1% is the general population risk."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The risk of mental illness in the general population is stated as:","options":["5 to 10%","0.5 to 1%","15%","20%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The genetic list gives general population 0.5 to 1%.  Why A wrong: Not the value given.  Why C wrong: 15% is fraternal twins/one parent.  Why D wrong: 20% is brother & sister."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Under congenital factors, which personality type is associated with the development of schizophrenia?","options":["Cyclothymic personality","Melancholic personality","Schizoid/schizotypal personality","Extravert personality"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text links schizoid/schizotypal personality with schizophrenia.  Why A wrong: Cyclothymic personality is linked with bipolar.  Why B wrong: Melancholic personality is linked with depression.  Why D wrong: Extravert personality is linked with mania."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which personality type is associated with bipolar disorder?","options":["Melancholic","Cyclothymic","Anxious","Schizoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list states cyclothymic personality — bipolar.  Why A wrong: Melancholic — depression.  Why C wrong: Anxious personality — anxiety.  Why D wrong: Schizoid — schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the congenital/personality associations, an extravert personality is linked with ______ and an introvert personality with ______.","options":["Depression; mania","Mania; depression","Anxiety; bipolar","Bipolar; schizophrenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states personality-extravert-mania, introvert-depression.  Why A wrong: The associations are reversed.  Why C wrong: Anxiety is linked to anxious personality, not extravert.  Why D wrong: Not the associations given."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Under physical deprivation factors, malnutrition is associated with which conditions?","options":["Delirium","Mental retardation and infection","Hypothyroidism","Anxiety and stress"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list states malnutrition — mental retardation, infection.  Why A wrong: Head trauma is linked to delirium.  Why C wrong: Hormonal imbalance is linked to hypothyroidism.  Why D wrong: Handicapper is linked to anxiety, depression, stress."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Head trauma is listed as a physical deprivation factor associated with:","options":["Delirium","Mental retardation","Hypothyroidism","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The list states head trauma — delirium.  Why B wrong: Malnutrition is linked to mental retardation.  Why C wrong: Hormonal imbalance is linked to hypothyroidism.  Why D wrong: Schizophrenia is a genetic/biochemical link, not head trauma here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which neurotransmitter change is associated with schizophrenia according to the bio-chemical factors?","options":["Decreased dopamine","Increased dopamine","Decreased serotonin","Decreased acetylcholine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The bio-chemical factor list states increased dopamine in schizophrenia.  Why A wrong: Decreased dopamine is seen in Parkinson disease.  Why C wrong: Decreased serotonin is in depression.  Why D wrong: Decreased acetylcholine is in Alzheimer disease."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Decreased dopamine levels are characteristically associated with which condition?","options":["Mania","Schizophrenia","Parkinson disease","Anxiety disorder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list states decreased dopamine in Parkinson disease.  Why A wrong: Mania shows increased serotonin and norepinephrine.  Why B wrong: Schizophrenia shows increased dopamine.  Why D wrong: Anxiety disorder shows decreased GABA."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In mania, the bio-chemical change described is:","options":["Decreased serotonin and dopamine","Increased serotonin and norepinephrine","Decreased GABA","Decreased acetylcholine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The list states increased serotonin and norepinephrine in mania.  Why A wrong: Decreased serotonin is in depression.  Why C wrong: Decreased GABA is in anxiety disorder.  Why D wrong: Decreased acetylcholine is in Alzheimer disease."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which neurotransmitter is decreased in depression?","options":["Dopamine","GABA","Serotonin","Acetylcholine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list states decreased serotonin in depression.  Why A wrong: Dopamine changes relate to schizophrenia/Parkinson.  Why B wrong: GABA decrease is in anxiety disorder.  Why D wrong: Acetylcholine decrease is in Alzheimer disease."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Decreased GABA is associated with which disorder?","options":["Anxiety disorder","Alzheimer disease","Mania","Parkinson disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The list states GABA decreased in anxiety disorder.  Why B wrong: Alzheimer disease shows decreased acetylcholine.  Why C wrong: Mania shows increased serotonin/norepinephrine.  Why D wrong: Parkinson shows decreased dopamine."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Decreased acetylcholine is characteristically seen in:","options":["Anxiety disorder","Depression","Alzheimer disease","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The list states acetylcholine decreased in Alzheimer disease.  Why A wrong: Anxiety shows decreased GABA.  Why B wrong: Depression shows decreased serotonin.  Why D wrong: Schizophrenia shows increased dopamine."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which feature correctly distinguishes an ORGANIC mental disorder from a functional one?","options":["Gradual onset","Consciousness preserved","Acute onset with impaired consciousness and prominent visual hallucinations","Mainly auditory hallucinations with no neurological deficit"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Organic disorders have acute onset, impaired consciousness, prominent visual hallucinations, and present neurological deficit.  Why A wrong: Gradual onset is a functional feature.  Why B wrong: Preserved consciousness is functional.  Why D wrong: Auditory hallucinations with no deficit describe functional disorders."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following is an example of an organic mental disorder?","options":["Schizophrenia","Bipolar mood disorder","Delirium and dementia (Wernicke's, Korsakoff)","Panic disorder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table gives examples of organic disorders as delirium, dementia, Wernicke's, Korsakoff psychiatry.  Why A wrong: Schizophrenia is a functional disorder.  Why B wrong: Bipolar mood disorder is functional.  Why D wrong: Panic disorder is a neurosis (functional)."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which is the most common organic mental disorder?","options":["Dementia","Delirium","Korsakoff psychosis","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states delirium is the most common organic mental disorder.  Why A wrong: Dementia is an organic disorder but not stated as most common.  Why C wrong: Korsakoff is an example but not most common.  Why D wrong: Schizophrenia is a functional disorder."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The gold standard test to diagnose a mental disorder (RRB-2019) is the:","options":["MMSE","Bender-Gestalt Test","Rorschach Test","TAT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the gold standard test to diagnose mental disorder is the Bender-Gestalt Test (tagged RRB-2019).  Why A wrong: MMSE is a cognitive screen, not named as gold standard here.  Why C wrong: Rorschach is a projective test, not named here.  Why D wrong: TAT is a projective test, not named here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Functional mental disorder is further classified into which two major types?","options":["Organic and inorganic","Neurosis and psychosis","Acute and chronic","Mood and thought disorders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states functional mental disorder is further classified into two major types: neurosis and psychosis.  Why A wrong: Organic vs functional is the higher-level split.  Why C wrong: Acute/chronic is not the classification given.  Why D wrong: Mood/thought is not the split used here."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In PSYCHOSIS, the status of insight and reality testing is:","options":["Insight preserved; reality testing preserved","Insight absent; judgment and reality testing impaired","Insight absent; reality testing preserved","Insight preserved; reality testing impaired"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The psychosis column states insight absent and judgment and reality testing impaired.  Why A wrong: That describes neurosis.  Why C wrong: Reality testing is impaired, not preserved, in psychosis.  Why D wrong: Insight is absent in psychosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which feature is characteristic of NEUROSIS?","options":["Delusions and hallucinations always present","Insight absent","Delusions and hallucinations absent; insight preserved","Personality and behaviour impaired"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In neurosis, delusions and hallucinations are absent, insight and reality testing are preserved, and personality is relatively preserved.  Why A wrong: Always-present delusions/hallucinations describe psychosis.  Why B wrong: Absent insight describes psychosis.  Why D wrong: Impaired personality describes psychosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following is an example of a psychosis?","options":["Panic disorder","Agoraphobia","Mania / schizophrenia / acute and transient psychotic disorder","OCD"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of psychosis given are mania, schizophrenia, acute and transient psychotic disorder.  Why A wrong: Panic disorder is a neurosis.  Why B wrong: Agoraphobia is a neurosis.  Why D wrong: OCD is a neurosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following is listed as an example of neurosis? (Select all that apply)","options":["Panic disorder","Agoraphobia","Schizophrenia","OCD (obsessive-compulsive disorder)","GAD (generalized anxiety disorder)"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Neurosis examples include panic disorder, agoraphobia, PTSD, OCD, GAD, and personality disorder.  Why C wrong: Schizophrenia is a psychosis, not a neurosis."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) was launched by the Government of India in:","options":["1976","1982","1987","1993"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The GOI launched the NMHP in 1982 in view of the heavy burden of mental illness.  Why A wrong: 1976 relates to M.Sc. Nursing.  Why C wrong: 1987 is the Mental Health Act.  Why D wrong: 1993 is when the 1987 Act came into force."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which is NOT one of the three components of the National Mental Health Programme (NMHP)?","options":["Treatment of mentally ill patients","Rehabilitation","Prevention and promotion of positive mental health","Compulsory institutionalization of all patients"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: NMHP components are treatment of mentally ill patients, rehabilitation, and prevention/promotion of positive mental health; compulsory institutionalization is not a component.  Why A wrong: Treatment is component 1.  Why B wrong: Rehabilitation is component 2.  Why C wrong: Prevention/promotion is component 3."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The Indian Mental Health Act-1987 was passed in Parliament on 22 May 1987 and came into force on:","options":["22 May 1987","1 April 1993","29 May 2018","1 April 1987"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book states the Act passed on 22 May 1987 and came into force on 1 April 1993.  Why A wrong: 22 May 1987 is the passing date, not the enforcement date.  Why C wrong: 29 May 2018 is enforcement of MHCA-2017.  Why D wrong: Not a date given."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The Indian Mental Health Act-1987 includes how many chapters?","options":["5","10","16","12"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the Act includes 10 chapters for rights of the mentally ill patient and property matters.  Why A wrong: 5 chapters is the NDPSA Act.  Why C wrong: 16 chapters is the MHCA-2017.  Why D wrong: 12 is where admission/discharge falls in MHCA-2017."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Mental Health Act-1987, which chapter deals with all terminology and definitions related to mental illness?","options":["Chapter 1","Chapter 3","Chapter 8","Chapter 10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 1 deals with all terminology & definitions with mental illness.  Why B wrong: Chapter 3 issues guidelines for opening a new psychiatric hospital.  Why C wrong: Chapter 8 deals with patient rights (refusal of medication, ECT consent).  Why D wrong: Chapter 10 deals with local rules and regulations."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the MHA-1987, admission procedure in a mental hospital is dealt with in which chapter?","options":["Chapter 2","Chapter 4","Chapter 5","Chapter 7"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 4 deals with the admission procedure in a mental hospital (FAQ item).  Why A wrong: Chapter 2 deals with emergency fund preparation.  Why C wrong: Chapter 5 deals with the discharge procedure.  Why D wrong: Chapter 7 deals with cost maintenance."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the MHA-1987, the discharge procedure in a mental hospital is covered under:","options":["Chapter 4","Chapter 5","Chapter 6","Chapter 9"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 5 deals with the discharge procedure in a mental hospital.  Why A wrong: Chapter 4 deals with admission.  Why C wrong: Chapter 6 deals with possession of poverty by district court within 6 months.  Why D wrong: Chapter 9 deals with punishment/penalty to the hospital."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Under MHA-1987, which chapter deals with the mentally ill patient's right to refusal of medication, consent for ECT, and right to behave as a normal human being?","options":["Chapter 6","Chapter 7","Chapter 8","Chapter 9"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 8 deals with the mentally ill patient's right to refusal of medication, consent for ECT, and right to behave as a normal human being.  Why A wrong: Chapter 6 deals with possession of poverty decided by district court within 6 months.  Why B wrong: Chapter 7 deals with cost maintenance.  Why D wrong: Chapter 9 deals with punishment/penalty for deficiencies in patient care."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the MHA-1987, cost maintenance — being first the responsibility of the family, but the government responsible if admission source is unknown — is covered under which chapter?","options":["Chapter 6","Chapter 7","Chapter 8","Chapter 10"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 7 deals with cost maintenance: first responsibility of family, but if admission unknown, govt is responsible.  Why A wrong: Chapter 6 covers possession of poverty by district court.  Why C wrong: Chapter 8 covers patient rights.  Why D wrong: Chapter 10 covers local rules and regulations."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The full form of the NDPSA Act (1985) is:","options":["National Drug Prevention and Substance Act","Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substance Act","National Drug Policy and Safety Act","Narcotic Drug Prohibition and Sale Act"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book gives the full form as Narcotic Drugs and Psychotropic Substance Act.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the full form given in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"How many chapters does the NDPSA Act have?","options":["5","10","16","12"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the NDPSA Act has 5 chapters.  Why B wrong: 10 chapters is the MHA-1987.  Why C wrong: 16 chapters is the MHCA-2017.  Why D wrong: 12 is a specific MHCA-2017 chapter."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the NDPSA Act, which chapter relates to offense and penalties?","options":["Chapter 1","Chapter 2","Chapter 3","Chapter 4"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter 4 relates to offense and penalties.  Why A wrong: Chapter 1 relates to definitions and terminology.  Why B wrong: Chapter 2 relates to authorities and officers.  Why C wrong: Chapter 3 relates to prohibition, control and regulation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The Mental Health Care Act 2017 (MHCA-2017), which repeals and replaces the Mental Health Act 1987, came into force on:","options":["22 May 1987","1 April 1993","29 May 2018","April 2017"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The MHCA-2017 was passed in April 2017 and came into force on 29 May 2018.  Why A wrong: 22 May 1987 is when the 1987 Act was passed.  Why B wrong: 1 April 1993 is enforcement of the 1987 Act.  Why D wrong: April 2017 is when it was passed, not enforced."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The total number of chapters in the Mental Health Care Act 2017 is:","options":["10","12","14","16"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the total number of chapters in the MHCA-2017 is 16.  Why A wrong: 10 chapters is the 1987 Act.  Why B wrong: 12 is a specific chapter (admission/discharge).  Why C wrong: 14 is not the total."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the MHCA-2017, admission and discharge of mentally ill clients is done under which chapter number?","options":["Chapter 3","Chapter 5","Chapter 12","Chapter 16"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states admission & discharge of mentally ill clients is done under chapter 12 (Admission, treatment and discharge).  Why A wrong: Chapter 3 covers Advance Directives.  Why B wrong: Chapter 5 covers rights of persons with mental illness.  Why D wrong: Chapter 16 is Miscellaneous."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the revised MHA-2017, ECT is NOT recommended in which situation(s)? (Select all that apply)","options":["For minors","Without the use of muscle relaxant","Without the use of anaesthesia","In elderly patients above 60 years"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The revised MHA-2017 states ECT is not recommended for mentally ill persons without muscle relaxant or anaesthesia, and not recommended for minors.  Why D wrong: Age above 60 is not mentioned as a contraindication in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Under the revised MHA-2017, violation of the Act by a person/institute attracts what penalty?","options":["Fine of Rs 10,000/- or up to 6 months imprisonment","Fine of Rs 50,000/- only","Up to 2 years imprisonment","License cancellation only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states financial punishment of Rs 10,000/- or up to 6 months imprisonment for violating MHA-2017.  Why B, C, D wrong: These figures/penalties are not stated in the book."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Regarding decriminalisation of suicide under MHA-2017, a person attempting suicide is:","options":["Punished under Section 309 IPC","Presumed to be under severe stress and shall not be punished","Sent to prison for 6 months","Fined Rs 10,000/-"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MHA-2017 decriminalises suicide — the person is presumed to be under severe stress and shall not be punished; previously (IMHA-1987) it was punishable under Section 309 IPC.  Why A wrong: Section 309 IPC punishment was the previous position, now removed.  Why C, D wrong: These penalties apply to Act violations, not suicide attempts."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Previously, under IMHA-1987, an attempted suicide was punishable under which section of the Indian Penal Code?","options":["Section 302","Section 309","Section 376","Section 420"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states attempted suicide was previously punishable under Section 309 of the Indian Penal Code.  Why A wrong: Section 302 relates to murder.  Why C wrong: Section 376 relates to rape.  Why D wrong: Section 420 relates to cheating."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the MHA-2017, the provision of an Advance Directive falls under which chapter?","options":["Chapter I","Chapter III","Chapter V","Chapter XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the provision of Advance Directive is under chapter number 3rd (Chapter III — Advance Directives / Living will).  Why A wrong: Chapter I is Preliminary.  Why C wrong: Chapter V is Rights of persons with mental illness.  Why D wrong: Chapter XII is Admission, treatment and discharge."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the MHA-2017 chapter table, Chapter II deals with:","options":["Preliminary","Mental illness and capacity to make mental healthcare and treatment decisions","Advance Directives","Nominated Representative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter II is Mental Illness and capacity to make mental healthcare and treatment decisions.  Why A wrong: Chapter I is Preliminary.  Why C wrong: Chapter III is Advance Directives.  Why D wrong: Chapter IV is Nominated Representative."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the MHA-2017, \"Rights of persons with mental illness\" is covered under which chapter?","options":["Chapter IV","Chapter V","Chapter VI","Chapter VII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter V covers Rights of persons with mental illness.  Why A wrong: Chapter IV is Nominated Representative.  Why C wrong: Chapter VI is Duties of appropriate government.  Why D wrong: Chapter VII is Central Mental Health Authority."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the MHA-2017 chapter table, the Central Mental Health Authority and State Mental Health Authority are covered respectively under:","options":["Chapter V and Chapter VI","Chapter VII and Chapter VIII","Chapter IX and Chapter X","Chapter XI and Chapter XII"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chapter VII is Central Mental Health Authority and Chapter VIII is State Mental Health Authority.  Why A wrong: Chapters V/VI are Rights and Duties of government.  Why C wrong: Chapters IX/X are Finance and Mental Health Establishments.  Why D wrong: Chapters XI/XII are Review Boards and Admission/treatment/discharge."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The full form of POLST (used in the context of advance directives) is:","options":["Palliative Orders for Life-Saving Treatment","Physician Orders for Life Sustaining Treatment","Patient Options for Long-term Support and Therapy","Personal Orders for Living and Sustaining Treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text gives the full form of POLST as Physician Orders for Life Sustaining Treatment.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the full form given."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"A DNR (Do Not Resuscitate) Order, listed as a type of advance directive, states that:","options":["All medications should be stopped","You don't want CPR if your heart stops or you cease breathing","Your organs should be donated","Someone else manages your finances"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines a DNR order as stating you don't want CPR if your heart stops or you cease breathing.  Why A wrong: Stopping medication is not the definition given.  Why C wrong: That describes organ donation directive.  Why D wrong: That describes durable power of attorney."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"A \"durable power of attorney for health care (DPA)\" as a type of advance directive:","options":["Is a verbal instruction used in the absence of written instructions","Appoints someone to manage your healthcare decisions when you're incapacitated","Instructs others to donate your organs","States you don't want CPR"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DPA appoints someone to manage your healthcare decisions when you're incapacitated.  Why A wrong: That describes verbal instructions.  Why C wrong: That describes organ donation directive.  Why D wrong: That describes a DNR order."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In the Nurse-Client Relationship (NCR), which is described as the MOST important factor for the development of trust?","options":["Giving advice","Honest and open communication","Providing medication","Physical touch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The book states honest and open communication is the most important for the development of trust.  Why A wrong: Giving advice is a non-therapeutic technique.  Why C wrong: Medication is not the stated key to trust.  Why D wrong: Touch (haptic communication) is a technique but not stated as most important for trust."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The major aim of the nurse-client relationship is to develop:","options":["Dependency on the nurse","Problem-solving abilities and defence mechanisms","Medication compliance only","Physical rehabilitation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the major aim is to develop problem-solving abilities and defence mechanisms.  Why A wrong: Dependency is not a goal.  Why C, D wrong: These are not stated as the major aim."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following are principle components of the Nurse-Client Relationship? (Select all that apply)","options":["Trust","Respect","Eye contact","Empathy","Giving advice"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The principle components of NCR are trust, respect, eye contact, genuine interest and empathy.  Why E wrong: Giving advice is a non-therapeutic technique, not a principle component."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The phase of the nurse-client relationship that begins BEFORE the nurse's initial contact with the client — also called the self-exploration and self-awareness phase — is the:","options":["Orientation phase","Pre-interaction phase","Working phase","Termination phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The pre-interaction phase begins before the nurse's initial contact with the client and is known as the self-exploration & self-awareness phase.  Why A wrong: Orientation begins when nurse and client meet.  Why C wrong: Working phase is where problems are discussed.  Why D wrong: Termination phase ends the relationship."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"During the pre-interaction phase, the nurse is expected to:","options":["Discuss the client's problems in depth","Explore own feelings, values, attitude and fears, and analyze own strengths/limitations","Terminate the relationship","Ask the client to identify problems"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the pre-interaction phase the nurse explores own feelings, fantasies, personal values, attitudes and fears and analyzes own professional strengths and limitations.  Why A wrong: Discussing problems in depth is the working phase.  Why C wrong: Terminating is the termination phase.  Why D wrong: Client identifying problems marks the orientation phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The orientation (introductory) phase of the nurse-client relationship begins when the nurse and client meet and ends when:","options":["The nurse explores own feelings","The client begins to identify problems","The goals are achieved and relationship ends","Transference becomes an issue"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The orientation phase begins when nurse and client meet and ends when the client begins to identify problems.  Why A wrong: Exploring own feelings is the pre-interaction phase.  Why C wrong: Goals achieved/relationship ending is the termination phase.  Why D wrong: Transference is an issue in the working phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"During which phase are transference and counter-transference likely to become an issue?","options":["Pre-interaction phase","Orientation phase","Working phase","Termination phase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The text states in the working phase, transference and counter-transference may become an issue, and anxiety level is also an issue.  Why A, B, D wrong: These phases do not specifically list transference/counter-transference as their key issue."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the text, which is described as the MOST difficult but most important phase of the therapeutic nurse-client relationship?","options":["Pre-interaction phase","Orientation phase","Working phase","Termination phase"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The text states the termination phase is the most difficult phase but the most important phase of the therapeutic relationship (FAQ).  Why A, B, C wrong: These are not described as the most difficult/important phase."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"During the termination phase, if the desired goals are NOT achieved, the appropriate action is to:","options":["Discharge the client immediately","Restart the nurse-client relationship again","Refer to another hospital","Continue medication only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states if desired goals are not achieved, then restart again the nurse-client relationship.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the actions specified in the text."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which is an \"Important Task for a Therapeutic Relationship\" per the text?","options":["Using room number or diagnosis to refer to patients","Using the name of the patient rather than labels such as room number or diagnosis","Delaying answering the call bell","Withholding explanations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists using the name of the patient rather than labels such as room number or diagnosis as an important task.  Why A wrong: Using room number/diagnosis is what should be avoided.  Why C wrong: The text says to answer the call bell immediately.  Why D wrong: The text says to provide explanations at the client's level of understanding."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Regarding a client's call bell, the therapeutic nursing task is to:","options":["Answer it within an hour","Answer it immediately","Ignore repeated calls","Delegate all responses to family"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text lists \"answering client's call bell immediately\" as an important task.  Why A, C, D wrong: These contradict the immediate-response instruction."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following are elements of a Nurse-Patient Contract? (Select all that apply)","options":["Roles of nurse and patient","Purpose of the relationship","Conditions for termination","Confidentiality","Client's medication doses"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Elements of a nurse-patient contract include names of individuals, roles, responsibilities, expectations, purpose, meeting location and time, conditions for termination, and confidentiality.  Why E wrong: Medication doses are not listed among the contract elements."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following is listed under a client's rights WHILE HOSPITALIZED?","options":["Right to marry or divorce","Right to vote","Right to consent or refuse a treatment","Right to habeas corpus"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Client's rights while hospitalized include the right to respectful care and the right to consent or refuse a treatment.  Why A, B, D wrong: Right to marry/divorce, vote, and habeas corpus are listed specifically under \"Mentally Ill Client's Rights.\""},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following are specifically listed among a \"Mentally Ill Client's Rights\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Right to marry or divorce","Right to vote","Right to habeas corpus (written request for release from the hospital)","Right to education","Right to unlimited visitors at any hour"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Mentally ill client's rights include right to marry/divorce, privacy & confidentiality, informed consent, treatment and refuse treatment, refuse experimental research, dignity and respect, keep clothing, religious freedom and manage property, contractual agreement, education, habeas corpus, and vote.  Why E wrong: Unlimited visitors at any hour is not listed among these rights."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The right that allows a mentally ill client to make a written request for release from the hospital is called:","options":["Right to informed consent","Right to habeas corpus","Right to privacy","Right to refuse treatment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines the right to habeas corpus as a written request for release from the hospital.  Why A wrong: Informed consent is about agreeing to treatment.  Why C wrong: Privacy relates to confidentiality.  Why D wrong: Refuse treatment is a separate right."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In therapeutic communication, the technique of repeating what the client says to provide an opportunity for clarification is:","options":["Paraphrasing","Restating","Reflecting","Focusing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines restating as repeating what the client says to provide the opportunity for clarification if needed.  Why A wrong: Paraphrasing conveys feelings/thoughts in similar words.  Why C wrong: Reflecting is not the listed term.  Why D wrong: Focusing brings a digressing client back to the topic."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The therapeutic technique of bringing a client or family member, who may digress, back to what was originally being discussed is called:","options":["Restating","Focusing","Validating","Summarizing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Focusing is defined as bringing the client or family member, who may digress, back to what was originally being discussed.  Why A wrong: Restating is repeating client's words.  Why C wrong: Validating ensures shared interpretation of the topic.  Why D wrong: Summarizing states briefly what has been discussed."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The therapeutic communication technique of conveying the client's feelings and thoughts in similar words to show the message was understood is:","options":["Restating","Paraphrasing","Informing","Accepting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paraphrasing is defined as putting the client's feelings and thoughts in similar words to convey that the message was understood.  Why A wrong: Restating is repeating the client's exact words.  Why C wrong: Informing provides information to decrease anxiety.  Why D wrong: Accepting means always accepting the client's conversation."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Touch, as a therapeutic communication technique, is also called:","options":["Kinesics","Haptic communication","Proxemics","Paralanguage"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states touch is also called haptic communication.  Why A, C, D wrong: These terms are not used in the text for touch."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"According to the text, which is an example of an explorative (open) question rather than a closed-ended question?","options":["\"You had dinner?\"","\"What food will you be taking in dinner?\"","\"Are you feeling better?\"","\"Did you sleep well?\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text uses \"what food you will be taking in dinner\" as an example of an explorative question, whereas \"you had dinner\" is a closed-ended question.  Why A wrong: \"You had dinner?\" is given as the closed-ended example.  Why C, D wrong: These are yes/no (closed) questions, not explorative."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The therapeutic technique of informing (providing the client with information) is beneficial because it:","options":["Increases client dependency","Decreases client anxiety","Encourages digression","Replaces the need for consent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states informing provides the client with information and information decreases client anxiety (most people harbour fear of the unknown).  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not stated benefits of informing."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which of the following is a NON-THERAPEUTIC communication technique?","options":["Validating","Silence","False reassurance / giving advice","Clarification"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The non-therapeutic list includes false reassurance and giving advice (reassuring \"attempts to do magic with words\").  Why A wrong: Validating is therapeutic.  Why B wrong: Silence is therapeutic (gives time for interaction).  Why D wrong: Clarification is therapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In non-therapeutic communication, \"continual repetition of the client's phrases\" is termed:","options":["Restating","Parroting","Reflecting","Focusing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines parroting as continual repetition of the client's phrases (a non-therapeutic technique).  Why A wrong: Restating (therapeutic) is repeating for clarification, not continual repetition.  Why C wrong: Reflecting is not the listed term.  Why D wrong: Focusing brings the client back to the topic."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Why is asking the client \"why\" questions considered non-therapeutic?","options":["It saves time","It may put the client on the defensive","It shows empathy","It provides reassurance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text states asking for explanations using the word \"why\" may put the client on the defensive.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the stated reasons; \"why\" questions are harmful, not helpful."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"In non-therapeutic communication, \"reassuring\" is described as:","options":["Refusal to discuss topics with the client","Attempts to do magic with words","Failure to receive the client's message","Approving or disapproving statements"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines reassuring as \"attempts to do magic with words.\"  Why A wrong: That defines rejecting.  Why C wrong: That defines failing to listen.  Why D wrong: That defines being judgmental."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The non-therapeutic technique described as \"refusal to discuss topics with the client\" is:","options":["Parroting","Rejecting","Failing to probe","Being judgmental"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines rejecting as refusal to discuss topics with the client.  Why A wrong: Parroting is continual repetition of phrases.  Why C wrong: Failing to probe is inadequate data collection.  Why D wrong: Being judgmental is approving/disapproving statements."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"\"Being judgmental\" as a non-therapeutic technique refers to:","options":["Approving or disapproving statements","Continual repetition of phrases","Attempts to do magic with words","Failure to receive the intended message"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text defines being judgmental as approving or disapproving statements.  Why B wrong: That is parroting.  Why C wrong: That is reassuring.  Why D wrong: That is failing to listen."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"\"Failing to probe\" as a non-therapeutic technique represents:","options":["Continual repetition of client phrases","Inadequate data collection with vague descriptions and inadequate answers","Refusal to discuss topics","Approving statements"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Failing to probe is defined as inadequate data collection represented by eliciting vague descriptions, getting inadequate answers, following standard forms too closely, and not exploring the client's interpretation.  Why A wrong: That is parroting.  Why C wrong: That is rejecting.  Why D wrong: That is being judgmental."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"Which therapeutic technique is used to \"make sure you and the client are interpreting the topic and message the same way\"?","options":["Restating","Validating","Summarizing","Silence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Validating is defined as making sure you and the client are interpreting the topic and message the same way, encouraging correct understanding.  Why A wrong: Restating repeats the client's words for clarification.  Why C wrong: Summarizing briefly states what was discussed.  Why D wrong: Silence provides time for interaction."},{"section":"Batch 001 · Introduction to psychiatry/mental health nursing…","question":"The therapeutic technique of \"broad openings and general leads\" is used to:","options":["Repeat the client's words","Allow the client to lead the conversation and select topics of concern","Bring a digressing client back to topic","Provide the client with information"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Broad openings and general leads allow the client to lead the conversation and select topics that may be of concern (e.g., \"What would you like to discuss today?\").  Why A wrong: Repeating words is restating.  Why C wrong: Bringing back to topic is focusing.  Why D wrong: Providing information is informing."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which assessment method is described as the most important and commonly used method to diagnose mental disorders?","options":["Mini-Mental Status Examination (MMSE)","Mental Status Examination (MSE)","Rorschach inkblot test","Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the MSE \"is most important and commonly used method to diagnosis of mental disorders.\"  Why A wrong: MMSE is a brief neuropsychological screening test for dementia/cognitive impairment, not the general diagnostic method.  Why C wrong: Rorschach is a projective test, not described here.  Why D wrong: TAT is a projective test, not mentioned as the primary diagnostic method."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Mental Status Examination (MSE) is an important part of the clinical assessment process in which fields of practice?","options":["Orthopaedic and surgical practice","Neurological and psychiatric practice","Obstetric and paediatric practice","Dermatological and cardiac practice"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MSE is an important part of the clinical assessment process in neurological and psychiatric practice.  Why A/C/D wrong: These fields are not mentioned; MSE specifically applies to neurological and psychiatric practice."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Mental Status Examination is best defined as a structured way of observing and describing a patient's:","options":["Physical functioning over a week","Psychological functioning at a given point in time","Motor reflexes and gait","Nutritional status over months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines MSE as \"a structured way of observing and describing a patient's psychological functioning at a given point in time.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: MSE assesses psychological functioning at a point in time, not physical, motor, or nutritional parameters."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The mnemonic \"ASEPTIC\" is used to remember the components/domains of the MSE. What does the letter \"A\" stand for?","options":["Attention","Appearance/Behaviour","Affect","Anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the ASEPTIC mnemonic, A → Appearance/Behaviour.  Why A wrong: Attention is part of MMSE scoring, not the \"A\" of ASEPTIC.  Why C/D wrong: Affect and anxiety are not the \"A\" component; emotion (mood and affect) is the \"E.\""},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the ASEPTIC mnemonic for the MSE components, the letter \"E\" stands for:","options":["Emotion (Mood and Affect)","Energy","Eye contact","Environment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: E → Emotion (Mood and Affect) per the ASEPTIC mnemonic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed as MSE domains in the mnemonic."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which of the following are components/domains of the Mental Status Examination as given by the ASEPTIC mnemonic? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Appearance/Behaviour","Speech","Perception","Insight and Judgement","Cognition"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: ASEPTIC = Appearance/Behaviour, Speech, Emotion (mood and affect), Perception, Thought content and process, Insight and judgement, and Cognition — all listed options are valid domains.  Why all correct: Each option is explicitly one of the seven MSE domains in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the ASEPTIC mnemonic, the letter \"T\" refers to which MSE component?","options":["Thought Content and Process","Touch","Temperament","Tremor"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: T → Thought Content and Process.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not listed MSE domains."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The MSE should not be confused with the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE), which is a brief neuropsychological screening test primarily for:","options":["Depression","Dementia","Schizophrenia","Anxiety disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MMSE is a brief neuropsychological screening test for dementia.  Why A/C/D wrong: MMSE screens for cognitive impairment/dementia, not these disorders."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"MSE can be done by which two ways?","options":["By observation and by interview","By blood test and imaging","By questionnaire and by reflex testing","By EEG and by CT scan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"MSE can be done by two way by observation and by interview.\"  Why B/C/D wrong: These methods are not mentioned for MSE."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The MMSE (Mini-Mental Status Examination) is also known by which other name(s)?","options":["Higher mental function test or Folstein Test","Beck's test","Hamilton scale","Glasgow Coma Scale"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MMSE is also known as higher mental function test or \"Folkston's Test\" — this is a misspelling of Folstein Test, named after Marshal Folstein who introduced the MMSE in 1975 (see MCQ 11).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different, unrelated scales.  Note: Source printed \"Folkston's Test\"; keyed as the factually-correct Folstein Test per repo convention."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Who originally introduced the MMSE, and in which year?","options":["Folstein et al. in 1975","Selye in 1969","Maslow in 1954","Kübler-Ross in 1969"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MMSE was originally introduced by Folstein et al. in 1975.  Why B wrong: Selye is associated with GAS.  Why C wrong: Maslow (1954) introduced the hierarchy of needs.  Why D wrong: Kübler-Ross (1969) gave the grief stages model."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The MMSE is used to assess which of the following in mentally ill clients?","options":["Motor strength","Cognitive impairment","Sensory acuity","Emotional lability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states MMSE is used to assess cognitive impairment in mentally ill clients.  Why A/C/D wrong: MMSE assesses cognition, not motor strength, sensory acuity, or emotional lability."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"What is the maximum score for the MMSE?","options":["24","25","30","100"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the maximum score for the MMSE is 30.  Why A wrong: 24 is the cut-off below which results are usually abnormal.  Why B wrong: 25 or higher is classed as normal, but the maximum is 30.  Why D wrong: 100 is not the MMSE maximum."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"On the MMSE, a score of what value or higher is classed as normal?","options":["20 or higher","25 or higher","15 or higher","30 or higher"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states a score of 25 or higher is classed as normal.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated normal cut-off of 25."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"An MMSE score below which value is usually considered abnormal, indicating possible cognitive impairment?","options":["Below 24","Below 30","Below 19","Below 10"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states if the MMSE score is below 24, the result is usually considered abnormal, indicating possible cognitive impairment.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the stated abnormal threshold of below 24."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the MMSE interpretation table, a score of 24–30 indicates which degree of impairment?","options":["Mild cognitive impairment","No cognitive impairment","Moderate cognitive impairment","Severe cognitive impairment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The interpretation table maps a score of 24–30 to \"No cognitive impairment.\"  Why A wrong: Mild = 19–23.  Why C wrong: Moderate = 10–18.  Why D wrong: Severe = <9."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the MMSE interpretation table, which score range indicates mild cognitive impairment?","options":["24–30","19–23","10–18","<9"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table maps 19–23 to mild cognitive impairment.  Why A wrong: 24–30 = no impairment.  Why C wrong: 10–18 = moderate.  Why D wrong: <9 = severe."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"On the MMSE interpretation table, a score range of 10–18 corresponds to which degree of cognitive impairment?","options":["No impairment","Mild","Moderate","Severe"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The table maps 10–18 to moderate cognitive impairment.  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to 24–30, 19–23, and <9 respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the MMSE interpretation table, a score of less than 9 (<9) indicates:","options":["No cognitive impairment","Mild cognitive impairment","Moderate cognitive impairment","Severe cognitive impairment"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The table maps <9 to severe cognitive impairment.  Why A/B/C wrong: These correspond to higher score ranges."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the components of the MMSE, \"Orientation to time\" and \"Orientation to place\" are each allotted how many points?","options":["3 points each","5 points each","2 points each","6 points each"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The MMSE components table gives Orientation to time = 5 and Orientation to place = 5.  Why A wrong: 3 points is for Registration and Recall.  Why C wrong: 2 points is for Language.  Why D wrong: 6 points is for Complex commands."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the MMSE components table, the \"Attention and calculation\" domain (e.g., serial sevens or spelling a word backwards) is worth how many points?","options":["5 points","3 points","2 points","1 point"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Attention and calculation = 5 points; it involves serial sevens or spelling word backwards (serial sevens may be more appropriate where English is not the first language).  Why B/C/D wrong: 3 = registration/recall, 2 = language, 1 = repetition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the MMSE, \"Registration\" (repeating named prompts) and \"Recall\" (registration recall) are each worth:","options":["5 points","3 points","2 points","1 point"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Registration = 3 (repeating named prompts) and Recall = 3 (registration recall).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are values for other MMSE domains."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the MMSE components, the \"Language\" domain (naming a pencil and a watch) is worth how many points?","options":["1 point","2 points","3 points","5 points"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Language = 2 points (naming a pencil and a watch).  Why A wrong: 1 point is for Repetition (speaking back a phrase).  Why C/D wrong: These are values for other domains."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the MMSE, \"Repetition\" (speaking back a phrase) is worth how many point(s)?","options":["1 point","2 points","3 points","6 points"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Repetition = 1 point (speaking back a phrase).  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to other domains."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the MMSE components, \"Complex commands\" (which can involve drawing figures) is allotted how many points?","options":["3 points","5 points","6 points","2 points"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Complex commands = 6 points, varies and can involve drawing figures shown.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are values for other MMSE domains."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Personality is best defined as the enduring characteristics and behavior that comprise a person's unique adjustment to life, including major traits, interests, drives, values, self-concept, abilities, and:","options":["Reflexes","Emotional patterns","Blood group","Height and weight"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (KPSC-2022) defines personality as including major traits, interests, drives, values, self-concept, abilities, and emotional patterns.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not part of the definition of personality given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which of the following are listed as factors affecting personality? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Environmental factors","Hereditary","Cultural factors","Intelligence","Sex differences"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists 8 factors: environmental, physical, situational, hereditary, family and social, cultural, intelligence, and sex differences — all listed options are valid.  Why all correct: Each option is explicitly one of the factors affecting personality."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Jung's classification of personality divides people into which two types?","options":["Endomorph and ectomorph","Extrovert and introvert","Pyknic and asthenic","Sanguine and choleric"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Jung's classification of personality = extrovert and introvert.  Why A wrong: Endomorph/ectomorph belong to Sheldon.  Why C wrong: Pyknic/asthenic belong to Kretschmer.  Why D wrong: Sanguine/choleric belong to Hippocrates."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Sheldon's classification of personality includes which three body/personality types?","options":["Endomorph, mesomorph, and ectomorph","Pyknic, athletic, and asthenic","Sanguine, choleric, and phlegmatic","Extrovert, introvert, and ambivert"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sheldon's classification = endomorph, mesomorph, and ectomorph.  Why B wrong: These are Kretschmer's types.  Why C wrong: These are Hippocrates' temperaments.  Why D wrong: Extrovert/introvert are Jung's; ambivert is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Sheldon's classification, which type is characterized as \"Relaxed, sociable, tolerant, comfort loving, peaceful\" with a \"plump, buxom, developed visceral structure\" shape?","options":["Ectomorph","Mesomorph","Endomorph","Pyknic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sheldon's endomorph = relaxed, sociable, tolerant, comfort loving, peaceful; plump, buxom, developed visceral structure.  Why A wrong: Ectomorph = quiet, fragile, restrained, non-assertive, sensitive; lean, delicate, poor muscles.  Why B wrong: Mesomorph = active, assertive, vigorous, combative; muscular.  Why D wrong: Pyknic is a Kretschmer body type, not Sheldon's."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Sheldon's classification, the \"Muscular\" body shape with an \"active, assertive, vigorous, combative\" character corresponds to which type?","options":["Endomorph","Mesomorph","Ectomorph","Athletic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mesomorph = muscular shape, active/assertive/vigorous/combative character.  Why A wrong: Endomorph = plump, comfort-loving.  Why C wrong: Ectomorph = lean, quiet, sensitive.  Why D wrong: Athletic is Kretschmer's type."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Sheldon's classification, which type has a \"lean, delicate, poor muscles\" shape and is \"quiet, fragile, restrained, non-assertive, sensitive\"?","options":["Endomorph","Mesomorph","Ectomorph","Asthenic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sheldon's ectomorph = lean, delicate, poor muscles; quiet, fragile, restrained, non-assertive, sensitive.  Why A/B wrong: Endomorph is plump/sociable; mesomorph is muscular/assertive.  Why D wrong: Asthenic is Kretschmer's thin body type, not Sheldon's."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Kretschmer's classification of personality includes which of the following body types?","options":["Pyknic, athletic, and asthenic","Endomorph, mesomorph, and ectomorph","Extrovert and introvert","Choleric, melancholic, and phlegmatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kretschmer's classification = pyknic, athletic, and asthenic.  Why B wrong: These are Sheldon's types.  Why C wrong: These are Jung's types.  Why D wrong: These are Hippocrates' temperaments."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Kretschmer's classification, the \"pyknic\" body type is described as:","options":["Thin and lean tall, no muscle, only bone","Fat types, in whom fat is more than muscle; social and helping to others","Healthy, balance between muscles and bone; energetic optimist","Muscular, active and combative"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kretschmer's pyknic = fat types (fat more than muscle); personality is social and helping to others.  Why A wrong: That describes the asthenic type.  Why C wrong: That describes the athletic type.  Why D wrong: That describes Sheldon's mesomorph."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Kretschmer's classification, which body type is \"healthy, balance between muscles and bone development\" with an \"energetic, optimist can adjust to any situation\" personality?","options":["Pyknic","Athletic","Asthenic","Ectomorph"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kretschmer's athletic type = healthy, balanced muscle/bone; energetic, optimist, adjusts to any situation.  Why A wrong: Pyknic = fat, social/helping.  Why C wrong: Asthenic = thin, unsociable/shy/pessimist.  Why D wrong: Ectomorph is Sheldon's, not Kretschmer's."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Kretschmer's classification, the \"asthenic\" body type (thin and lean tall, no muscle, only bone) has which personality characteristics?","options":["Social and helping to others","Energetic optimist, adjusts to any situation","Unsociable, shy, pessimist, and always alone","Relaxed, sociable, comfort loving"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Kretschmer's asthenic = unsociable, shy, pessimist, and always alone.  Why A wrong: Social/helping = pyknic.  Why B wrong: Energetic optimist = athletic.  Why D wrong: That describes Sheldon's endomorph."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Hippocrates' classification of personality (temperaments) includes which four types?","options":["Sanguine, choleric, melancholic, and phlegmatic","Endomorph, mesomorph, ectomorph, and athletic","Pyknic, athletic, asthenic, and pyretic","Extrovert, introvert, ambivert, and neurotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hippocrates' classification = sanguine, choleric, melancholic, and phlegmatic.  Why B wrong: Sheldon's/Kretschmer's mix.  Why C wrong: Kretschmer-based, \"pyretic\" is invented.  Why D wrong: Jung-based, not Hippocrates."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Hippocrates' classification, which temperament is described as \"emotionally weak, bodily strong and easily tempted\"?","options":["Melancholic","Phlegmatic","Choleric","Sanguine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Choleric = emotionally weak, bodily strong and easily tempted.  Why A wrong: Melancholic = emotionally and bodily weak, pessimist.  Why B wrong: Phlegmatic = emotionally strong, able to control emotions, bodily weak, lazy, always happy.  Why D wrong: Sanguine = bodily strong, energetic, control type, optimist."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Hippocrates' classification, which temperament is \"emotionally and bodily weak - pessimist\"?","options":["Sanguine","Choleric","Melancholic","Phlegmatic"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Melancholic = emotionally and bodily weak, pessimist.  Why A/B/D wrong: These correspond to other temperaments (sanguine=optimist, choleric=easily tempted, phlegmatic=lazy/always happy)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Hippocrates, which temperament is \"emotionally strong, able to control his emotions, bodily weak, lazy type, always happy\"?","options":["Phlegmatic","Sanguine","Choleric","Melancholic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phlegmatic = emotionally strong, controls emotions, bodily weak, lazy, always happy.  Why B wrong: Sanguine = bodily strong, energetic, optimist.  Why C/D wrong: Choleric = easily tempted; melancholic = pessimist."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Hippocrates' classification, the temperament described as \"bodily strong, energetic, control type, an optimist\" is:","options":["Phlegmatic","Melancholic","Sanguine","Choleric"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Sanguine = bodily strong, energetic, control type, an optimist.  Why A/B/D wrong: These match other temperaments."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Big Five personality traits are also known as which model of personality?","options":["The Three-Factor Model","The Five-Factor Model of Personality","The Topographical Model","The Structural Model"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Big Five is also known as \"The Five-Factor Model of Personality,\" a grouping of five unique characteristics.  Why A wrong: There is no \"Three-Factor Model\" named here.  Why C/D wrong: These are Freud's models, not the Big Five."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the Big Five (Five-Factor Model) figure, which of the following are the five personality traits? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Openness to experience","Conscientiousness","Extraversion","Agreeableness","Neuroticism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The Big Five figure labels Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism.  Why all correct: Each is one of the five traits shown in the figure (OCEAN)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud's Organisation of Personality (Topographical model), which part of the mind \"works at present time\"?","options":["Conscious mind","Sub conscious mind","Unconscious mind","Preconscious mind"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The conscious mind works at present time.  Why B wrong: Sub conscious (semi conscious) works between present and past.  Why C wrong: Unconscious mind is the large part, works in the past.  Why D wrong: Preconscious is not separately defined in this list (the three are conscious, sub conscious, unconscious)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Freud's topographical model, the \"sub conscious mind\" (also known as the semi conscious mind) works:","options":["Only at present time","In between present and past","Only in the distant past","Only during sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The sub conscious (semi conscious) mind is worked in between present and past.  Why A wrong: That is the conscious mind.  Why C wrong: The unconscious mind works in the past.  Why D wrong: Not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud, which part of the mind is described as the large part of the mind that works in the past?","options":["Conscious mind","Sub conscious mind","Unconscious mind","Ego"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The unconscious mind is the large part of the mind, working in the past.  Why A/B wrong: Conscious works in present; sub conscious between present and past.  Why D wrong: Ego is part of the structural model, not the topographical model."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud's Structure of the Personality (ESIC-2024), the three components are the Id, Ego, and Super ego. Which one is based on morality?","options":["Id","Ego","Super ego","Libido"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Super ego is based on morality.  Why A wrong: Id is \"pleasure full.\"  Why B wrong: Ego is based on reality.  Why D wrong: Libido is not one of the three structures listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which component of Freud's structural model is a primary thinking level process, works in the unconscious mind, and always works as the pleasure principle?","options":["The Id","The Ego","The Super Ego","The Preconscious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Id is a primary thinking level process, works in the unconscious mind, and always works as the pleasure principle.  Why B wrong: Ego is secondary thinking, reality principle.  Why C wrong: Super ego is tertiary thinking, morality principle.  Why D wrong: Preconscious is a topographical term, not a structure."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Freud's structural model, which component works as the reality principle, works in the conscious mind, and maintains balance between the Id and the Super ego?","options":["The Id","The Ego","The Super Ego","The Libido"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Ego is secondary thinking process, works on reality principle, works in the conscious mind, regulates the Id, and maintains balance between Id and super ego.  Why A wrong: Id = pleasure principle, unconscious.  Why C wrong: Super ego = morality principle.  Why D wrong: Libido is not a structural component."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Ego is also known as which \"branch\" of the mind?","options":["Moral branch of the mind","Executive branch of the mind","Pleasure branch of the mind","Conscience branch of the mind"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Ego is also known as the executive branch of the mind.  Why A wrong: The Super ego is the moral branch of the mind.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the terms used for the Ego."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud, the Super Ego is tertiary thinking level and develops after what age?","options":["After 6 years of age","After 3 years of age","After 12 years of age","At birth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Super Ego is tertiary thinking level and developed after 6 years of age.  Why B/C/D wrong: These ages are not stated for Super Ego development."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Super Ego acts as the \"co-science\" (conscience), is based on the morality principle (what is right or wrong), works in the unconscious mind, and is also known as the:","options":["Executive branch of the mind","Moral branch of the mind","Reality branch of the mind","Pleasure branch of the mind"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Super Ego is also known as the moral branch of the mind and is based on morality (right or wrong).  Why A wrong: Executive branch = Ego.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the terms used for the Super Ego."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Freud's Structural Model table, the Id works on which principle, and what type of process is it?","options":["Reality principle (Psychological)","Pleasure principle (Biological)","Moral/ideal principle (social)","Spiritual principle (cultural)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table states the Id works on the pleasure principle (Biological).  Why A wrong: Reality principle (Psychological) is the Ego.  Why C wrong: Moral/ideal principle (social) is the Super ego.  Why D wrong: Not listed."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Freud's Structural Model examples, the statement \"I want that right now\" represents which component?","options":["Id","Ego","Super ego","Preconscious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The example \"I want that right now\" is listed under the Id (pleasure principle).  Why B wrong: Ego example = \"Let's figure out a way to work together.\"  Why C wrong: Super ego example = \"Good people don't think about those things.\"  Why D wrong: Not a structural component."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud's psychosexual stages, the Oral Stage (birth to 18 months) is characterized by the child getting pleasure from the mouth by:","options":["Holding and letting go of body waste","Sucking, eating, biting, and chewing","Own primary sex organs","Heterosexual love"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Oral stage (birth to 18 months) = pleasure from mouth by sucking, eating, biting, and chewing.  Why A wrong: That is the Anal stage.  Why C wrong: That is the Phallic stage.  Why D wrong: That is the Genital stage."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Freud's psychosexual development, the Anal Stage occurs at which age and is characterized by pleasure from what?","options":["Birth to 18 months; sucking and chewing","18 months to 3 years; holding and letting go of body waste","3 to 6 years; own primary sex organs","6 to 12 years; socialization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anal stage = 18 months to 3 years; pleasure from holding and letting go of body waste.  Why A wrong: That is the Oral stage.  Why C wrong: That is the Phallic stage.  Why D wrong: That is the Latency stage."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud, during which stage (3 to 6 years) does the child derive pleasure from his or her own primary sex organs, with development of the electra complex (female) and oedipus complex (male)?","options":["Oral stage","Anal stage","Phallic stage","Latency stage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Phallic stage (3–6 years) involves pleasure from own primary sex organs and the electra (female) & oedipus (male) complexes.  Why A/B wrong: Oral and anal stages precede it with different characteristics.  Why D wrong: Latency stage is 6–12 years with denial of opposite-sex attraction."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Electra complex (female) and Oedipus complex (male) develop during which Freudian psychosexual stage?","options":["Oral","Phallic","Genital","Latency"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source places the electra and oedipus complexes in the Phallic stage.  Why A/C/D wrong: These stages do not involve these complexes per the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Freud's Latency Stage (6 years to about 12 years), the child:","options":["Awakens sexuality and desires heterosexual love","Denies attraction for parent of opposite sex, identifies with same-sex parent, and learns to socialize","Gets pleasure from holding body waste","Gets pleasure from sucking and biting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Latency stage = child denies attraction for parent of opposite sex, identifies with same-sex parent, and learns to socialize.  Why A wrong: That is the Genital stage.  Why C wrong: That is the Anal stage.  Why D wrong: That is the Oral stage."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Freud's Genital Stage (12 years to adulthood) is characterized by:","options":["Awakening of sexuality and desire for heterosexual love","Toilet training and control","Pleasure from the mouth","Socialization and denial of opposite-sex attraction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Genital stage (12 yrs–adulthood) = awakening of sexuality and desire for heterosexual love.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe anal, oral, and latency stages respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Freud, how many psychosexual stages of personality development are there, and what are they called collectively?","options":["3 stages; topographical development","5 stages; psychosexual development of the personality","8 stages; psychosocial development","4 stages; cognitive development"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Freud's Development of Personality consists of 5 stages known as psychosexual development of the personality (oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital).  Why A wrong: The topographical model has 3 levels of mind, not 5 stages.  Why C wrong: 8 stages/psychosocial is Erikson.  Why D wrong: 4 stages/cognitive is Piaget."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Erikson's stages, the \"Infants\" stage (birth to 18 months) involves the conflict of Trust vs Mistrust, with feeding as the important event. What virtue is associated with this stage?","options":["Will","Hope","Purpose","Fidelity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The infant stage (Trust vs Mistrust) has the virtue Hope.  Why A wrong: Will is the toddler stage virtue.  Why C wrong: Purpose is the pre-school virtue.  Why D wrong: Fidelity is the adolescence virtue."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Erikson's model, the Toddler stage (18 months–3 years) is characterized by which psychosocial conflict, important event, and virtue?","options":["Trust vs Mistrust; feeding; Hope","Autonomy vs Shame/doubt; toilet training; Will","Initiative vs Guilt; explorative; Purpose","Industry vs Inferiority; schooling; Competence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Toddler = Autonomy vs Shame/doubt, important event toilet training, virtue Will.  Why A wrong: That is Infants.  Why C wrong: That is Pre-school.  Why D wrong: That is School age."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Erikson, the Pre-school stage (3–6 years) involves which conflict and virtue?","options":["Initiative vs Guilt; Purpose","Autonomy vs Shame/doubt; Will","Identity vs Role Confusion; Fidelity","Intimacy vs Isolation; Love"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-school = Initiative vs Guilt, virtue Purpose (explorative important event).  Why B wrong: That is Toddler.  Why C wrong: That is Adolescence.  Why D wrong: That is Young adult."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Erikson's model, the School age stage (6–12 years) involves Industry vs Inferiority, with schooling as the important event. What is the associated virtue?","options":["Fidelity","Competence","Will","Care"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: School age (Industry vs Inferiority) has the virtue Competence.  Why A wrong: Fidelity = adolescence.  Why C wrong: Will = toddler.  Why D wrong: Care = middle adult."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Erikson, the Adolescence stage (12–19 years) is characterized by which psychosocial conflict and virtue?","options":["Identity vs Role Confusion; Fidelity","Intimacy vs Isolation; Love","Generativity vs Stagnation; Care","Ego integrity vs Despair; Wisdom"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Adolescence = Identity vs Role Confusion, important event social relationship, virtue Fidelity.  Why B wrong: That is Young adult.  Why C wrong: That is Middle adult.  Why D wrong: That is Maturity."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Erikson's model, the Young adult stage (20–40 years) involves Intimacy vs Isolation. Which virtue and unsuccessful outcome are associated with it?","options":["Fidelity; substance abuse","Love; emotional immaturity, may deny need for personal relationship","Care; inability to show concern for anyone but self","Wisdom; feeling of hopelessness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Young adult (Intimacy vs Isolation) = virtue Love; unsuccessful completion = emotional immaturity, may deny and need for personal relationship.  Why A wrong: Fidelity/substance abuse = adolescence.  Why C wrong: Care/self-concern = middle adult.  Why D wrong: Wisdom/hopelessness = maturity."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Erikson, the Middle adult stage (41–65 years) involves which conflict, important event, and virtue?","options":["Generativity vs Stagnation; risk and parenthood; Care","Intimacy vs Isolation; relationship; Love","Ego integrity vs Despair; reflection of life; Wisdom","Industry vs Inferiority; schooling; Competence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Middle adult = Generativity vs Stagnation, important event risk and parenthood, virtue Care.  Why B wrong: That is Young adult.  Why C wrong: That is Maturity.  Why D wrong: That is School age."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Erikson's final stage, Maturity (65 years to death), the psychosocial conflict is Ego integrity vs Despair. The associated virtue is Wisdom and the important event is:","options":["Feeding","Reflection of life","Toilet training","Social relationship"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maturity stage's important event is \"Reflection of life,\" virtue Wisdom.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are important events for the infant, toddler, and adolescence stages respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Piaget's Cognitive Development Theory, the Sensory motor stage (1–2 years) is characterized by the development of:","options":["Object permanent (object permanence)","Symbolic gesture","Language, thinking","Abstract problem solving"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sensory motor (1–2 year) = object permanent (object permanence).  Why B wrong: Symbolic gesture = pre-operation.  Why C wrong: Language, thinking = concrete operation.  Why D wrong: Abstract problem solving = abstract operation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Piaget's theory, the Pre-operation stage (2–6 years) is characterized by:","options":["Object permanence","Symbolic gesture","Language and thinking","Problem solving and intellectual power"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pre-operation (2–6 year) = symbolic gesture.  Why A wrong: Object permanence = sensory motor.  Why C wrong: Language, thinking = concrete operation.  Why D wrong: Problem solving = abstract operation."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Piaget, the Concrete operation stage (6–12 years) is characterized by the development of:","options":["Object permanence","Symbolic gesture","Language, thinking","Abstract reasoning only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Concrete operation (6–12 year) = language, thinking.  Why A/B wrong: These belong to earlier stages.  Why D wrong: Abstract reasoning belongs to the abstract operation stage (>12 year)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Piaget's Abstract operation stage (>12 years), the child develops the ability to:","options":["Attain object permanence","Use symbolic gestures","Perform problem solving, and intellectual power can assume","Learn toilet training"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Abstract operation (>12 year) = problem solving & intellectual power can assume.  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier Piaget stages.  Why D wrong: Toilet training relates to Erikson/Freud, not Piaget's abstract stage."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In which year was Maslow's theory (hierarchy of needs) introduced, and in which book?","options":["1954; in the book of Motivation and Personality","1969; On Death and Dying","1975; Mini-Mental State Examination","1943; Interpretation of Dreams"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states Maslow's theory was introduced in 1954 in the book of Motivation and Personality.  Why B wrong: 1969 On Death and Dying is Kübler-Ross.  Why C wrong: 1975 relates to Folstein's MMSE.  Why D wrong: Not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Maslow, before an individual can move to the next level of the hierarchy, it is necessary to:","options":["Achieve self-actualization first","Fulfil the needs at lower levels","Fulfil the esteem needs first","Skip the physiological needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states before moving to the next level of stage, it is necessary to fulfil the needs at lower levels.  Why A wrong: Self-actualization is the highest, achieved last.  Why C wrong: Esteem needs are a higher level, not the first to fulfil.  Why D wrong: Physiological needs are the base and cannot be skipped."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Maslow's hierarchy, which category is the highest priority need and the lowest level in the model?","options":["Self-actualization needs","Basic physiological needs","Esteem needs","Safety needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Basic physiological needs are the highest priority need and the lowest level in Maslow's model (AIIMS, NHM, PSC, ESIC, DSSSB).  Why A wrong: Self-actualization is the highest level but the least/lowest priority.  Why C/D wrong: Esteem and safety are intermediate levels, not the highest priority."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which of the following are examples of basic physiological needs in Maslow's hierarchy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Air, oxygen, and respiratory efforts","Water and food","Sleep and elimination","Body temperature and homeostasis","Self-confidence and recognition"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Physiological examples include air, respiratory efforts, heart rate, water, food, shelter, sleep, clothing, sex/reproduction, oxygen, nutrition, body temperature, elimination, homeostasis.  Why E wrong: Self-confidence and recognition are esteem needs, not physiological."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Maslow's hierarchy, \"protection from injury and promotion of a feeling of security and trust in the nurse-client relationship\" describes which need?","options":["Physiological needs","Safety and security needs","Love and belonging needs","Esteem needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source describes safety and security needs as protection from injury and promoting a feeling of security and trust.  Why A wrong: Physiological needs are basic biological needs.  Why C wrong: Love and belonging = friendship, intimacy, family.  Why D wrong: Esteem = respect, self-esteem, recognition."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Examples such as personal security, emotional security, financial security, employment, resources, health & well-being, and property belong to which Maslow's level?","options":["Physiological needs","Safety and security needs","Esteem needs","Self-actualization needs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: These examples (personal/emotional/financial security, employment, resources, health, property) are listed under safety and security needs.  Why A/C/D wrong: These belong to other levels of the hierarchy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Maslow's hierarchy, maintaining support systems, protection from isolation, friendship, intimacy, family, and sense of connection describe which need?","options":["Esteem needs","Love and belonging","Safety needs","Self-actualization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Love and belonging = maintain support systems, protection from isolation, friendship, intimacy, family, sense of connection.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe esteem, safety, and self-actualization respectively."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which of the following are components of esteem needs in Maslow's hierarchy? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Respect and self-esteem","Status and recognition","Self-confidence and independence","Prestige and feeling of accomplishment","Air, food, and water"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Esteem needs include respect, self-esteem & respect, status, recognition (identification), strength, self-confidence, independence, freedom, prestige, feeling of accomplishment (achievement), control, competence, positive regard.  Why E wrong: Air, food, water are physiological needs, not esteem."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In Maslow's hierarchy, which need is described as the highest level need and the least or less priority need?","options":["Physiological needs","Safety needs","Esteem needs","Self-actualization needs"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Self-actualization is the highest level need and the least/less priority need (AIIMS, RRB, PGI).  Why A wrong: Physiological is the lowest level but highest priority.  Why B/C wrong: Safety and esteem are intermediate levels."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Self-actualization in Maslow's model involves achieving one's full potential, including creative activities, mate acquisition (achievement of sex), parenting, utilizing and developing abilities and talents, pursuing goals, spiritual well-being, and:","options":["Financial security","Hope","Friendship","Food and shelter"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists spiritual well-being and hope among self-actualization components.  Why A wrong: Financial security is a safety need.  Why C wrong: Friendship is love/belonging.  Why D wrong: Food and shelter are physiological."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to Kübler-Ross, what are the five stages of grief (popularly known as DABDA)?","options":["Denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance","Denial, anxiety, blame, despair, adjustment","Disbelief, anger, bargaining, dependence, acceptance","Denial, aggression, bargaining, distress, adaptation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DABDA = denial, anger, bargaining, depression, and acceptance.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contain incorrect substitute terms not in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Kübler-Ross grief model was introduced by Elisabeth Kübler-Ross in her 1969 book titled:","options":["On Death and Dying","Motivation and Personality","The Interpretation of Dreams","Death: The Final Stage of Growth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the model was introduced in her 1969 book \"On Death and Dying.\"  Why B wrong: That is Maslow's book.  Why C wrong: That is Freud's book.  Why D wrong: Not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the source, \"bereavement\" is best defined as:","options":["Objective or behavioural response to a loss","Subjective or emotional response to a loss","A natural response common to all cultures","A pathological form of grief"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (AIIMS Nagpur-2020) defines bereavement as the subjective or emotional response to a loss.  Why A wrong: That defines mourning.  Why C/D wrong: Not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In grieving, \"mourning\" is defined as the objective or behavioural response to a loss, which is affected by:","options":["Genetics only","Culture and society","Hormones only","Age and gender only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states mourning is affected by culture and society.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the influencing factors stated for mourning."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the Kübler-Ross model, what is the first reaction to a loss?","options":["Anger","Denial","Bargaining","Acceptance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source (FAQs) states the first reaction of a loss is denial.  Why A/C/D wrong: Anger, bargaining, and acceptance follow denial in the DABDA sequence."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the Denial stage of grieving, the client's response is disbelief and intellectualization, and the nursing care is to:","options":["Reinforce the denial","Accept the response but do not reinforce denial","Confront the client aggressively","Ignore the client"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care in denial = accept the response but do not reinforce denial.  Why A wrong: The nurse should NOT reinforce denial.  Why C/D wrong: Confrontation or ignoring are not recommended."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the Anger stage of grief, statements like \"Why me,\" \"It's not fair,\" \"How can this happen to me,\" and \"Why would this happen?\" reflect a client who is:","options":["Verbally hostile","Silent and withdrawn","Seeking to avoid loss","Accepting the loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In the anger stage the client response is verbally hostile; these statements occur when the individual feels the diagnosis is right and becomes frustrated.  Why B wrong: Silence and withdrawal characterize depression.  Why C wrong: Seeking to avoid loss characterizes bargaining.  Why D wrong: Acceptance is a separate final stage."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"During the Anger stage of grieving, the appropriate nursing care is to:","options":["Reinforce denial","Not become defensive and meet client needs","Use a spiritual counsellor","Sit quietly and use touch"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care in anger = do not become defensive, meet client needs.  Why A wrong: That relates to denial.  Why C wrong: Spiritual counsellor use is advised in bargaining.  Why D wrong: Sitting quietly/using touch is advised in depression."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which is the third stage of the grieving process, in which the individual can avoid a cause of grief and the client seeks to avoid loss and may express feelings of guilt?","options":["Denial","Anger","Bargaining","Depression"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bargaining is the third stage; the individual tries to avoid a cause of grief and may express guilt.  Why A/B wrong: These are the first two stages.  Why D wrong: Depression is the fourth stage (silent, withdrawn)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the Bargaining stage of grief, the recommended nursing care is to:","options":["Listen carefully and use a spiritual counsellor if appropriate","Not become defensive","Reinforce denial","Help family accept the client's behaviour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care in bargaining = listen carefully and use spiritual counsellor if appropriate.  Why B wrong: That is anger-stage care.  Why C wrong: That contradicts denial-stage guidance.  Why D wrong: That is acceptance-stage care."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the Depression stage of grief, the individual may become silent, refuse visitors, and be mournful. The recommended nursing care includes:","options":["Do not become defensive","Listen carefully & attentively, sit quietly, use touch if appropriate","Use a spiritual counsellor","Reinforce the denial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care in depression = listen carefully & attentively, sit quietly, use touch if appropriate. The client response is sadness.  Why A wrong: That is anger-stage care.  Why C wrong: That is bargaining-stage care.  Why D wrong: Contradicts denial-stage guidance."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the Acceptance stage of grief, the client comes to terms with the loss and may make future plans. The recommended nursing care is to:","options":["Be quiet and help family to accept the client's behaviour","Confront the client about denial","Use serial sevens testing","Reinforce hostility"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nursing care in acceptance = be quiet, and help family to accept the client's behaviour.  Why B/D wrong: Confrontation and reinforcing hostility are inappropriate.  Why C wrong: Serial sevens is an MMSE technique, unrelated."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the DABDA \"Stage of Grief Resolution,\" which stage is characterized by the statement \"This cannot happen to me\"?","options":["I stage (Denial)","II stage (Anger)","III stage (Bargaining)","IV stage (Depression)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: I stage (Denial) = \"This cannot happen to me.\"  Why B wrong: Anger = \"Why is this happening?\"  Why C wrong: Bargaining = \"If this avoided I will worship daily.\"  Why D wrong: Depression = \"I do not like anything.\""},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the DABDA grief resolution, the statement \"Deaths are natural\" corresponds to which stage?","options":["Denial","Anger","Bargaining","Acceptance"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: V stage (Acceptance) = \"Deaths are natural.\"  Why A/B/C wrong: These stages have different characteristic statements (cannot happen to me / why is this happening / worship daily)."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) describes stress as the biological and psychological response of the body and is also described as which emergency reaction system?","options":["\"Rest and digest\" response","\"Fight or flight\" response","\"Freeze\" response","\"Tend and befriend\" response"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states GAS is the \"fight or flight\" response, the emergency reaction system of the body.  Why A/C/D wrong: These responses are not named in the source for GAS."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"When the stress response is turned on, the body may release which hormones?","options":["Insulin and glucagon","Adrenaline and cortisol","Thyroxine and calcitonin","Estrogen and progesterone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the body may release lots of hormones like adrenaline and cortisol.  Why A/C/D wrong: These hormones are not the ones cited for the stress response."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to the source, the two types of stress are:","options":["Eustress (good stress) and distress (bad stress)","Acute stress and chronic stress","Physical stress and mental stress","Internal stress and external stress"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Types of stress include eustress (good stress) and distress (bad stress).  Why B/C/D wrong: These classifications are not given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) model was given by which scientist?","options":["Selye","Freud","Maslow","Folstein"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source (GMCH-2019) states the GAS model was given by Selye.  Why B wrong: Freud gave the personality models.  Why C wrong: Maslow gave the hierarchy of needs.  Why D wrong: Folstein introduced the MMSE."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to GAS theory, the stress response occurs in which three stages?","options":["Alarm phase, Resistive phase, and Exhaustion phase","Denial, Anger, and Acceptance","Oral, Anal, and Genital","Conscious, Preconscious, and Unconscious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: GAS occurs in three stages: Alarm phase, Resistive phase, and Exhaustion phase.  Why B wrong: These are grief stages.  Why C wrong: These are Freud's psychosexual stages.  Why D wrong: These are topographical levels of mind."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"Which are the major hormones acting in the Alarm phase of GAS?","options":["Cortisol only","Adrenaline and noradrenalin","Insulin and glucagon","Thyroxine and cortisol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the alarm phase, the major hormones are adrenaline and noradrenalin (ADR & NORAD), whose secretion suddenly increases to peak/\"pick\" level.  Why A wrong: Cortisol is the major hormone of the resistive phase.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the alarm-phase hormones."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"The Alarm phase of GAS is mediated by which nervous system, preparing the body for the \"fight and flight\" response by stimulating the adrenal medulla to secrete epinephrine and nor-epinephrine?","options":["Parasympathetic nervous system","Sympathetic nervous system","Enteric nervous system","Somatic nervous system"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The alarm phase is mediated by the sympathetic nervous system, stimulating the adrenal medulla to secrete epinephrine and nor-epinephrine.  Why A/C/D wrong: These systems are not responsible for the fight-or-flight alarm response."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"During the Alarm phase of GAS, which of the following physiological responses occur due to sympathetic stimulation? (Select All That Apply)","options":["Heart beat increases to pump more blood to muscles","Peripheral blood vessels constrict; coronary blood vessels dilate","Bronchioles and pupils dilate","Liver releases glucose for quick energy; prothrombin time shortened","All intestine secretions increase to aid digestion"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: In alarm phase, heart rate increases, peripheral vessels constrict, coronary vessels dilate, bronchioles dilate, pupils dilate, liver releases glucose, prothrombin time shortens, and sodium is reabsorbed.  Why E wrong: All intestine secretions DECREASE (not increase) to preserve fluid volume of the body."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"During the alarm phase, why is prothrombin time shortened?","options":["To increase vision","To protect the body from loss of blood in the event of injury","To maintain blood volume","To provide more oxygen"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Prothrombin time is shortened to protect the body from loss of blood in the event of injury (faster clotting).  Why A wrong: Pupils dilate to increase vision.  Why C wrong: Sodium is reabsorbed to maintain blood volume.  Why D wrong: Bronchioles dilate for more oxygen."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"During the alarm phase of GAS, why is sodium reabsorbed?","options":["To increase vision","To maintain blood volume","To dilate the bronchioles","For quick energy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sodium is reabsorbed to maintain blood volume.  Why A wrong: Pupils dilate for vision.  Why C wrong: Bronchioles dilate for oxygen, not via sodium.  Why D wrong: Liver glucose release provides quick energy."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"In the Resistive stage of GAS, which is the major hormone that plays a role, and what happens if secretion is prolonged?","options":["Adrenaline; increased heart rate","Cortisol; prolonged secretion leads to immune suppression","Noradrenalin; pupil dilation","Insulin; hypoglycemia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the resist phase the major hormone is cortisol; increased/prolonged secretion enables the body to cope with stress, and prolonged cortisol secretion leads to immune suppression.  Why A/C wrong: Adrenaline and noradrenalin dominate the alarm phase.  Why D wrong: Insulin is not the resistive-phase hormone."},{"section":"Batch 002 · Mental Status Examination & its ASEPTIC components…","question":"According to GAS, what happens in the Exhaustion stage if stresses continue and responses are no longer effective?","options":["The body fully recovers","The client will not be able to cope with stresses due to high cortisol; the last stage is exhaustion and death","The alarm phase restarts","Cortisol secretion stops entirely and health improves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In exhaustion, if stresses continue and responses are no longer effective, the client cannot cope due to high cortisol; the last stage is exhaustion and death.  Why A/D wrong: Recovery/health improvement is not the outcome of exhaustion.  Why C wrong: The alarm phase does not restart; exhaustion is the terminal stage."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A defence mechanism is also known as which of the following?","options":["Coping mechanism","Grief mechanism","Adjustment reflex","Insight mechanism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states defence mechanism is also known as coping mechanism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the synonyms given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The primary purpose for which a client uses defence mechanisms is to:","options":["Increase self-awareness","Decrease anxiety","Improve memory","Enhance social skills"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Coping mechanisms involve efforts to decrease anxiety; the client uses defence mechanisms to decrease anxiety.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the stated purpose; the mechanism is triggered when anxiety increases."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Defence mechanisms are always operated by which part of the mind?","options":["Conscious mind","Preconscious mind","Unconscious mind","Subconscious mind"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states defence mechanism is always operated by the unconscious mind.  Why A/B/D wrong: The text specifically names the unconscious mind."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The concept of defence mechanisms was described by:","options":["Carl Jung","Sigmund Freud","Erik Erikson","Ivan Pavlov"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The defence mechanism concept was described by Sigmund Freud.  Why A/C/D wrong: These theorists are not credited with the concept in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"(Daman & Diu 2018) The defence mechanism most commonly seen in Obsessive Compulsive Disorder (OCD) is:","options":["Undoing","Projection","Denial","Sublimation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states undoing is most commonly seen in OCD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate mechanisms not linked to OCD here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A person with terminal cancer insists that he/she is not going to die. This is an example of which defence mechanism?","options":["Repression","Denial","Suppression","Displacement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Denial is the refusal to accept the reality or fact that causes anxiety; refusing to accept terminal illness illustrates it.  Why A/C/D wrong: These involve forgetting or redirecting feelings, not refusing reality."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A man drinks multiple drinks every night but says he is not an alcoholic because he never drinks before 5 PM. This best illustrates:","options":["Rationalization","Denial","Projection","Reaction formation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This is the source's example for denial — refusal to accept the reality of alcoholism.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the mechanism the text assigns to this example."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A deliberate or conscious attempt to push away unwanted or disturbing thoughts is called:","options":["Repression","Suppression","Dissociation","Regression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suppression is a deliberate/conscious attempt to push away unwanted thoughts (consciously suppress impulse).  Why A/C/D wrong: Repression is unconscious; dissociation and regression are different mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A woman makes a voluntary decision not to think about her arguments with her girlfriend while going for an interview. This is an example of:","options":["Repression","Suppression","Denial","Sublimation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A voluntary/conscious decision to push away a disturbing thought is suppression.  Why A/C/D wrong: Repression is unconscious; the others do not fit this conscious act."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"An unconscious mechanism that keeps painful ideas and disturbing thoughts away from consciousness (unconsciously denying impulse) is:","options":["Suppression","Repression","Compensation","Conversion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Repression is the unconscious mechanism that keeps painful/disturbing thoughts away from consciousness.  Why A/C/D wrong: Suppression is conscious; compensation and conversion are unrelated mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A child abused by a parent later has no recollection of the events but has trouble forming relationships. This is an example of:","options":["Repression","Displacement","Suppression","Undoing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source uses this as the example of repression — unconscious blocking of painful memories.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms are not what the text assigns to this example."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A husband comes home after a bad day at work and shouts at his wife or children. This defence mechanism is:","options":["Projection","Displacement","Reaction formation","Rationalization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Displacement is discharging unwanted feelings/frustration onto a less threatening object or person.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not involve redirecting feelings to a safer target."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Angered by a neighbour's hateful comment, a mother spanks her daughter for accidentally spilling her milk. This illustrates:","options":["Displacement","Denial","Sublimation","Compensation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frustration from one source (neighbour) is discharged onto a less threatening target (daughter) — displacement.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms do not describe redirecting anger."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"When anxiety-producing or unacceptable feelings are replaced with their exact opposite feelings and emotions, the defence mechanism is:","options":["Reaction formation","Sublimation","Undoing","Intellectualization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reaction formation replaces unacceptable feelings with their exact opposite.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not involve reversing feelings into their opposite."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A jealous boy who hates his elder brother shows exaggerated respect and affection towards him. This is an example of:","options":["Reaction formation","Compensation","Projection","Regression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Displaying the exact opposite (affection) of the true feeling (hatred) is reaction formation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms do not describe expressing opposite emotions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Justification of one's unacceptable behaviour or failure in a logical manner by giving socially approved reasons is called:","options":["Rationalization","Intellectualization","Sublimation","Compensation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rationalization is justifying unacceptable behaviour/failure with socially approved logical reasons.  Why B/C/D wrong: Intellectualization blocks emotional confrontation; the others differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A student who comes late to class complains that the hostel mess served the food late. This best illustrates:","options":["Rationalization","Denial","Projection","Displacement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The student justifies his failure (being late) with a socially acceptable reason — rationalization.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the mechanism assigned to this example."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Channelling socially unacceptable impulses and urges into socially acceptable activities (e.g., aggressiveness transformed into competitiveness in studies and sports) is:","options":["Sublimation","Compensation","Reaction formation","Undoing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sublimation is acting out socially unacceptable impulses into socially acceptable activities.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms do not describe channelling impulses into acceptable outlets."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A student who fails in his studies becomes the college champion in sports. This is an example of:","options":["Compensation","Sublimation","Projection","Rationalization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Compensation is over-achieving in one area to make up for shortcomings/failure in another.  Why B/C/D wrong: Sublimation redirects unacceptable impulses; the others do not fit covering a weakness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A surgeon whose patient does not recover as anticipated tends to blame the scrub nurse who assisted at surgery. This defence mechanism is:","options":["Projection","Displacement","Rationalization","Denial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Projection is blaming another person for one's own failure or difficulties.  Why B/C/D wrong: Displacement redirects feelings, not blame for one's own failure; the others differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A partner who is unfaithful (or has unfaithful thoughts) accuses the other partner of infidelity. This is an example of:","options":["Projection","Reaction formation","Undoing","Compensation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to another person is projection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe attributing one's own faults to another."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Using reasoning (thinking) to block confrontation with an unconscious conflict and its associated emotional stress is called:","options":["Intellectualization","Rationalization","Sublimation","Dissociation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intellectualization uses reasoning to block confrontation with emotional stress; it may accompany but differs from rationalization.  Why B/C/D wrong: Rationalization justifies behaviour; sublimation and dissociation are separate mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A person shows no emotional expression when discussing a serious car accident, and \"just goes through\" the facts. This illustrates:","options":["Intellectualization","Denial","Repression","Conversion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Blocking the emotional stress of the event by staying in reasoning/facts is intellectualization.  Why B/C/D wrong: Denial refuses reality; repression is unconscious forgetting; conversion produces physical symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A person tries to undo an unhealthy, destructive, or threatening thought or action by engaging in contrary behaviour. This defence mechanism is:","options":["Undoing","Reaction formation","Sublimation","Regression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Undoing is attempting to undo a threatening thought/action by engaging in contrary behaviour.  Why B/C/D wrong: Reaction formation expresses opposite feelings but is not the \"undoing\" of a prior act; others differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Giving a treat to a child who is being punished for wrongdoing is an example of which defence mechanism?","options":["Undoing","Compensation","Displacement","Denial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source uses this as the example of undoing.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the mechanism assigned to this example."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"An unconscious return to an earlier and more comfortable developmental stage (e.g., an overwhelmed child reverting to bedwetting or thumb-sucking) is called:","options":["Regression","Repression","Dissociation","Fixation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Regression is the unconscious return to an earlier developmental stage to feel safe and reduce anxiety.  Why B/C/D wrong: Repression forgets memories; dissociation detaches; fixation is not the term used."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"An adult throws a temper tantrum when he does not get his own way. This is an example of:","options":["Regression","Displacement","Reaction formation","Undoing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Reverting to childlike behaviour (tantrum) is regression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These mechanisms do not describe reverting to childlike behaviour."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The unconscious detachment from immediate surroundings or physical/emotional experiences that cause anxiety (e.g., amnesia preventing recall of the previous day's car accident) is:","options":["Dissociation","Conversion","Repression","Denial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dissociation is unconscious detachment from surroundings/experiences causing anxiety.  Why B/C/D wrong: Conversion produces physical symptoms; repression/denial are distinct mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A student awakens with a migraine headache on the morning of a final examination and feels too ill to take the test. This best illustrates:","options":["Conversion","Dissociation","Malingering","Regression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Conversion is when psychological/interpersonal conflicts manifest as physical symptoms — this is the source's example.  Why B/C/D wrong: Dissociation is detachment; malingering is not listed; regression differs."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, which defence mechanism is described as unconscious and involuntary forgetting (circumscribed amnesia) and is the primary defence mechanism?","options":["Suppression","Repression","Denial","Dissociation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Repression = unconscious and involuntary forgetting (circumscribed amnesia) and is the primary defence mechanism.  Why A/C/D wrong: Suppression is conscious/voluntary; denial and dissociation differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The TRICK \"SCRU\" for Suppression and Repression means: S = Suppression, C = Consciously; R = Repression, U = ____.","options":["Unconsciously","Unintentionally","Uncontrollably","Universally"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The mnemonic SCRU: Suppression–Consciously, Repression–Unconsciously.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source expands U as \"Unconsciously\"."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the source, rationalisation as a defence mechanism is commonly seen in which clients?","options":["Alcoholic clients","Hysteria clients","OCD clients","Depressed clients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The text states rationalisation defence is commonly seen in alcoholic clients.  Why B wrong: Conversion is seen in hysteria clients. Why C wrong: Undoing is seen in OCD. Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The conversion defence mechanism is most commonly seen in which type of client?","options":["Hysteria clients","Alcoholic clients","OCD clients","Manic clients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states conversion defence mechanism is commonly seen in hysteria clients.  Why B/C/D wrong: Alcoholic = rationalisation; OCD = undoing; manic is not linked here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the Indian Classification of Mental Disorders, mental disorders are divided into which two broad categories?","options":["Neurosis and Psychosis","Organic and Inorganic","Mood and Anxiety","Acute and Chronic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Indian classification lists (1) Neurosis and (2) Psychosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the two Indian classification categories."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the classification flowchart, which two conditions fall under Organic psychosis?","options":["Delirium and Dementia","Schizophrenia and Mood affective disorders","Anxiety and Phobic disorders","Hysteria and DCD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The flowchart lists Delirium and Dementia under organic psychosis.  Why B wrong: These fall under functional psychosis. Why C/D wrong: These are neurosis branches."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Under Functional psychosis, the flowchart lists which of the following?","options":["Schizophrenia and Mood affective disorders","Delirium and Dementia","Phobic disorders and Hysteria","Anxiety disorders and DCD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Functional psychosis branches into Schizophrenia and Mood affective disorders.  Why B wrong: These are organic psychosis. Why C/D wrong: These are under neurosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a Neurosis in the classification flowchart? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anxiety disorders","Phobic disorders","OCD/OCN disorders","Schizophrenia","Hysteria"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Neurosis branch includes anxiety disorders, phobic disorders, OCD/OCN disorders, hysteria, and DCD.  Why D wrong: Schizophrenia is listed under functional psychosis, not neurosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which statement correctly defines \"Organic disorders\" per the source?","options":["Related to pathological or trauma condition of the brain","No evidence of pathological condition of the brain","Present only in childhood","Always reversible within 24 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Organic disorders relate to pathological or trauma condition of the brain.  Why B wrong: That defines functional disorders. Why C/D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"\"Functional disorders\" are defined by the source as those in which:","options":["There is no evidence of a pathological condition of the brain","There is brain trauma","There is a structural brain tumour","There is cerebral infarction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Functional disorders have no evidence of any pathological condition of the brain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are organic/pathological findings, contrary to the definition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the Neurosis vs Psychosis table, which feature is characteristic of NEUROSIS?","options":["Reality testing judgment present","Insight is absent","Delusions are present","Disorganized behaviour present"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In neurosis, reality testing judgment is present, personality is not affected, insight is present, and delusions/hallucinations are absent.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are features of psychosis."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which set of features correctly describes PSYCHOSIS according to the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Reality testing judgment absent","Personality is affected","Insight is present","Hallucinations are present","Disorganized speech present"],"correct":"A,B,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Psychosis: reality testing absent, personality affected, insight absent, delusions and hallucinations present, disorganized speech and behaviour present.  Why C wrong: In psychosis insight is absent (present is a neurosis feature)."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Under the \"Mental illness/Disorders\" table, which examples are listed under Anxiety Disorders? (Select all that apply)","options":["Acute stress disorder","Panic disorder","Social phobia","Anorexia nervosa","Bipolar disorder"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Anxiety disorders examples: acute stress disorder, panic disorder, social phobia.  Why D wrong: Anorexia nervosa is an eating disorder. Why E wrong: Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder, Asperger's disorder, and Autistic disorder are grouped under which category in the Mental illness/Disorders table?","options":["Childhood Disorders","Personality Disorders","Mood Disorders","Eating Disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists ADHD, Asperger's disorder, and autistic disorder under Childhood Disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: These categories contain different examples."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the Mental illness/Disorders table, Anorexia nervosa and Bulimia nervosa are examples of:","options":["Eating Disorders","Anxiety Disorders","Mood Disorders","Personality Disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa are listed under Eating Disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: These categories list different examples."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Major depressive disorder and bipolar disorder (manic depression) are grouped under which category?","options":["Mood Disorders","Personality Disorders","Anxiety Disorders","Substance Related Disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists major depressive disorder and bipolar disorder (manic depression) under Mood Disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: These categories list different examples."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which examples are listed under Personality Disorders in the table? (Select all that apply)","options":["Paranoid personality disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","Obsessive & compulsive personality disorder","Alcohol dependence","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Personality Disorders examples: paranoid, antisocial, and obsessive & compulsive personality disorders.  Why D wrong: Alcohol dependence is a substance-related disorder. Why E wrong: Schizophrenia is under schizophrenic/psychotic disorders."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Alcohol dependence and amphetamine dependence are listed under which category in the table?","options":["Substance Related Disorders","Mood Disorders","Anxiety Disorders","Childhood Disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists alcohol dependence and amphetamine dependence under Substance Related Disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: These categories list different examples."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-IV) is published by which body?","options":["American Psychiatric Association (APA)","World Health Organization (WHO)","American Psychological Society","National Institute of Mental Health"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DSM-IV (also known as DSM-IV-TR) is published by the American Psychiatric Association (APA); DSM-V was also given by APA.  Why B/C/D wrong: WHO publishes the ICD, not the DSM."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"DSM-IV is also known by which alternate name?","options":["DSM-IV-TR","ICD-10","GAF scale","DSM-III-R"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states DSM-IV is also known as DSM-IV-TR.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the alternate name given."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the DSM-IV classification system, which axis covers clinical mental disorders?","options":["Axis I","Axis II","Axis III","Axis V"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Axis I = clinical mental disorders.  Why B wrong: Axis II = mental retardation/personality disorders/habit of using a defence mechanism. Why C wrong: Axis III = general medical/physical condition. Why D wrong: Axis V = psychosocial/environmental stressor and global assessment of functioning."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the DSM-IV axes described, mental retardation, personality disorders, and habitual use of a particular defence mechanism are coded under:","options":["Axis II","Axis I","Axis III","Axis V"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Axis II covers mental retardation/personality disorders/habit of using a particular defence mechanism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These axes cover clinical disorders, medical conditions, and stressor/functioning respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"General medical/physical conditions are recorded on which DSM-IV axis?","options":["Axis III","Axis I","Axis II","Axis V"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Axis III = general medical/physical condition.  Why B/C/D wrong: These axes cover clinical disorders, personality/MR, and psychosocial stressor/GAF."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which DSM-IV axis includes the global assessment of functioning (level of social, occupational, and psychological functioning)?","options":["Axis V","Axis I","Axis II","Axis III"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists Axis V as psychosocial/environmental stressor and global assessment of functioning (GAF). (Note: the source lists \"Axis V\" twice and omits Axis IV; standard DSM-IV places psychosocial stressors on Axis IV and GAF on Axis V.)  Why B/C/D wrong: These axes cover clinical disorders, personality/MR, and medical conditions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The International Classification of Mental Disorders (ICD-10) was published in 1992 by which organisation?","options":["WHO","APA","UNICEF","NIMHANS"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICD-10 was published by WHO in 1992.  Why B/C/D wrong: APA publishes the DSM; UNICEF and NIMHANS did not publish it."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the ICD-10 classification of mental disorders, the code range F20–F29 covers:","options":["Schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders","Mood (affective) disorders","Organic, including symptomatic, mental disorders","Mental retardation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F20–F29 = Schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional disorders.  Why B wrong: F30–F39 = mood disorders. Why C wrong: F00–F09 = organic mental disorders. Why D wrong: F70–F79 = mental retardation."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which ICD-10 code range corresponds to \"Mental and behavioural disorders due to psychoactive substance use\"?","options":["F10–F19","F00–F09","F40–F48","F50–F59"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F10–F19 = mental and behavioural disorders due to psychoactive substance use.  Why B wrong: F00–F09 = organic disorders. Why C wrong: F40–F48 = neurotic, stress-related and somatoform. Why D wrong: F50–F59 = behavioural syndromes with physiological disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Match the ICD-10 range to its category: F40–F48 corresponds to:","options":["Neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders","Mood (affective) disorders","Disorders of adult personality and behaviour","Behavioural/emotional disorders with childhood onset"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F40–F48 = neurotic, stress-related and somatoform disorders.  Why B wrong: F30–F39 = mood disorders. Why C wrong: F60–F69 = adult personality/behaviour. Why D wrong: F90–F98 = childhood/adolescent onset."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In ICD-10, \"Disorders of adult personality and behaviour\" fall under which code range?","options":["F60–F69","F50–F59","F70–F79","F90–F98"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F60–F69 = disorders of adult personality and behaviour.  Why B wrong: F50–F59 = behavioural syndromes with physiological disturbances. Why C wrong: F70–F79 = mental retardation. Why D wrong: F90–F98 = childhood/adolescent onset."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The ICD-10 code range F90–F98 covers:","options":["Behavioural and emotional disorders with onset usually in childhood and adolescence","Mental retardation","Mood (affective) disorders","Organic mental disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F90–F98 = behavioural and emotional disorders with onset usually in childhood and adolescence.  Why B wrong: F70–F79 = mental retardation. Why C/D wrong: F30–F39 and F00–F09 respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In \"Some Psychological Disorders and their Codes,\" which ICD code corresponds to Schizophrenia?","options":["F20","F10","F30","F40"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F20 = Schizophrenia.  Why B wrong: F10 = mental/behavioural disorders due to alcohol use. Why C wrong: F30 = manic episode. Why D wrong: F40 = phobic anxiety disorder."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The code F31 in the list refers to which disorder?","options":["Bipolar Affective Disorder (BPAD)","Depressive Episode","Manic episode","Obsessive Compulsive Disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F31 = Bipolar Affective Disorder (BPAD).  Why B wrong: F32 = depressive episode. Why C wrong: F30 = manic episode. Why D wrong: F42 = OCD."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which code is assigned to Obsessive Compulsive Disorder in the codes list?","options":["F42","F40","F50","F52"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F42 = Obsessive Compulsive Disorder.  Why B wrong: F40 = phobic anxiety disorder. Why C wrong: F50 = eating disorders. Why D wrong: F52 = sexual dysfunction."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the psychological codes list, which condition corresponds to F95?","options":["Tic disorders","Eating disorders","Sexual dysfunction","Depressive episode"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: F95 = Tic disorders.  Why B wrong: F50 = eating disorders. Why C wrong: F52 = sexual dysfunction. Why D wrong: F32 = depressive episode."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the definition, \"the aspect of mental process directed by change and including impulse, desire, volition and striving\" defines:","options":["Mind","Consciousness","Perception","Cognition"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This is the Standard Comprehensive International Dictionary-1977 definition of Mind.  Why B/C/D wrong: These terms have separate definitions in the text."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"Components of mind\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Consciousness","Memory","Perception","Insight","Reflexes"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Components of mind = consciousness, memory, activity, perception, attention, thought, concentration, emotions, judgement and insight.  Why E wrong: Reflexes are not listed among the components of mind."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which of the following is a listed characteristic of a mentally healthy person? (Select all that apply)","options":["Adjustability","Ability to cope with problem","Self esteem","Ability to make decision","Absence of all emotions"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Characteristics include adjustability, ability to cope, acceptance (self and other), self esteem, ability to make social relationships, and ability to make decisions.  Why E wrong: Absence of all emotions is not a characteristic listed."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The state of being aware of and responsive to one's surroundings, and awareness about time, place, and response to painful/external stimuli, is known as:","options":["Consciousness","Perception","Cognition","Attention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Consciousness is defined as awareness/responsiveness to surroundings and to time, place, and stimuli.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct mental functions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Consciousness is assessed by which of the following scales?","options":["Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)","MMSE","Hamilton Rating Scale","Apgar Score"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states consciousness is assessed by the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS).  Why B/C/D wrong: MMSE assesses cognition; Hamilton is for depression; Apgar is for newborns."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"(AIIMS, DSSSB 2019) Which is described as the disorder of consciousness?","options":["Delirium","Depression","Mania","Phobia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium is the disorder of consciousness per the source (AIIMS, DSSSB-2019).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are mood/anxiety disorders, not disorders of consciousness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as disorders of consciousness? (Select all that apply)","options":["Coma","Stupor","Delirium","Dementia","Phobia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Disorders of consciousness = coma, stupor, delirium and dementia.  Why E wrong: Phobia is not listed as a disorder of consciousness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the levels of consciousness, which level \"contains thoughts and ideas just out of our awareness\" (just below the level of awareness)?","options":["Subconscious","Conscious","Unconscious","Preconscious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Subconscious = consciousness just below the level of awareness, containing thoughts/ideas just out of awareness.  Why B wrong: Conscious is current awareness. Why C wrong: Unconscious is a deeper level with no direct knowledge. Why D wrong: Preconscious is not the term used here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The deeper level of awareness that contains thoughts and desires about which we have no true or direct knowledge is the:","options":["Unconscious","Subconscious","Conscious","Metaconscious"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Unconscious is a deeper level containing thoughts/desires of which we have no true or direct knowledge.  Why B/C/D wrong: Subconscious is just below awareness; conscious is current awareness; metaconscious is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A state of absence of clear mindedness/consciousness, also known as \"mental fog\" and commonly seen in delirium, is:","options":["Clouding of consciousness","Coma","Stupor","Coma vigil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clouded/clouding of consciousness = mental fog, absence of clear mindedness, commonly seen in delirium.  Why B/C/D wrong: Coma is sustained unconsciousness; stupor is mute/waxy-flexibility; coma vigil is eyes-open coma."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"(NORCET-4, 2023) In deep coma seen in organic brain syndrome, the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score would be:","options":["3","8","13","15"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states deep coma GCS score would be 3 (the minimum possible GCS).  Why B/C/D wrong: 8 = severe injury threshold; 13–15 indicate mild impairment/normal, not deep coma."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A state of coma in which the patient lies unconscious but with the eyes open is called:","options":["Coma vigil","Stupor","Clouding of consciousness","Deep coma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Coma vigil = a state of coma in which the patient lies unconscious but with the eyes open.  Why B/C/D wrong: Stupor is mute with waxy flexibility; the others do not have eyes open by definition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient who is speechless (mute) and shows waxy flexibility is displaying which disorder of consciousness?","options":["Stupor","Coma vigil","Delirium","Clouding of consciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stupor = speechless (mute), with waxy-flexibility seen.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct states without the mute/waxy-flexibility description."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Whatever an individual experiences through the reaction of his/her sense organs to objects is called:","options":["Perception","Consciousness","Cognition","Attention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Perception is defined as what an individual experiences through his/her sense organs' reaction to objects.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate mental functions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A false perception occurring WITHOUT any real or actual external stimulus (e.g., perceiving a snake in the dark with no object present) is called:","options":["Hallucination","Illusion","Delusion","Macropsia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hallucination = false perception without real or actual stimuli.  Why B wrong: Illusion is a misperception WITH an actual external stimulus. Why C wrong: Delusion is a false belief (thought disorder). Why D wrong: Macropsia is seeing objects too large."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A false perception/misinterpretation occurring WITH an actual external stimulus (e.g., mistaking a rope for a snake in dim light) is called:","options":["Illusion","Hallucination","Delusion","Micropsia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Illusion = false perception/misinterpretation with actual external stimuli.  Why B wrong: Hallucination occurs without a real stimulus. Why C wrong: Delusion is a false belief. Why D wrong: Micropsia is seeing objects too small."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The perceptual disorder in which an object appears too large in size is called:","options":["Macropsia","Micropsia","Hyperesthesia","Coenaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Macropsia = object seen too large in size.  Why B wrong: Micropsia = object too small. Why C/D wrong: These relate to sensation intensity/bodily sensation, not size."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"When an object is seen as too small in size, the disorder is termed:","options":["Micropsia","Macropsia","Hypoesthesia","Coenaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Micropsia = object seen too small in size.  Why B wrong: Macropsia = too large. Why C/D wrong: These are sensation-related terms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Diminished sensation (as in depression, requiring a more stimulant environment) is termed:","options":["Hypoesthesia","Hyperesthesia","Macropsia","Coenaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypoesthesia = diminished sensation, like depression (requires more stimulant environment).  Why B wrong: Hyperesthesia is increased sensation (mania). Why C/D wrong: These are size/bodily-sensation terms."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Increased sensation, as seen in mania (requiring a less stimulant environment), is called:","options":["Hyperesthesia","Hypoesthesia","Coenaesthesia","Anesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hyperesthesia = increased sensation like mania (requires less stimulant environment).  Why B wrong: Hypoesthesia is diminished sensation (depression). Why C/D wrong: Coenaesthesia is bodily blend; anesthesia is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The blend of numerous bodily sensations producing an implicit awareness of being alive and being in a particular physical condition (e.g., well or ill, energetic or tired) is called:","options":["Coenaesthesia","Hyperesthesia","Macropsia","Hypoesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Coenaesthesia (coenesthesia) is the blend of bodily sensations producing implicit awareness of being alive and in a physical condition.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are sensation-intensity or object-size disorders."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the Types of Hallucinations table, which is the MOST COMMON type of hallucination and is common in schizophrenic patients?","options":["Auditory hallucination","Visual hallucination","Olfactory hallucination","Tactile hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Auditory hallucination (hearing nonexistent sounds) is the most common type and common in schizophrenia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other, less common types with different associations."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"(NIMHANS 2018) Visual hallucination is most commonly seen in which condition?","options":["Alzheimer's disease","Schizophrenia","Depression","Epilepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Visual hallucination is most commonly seen in Alzheimer's disease; it is common in acute organic brain stem conditions and second most common in alcohol withdrawal.  Why B/C/D wrong: Auditory is common in schizophrenia; gustatory in depression; olfactory can be caused by epilepsy."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Visual hallucination is the SECOND most commonly seen in which clients?","options":["Alcohol withdrawal clients","Depressed clients","Epileptic clients","Manic clients"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table states visual hallucination is second most commonly seen in alcohol withdrawal clients.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the association given for visual hallucination."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A person who smells unpleasant odours that do not exist, most commonly seen in schizophrenia and organic states (and can be caused by epilepsy), has which type of hallucination?","options":["Olfactory hallucination","Gustatory hallucination","Tactile hallucination","Auditory hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Olfactory hallucination = smelling odours that don't exist, usually unpleasant, most common in schizophrenia/organic states, can be caused by epilepsy.  Why B/C/D wrong: These involve taste, touch, or sound respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A person who tastes something that is not present has a gustatory hallucination, which is commonly seen in:","options":["Depression","Schizophrenia","Mania","Epilepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Gustatory hallucination (tasting something not present) is commonly seen in depression per the table.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the association given for gustatory hallucination."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A person senses that they are being touched when they are not; this hallucination is commonly seen in substance use such as cocaine or amphetamine. This is:","options":["Tactile hallucination","Kinetic hallucination","Auditory hallucination","Olfactory hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tactile hallucination = sensing being touched when not, commonly seen in cocaine/amphetamine use.  Why B wrong: Kinetic is sensation of flying/moving the body. Why C/D wrong: These involve sound and smell."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The sensation of flying or moving the body or a part of the body, also known as motor hallucination, is called:","options":["Kinetic hallucination","Tactile hallucination","Pseudo hallucination","Gustatory hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kinetic hallucination = sensation of flying/moving body or a body part; also known as motor hallucination.  Why B/C/D wrong: These involve touch, distinguishable-from-reality, and taste respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which type of hallucination is characterized by the patient being able to distinguish it from reality?","options":["Pseudo hallucination","Auditory hallucination","Kinetic hallucination","Tactile hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pseudo hallucination = the patient can distinguish them from reality.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are true hallucinations without that insight."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The phenomenon of a phantom limb feeling (like the absence of extremities) is described in the source as:","options":["Phantom phenomenon","Kinaesthetic hallucination","Hypnagogic hallucination","Coenaesthesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phantom phenomenon = phantom limb feeling like absence of extremities.  Why B/C/D wrong: Kinaesthetic relates to body-movement hallucination; hypnagogic occurs while falling asleep; coenaesthesia is bodily-sensation blend."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A hallucination related to body movement (like a feeling of paralysis), which can be due to LSD (Lysergic acid Diethylamide), is called:","options":["Kinaesthetic hallucination","Hypnopompic hallucination","Gustatory hallucination","Pseudo hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kinaesthetic hallucination = hallucination related to body movement (like paralysis); the source notes it is due to LSD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are unrelated to body-movement/LSD."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A vivid perception occurring while falling asleep is termed:","options":["Hypnagogic hallucination","Hypnopompic hallucination","Kinaesthetic hallucination","Auditory hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypnagogic hallucination occurs while falling asleep (vivid perception before sleep).  Why B wrong: Hypnopompic occurs while waking up. Why C/D wrong: Unrelated to sleep transition."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A hallucination occurring while waking up is called:","options":["Hypnopompic hallucination","Hypnagogic hallucination","Kinaesthetic hallucination","Pseudo hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypnopompic hallucination occurs while waking up.  Why B wrong: Hypnagogic occurs while falling asleep. Why C/D wrong: Not sleep-transition related."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Match perception to its lobe of interpretation. Visual perception is interpreted in which lobe?","options":["Occipital lobe","Temporal lobe","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Visual perception is interpreted in the occipital lobe.  Why B wrong: Auditory & olfactory in temporal lobe. Why C wrong: Touch & taste in parietal lobe. Why D wrong: Frontal is not stated for these."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Touch and taste perception are interpreted in which lobe of the brain?","options":["Parietal lobe","Occipital lobe","Temporal lobe","Frontal lobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Touch & taste perception are interpreted in the parietal lobe.  Why B wrong: Occipital = visual. Why C wrong: Temporal = auditory & olfactory. Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Auditory and olfactory perception are interpreted in which lobe?","options":["Temporal lobe","Occipital lobe","Parietal lobe","Frontal lobe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Auditory & olfactory perception are interpreted in the temporal lobe.  Why B wrong: Occipital = visual. Why C wrong: Parietal = touch & taste. Why D wrong: Not stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Among the negative symptoms of schizophrenia, the loss of pleasure in things which were previously pleasurable is called:","options":["Anhedonia","Avolition","Alogia","Asociality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anhedonia = loss of pleasure in previously pleasurable things.  Why B wrong: Avolition = lack of motivation. Why C wrong: Alogia = poverty of speech. Why D wrong: Asociality = social withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The negative symptom described as \"lack of desire or motivation to do anything/any work\" is:","options":["Avolition","Anhedonia","Alogia","Asociality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avolition = lack of desire or motivation to do any work.  Why B wrong: Anhedonia = loss of pleasure. Why C wrong: Alogia = poverty of speech. Why D wrong: Asociality = social withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"\"Poverty of speech\" as a negative symptom of schizophrenia is termed:","options":["Alogia","Avolition","Anhedonia","Asociality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alogia = poverty of speech.  Why B/C/D wrong: These refer to motivation, pleasure, and social withdrawal respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The negative symptom in which the patient is socially withdrawn is called:","options":["Asociality","Alogia","Avolition","Anhedonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asociality = patient is socially withdrawn.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are poverty of speech, lack of motivation, and loss of pleasure respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A false, unshakable belief that is out of keeping with the patient's social and cultural background is defined as:","options":["Delusion","Hallucination","Illusion","Obsession"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion = a false, unshakable belief out of keeping with the patient's social/cultural background; it is a thought disorder.  Why B wrong: Hallucination is a perception disorder. Why C wrong: Illusion is misperception of a real stimulus. Why D wrong: Obsession is an unwelcome recurring idea."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Delusion is classified as which category of disorder?","options":["Thought disorder","Perception disorder","Mood disorder","Motor activity disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states delusion is a thought disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are separate categories; delusion is specifically a thought disorder."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient who thinks that people are planning to kill or harm him/her has which type of delusion?","options":["Delusion of persecution","Delusion of grandiosity","Delusion of reference","Delusion of control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of persecution = patient thinks people are planning to kill or harm him/her.  Why B/C/D wrong: These involve superiority, being talked about, and being controlled respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient who feels more superior and powerful than he/she really is has delusion of grandiosity, which is commonly seen in:","options":["Mania","Depression","Schizophrenia","OCD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of grandiosity (feeling superior/powerful) is commonly seen in mania.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the association given in the table."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient believes his/her spouse is unfaithful, usually seen in chronic alcoholic clients, and this is also known as \"Othello syndrome.\" This is:","options":["Delusion of infidelity","Delusion of erotomania","Delusion of reference","Delusion of nihilism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of infidelity = belief that spouse is unfaithful, seen in chronic alcoholic clients, also called Othello syndrome.  Why B wrong: Erotomania is belief another person is in love with the patient. Why C/D wrong: Reference and nihilism differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The TRICK given for Othello syndrome (\"My wife is doing Hell O with Other\") is used to remember which delusion?","options":["Delusion of infidelity","Delusion of jealousy","Delusion of erotomania","Delusion of somatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The TRICK \"My wife is doing Hell O with Other\" is a mnemonic for Othello syndrome = delusion of infidelity.  Why B/C/D wrong: These delusions have different definitions/tricks."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient feels that things do not exist (including self) and that everything is unreal; commonly seen in depression/elderly with severe depression. This delusion is also known as Cotard syndrome or walking corpse syndrome. It is:","options":["Delusion of nihilism","Delusion of persecution","Delusion of grandiosity","Delusion of double"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of nihilism = belief that everything (including self) does not exist/is unreal; also known as Cotard syndrome/walking corpse syndrome; seen in depression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These have distinct definitions."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The TRICK \"kuch hai hi NAHI\" is used to recall which delusion?","options":["Nihilism","Grandiosity","Persecution","Erotomania"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The TRICK \"kuch hai hi NAHI\" is the mnemonic for delusion of Nihilism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These delusions are not linked to this trick."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient believes that another person, usually of higher status, is in love with them. This delusion, found exclusively in girls and commonly seen in schizophrenia, is also known as:","options":["de Clérambault syndrome / Psychoses passionate","Othello syndrome","Cotard syndrome","Capgras syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of erotomania = belief a higher-status person loves the patient; found exclusively in girls, seen in schizophrenia; also known as de Clérambault syndrome or Psychoses passionate. (Source spells it \"Declerambault\"; correct spelling is de Clérambault.)  Why B wrong: Othello = infidelity. Why C wrong: Cotard = nihilism. Why D wrong: Capgras = delusion of double."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient thinks that his/her sexual partner or spouse/lover is unfaithful. This is termed:","options":["Delusion of jealousy","Delusion of infidelity","Delusion of erotomania","Delusion of reference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of jealousy = patient thinks that his/her sexual partner/spouse/lover is unfaithful.  Why B wrong: Infidelity (Othello) is worded around the spouse cheating and is linked to alcoholism; jealousy is its own listed row. Why C/D wrong: Erotomania and reference differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient thinks \"I have a physical defect or general medical condition.\" This delusion is called:","options":["Delusion of somatic","Delusion of nihilism","Delusion of sexual","Delusion of guilty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of somatic = patient thinks he/she has a physical defect or general medical condition.  Why B/C/D wrong: Nihilism is non-existence; sexual and guilt delusions differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The delusion that others know about one's sexual activity and that the activity causes illness is called:","options":["Delusion of sexual","Delusion of somatic","Delusion of reference","Delusion of guilty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of sexual = belief others know about one's sexual activity and that the activity causes illness.  Why B/C/D wrong: Somatic is physical defect; reference is being talked about; guilt is remorse."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A client thinks \"People are talking about me,\" or believes that news bulletins/radio music have a direct reference to him/her. This is:","options":["Delusion of reference","Delusion of persecution","Delusion of control","Delusion of grandiosity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of reference = belief that people/news bulletins/radio music refer directly to the patient.  Why B wrong: Persecution is fear of harm. Why C wrong: Control is being controlled by external force. Why D wrong: Grandiosity is superiority."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The false belief that another person or external force controls one's general thoughts, feelings, impulses, or behaviours is called:","options":["Delusion of control","Delusion of reference","Delusion of persecution","Delusion of guilty"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of control = belief that another person/external force controls one's thoughts, feelings, impulses, or behaviours.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe being referenced, harmed, or feeling guilt."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"An ungrounded feeling of remorse or guilt of delusional intensity is called:","options":["Delusion of guilty","Delusion of worthlessness","Delusion of somatic","Delusion of nihilism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of guilty = ungrounded feeling of remorse or guilt of delusional intensity.  Why B wrong: Worthlessness/poverty is assumption of no existence of self. Why C/D wrong: Somatic and nihilism differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The \"Delusion of Worthlessness/Hopelessness/Helplessness,\" in which the client assumes no existence of self and is usually seen in depression, is also known as:","options":["Delusion of poverty","Cotard syndrome","Capgras syndrome","Othello syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: This delusion is also known as delusion of poverty, usually seen in depression.  Why B wrong: Cotard = nihilism. Why C wrong: Capgras = delusion of double. Why D wrong: Othello = infidelity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"When a familiar person is believed to be replaced by an unfamiliar person (or an unfamiliar person replaces the familiar one), the delusion is called delusion of double, also known as:","options":["Capgras syndrome","Cotard syndrome","de Clérambault syndrome","Othello syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of double = familiar person replaced by an unfamiliar one; also known as Capgras syndrome.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are nihilism, erotomania, and infidelity respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The delusion in which persons are replaced or changed into one another (e.g., brother became sister; sister became mother) is called:","options":["Delusion of inter-metamorphosis","Delusion of double","Delusion of reference","Delusion of control"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of inter-metamorphosis = persons are replacing/changing into one another (brother→sister; sister→mother).  Why B wrong: Double (Capgras) is replacement by an unfamiliar person. Why C/D wrong: Reference and control differ."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In the comparison table, which statement best characterises DELUSIONS?","options":["Fixed, false, unshakable beliefs that cannot be corrected by logic and are not consistent with the culture/education of the patient","False sensory perception experienced without a real external stimulus","Misperception with the presence of a real external stimulus","A coordinated body movement due to motor response"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusions = fixed, false, unshakable beliefs not correctable by logic and inconsistent with culture/education.  Why B wrong: That describes hallucinations. Why C wrong: That describes illusions. Why D wrong: That describes activity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the comparison table, hallucinations are usually experienced as originating:","options":["In the outside world, not within the mind as imagination","Only within the mind as imagination","As a misperception of a real object","As a fixed false belief"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hallucinations = false sensory perception without a real stimulus, usually experienced as originating in the outside world (not within the mind as imagination).  Why B wrong: The table specifically says NOT within the mind as imagination. Why C wrong: That is an illusion. Why D wrong: That is a delusion."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Per the comparison table, illusions are most likely to occur when:","options":["The general level of sensory stimulation (consciousness) is reduced","Sensory stimulation is at its maximum","There is no external stimulus at all","The patient is fully alert and oriented"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Illusions (misperception with a real external stimulus) are most likely when the general level of sensory stimulation/consciousness is reduced.  Why B/D wrong: Reduced (not maximal/fully alert) stimulation favours illusions. Why C wrong: Illusions require a real external stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The limbic system is also known by which of the following names? (Select all that apply)","options":["Emotional brain","Paleomammalian cortex","Neocortex","Reticular formation"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The limbic system is also known as the emotional brain or paleomammalian cortex.  Why C/D wrong: Neocortex and reticular formation are not given as synonyms in the source."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which are listed as functions of the limbic system? (Select all that apply)","options":["Motivation","Emotion","Long-term memory","Olfaction","Regulation of blood pressure"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Functions of the limbic system = motivation, emotion, behaviour, long-term memory, learning and olfaction.  Why E wrong: Blood pressure regulation is not listed as a limbic function."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which of the following are important structures within the limbic system per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Amygdala","Hippocampus","Hypothalamus","Cingulate gyrus","Cerebellum"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Structures within the limbic system = amygdala, hippocampus, thalamus, hypothalamus, basal ganglia, and cingulate gyrus.  Why E wrong: The cerebellum is not listed among the limbic structures."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The limbic system is located on both sides of which structure, immediately beneath the medial temporal lobe?","options":["Thalamus","Cerebellum","Pituitary gland","Medulla"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The limbic system is a set of brain structures located on both sides of the thalamus, beneath the medial temporal lobe, primarily in the forebrain.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the reference structure stated."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Narcissistic, egocentric thought processes such as fantasizing and daydreaming that have little or no relation to reality describe:","options":["Autistic thinking","Flight of ideas","Clang association","Thought blocking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autistic thinking = narcissistic, egocentric thought processes (fantasizing/daydreaming) with little/no relation to reality.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other thought disorders."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A developmental disorder characterized by difficulties with social interaction and communication, and by restricted and repetitive behaviour, with signs often noticed within the first three years of life, is:","options":["Autism","Asperger syndrome","Autistic thinking","Flight of ideas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autism = developmental disorder with social/communication difficulties and restricted/repetitive behaviour; signs noticed in the first three years.  Why B wrong: Asperger is a neurodevelopmental disorder with social/nonverbal difficulties. Why C/D wrong: These are thought-process disorders."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Autism and Asperger syndrome together are generally known as:","options":["Autism spectrum disorders (ASD)","Personality disorders","Mood disorders","Psychotic disorders"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The autism spectrum encompasses a range of neurodevelopmental conditions including autism and Asperger syndrome, generally known as autism spectrum disorders (ASD).  Why B/C/D wrong: These categories do not describe the autism spectrum."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A neurodevelopmental disorder characterized by significant difficulties in social interaction and nonverbal communication, along with restricted and repetitive patterns of behaviour and interests, is:","options":["Asperger syndrome (AS)","Autism","Clang association","Word salad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Asperger syndrome = neurodevelopmental disorder with difficulties in social interaction/nonverbal communication and restricted, repetitive behaviour/interests.  Why B wrong: Autism has broader social/communication impairment noticed in first 3 years. Why C/D wrong: These are speech/thought disorders."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient quickly jumps from one idea/topic to another, with association between the ideas, in continuous rapid speech; this is most commonly seen in mania and schizophrenia. This is:","options":["Flight of ideas","Clang association","Circumstantiality","Thought blocking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flight of ideas = rapid movement between ideas WITH association; most commonly seen in mania and schizophrenia.  Why B wrong: Clang is based on pronunciation, not meaning. Why C wrong: Circumstantiality eventually reaches the goal. Why D wrong: Thought blocking is sudden stoppage."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Which thought disorder is included as one of the \"4A symptoms\" of schizophrenia and is characterised by rapid shifting from one topic to another WITHOUT any association?","options":["Associative looseness","Flight of ideas","Tangentiality","Perseveration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Associative looseness is included in the 4A symptoms of schizophrenia; rapid shifting between topics without any association.  Why B wrong: Flight of ideas has association between ideas. Why C/D wrong: These describe other thought patterns."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A patient uses words according to their pronunciation (rhyming) rather than their meaning (e.g., CAT, NAT, MAT, HAT); usually seen in schizophrenia. This is:","options":["Clang association","Word salad","Flight of ideas","Incoherence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clang association = using words by pronunciation not meaning (same pronunciation, non-existence words), usually seen in schizophrenia (e.g., CAT, NAT, MAT, HAT).  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe different speech/thought disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Sudden stoppage of ideas or thought, usually seen in depressive patients, is called:","options":["Thought blocking","Thought retardation","Thought perseveration","Incoherence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thought blocking = sudden stoppage of ideas/thought, usually seen in depressive patients.  Why B wrong: Thought retardation is slowness of ideas. Why C wrong: Perseveration is continuous thinking about negative events. Why D wrong: Incoherence is word salad."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"\"Slowness of ideas,\" such that the patient takes too much time to complete a sentence, describes:","options":["Thought retardation","Thought blocking","Flight of ideas","Tangentiality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thought retardation = slowness of ideas; patient takes too much time to complete the sentence.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe stoppage, rapid ideas, and off-topic talk respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Continuous thinking about negative events in the past or future (like worry, rumination, brooding, and mind-wandering about negative topics), usually seen in schizophrenia, is called:","options":["Thought perseveration","Thought retardation","Incoherence","Obsession"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thought perseveration (perseverative cognition) = continuous thinking about negative events, seen in schizophrenia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe slowness, word salad, and recurring unwanted ideas respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Speech that is semantically unconnected and conveys no meaning to the listener (word salad), where the patient does not give an exact answer, is termed:","options":["Incoherence","Clang association","Tangentiality","Circumstantiality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Incoherence (word salad) = speech that is semantically unconnected and conveys no meaning; patient does not give an exact answer.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe different disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A confused or unintelligible mixture of seemingly random words and phrases, specifically indicative of advanced schizophrenia, is called:","options":["Word salad","Clang association","Flight of ideas","Circumstantiality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Word salad = confused/unintelligible mixture of random words and phrases, a form of speech indicative of advanced schizophrenia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other speech/thought disorders."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"(AIIMS, PGI, JIPMER, ESIC, CHO) A condition also known as \"Doctor shopping,\" where the patient falsely believes he/she has a major illness despite minor symptoms and diagnosis indicates absence of illness, is:","options":["Hypochondriasis","Phobia","Obsession","Word salad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypochondriasis = also known as \"Doctor shopping\"; false belief of major illness despite minor symptoms with diagnosis showing absence of illness.  Why B wrong: Phobia is irrational fear. Why C wrong: Obsession is recurring unwelcome ideas. Why D wrong: Word salad is disorganized speech."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"An irrational/illogical fear of any situation, object, or activity is called:","options":["Phobia","Obsession","Compulsion","Hypochondriasis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Phobia = irrational/illogical fear of any situation/object/activity.  Why B wrong: Obsession is a recurring unwanted idea. Why C wrong: Compulsion is an act to relieve anxiety. Why D wrong: Hypochondriasis is false belief of illness."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"In obsessive-compulsive phenomena, the frequently unwelcomed stereotyped ideas that come into the mind represent which component?","options":["Obsession (cognition)","Compulsion (conation)","Affection","Perception"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Obsession = frequently unwelcomed stereotyped ideas coming into mind, known as cognition.  Why B wrong: Compulsion (acting on obsession) is conation. Why C wrong: The fear/anxiety caused is affection. Why D wrong: Perception is unrelated here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"The act a person performs on an obsession to resolve anxiety/fear is called compulsion, which is described as a disorder of:","options":["Activity (conation)","Cognition","Affection","Perception"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Person acts on obsession to resolve anxiety/fear = compulsion (conation); compulsion is a disorder of activity.  Why B wrong: Cognition = obsession/idea. Why C wrong: Affection = the fear/anxiety. Why D wrong: Perception is not involved."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"A client who cannot reach the main topic even after unnecessary talking, seen in mania, is displaying:","options":["Tangentiality","Circumstantiality","Flight of ideas","Incoherence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tangentiality = client cannot reach the main topic after unnecessary talking; also seen in mania.  Why B wrong: In circumstantiality the client eventually reaches the central topic. Why C/D wrong: These are different disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"(FAQs) A client who eventually reaches the central topic after unnecessary talking, usually seen in mania patients, is displaying:","options":["Circumstantiality","Tangentiality","Clang association","Thought blocking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Circumstantiality = client WILL reach the centre/central topic after unnecessary talking; usually seen in mania.  Why B wrong: In tangentiality the client never reaches the main topic. Why C/D wrong: Different disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"According to the source, an \"Idea\" is best described as:","options":["A unit of thinking","A false belief","A perception disorder","A motor response"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states idea is a unit of thinking.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe delusion, perception disorders, and activity respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"\"Activity,\" as defined under Disorder of Motor Activity, is:","options":["A coordinated body movement due to motor response","A false sensory perception","A fixed false belief","A unit of thinking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Activity = coordinated body movement due to motor response.  Why B/C/D wrong: These define hallucination, delusion, and idea respectively."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Increased psychomotor activity, described as purposeless activity, is seen in which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mania","Catatonic schizophrenia","Agitated depression","Grief","Coma"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased psychomotor activity (purposeless) is seen in mania, catatonic schizophrenia, and agitated depression.  Why D wrong: Grief is linked to decreased psychomotor activity. Why E wrong: Coma is not listed here."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Decreased psychomotor activity (in which purposeful activities are decreased) is seen in which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Depression","Grief","Catatonic schizophrenia [stupor]","Mania"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased psychomotor activity is seen in depression, grief, and catatonic schizophrenia [stupor], with purposeful activities decreased.  Why D wrong: Mania shows increased psychomotor activity."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"\"Dysactivity,\" characterised by repeated activity/position/movement/speech, includes automatic activity such as echolalia and echopraxia. Echolalia refers to:","options":["Automatic repetition of others' speech","Automatic imitation of others' movements","Sudden stoppage of thought","Poverty of speech"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under dysactivity, automatic activity includes echolalia (repetition of speech) and echopraxia; echolalia is the automatic repetition of speech.  Why B wrong: Echopraxia is imitation of movements. Why C wrong: That is thought blocking. Why D wrong: That is alogia."},{"section":"Batch 003 · Defence mechanisms and examples, classification of mental disorders…","question":"Under Dysactivity in the Disorder of Motor Activity section, which are listed as forms of \"Violence\"? (Select all that apply)","options":["Suicide","Compulsion","Negativism","Echolalia","Anhedonia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Violence under dysactivity = suicide, compulsion, negativism.  Why D wrong: Echolalia is listed under automatic activity, not violence. Why E wrong: Anhedonia is a negative symptom, not listed here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In activity disorders, the term \"Stereotype activity\" refers to:","options":["Repeated position","Repeated activity","Repetition of voice","Loss of muscle tone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stereotype activity is defined as repeated activity.  Why A wrong: Repeated position is \"stereotype position.\"  Why C wrong: Repetition of voice/imitation of voice is echolalia.  Why D wrong: Loss of muscle tone describes cataplexy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Waxy-flexibility\" in psychiatric activity disorders is best described as:","options":["Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion","A decreased response to stimuli and a tendency to remain in an immobile posture","Repetition of self-spoken words with decreasing volume","Obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Waxy-flexibility is a decreased response to stimuli and a tendency to remain in an immobile posture.  Why A wrong: Sudden emotion-triggered loss of muscle tone is cataplexy.  Why C wrong: Repetition of self-spoken words with decreasing volume is palilalia.  Why D wrong: Obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus is verbigeration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Catalepsy is a neurological symptom due to loss of muscle tone with loss of consciousness and sensation, commonly seen in:","options":["Narcolepsy","Parkinson's disease","Depression","Mania"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catalepsy is described as a neurological symptom due to loss of muscle tone with loss of consciousness and sensation, like seen in Parkinson's disease.  Why A wrong: Narcolepsy is associated with cataplexy.  Why C wrong: Suicide (self-harm) is usually seen in depression, not catalepsy.  Why D wrong: Violence is usually seen in mania."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Cataplexy — loss of muscle tone without warning often triggered by emotion/conditions — is usually seen in:","options":["Parkinson's disease","Schizophrenia","Narcolepsy","Depression"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Cataplexy is loss of muscle tone without warning, often triggered by emotion or conditioning, usually seen in narcolepsy.  Why A wrong: Catalepsy (not cataplexy) is linked to Parkinson's disease.  Why B wrong: Coprophakia/schizophrenia link is different.  Why D wrong: Depression is linked to suicide."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"A \"TIC\" is defined as:","options":["Repetition of movements in a stereotyped or habitual manner","Abnormal or involuntary purposeless movement in children","Difficulty in learning to read or interpret words","Obsessive repetition of random words"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: TIC is an abnormal or involuntary purposeless movement in children.  Why A wrong: That describes mannerism.  Why C wrong: That describes dyslexia.  Why D wrong: That describes verbigeration."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Repetition of movements in a stereotyped manner or a habitual gesture or way of speaking/behaving is termed:","options":["Verbigeration","Mannerism","Echopraxia","Stereotypy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mannerism (FAQs) is repetition of movements in a stereotyped manner or a habitual gesture or way of speaking/behaving.  Why A wrong: Verbigeration is obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus.  Why C wrong: Echopraxia is repetition/imitation of another's movement.  Why D wrong: Stereotypy is repeated activity/position."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Verbigeration\" is defined as:","options":["Repeated speech, also known as stereotype speech","Obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus","Repetition of self-spoken words with decreased tone","Use of obscene words in public"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Verbigeration is the obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus.  Why A wrong: That is repeated speech (stereotype speech).  Why C wrong: That is palilalia.  Why D wrong: That is coprolalia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Repeated speech is also known as:","options":["Palilalia","Stereotype speech","Echolalia","Verbigeration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Repeated speech is also known as stereotype speech.  Why A wrong: Palilalia is repetition of self-spoken words with decreasing tone/volume.  Why C wrong: Echolalia is repetition/imitation of another's voice.  Why D wrong: Verbigeration is obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Difficulty in learning to read or interpret words, letters, and other symbols, but that does not affect general intelligence, is called:","options":["Dyslalia","Dyslexia","Agnosia","Alexithymia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dyslexia is difficulty in learning to read or interpret words, letters, and other symbols, not affecting general intelligence.  Why A wrong: Dyslalia is inability to articulate comprehensible speech, especially with use of private words/sounds.  Why C wrong: Agnosia is inability to recognise familiar objects.  Why D wrong: Alexithymia is inability to identify/describe emotions."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Echolalia — repetition of voice or imitation of voice — is usually seen in which condition, e.g., a doctor asking a patient \"what is your name?\" and the patient repeats the same sentence?","options":["Catatonic schizophrenia (stupor)","Depression","Mania","Narcolepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Echolalia (repetition/imitation of voice) is usually seen in catatonic schizophrenia [stupor].  Why B wrong: Depression is linked to suicide.  Why C wrong: Mania is linked to violence.  Why D wrong: Narcolepsy is linked to cataplexy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Repetition of movement/physical activity or imitative activity/behaviour is called (NIMHANS 2018):","options":["Echolalia","Echopraxia","Coprolalia","Palilalia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Echopraxia is repetition of movement/physical activity or imitative activity/behaviour.  Why A wrong: Echolalia is imitation of voice.  Why C wrong: Coprolalia is use of obscene words.  Why D wrong: Palilalia is repetition of self-spoken words with decreased tone."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Repetition of self-spoken words with a decrease in tone, pressure, and volume is termed:","options":["Echolalia","Palilalia","Coprolalia","Verbigeration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Palilalia is repetition of self-spoken words with a decrease in tone, pressure and volume.  Why A wrong: Echolalia is imitation of another's voice.  Why C wrong: Coprolalia is use of obscene words in public.  Why D wrong: Verbigeration is obsessive repetition of random words without a stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The use of obscene (socially unaccepted) words in language in a public place is called:","options":["Coprolalia","Copropraxia","Coprophakia","Dyslalia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Coprolalia is use of obscene words in language in a public place.  Why B wrong: Copropraxia is use of obscene movement in public place (a tic disorder/socially unaccepted movements).  Why C wrong: Coprophakia is stool eating.  Why D wrong: Dyslalia is inability to articulate comprehensible speech."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Copropraxia refers to:","options":["Use of obscene words in public","Use of obscene movement in a public place (tic disorder / socially unaccepted movements)","Stool eating","Repetition of self-spoken words"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Copropraxia is use of obscene movement in a public place — a tic disorder / socially unaccepted movements.  Why A wrong: Use of obscene words is coprolalia.  Why C wrong: Stool eating is coprophakia.  Why D wrong: Repetition of self-spoken words is palilalia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Coprophakia (stool eating) is usually seen in animals and in clients with:","options":["Depression","Mania","Schizophrenia","Narcolepsy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Coprophakia (stool eating) is usually seen in animals and clients with schizophrenia.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifically links coprophakia to schizophrenia, not depression, mania, or narcolepsy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Dyslalia is defined as:","options":["Difficulty in learning to read/interpret words","Inability to articulate comprehensible speech, especially associated with use of private words or sounds","Inability to recognise faces","Inability to recall familiar names"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dyslalia is the inability to articulate comprehensible speech, especially when associated with the use of private words or sounds.  Why A wrong: That is dyslexia.  Why C wrong: Inability to recognise faces is prosopagnosia.  Why D wrong: Inability to recall familiar names is lethologica."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"An act to harm others, usually seen in mania (manic episode) and considered a psychiatric emergency, is termed:","options":["Suicide","Violence","TIC","Mannerism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Violence is an act to harm others, usually seen in mania (manic episode) and is a psychiatric emergency.  Why A wrong: Suicide is an act to harm one's self, usually seen in depression.  Why C wrong: TIC is involuntary purposeless movement in children.  Why D wrong: Mannerism is repetition of habitual gestures."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Suicide (act to harm one's self) is usually seen in:","options":["Mania","Depression","Narcolepsy","Delirium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suicide is an act to harm one's self, usually seen in depression.  Why A wrong: Violence (harming others) is seen in mania.  Why C/D wrong: Not the associated conditions per the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"During a manic episode, the level of serotonin and non-epinephrine is:","options":["Decreased","Increased","Unchanged","Absent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During the manic episode, the level of serotonin and non-epinephrine has been increased.  Why A wrong: Decrease of serotonin/non-epinephrine occurs during the depression episode.  Why C/D wrong: The source specifies an increase in mania."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Euphoria — a mild elevation of mood characterised by an increased sense of happiness and psychological wellbeing — is also known as:","options":["Elation","Hypomania","Ecstasy","Exaltation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Euphoria is a mild elevation of mood, also known as hypomania.  Why A wrong: Elation is a moderate elevation of mood with increased psychomotor activity.  Why C wrong: Ecstasy is a very severe elevation of mood.  Why D wrong: Exaltation is a severe elevation of mood with delusion of grandiosity."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"A moderate elevation of mood with increased psychomotor activity is termed:","options":["Euphoria","Elation","Exaltation","Ecstasy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Elation is a moderate elevation of mood with increase in psychomotor activity.  Why A wrong: Euphoria is a mild elevation of mood.  Why C wrong: Exaltation is severe elevation with delusions of grandiosity.  Why D wrong: Ecstasy is very severe elevation of mood."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Exaltation — a severe elevation of mood with delusion of grandiosity — is usually seen in:","options":["Mild mania","Severe mania","Depression","Hypomania"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exaltation is severe elevation of mood with delusion of grandiosity, usually seen in severe mania.  Why A/D wrong: Mild elevation/hypomania corresponds to euphoria.  Why C wrong: Depression is an un-pleasurable affect disorder."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Ecstasy — a very severe elevation of mood characterised by a sense of reborn, feeling out of the world and decreased activity — is also known as:","options":["Hypomania","Stuporous mania / delirious mania","Elation","Euphoria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ecstasy is a very severe elevation of mood, also known as stuporous mania / delirious mania.  Why A wrong: Hypomania corresponds to euphoria.  Why C/D wrong: Elation and euphoria are lesser elevations of mood."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The inability to identify and describe emotions experienced by one's self or others, or inability to describe emotions verbally, is called:","options":["Alexithymia","Anhedonia","Depersonalisation","Ambivalence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alexithymia is the inability to identify and describe emotions experienced by one's self or others, or inability to describe emotions verbally.  Why B wrong: Anhedonia is lack/loss of pleasure.  Why C wrong: Depersonalisation is a state where one's thoughts/feelings seem unreal.  Why D wrong: Ambivalence is two contradictory ideas about the same thing at the same time."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"A state in which one's thoughts and feelings seem unreal is termed:","options":["Depersonalisation","Alexithymia","Anhedonia","Fear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Depersonalisation is a state in which one's thoughts and feelings seem unreal.  Why B wrong: Alexithymia is inability to identify/describe emotions.  Why C wrong: Anhedonia is loss of pleasure.  Why D wrong: Fear is an unpleasant emotion caused by threat of danger, pain, or harm."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Labile affect, in which a patient has excessive display of emotions or expresses emotions not congruent with the situation, is also known as:","options":["Blunt affect","Flat affect","Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) / emotional incontinence","Anhedonia"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Labile affect is also known as pseudobulbar affect (PBA) or emotional incontinence.  Why A wrong: Blunt affect is decreased ability to express emotion.  Why B wrong: Flat affect is diminished emotional expression via facial expression.  Why D wrong: Anhedonia is loss of pleasure."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"A decreased ability to express emotion through facial expressions, tone of voice, and physical movements describes:","options":["Flat affect","Blunt affect","Labile affect","Ambivalence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blunt affect is a decreased ability to express emotion through facial expressions, tone of voice, and physical movements.  Why A wrong: Flat affect is diminished emotional expression via facial expression.  Why C wrong: Labile affect is excessive/incongruent display of emotions.  Why D wrong: Ambivalence is contradictory ideas at the same time."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Apathy,\" associated with flat affect, is best defined as:","options":["Loss of pleasure","Lack of interest","Unstable mood","Unreal feelings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Apathy is defined as lack of interest (accompanying flat affect — diminished emotional expression via facial expression).  Why A wrong: Loss of pleasure is anhedonia.  Why C wrong: Unstable mood is mood swing.  Why D wrong: Unreal feelings describe depersonalisation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Anhedonia is defined as:","options":["Lack of pleasure or complete loss of pleasure","Unstable mood","Two contradictory ideas at the same time","Display of reactions not matching the situation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anhedonia is lack of pleasure or complete loss of pleasure.  Why B wrong: Unstable mood is a mood swing.  Why C wrong: That describes ambivalence.  Why D wrong: That describes inappropriate affect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Two contradictory ideas about the same thing, at the same time, in the same person, is termed:","options":["Ambivalence","Depersonalisation","Alexithymia","Mood swing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ambivalence is two contra-indicatory ideas about the same thing, same time, same person.  Why B wrong: Depersonalisation is unreal thoughts/feelings.  Why C wrong: Alexithymia is inability to describe emotions.  Why D wrong: Mood swing is unstable mood."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Display of reactions that do not match the situation one is in is called (NHM-2022 context includes Fear):","options":["Inappropriate affect","Flat affect","Blunt affect","Labile affect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Affect inappropriate is display of reactions that do not match the situation you are in.  Why B wrong: Flat affect is diminished emotional expression.  Why C wrong: Blunt affect is decreased ability to express emotion.  Why D wrong: Labile affect is excessive/incongruent emotional display (PBA)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Memory is studied in a science known as:","options":["Neurology","Cognitive psychology","Psychoanalysis","Behaviourism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Memory is studied in a science known as \"cognitive psychology.\"  Why A/C/D wrong: The source names cognitive psychology specifically."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The two types of memory — short-term and long-term — are also known respectively as:","options":["Sensory memory and reference memory","Working memory and reference memory","Immediate memory and remote memory","Recent memory and remote memory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Short-term and long-term memory are also known as working memory and reference memory.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mix in other memory subtypes not used for this pairing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Events of memory\" consist of which three processes?","options":["Encoding, storage, retrieval","Collection, storage, and recall","Sensation, perception, cognition","Input, process, output"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Events of memory are collection, storage and recall.  Why A wrong: Encoding/retrieval terms are not used by the source here.  Why C/D wrong: Not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The stage model of memory, which outlines three separate stages (sensory memory, short-term memory, long-term memory), was proposed in 1968 by:","options":["Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin","Eugen Bleuler","Kurt Schneider","Alois Alzheimer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The stage model of memory was proposed in 1968 by Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin.  Why B wrong: Bleuler coined \"schizophrenia.\"  Why C wrong: Schneider described first-rank symptoms.  Why D wrong: Alzheimer is associated with Alzheimer's disease."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Immediate memory works within which time frame and always in the conscious mind (e.g., repeat 6 digits forward and backward)?","options":["Seconds to minutes","Minutes to hours","Months to years","Hours to days"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Immediate memory works within seconds to minutes, always in the conscious mind, allowing instant recall (e.g., repeat 6 digits forward and backward).  Why B wrong: Minutes to hours describes recent memory.  Why C wrong: Months to years describes remote memory.  Why D wrong: Not a described range."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Recent memory works in minutes to hours, in the semi/sub-conscious mind, and requires focus/concentration. Which is a classic example?","options":["\"Repeat 6 digits forward and backward\"","\"Did you have breakfast today?\"","\"Which year celebrated your 1st birthday?\"","\"What is your name?\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The example given for recent memory is \"Did you have breakfast today?\"  Why A wrong: Digit repetition is an immediate memory example.  Why C wrong: \"Which year celebrated your 1st birthday?\" is a remote memory example.  Why D wrong: Not used as a memory-type example."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Remote memory (months/years) works in which state of mind?","options":["Conscious mind","Semi/sub-conscious mind","Unconscious mind","Preconscious mind"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Remote memory works in the unconscious mind and cannot be consciously recalled.  Why A wrong: Immediate memory works in the conscious mind.  Why B wrong: Recent memory works in the semi/sub-conscious mind.  Why D wrong: Not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Remote memory can be recalled with the help of hypnosis and abreaction, e.g.:","options":["MMSE","Narco-test","CBC","MSE"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Remote memory can be recalled with the help of hypnosis and abreaction, e.g., narco-test.  Why A wrong: MMSE assesses cognition/dementia.  Why C wrong: CBC monitors clozapine side effects.  Why D wrong: MSE is a mental status exam used for diagnosis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Impairment of memory for events that occurred BEFORE an injury is called:","options":["Retrograde amnesia","Antero-grade amnesia","Global amnesia","Hypermnesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Retrograde amnesia is impairment of memory for events before the injury (past events).  Why B wrong: Antero-grade amnesia is impairment for events after an injury.  Why C wrong: Global amnesia is a sudden episode of complete memory loss.  Why D wrong: Hypermnesia is excessive retention of memory."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Impairment of memory for events AFTER an injury (future events) is termed:","options":["Retrograde amnesia","Antero-grade amnesia","Global amnesia","Paramnesia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antero-grade amnesia is impairment of memory for events after an injury.  Why A wrong: Retrograde amnesia affects events before the injury.  Why C wrong: Global amnesia is sudden complete memory loss.  Why D wrong: Paramnesia is abnormal collection of memory."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"A sudden episode of complete memory loss is termed:","options":["Global amnesia","Retrograde amnesia","Hypermnesia","Lethologica"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Global amnesia is a sudden episode of complete memory loss.  Why B wrong: Retrograde amnesia is limited to events before an injury.  Why C wrong: Hypermnesia is excessive retention of memory.  Why D wrong: Lethologica is inability to recall familiar names."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Excessive retention of memory related to a particular event, commonly seen in paranoid schizophrenia and mania, is called:","options":["Hypermnesia","Paramnesia","Confabulation","Amnesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypermnesia is excessive retention of memory related to a particular event, commonly seen in paranoid schizophrenia and mania.  Why B wrong: Paramnesia is abnormal collection of memory.  Why C wrong: Confabulation is creation of false memories.  Why D wrong: Amnesia is loss of memory."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Paramnesia is defined as:","options":["Loss of memory","Abnormal collection of memory","Excessive retention of memory","Sudden complete memory loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Paramnesia is an abnormal collection of memory.  Why A wrong: Loss of memory is amnesia.  Why C wrong: Excessive retention is hypermnesia.  Why D wrong: Sudden complete memory loss is global amnesia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Creation of false memories to fill up the gap of memory to maintain self-dignity, commonly seen in alcoholic clients or as \"black out,\" is termed:","options":["Confabulation","Déjà-vu","Agnosia","Lethologica"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Confabulation is creation of false memories to fill the memory gap to maintain self-dignity, commonly seen in alcoholic clients or as black out.  Why B wrong: Déjà-vu is a sense of familiarity to the unfamiliar.  Why C wrong: Agnosia is inability to recognise familiar objects.  Why D wrong: Lethologica is inability to recall familiar names."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Korsakoff's psychosis occurs due to a lack of:","options":["Vitamin B12","Vitamin B1 (thiamine)","Vitamin C","Folic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Korsakoff's psychosis occurs due to lack of Vitamin B1 (thiamine).  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies thiamine (B1) deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Déjà-vu is best described as:","options":["A sense of familiarity to the unfamiliar (already seen)","A sense of unfamiliarity to the familiar (never seen)","Inability to recognise familiar objects","Inability to recall familiar names"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Déjà-vu (already seen) is a sense of familiarity to the unfamiliar.  Why B wrong: That is Jamais-vu (never seen).  Why C wrong: That is agnosia.  Why D wrong: That is lethologica."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Jamais-vu (never seen) is defined as:","options":["A sense of familiarity to the unfamiliar","A sense of unfamiliarity to the familiar","Inability to recognise faces","Excessive retention of memory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Jamais-vu (never seen) is a sense of unfamiliarity to the familiar.  Why A wrong: Sense of familiarity to the unfamiliar is déjà-vu.  Why C wrong: Inability to recognise faces is prosopagnosia.  Why D wrong: Excessive retention is hypermnesia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Agnosia — the inability to recognise familiar objects — is usually seen in:","options":["Alzheimer's dementia","Delirium","Mania","Narcolepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Agnosia is inability to recognise familiar objects, usually seen in Alzheimer's dementia.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source links agnosia specifically with Alzheimer's dementia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Lethologica is defined as:","options":["Inability to recall familiar names","Inability to recognise familiar objects","Inability to recognise faces","Loss of memory for events before injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Lethologica is the inability to recall familiar names.  Why B wrong: That is agnosia.  Why C wrong: That is prosopagnosia.  Why D wrong: That is retrograde amnesia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The Narco test is also known by which set of names?","options":["Truth Serum Test, Drug Hypnosis, and Narco Interview Technique","MMSE, MSE, and CBC","Polygraph, brain mapping, and lie detector","Abreaction, catharsis, and hypnosis test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The narco test is also known as Truth Serum Test, Drug Hypnosis, and Narco Interview Technique.  Why B/C wrong: These are different tests/assessments.  Why D wrong: Abreaction/catharsis is the process, not the alternate names."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are mandatory pre-conditions for the Narco test? (Select all that apply)","options":["Permission from the judicial court is essential","Consent from the individual is mandatory","Medical fitness certificate by a physician is obtained prior to the test","Written permission of the police officer","Approval of the family"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The pre-conditions listed are judicial court permission, mandatory individual consent, and a physician's medical fitness certificate before the test.  Why D wrong: Police officer permission is not listed as a pre-condition.  Why E wrong: Family approval is not listed as a pre-condition."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The most common drug used in the Narco test is:","options":["Midazolam","Sodium thiopental","Flunitrazepam","Scopolamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common drug used in the narco test is sodium thiopental.  Why A/C/D wrong: Midazolam, flunitrazepam, and scopolamine are listed as \"other\" drugs, not the most common."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The Narco test is described as a process of:","options":["Sedation and anesthesia","Abreaction or catharsis","Hypnosis and regression","Conditioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The narco test is described as a process of abreaction or catharsis, after which the individual speaks the truth.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the terms the source uses for the narco test process."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Delirium is best characterised as an organic psychosis with:","options":["Impairment in consciousness (cloudy consciousness) and cognition with impairment in attention","Major disturbance in memory that is irreversible","Intact consciousness with impaired personality","Loss of ability to recognise objects"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium (NIMHANS-2018) is an organic psychosis characterised by impairment in consciousness (cloudy consciousness) and cognition with impairment in attention.  Why B wrong: Irreversible major disturbance in memory describes dementia.  Why C wrong: Intact consciousness with impaired personality is dementia.  Why D wrong: That describes agnosia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The neurotransmitter deficient in both delirium and dementia is:","options":["Dopamine","Serotonin","Acetylcholine","GABA"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The neurotransmitter deficient in delirium and dementia is acetylcholine, which is essential in learning and memory.  Why A wrong: Dopamine increase is linked to schizophrenia.  Why B wrong: Serotonin is linked to mood disorders.  Why D wrong: GABA is not identified as deficient here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Delirium is a/an ______ onset and ______ disorder of consciousness (JIPMER-2018, 19):","options":["Insidious; irreversible","Acute; reversible","Gradual; chronic","Abrupt; permanent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium is an acute onset and reversible disorder of consciousness.  Why A wrong: Insidious/irreversible describes dementia.  Why C/D wrong: Delirium is reversible, not chronic/permanent."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The duration of delirium is about:","options":["Less than 6 months","More than 6 months","Months to years","More than 1 year"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The duration of delirium is about < 6 months.  Why B wrong: Duration >6 months describes dementia.  Why C/D wrong: Longer durations describe dementia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Delirium is also known by all of the following names EXCEPT:","options":["Acute brain syndrome","Sun downing syndrome","ICU psychosis","Chronic brain syndrome"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic brain syndrome is the alternate name for dementia, not delirium.  Why A/B/C wrong: Delirium is also known as acute brain syndrome, sun downing syndrome, and ICU psychosis."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Diagnosis of delirium is made by:","options":["CBC and blood culture","MMSE and history taking","CT scan only","Narco test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of delirium is made by MMSE and history taking.  Why A wrong: CBC monitors clozapine side effects.  Why C wrong: CT scan is not the stated diagnostic method.  Why D wrong: Narco test is for verbal elicitation from suspects."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following is a sign/symptom of delirium? (Select all that apply)","options":["Clouded consciousness and confusion","Disorientation and visual hallucination","Diurnal variation / sun downing (worsening of symptoms in the evening)","Impaired memory and impaired personality","Disturbed sleep-wake cycle"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium signs include clouded consciousness, confusion, disorientation, visual hallucination, diurnal variation/sun downing (evening worsening), and disturbed sleep-wake cycle; personal care, personality, and memory remain intact.  Why D wrong: In delirium, memory and personality are intact (impairment of these is characteristic of dementia)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In delirium, which of the following remains INTACT?","options":["Attention","Consciousness","Personal care, personality, and memory","Orientation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In delirium, personal care, personality, and memory remain intact.  Why A wrong: Attention is impaired in delirium.  Why B wrong: Consciousness is clouded/impaired in delirium.  Why D wrong: Orientation is impaired (disorientation)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Dementia is an organic psychosis primarily characterised by:","options":["Impairment in consciousness","Major disturbances in memory (irreversible, chronic)","Acute reversible confusion","Elevated mood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dementia is an organic psychosis characterised by major disturbances in memory; it is irreversible and chronic, primarily affecting memory (FAQs, JIPMER, NIMHANS-2019, 20).  Why A wrong: Impairment in consciousness is delirium.  Why C wrong: Acute reversible confusion is delirium.  Why D wrong: Elevated mood is mania."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The most common type of dementia, commonly occurring in old age people, is (AIIMS, PGI, ESIC):","options":["Vascular dementia","Alzheimer's dementia","Lewy body dementia","Frontotemporal dementia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alzheimer's dementia is the most common type of dementia, commonly occurring in old-age people.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source names Alzheimer's dementia as the most common type."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The duration of dementia is:","options":["Less than 6 months","More than 6 months","Hours to weeks","Minutes to hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The duration of the disorder (dementia) is >6 months.  Why A wrong: <6 months is delirium.  Why C wrong: Hours to weeks is delirium duration in the comparison table.  Why D wrong: Not applicable."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In dementia, which of the following is INTACT?","options":["Memory","Intellectual functioning","Consciousness","Personality"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In dementia, consciousness is intact, while personality, memory, and intellectual functioning are impaired.  Why A/B/D wrong: Memory, intellectual functioning, and personality are all impaired in dementia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The \"4 A's symptoms\" of dementia include Agnosia, Apraxia, Ataxia, and Aphasia. Apraxia is defined as:","options":["Inability to recognise familiar objects","Inability to perform familiar tasks/objects","Loss of control on body movements","Inability to speak familiar words"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A-Apraxia is inability to perform familiar object/task.  Why A wrong: Inability to recognise familiar objects is agnosia.  Why C wrong: Loss of control of body movements is ataxia.  Why D wrong: Inability to speak familiar words is aphasia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the 4 A's of dementia, \"Ataxia\" refers to:","options":["Inability to recognise familiar objects","Loss of control on body movements","Inability to speak familiar words","Inability to perform familiar tasks"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A-Ataxia is loss of control on body movements.  Why A wrong: Recognition failure is agnosia.  Why C wrong: Speech failure is aphasia.  Why D wrong: Task performance failure is apraxia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the 4 A's of dementia, \"Aphasia\" is:","options":["Inability to recognise familiar objects","Inability to perform familiar tasks","Loss of control on body movements","Inability to speak familiar words"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: A-Aphasia is inability to speak familiar words.  Why A wrong: Agnosia is failure to recognise objects.  Why B wrong: Apraxia is failure to perform tasks.  Why C wrong: Ataxia is loss of body movement control."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The inability to recognise a face, seen in dementia, is termed:","options":["Prosopagnosia","Anosognosia","Dysarthria","Iculia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Prosopagnosia is the inability to recognise a face.  Why B wrong: Anosognosia is being unaware of the existence of one's own disability.  Why C wrong: Dysarthria is disturbance in articulation of speech.  Why D wrong: Iculia is disturbance in mathematical skills."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Being unaware of the existence of one's own disability is called:","options":["Prosopagnosia","Anosognosia","Agnosia","Dysarthria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anosognosia is being unaware of the existence of his/her disability.  Why A wrong: Prosopagnosia is inability to recognise faces.  Why C wrong: Agnosia is inability to recognise familiar objects.  Why D wrong: Dysarthria is disturbance in articulation of speech."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Disturbance in the articulation of speech is termed:","options":["Aphasia","Dysarthria","Iculia","Dyslalia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dysarthria is disturbance in the articulation of speech.  Why A wrong: Aphasia is inability to speak familiar words.  Why C wrong: Iculia is disturbance in mathematical skills.  Why D wrong: Dyslalia is inability to articulate comprehensible speech associated with private words."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Iculia\" (as printed) refers to a disturbance in:","options":["Speech articulation","Mathematical skills","Face recognition","Body movement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Iculia is disturbance in mathematical skills. (Note: this appears to correspond to the standard term \"acalculia,\" the inability to perform calculations.)  Why A wrong: Speech articulation disturbance is dysarthria.  Why C wrong: Face recognition failure is prosopagnosia.  Why D wrong: Body movement failure is ataxia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"According to the Delirium vs Dementia table, the ONSET of delirium and dementia respectively is:","options":["Insidious/irreversible; abrupt/reversible","Abrupt, reversible; insidious, irreversible","Gradual; acute","Fluctuating; slow"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Onset in delirium is abrupt, reversible; in dementia it is insidious, irreversible.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These describe course, not onset."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the Delirium vs Dementia table, the COURSE of delirium versus dementia is:","options":["Fluctuates; slow decline","Slow decline; fluctuates","Stable; progressive","Rapid; stable"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The course fluctuates in delirium and shows slow decline in dementia.  Why B wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the terms used."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Per the comparison table, the DURATION of delirium versus dementia is:","options":["Months to years; hours to weeks","Hours to weeks; months to years","Days to months; years","Minutes; days"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium duration is hours to weeks; dementia duration is months to years.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the comparison table, SLEEP-WAKE pattern in delirium versus dementia is:","options":["Usually normal; disrupted","Disrupted; usually normal","Disrupted; disrupted","Normal; normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sleep-wake is disrupted in delirium and usually normal in dementia.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the comparison table, SPEECH in delirium versus dementia is described as:","options":["Word-finding problems; incoherent, rapid/slowed","Incoherent, rapid/slowed; word-finding problems","Impoverished; hallucinations","Disorganized; normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Speech is incoherent, rapid/slowed in delirium and shows word-finding problems in dementia.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Impoverished refers to thoughts (dementia); the others are mismatched."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the comparison table, PERCEPTIONS in delirium versus dementia are:","options":["Intact early; hallucinations/illusions","Hallucinations/illusions; intact early","Delusions; impoverished","Impaired; normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Perceptions show hallucinations/illusions in delirium and are intact early in dementia.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These describe thoughts/attention."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the comparison table, THOUGHTS in delirium versus dementia are:","options":["Impoverished; disorganized/delusions","Disorganized, delusions; impoverished","Normal; delusions","Delusions; normal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thoughts are disorganized with delusions in delirium and impoverished in dementia.  Why A wrong: The pairing is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the values given."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"According to the Should Remember box, acetylcholine (ACh) is decreased in both concentration and function in patients with:","options":["Schizophrenia and mania","Delirium and dementia (Alzheimer's disease)","Depression and anxiety","Bipolar disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ACh, essential for processing memory and learning, is decreased in both concentration and function in patients with delirium and dementia (Alzheimer's disease).  Why A/C/D wrong: The box specifically names delirium and dementia (Alzheimer's)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Alzheimer's disease (AD) most commonly occurs in older adults over which age, though it can also affect people in their 30s or 40s?","options":[">50 years",">60 years",">65 years",">70 years"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: AD is a progressive and irreversible disease most commonly seen in older adults (>65).  Why A/B/D wrong: The source states >65 years."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"When Alzheimer's disease occurs in someone under age 65, it is known as:","options":["Late-onset AD","Early-onset AD","Vascular dementia","Presenile psychosis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: When AD occurs in someone under age 65, it is known as early-onset AD.  Why A wrong: Late-onset refers to onset at older age.  Why C/D wrong: Not the terms used."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following statements about Alzheimer's disease is TRUE?","options":["AD is the most common cause of dementia and is more commonly seen in women than men","AD is the least common cause of dementia","AD is more common in men than women","AD only affects people over 80"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: AD is the most common cause of dementia and is more commonly seen in women than in men.  Why B wrong: AD is the most common cause, not the least.  Why C wrong: It is more common in women.  Why D wrong: It commonly occurs >65 but can affect people in their 30s-40s."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"One of the earliest symptoms of Alzheimer's disease, along with a gradual decline of intellectual and thinking abilities, is:","options":["Memory loss","Aggression","Seizures","Paralysis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Memory loss is one of the earliest symptoms of AD, along with a gradual decline in intellectual and thinking abilities; memory loss is the most common and earliest symptom.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not identified as the earliest symptom."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed symptoms of Alzheimer's disease? (Select all that apply)","options":["Disorientation to time and place","Language difficulty and loss of judgment","Problems with abstract thinking","Mood swings and personality changes","Improved memory retention"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed AD symptoms include disorientation to time and place, memory loss (most common/earliest), language difficulty, loss of judgment, problems with abstract thinking, mood swings and personality changes, and loss of interest in usual activities.  Why E wrong: AD causes memory loss, not improved memory retention."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the staging of Alzheimer's disease, Stage 3 is described as:","options":["Normal","Normal and aged forgetfulness","Mild cognitive impairment","Moderate Alzheimer's disease"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 3 is mild cognitive impairment.  Why A wrong: Stage 1 is normal.  Why B wrong: Stage 2 is normal and aged forgetfulness.  Why D wrong: Stage 5 is moderate Alzheimer's disease."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"According to the 7-stage model, Stage 7 of Alzheimer's disease is:","options":["Mild Alzheimer's disease","Moderate Alzheimer's disease","Moderately severe Alzheimer's disease","Severe Alzheimer's disease"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 7 is severe Alzheimer's disease.  Why A wrong: Stage 4 is mild AD.  Why B wrong: Stage 5 is moderate AD.  Why C wrong: Stage 6 is moderately severe AD."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The word \"schizophrenia\" was coined by:","options":["Kurt Schneider","Eugen Bleuler","Sigmund Freud","Emil Kraepelin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The word schizophrenia was coined by Eugen Bleuler.  Why A wrong: Kurt Schneider described the first-rank symptoms.  Why C/D wrong: Not credited with coining the term in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The word \"schizophrenia\" originates from Greek: \"Schizen\" means ______ and \"phren\" means ______.","options":["To split; mind","Mind; to split","To break; body","Divided; soul"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schizen means \"to split\" and phren means \"mind.\"  Why B wrong: The meanings are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the meanings given."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Diagnosis of schizophrenia is made by:","options":["MMSE and CBC","MSE and history taking","CT and MRI only","Narco test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of schizophrenia is made by MSE (mental status exam) and history taking.  Why A wrong: MMSE/CBC are used elsewhere (dementia/clozapine).  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated diagnostic methods."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which stage of schizophrenia is characterised by insidious onset over months or years, with subtle behaviour changes including social withdrawal, work impairment, blunting of emotion, avolition, and strange ideation?","options":["Prodromal phase","Active phase","Residual phase","Recovery phase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The prodromal phase has insidious onset over months/years with subtle behaviour changes (social withdrawal, work impairment, blunting of emotion, avolition, strange ideation).  Why B wrong: The active phase involves psychotic symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech).  Why C wrong: In the residual phase, active-phase symptoms are absent or no longer prominent.  Why D wrong: Not a stage named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In which phase of schizophrenia do psychotic symptoms (hallucinations, delusions, disorganized speech/behaviour) develop and eventually lead to medical intervention?","options":["Prodromal phase","Active phase","Residual phase","Latent phase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The active phase is when psychotic symptoms develop and eventually lead to medical intervention.  Why A wrong: The prodromal phase is the insidious early phase.  Why C wrong: The residual phase has absent/attenuated symptoms.  Why D wrong: Not a stage named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"During which phase of schizophrenia are active-phase symptoms absent or no longer prominent, with role impairment, negative symptoms, or attenuated positive symptoms, though acute symptoms may re-emerge?","options":["Prodromal phase","Active phase","Residual phase","Terminal phase"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: In the residual phase, active-phase symptoms are absent/no longer prominent, with role impairment, negative or attenuated positive symptoms; acute-phase symptoms may re-emerge.  Why A/B wrong: These are earlier phases.  Why D wrong: Not a stage named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed causes of schizophrenia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Genetic cause","Bio-chemical cause — dopamine increase","Lower socio-economic status","Schizoid type personality","Thiamine deficiency"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Listed causes are genetic cause, bio-chemical cause (dopamine increase), lower socio-economic status, and schizoid type personality.  Why E wrong: Thiamine (Vitamin B1) deficiency causes Korsakoff's psychosis, not schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The bio-chemical cause of schizophrenia is associated with which neurotransmitter change?","options":["Dopamine increase","Serotonin decrease","Acetylcholine decrease","GABA increase"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The bio-chemical cause of schizophrenia is dopamine increase.  Why B wrong: Serotonin decrease is linked to depression.  Why C wrong: Acetylcholine decrease is linked to delirium/dementia.  Why D wrong: Not stated for schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The \"4 A's\" symptoms of schizophrenia given by Eugen Bleuler (AIIMS, NIMHANS, ESIC) include Autism, Associative looseness, Ambivalence, and Affect. \"Autism\" here means:","options":["A mode of thinking governed by illogical rules set by the patient himself","Loosening of association between thoughts","Confusion, inability to decide for or against","Facial expression not according to the situation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autism (in Bleuler's 4 A's) is a mode of thinking in which thoughts are governed by illogical rules set by the patient himself.  Why B wrong: That is associative looseness.  Why C wrong: That is ambivalence.  Why D wrong: That describes affect (flat/blunt)."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Bleuler's 4 A's of schizophrenia, \"Associative looseness\" refers to:","options":["Illogical self-set thinking rules","Loosening of association between thoughts","Facial expression mismatched to situation","Inability to decide for or against"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Associative looseness is loosening of association between the thoughts.  Why A wrong: That describes autism.  Why C wrong: That describes affect.  Why D wrong: That describes ambivalence."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Bleuler's 4 A's of schizophrenia, \"Ambivalence\" is described as:","options":["Loosening of associations","Confusion, ability/inability to decide for or against","Illogical thinking rules","Flat facial expression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ambivalence (in the 4 A's) is confusion, ability to decide for or against.  Why A wrong: That is associative looseness.  Why C wrong: That is autism.  Why D wrong: That is affect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Bleuler's 4 A's of schizophrenia, \"Affect (flat/blunt)\" refers to:","options":["Facial expression according to the situation","Loosening of associations","Illogical thinking rules","Inability to decide"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Affect (flat/blunt) is described as facial expression according to the situation.  Why B wrong: That is associative looseness.  Why C wrong: That is autism.  Why D wrong: That is ambivalence."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which symptom is specifically noted as NOT being part of the 4 A's symptoms of schizophrenia (marked \"Very Imp.\")?","options":["Autism","Ambivalence","Anhedonia","Affect"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source specifically states the 4 A's symptoms of schizophrenia do NOT have Anhedonia (Very Imp.).  Why A/B/D wrong: Autism, ambivalence, and affect ARE part of the 4 A's."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Positive symptoms of schizophrenia represent:","options":["Presence of problematic behaviours","Absence of healthy behaviours","Loss of memory","Impairment of consciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Positive symptoms represent the presence of problematic behaviours.  Why B wrong: Absence of healthy behaviours describes negative symptoms.  Why C/D wrong: These are features of dementia/delirium."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are POSITIVE symptoms of schizophrenia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Hallucinations (illusory perceptions), especially auditory","Delusions (illusory beliefs), especially persecutory","Disorganized thought and nonsensical speech","Bizarre behaviours","Anhedonia (no feeling of enjoyment)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Positive symptoms include hallucinations (especially auditory), delusions (especially persecutory), disorganized thought/nonsensical speech, and bizarre behaviours.  Why E wrong: Anhedonia is a negative symptom."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are NEGATIVE symptoms of schizophrenia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Flat affect (no emotion showing in the face)","Reduced social interaction","Anhedonia and avolition (less motivation/initiative)","Alogia (speaking less) and catatonia (moving less)","Auditory hallucinations"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Negative symptoms include flat affect, reduced social interaction, anhedonia, avolition, alogia (speaking less), and catatonia (moving less).  Why E wrong: Auditory hallucinations are a positive symptom."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Alogia\" as a negative symptom of schizophrenia means:","options":["Speaking less","Moving less","Less motivation","No emotion in the face"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alogia means speaking less.  Why B wrong: Moving less is catatonia.  Why C wrong: Less motivation is avolition.  Why D wrong: No facial emotion is flat affect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"\"Avolition\" as a negative symptom of schizophrenia refers to:","options":["Less motivation, initiative, and focus on tasks","Speaking less","Reduced social interaction","Moving less"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avolition is less motivation, initiative, and focus on tasks.  Why B wrong: Speaking less is alogia.  Why C wrong: Reduced social interaction is a separate negative symptom.  Why D wrong: Moving less is catatonia."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Other NEGATIVE symptoms of schizophrenia listed include apathy, stupor, negativism, blunt effect, autism, thought retardation, and:","options":["Catalepsy and waxy-flexibility","Aggressive behaviour","Suicidal tendency","Agitation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Other negative symptoms listed include apathy, stupor, negativism, blunt effect, autism, thought retardation, catalepsy, and waxy-flexibility.  Why B/C/D wrong: Aggressive/violent behaviour, suicidal tendency, and agitation are listed as OTHER positive symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Other POSITIVE symptoms of schizophrenia include aggressive behaviour, violent behaviour, suicidal tendency, and:","options":["Agitation","Apathy","Stupor","Negativism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Other positive symptoms include aggressive behaviour, violent behaviour, suicidal tendency, and agitation.  Why B/C/D wrong: Apathy, stupor, and negativism are other negative symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Schneider's First-rank Symptoms of schizophrenia, \"Thought broadcasting\" is:","options":["The feeling people can read one's mind","Hearing one's thoughts spoken aloud","The feeling an external force is putting thoughts into one's mind","Thoughts are withdrawn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thought broadcasting is the feeling that people can read one's mind.  Why B wrong: Hearing one's thoughts aloud is audible thoughts.  Why C wrong: External force inserting thoughts is thought insertion.  Why D wrong: Thoughts being withdrawn is thought withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Schneider's First-rank Symptoms, \"Somatic passivity\" is defined as:","options":["Hearing one's thoughts spoken aloud","The feeling of being touched or strange unexplained sexual sensations","Thoughts being withdrawn","Voices commenting on one's actions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Somatic passivity is the feeling of being touched or strange unexplained sexual sensations.  Why A wrong: That is audible thoughts.  Why C wrong: That is thought withdrawal.  Why D wrong: That is voices commenting."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Schneider's First-rank Symptoms, the feeling that an external force is putting thoughts into one's mind is called:","options":["Thought withdrawal","Thought insertion","Thought broadcasting","Made feelings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thought insertion is the feeling an external force is putting thoughts into one's mind.  Why A wrong: Thought withdrawal is thoughts being withdrawn.  Why C wrong: Thought broadcasting is feeling people can read one's mind.  Why D wrong: Made feelings is an external force making you experience something."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Among Schneider's First-rank Symptoms, \"Made volition\" refers to:","options":["The feeling an external force is making you act a certain way","The feeling an external force is making you want something","The feeling an external force is making you experience something","Hearing one's thoughts aloud"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Made volition is the feeling that an external force is making you act a certain way.  Why B wrong: That is made impulses.  Why C wrong: That is made feelings.  Why D wrong: That is audible thoughts."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In Schneider's First-rank Symptoms, \"Made impulses\" is:","options":["The feeling an external force is making you want something","The feeling an external force is making you act a certain way","Thoughts are withdrawn","Voices arguing/discussing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Made impulses is the feeling an external force is making you want something.  Why B wrong: That is made volition.  Why C wrong: That is thought withdrawal.  Why D wrong: That is voices arguing."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Among Schneider's First-rank Symptoms, \"Voices commenting\" describes:","options":["Voices narrating one's actions as if giving a running commentary","Auditory hallucination of voices arguing","Hearing one's own thoughts aloud","A physical sensation interpreted as a special event"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Voices commenting are voices that narrate one's actions as if giving a running commentary.  Why B wrong: Voices arguing/discussing is a separate first-rank symptom.  Why C wrong: Hearing one's thoughts aloud is audible thoughts.  Why D wrong: That describes delusional perception."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Among Schneider's First-rank Symptoms, \"Delusional perception\" is:","options":["A physical sensation (e.g., seeing/feeling something) interpreted as a very special event that has happened and is important","Hearing thoughts spoken aloud","The feeling people can read one's mind","Thoughts being withdrawn"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusional perception is a physical sensation (such as seeing or feeling something) interpreted as a very special/important event that has happened.  Why B wrong: That is audible thoughts.  Why C wrong: That is thought broadcasting.  Why D wrong: That is thought withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which type of schizophrenia is also known as \"hebephrenic,\" typically occurs at an early age (<25 years), and has the worst prognosis?","options":["Catatonic","Paranoid","Disorganized","Residual"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Disorganized schizophrenia (hebephrenic) shows inappropriate behaviour/emotions, incoherent language or negative symptoms, usually occurs at early age <25 years, is the second most common type, and has the worst prognosis.  Why A wrong: Catatonic has good/excellent prognosis by ECT.  Why B wrong: Paranoid is the most common type with best prognosis.  Why D wrong: Residual is free from major symptoms with minor evidence."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Catatonic schizophrenia, characterised by frozen, rigid, or excitable motor behaviour, has:","options":["Worst prognosis","Good prognosis, excellent prognosis by ECT","No response to treatment","Late onset only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catatonic type shows frozen/rigid/excitable motor behaviour with good prognosis and excellent prognosis by ECT.  Why A wrong: Disorganized has the worst prognosis.  Why C wrong: It responds well to ECT.  Why D wrong: Late onset is characteristic of paranoid type."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which type of schizophrenia shows delusions of persecution or grandeur with very few negative symptoms, has late onset, is the most common type, and has the best prognosis?","options":["Disorganized","Catatonic","Paranoid","Undifferentiated"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Paranoid schizophrenia shows delusions of persecution/grandeur, very few negative symptoms, late onset, is the most common type, and has the best prognosis.  Why A wrong: Disorganized is second most common with worst prognosis.  Why B wrong: Catatonic shows motor behaviour disturbance.  Why D wrong: Undifferentiated is a mixed set of symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The type of schizophrenia showing a mixed set of symptoms with thought disorders and features from other types is:","options":["Residual","Undifferentiated","Catatonic","Paranoid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Undifferentiated schizophrenia is a mixed set of symptoms with thought disorders and features from other types.  Why A wrong: Residual is free from major symptoms with minor evidence of continuation.  Why C/D wrong: These are distinct defined types."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Residual schizophrenia is characterised by:","options":["Frozen or rigid motor behaviour","Delusions of grandeur","Freedom from major symptoms but evidence from minor symptoms of continuation of the disorder","Inappropriate behaviour and incoherent language"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Residual type is free from major symptoms but shows evidence from minor symptoms of continuation of the disorder.  Why A wrong: Frozen/rigid motor behaviour is catatonic.  Why B wrong: Delusions of grandeur is paranoid.  Why D wrong: Inappropriate behaviour/incoherent language is disorganized."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The most effective anti-psychotic drug for treatment of schizophrenia is:","options":["Risperidone","Clozapine","Chlorpromazine","Lithium carbonate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anti-psychotic clozapine is the most effective drug (along with risperidone) for schizophrenia treatment.  Why A wrong: Risperidone is listed but clozapine is named the most effective.  Why C wrong: Chlorpromazine was the first antipsychotic but not named the most effective.  Why D wrong: Lithium carbonate is for mania."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the treatment of schizophrenia, physical therapy consists of how many ECT sessions?","options":["4-6 ECT","6-8 ECT","8-12 ECT","12-16 ECT"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Physical therapy for schizophrenia is 8-12 ECT.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies 8-12 ECT."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are psycho-social therapies used in schizophrenia treatment? (Select all that apply)","options":["Group therapy","Behavioural therapy","Cognitive therapy","Family therapy","Electroconvulsive therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Psycho-social therapies listed are group therapy, behavioural therapy, cognitive therapy, and family therapy.  Why E wrong: ECT is a physical therapy, not a psycho-social therapy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are GOOD prognostic indicators of schizophrenia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Late onset and acute onset","Married status and mood symptoms (especially depression)","Good support system and positive symptoms","Obvious precipitating factor","Insidious onset and negative symptoms"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Good prognosis indicators include late onset, acute onset, married, mood symptoms (especially depression), family history of mood disorder, good support system, positive symptoms, good pre-morbid social/sexual/work history, and obvious precipitating factor.  Why E wrong: Insidious onset and negative symptoms are bad prognostic indicators."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are BAD prognostic indicators of schizophrenia? (Select all that apply)","options":["Young/early onset and insidious onset","Single, divorced, or widowed; withdrawn/autistic behaviour","Negative symptoms and poor support system","No remission in 3 years, many relapses, no precipitating factor","Acute onset with obvious precipitating factor"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Bad prognosis indicators include young/early onset, insidious onset, single/divorced/widowed, withdrawn/autistic behaviour, family history of schizophrenia, poor support system, negative symptoms, poor pre-morbid histories, neurological signs, no remission in 3 years, many relapses, and no precipitating factor.  Why E wrong: Acute onset with an obvious precipitating factor is a GOOD prognostic indicator."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The first antipsychotic drug, chlorpromazine, was first developed as a \"tranquilizer\" and antipsychotics were discovered in the:","options":["Late 1940s","Late 1950s","Late 1960s","Early 1970s"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antipsychotics were discovered in the late 1950s; chlorpromazine was first developed as a \"tranquilizer\" and its usefulness for treating psychosis was recognized by accident.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies the late 1950s."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Agranulocytosis (neutropenia and thrombocytopenia) is the most common side effect of which drug, with risk highest in the first year?","options":["Risperidone","Clozapine","Lithium","Chlorpromazine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Agranulocytosis (neutropenia and thrombocytopenia) is the most common side effect of clozapine, with risk highest in the first year of treatment.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically attributes this to clozapine."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"To monitor for clozapine-induced agranulocytosis, a CBC count should be done:","options":["Every week for the first 6 months, then bi-weekly for the next 6 months","Once a month for a year","Only at the start of treatment","Every day for the first week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: CBC count should be done every week for the first 6 months and then bi-weekly for the next 6 months.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies the weekly-then-biweekly schedule."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Clozapine is contraindicated if the TLC (total leucocyte count) is:","options":["<5000/cumm","<4500/cumm","<3500/cumm","<2500/cumm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Clozapine is contraindicated if TLC count is <3500/cumm.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies <3500/cumm."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Clozapine should be DISCONTINUED if the TLC count reaches ______ or the ANC (absolute neutrophil count) reaches ______:","options":["<3000/cumm; <1500/cumm","<3500/cumm; <2000/cumm","<2500/cumm; <1000/cumm","<4000/cumm; <2500/cumm"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clozapine should be discontinued if TLC reaches <3000/cumm or ANC reaches <1500/cumm.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies TLC <3000/cumm and ANC <1500/cumm.  Note: These are the source's (older, TLC-based) cut-offs. Current Clozapine REMS/FDA guidance monitors the ANC alone — general-population clozapine is discontinued when ANC <1000/cumm (severe neutropenia <500/cumm), not at ANC <1500/cumm."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In bipolar mood disorder, \"Mood\" is defined as:","options":["The objective appearance of mood / short-lived emotional response to an event","An individual's subjective emotional state or a sustained and pervasive emotional response that colours the whole psychic life","Loss of interest in previously pleasurable activities","A severe elevation of mood with delusions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mood is an individual's subjective emotional state or a sustained and pervasive emotional response that colours the whole psychic life.  Why A wrong: That defines affect.  Why C wrong: That defines anhedonia.  Why D wrong: That defines exaltation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In bipolar mood disorder, \"Affect\" is defined as:","options":["A sustained and pervasive subjective emotional state","The objective appearance of mood or a short-lived emotional response to an idea/event (e.g., expressed externally on the face during interview)","Loss of interest in things previously pleasurable","Very severe elevation of mood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Affect is the objective appearance of mood or a short-lived emotional response to an idea or event, e.g., expressed externally on the face during interview.  Why A wrong: That describes mood.  Why C wrong: That describes anhedonia.  Why D wrong: That describes ecstasy."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In bipolar mood disorder, anhedonia is defined as:","options":["Loss of interest in things/activities which were previously pleasurable","Increased sense of psychological well-being and happiness","Moderate elevation of mood","Severe elevation with delusions of grandeur"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anhedonia is loss of interest in things/activities which were previously pleasurable.  Why B wrong: That describes euphoria.  Why C wrong: That describes elation.  Why D wrong: That describes exaltation."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"According to the mood-elevation ladder diagram, match the correct stage. Stage I (mild elevation of mood) corresponds to:","options":["Hypomania (euphoria)","Mania (elation)","Severe mania (exaltation)","Stuporous mania (ecstasy)"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage I is hypomania, corresponding to euphoria — a mild elevation of mood.  Why B wrong: Mania (elation) is stage II.  Why C wrong: Severe mania (exaltation) is stage III.  Why D wrong: Stuporous mania (ecstasy) is stage IV."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the mood-elevation diagram, \"Severe mania (stage III)\" corresponds to which mood state, described as intense elation with delusion of grandeur?","options":["Euphoria","Elation","Exaltation","Ecstasy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe mania (stage III) corresponds to exaltation — severe elevation of mood with intense elation and delusion of grandeur.  Why A wrong: Euphoria (mild elevation) is stage I / hypomania.  Why B wrong: Elation (moderate elevation) is stage II / mania.  Why D wrong: Ecstasy (very severe elevation) is stage IV / stuporous mania."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In the mood-elevation diagram, \"Stuporous mania (stage IV)\" corresponds to ecstasy, described as:","options":["Mild elevation with happiness","Moderate elevation with increased psychomotor activity","Very severe elevation of mood — intense sense of rapture or blissfulness","A short-lived emotional response"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stuporous mania (stage IV) corresponds to ecstasy — a very severe elevation of mood with an intense sense of rapture or blissfulness.  Why A wrong: Mild elevation with happiness is euphoria (hypomania).  Why B wrong: Moderate elevation is elation (mania).  Why D wrong: A short-lived emotional response describes affect."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"According to the Should Remember box, while a manic patient is on lithium therapy, adequate hydration and sodium level should be maintained and a high sodium diet offered because a low sodium level (hyponatremia) may cause:","options":["Lithium toxicity","Agranulocytosis","Dehydration","Hyperkalemia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Low sodium level (hyponatremia) may cause lithium toxicity, so adequate hydration and a high sodium diet should be maintained.  Why B wrong: Agranulocytosis is a clozapine side effect.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the stated consequence of hyponatremia here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Mania is also known as \"manic syndrome\" and is defined as:","options":["A state of abnormally elevated arousal, affect, and energy level (heightened overall activation with enhanced affective expression together with lability of affect)","A state of pervasive low mood and low energy","Loss of memory","Impairment in consciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mania (manic syndrome) is a state of abnormally elevated arousal, affect, and energy level, or heightened overall activation with enhanced affective expression together with lability of affect.  Why B wrong: That describes depression.  Why C/D wrong: These describe dementia/delirium."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Extremely elevated and excitable mood is usually associated with which disorder?","options":["Unipolar depression","Bipolar disorder","Delirium","Dementia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Extremely elevated and excitable mood is usually associated with bipolar disorder.  Why A wrong: Depression involves low mood.  Why C/D wrong: These are organic mental disorders."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Hallmark symptoms of mania include increased psychomotor activity, flight of ideas, and:","options":["Increased pressure of speech","Thought retardation","Decreased psychomotor activity","Word-finding problems"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hallmark symptoms of mania include increased psychomotor activity, flight of ideas, and increased pressure of speech.  Why B/C wrong: Thought retardation and decreased psychomotor activity are classical depression symptoms.  Why D wrong: Word-finding problems relate to dementia speech."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Mania is most commonly seen in ______, whereas depression is most commonly seen in ______:","options":["Females; males","Males; females","Children; adults","Elderly; young"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mania is most commonly seen in males; depression (MDD) is most commonly seen in females.  Why A wrong: The genders are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the distinction made in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The most common risk factor for mania is:","options":["High socio-economic status","Low socio-economic status","Old age","Family conflict"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common risk factor for mania includes low socio-economic status.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies low socio-economic status."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Treatment of mania includes anti-manic, anti-psychotic, anti-depressant, and:","options":["ECT","Anticoagulants","Antibiotics","Antihistamines"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment of mania includes anti-manic, anti-psychotic, anti-depressant, and ECT.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not part of mania treatment per the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The most common drug used for mania is (NORCET-2021, NCL-2022):","options":["Lithium carbonate (anti-manic)","Clozapine","Chlorpromazine","Risperidone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common drug used for mania is lithium carbonate (anti-manic).  Why B/D wrong: Clozapine/risperidone are antipsychotics for schizophrenia.  Why C wrong: Chlorpromazine was the first antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In mania, the most common type of hallucination and the most common delusion are, respectively:","options":["Visual hallucination; delusion of persecution","Auditory hallucination; delusion of grandiosity","Tactile hallucination; delusion of reference","Olfactory hallucination; nihilistic delusion"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In mania, hallucination is auditory hallucination and the most common delusion is delusion of grandiosity.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies auditory hallucination and delusion of grandiosity."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed signs/symptoms of mania? (Select all that apply)","options":["Activity increase / hyperactivity or violence","Decreased desire or lack of sleep","Thought: flight of ideas","Memory - hypermnesia; insight and judgment absent","Increased desire to sleep"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Mania signs include increased activity/hyperactivity or violence, decreased desire or lack of sleep, flight of ideas, hypermnesia, and absent insight and judgment.  Why E wrong: In mania there is a DECREASED desire or lack of sleep, not increased."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In mania, the emotion is described as pleasurable (euphoria, elation, exhalation, ecstasy) or:","options":["Mood swing","Blunt affect","Flat affect","Anhedonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In mania, emotion is pleasurable (euphoria, elation, exhalation/exaltation, ecstasy) or mood swing.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are negative/depressive affect states, not described for mania here."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"In mania, insight and judgment are:","options":["Intact","Absent","Enhanced","Fluctuating"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In mania, insight is absent and judgment is absent.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source states both are absent."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Major depressive disorder (MDD) is characterised by at least how many weeks of pervasive low mood, low self-esteem, loss of interest, low energy, and pain without a clear cause?","options":["At least one week","At least two weeks","At least four weeks","At least six months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MDD is characterised by at least two weeks of pervasive low mood, low self-esteem, loss of interest in normally enjoyable activities, low energy, and pain without a clear cause.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies at least two weeks."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The classical symptoms of depression are decreased psychomotor activity, thought retardation, and:","options":["Sadness of mood","Flight of ideas","Increased pressure of speech","Grandiosity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Classical symptoms of depression are decreased psychomotor activity, thought retardation, and sadness of mood.  Why B/C/D wrong: Flight of ideas, increased pressure of speech, and grandiosity are manic features."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"The neurotransmitters found at low levels in depression are:","options":["Acetylcholine, glutamate, and GABA","Nor-epinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine","Histamine and adrenaline","Endorphin and oxytocin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Low levels of neurotransmitters associated in depression are nor-epinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine.  Why A wrong: Acetylcholine deficiency relates to delirium/dementia.  Why C/D wrong: Not named as depression neurotransmitters in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Depression is commonly seen in individuals of which personality type?","options":["Extrovert","Introvert","Schizoid","Antisocial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Depression is commonly seen in individuals of introvert personality.  Why A wrong: Extrovert is not the type stated.  Why C wrong: Schizoid personality is a cause of schizophrenia.  Why D wrong: Not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 004 · Activity disorders…","question":"Diagnosis of both mania and depression is made by:","options":["MMSE and CBC","MSE and history taking","Narco test","ECT"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Diagnosis of mania and depression is made by MSE (mental status exam) and history taking.  Why A wrong: MMSE is used for delirium/dementia; CBC monitors clozapine.  Why C wrong: Narco test is a forensic verbal elicitation technique.  Why D wrong: ECT is a treatment, not a diagnostic tool."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which type of depression is described as sadness or the \"blues\" seen with everyday disappointments and is not necessarily dysfunctional?","options":["Transient depression","Mild depression","Moderate depression","Severe depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features of transient depression are sadness or the \"blues\" as seen with everyday disappointments and it is not necessarily dysfunctional.  Why B wrong: Mild depression is a grief response (denial, sadness, withdrawal, somatic symptoms).  Why C wrong: Moderate depression involves negativism and low self-esteem.  Why D wrong: Severe depression involves total despair and suicidal thoughts."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Clinical features of mild depression include which of the following grief-response features?","options":["Total despair and delusional thinking","Denial, sadness, withdrawal, somatic symptoms, and frequent or continuous thoughts of the loss","Increased need for sleep and weight gain","Inflated self-esteem and euphoria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mild depression is a grief response such as denial, sadness, withdrawal, somatic symptoms, and frequent or continuous thoughts of the loss.  Why A wrong: These describe severe depression.  Why C wrong: These describe atypical depression.  Why D wrong: These describe mania, not depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Feelings of sadness, negativism, low self-esteem, rumination about life's failures, decreased interest in grooming and eating, and possibly sleep disturbances characterize which type of depression?","options":["Transient depression","Mild depression","Moderate depression","Seasonal affective disorder"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: These are the clinical features of moderate depression as listed in the source.  Why A wrong: Transient depression is everyday \"blues.\"  Why B wrong: Mild depression is a grief response.  Why D wrong: SAD is depression occurring corresponding to a season."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which clinical features are consistent with major (severe) depression? (PYQ: NORCET-4, NORCET-5 Mains 2023)","options":["Increased appetite and weight gain","Feelings of total despair, hopelessness, worthlessness, emptiness, inability to feel pleasure, extreme psychomotor retardation, delusional thinking, self-blame and suicidal thoughts","Inflated self-esteem and grandiose delusions","Mild transient sadness with normal functioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Severe depression = total despair, hopelessness, worthlessness, emptiness, inability to feel pleasure, possibly extreme psychomotor retardation, inattention to hygiene, delusional thinking, confusion, self-blame, and suicidal thoughts; consistent with major depression.  Why A wrong: Increased appetite/weight gain is atypical depression.  Why C wrong: These are manic features.  Why D wrong: That describes transient depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Major depressive disorder (MDD) is defined as a depressed (low/sad) mood episode lasting at least:","options":["Two or more days","Two or more weeks","Two or more months","Six or more months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MDD is a period of two or more weeks in a depressed (low or sad) mood episode.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies a duration of two or more weeks, not days, months, or six months."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Depression with melancholic features includes an additional symptom of:","options":["Increased need for sleep","Waking up two hours earlier than usual","Weight gain","Improved mood in the evening"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Depression with melancholic features is like severe depression but with additional symptoms such as waking up two hours earlier than usual.  Why A/C wrong: Increased sleep and weight gain are atypical depression features.  Why D wrong: Not stated as a melancholic feature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which type of depression includes symptoms like an increased need for sleep, increased appetite, and weight gain?","options":["Melancholic depression","Atypical depression","Endogenous depression","Transient depression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atypical depression includes symptoms like an increased need for sleep, increased appetite, and weight gain.  Why A wrong: Melancholic depression involves early morning awakening.  Why C wrong: Endogenous depression is caused by genetic/biological factors.  Why D wrong: Transient depression is everyday \"blues.\""},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Depressions that occur corresponding to a season are termed:","options":["Atypical depression","Seasonal affective disorder (SAD)","Melancholic depression","Dysthymia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Seasonal affective disorder (SAD) is depression that occurs corresponding to a season.  Why A wrong: Atypical depression relates to reversed vegetative symptoms.  Why C wrong: Melancholic depression relates to early morning awakening.  Why D wrong: Dysthymia is a chronic low-grade mood disorder (not defined by season here)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The classical hallmark triad seen in severe depression consists of:","options":["Hopelessness, helplessness, and worthlessness","Delusion, hallucination, and stupor","Anhedonia, apathy, and insomnia","Guilt, grandiosity, and euphoria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The hallmark symptoms of depression form the classical triad: hopelessness, helplessness, and worthlessness.  Why B/C wrong: These are other signs/symptoms but not the hallmark triad.  Why D wrong: Grandiosity and euphoria are manic features."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the depression hallmark triad, \"hopelessness\" refers to:","options":["Negative view about self","Negative view about environment","Negative view about future","Negative view about past"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Hopelessness = negative view about future.  Why A wrong: Negative view about self = worthlessness.  Why B wrong: Negative view about environment = helplessness.  Why D wrong: Not part of the defined triad."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the depression hallmark triad, a negative view about the environment is termed:","options":["Hopelessness","Helplessness","Worthlessness","Anhedonia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Helplessness = negative view about environment.  Why A wrong: Hopelessness = negative view about future.  Why C wrong: Worthlessness = negative view about self.  Why D wrong: Anhedonia = lack of pleasure."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In depression, \"worthlessness\" is described as:","options":["Negative view about future","Negative view about environment","Negative view about self","Lack of pleasure"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Worthlessness = negative view about self.  Why A wrong: That is hopelessness.  Why B wrong: That is helplessness.  Why D wrong: That is anhedonia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The type of hallucination most associated with depression is:","options":["Visual hallucination","Auditory hallucination","Tactile hallucination","Olfactory hallucination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states hallucination in depression is auditory hallucination.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the type specified for depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The most common type of delusion seen in depression is:","options":["Delusion of grandeur","Delusion of persecution","Delusion of nihilism","Delusion of reference"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Delusion of nihilism is the most common in depression; delusion of guilt is also seen.  Why A wrong: Grandeur is a manic feature.  Why B/D wrong: These are more typical of psychotic/schizophrenic states, not listed as most common in depression here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"\"Anhedonia,\" a symptom of depression, means:","options":["Loss of memory","Lack of pleasure","Excessive movement","Distorted taste"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anhedonia is defined as lack of pleasure.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe anhedonia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In depression, the \"dis-activity\" motor feature is described as:","options":["Flight of ideas","Waxy flexibility","Pressured speech","Hyperactivity"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Dis-activity – Waxy-flexibility\" as a sign of depression.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are manic/other features, not the listed dis-activity feature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the source, suicidal tendency in depression is usually higher in:","options":["Males","Females","Children","Elderly men only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states suicidal tendency is usually higher in females.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not stated as the higher-risk group in this source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Omega sign and Veraguth's eyelid fold are classically associated with which type of depression?","options":["Atypical depression","Melancholic depression","Seasonal affective disorder","Transient depression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Omega sign and Veraguth's eyelids fold are classically associated with melancholic depression, resulting from prolonged contraction of corrugator and procerus muscles in the glabellar region.  Why A/C/D wrong: These signs are specific to melancholic depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Omega sign in melancholic depression results from prolonged contraction of which muscles in the glabellar region?","options":["Frontalis and orbicularis oculi","Corrugator and procerus muscles","Masseter and temporalis","Zygomaticus and risorius"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Omega sign results from prolonged contraction of the corrugator and procerus muscles in the glabellar region; Omega sign = eyebrows narrowing and inner brow raising or excessive action of the corrugator muscle.  Why A/C/D wrong: These muscles are not the ones the source names for the Omega sign."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"\"Endogenous\" depression is best described as depression that:","options":["Comes from an external stressful or traumatic event","Arises 'from within,' primarily due to genetic and biological factors, not associated with external stress or trauma","Only occurs seasonally","Is always transient and non-dysfunctional"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endogenous means 'from within' — depression not associated with external stress/trauma, primarily caused by genetic and biological factors.  Why A wrong: That describes exogenous depression.  Why C/D wrong: Not the source definition of endogenous depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Exogenous depression is best described as depression that:","options":["Arises from within due to genetics","Comes from an external cause, such as a stressful or traumatic event","Occurs only with melancholic features","Is caused by dopamine excess"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Exogenous means 'from outside' — depression from an external cause such as a stressful or traumatic event.  Why A wrong: That is endogenous depression.  Why C/D wrong: Not the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the source, the most common and successful treatment of depression is:","options":["Antipsychotic drugs","ECT","Anti-anxiety drugs","Deep brain stimulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the most common and successful treatment of depression is ECT.  Why A/C wrong: These are listed among treatments but not as the most common/successful.  Why D wrong: Not mentioned in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which psychotherapy is described as the most common and effective for depression?","options":["Psychoanalysis","Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT)","Interpersonal therapy","Group therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states psychotherapy — Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) is most common and effective.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not identified as most common/effective in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the differences table, which is another name for endogenous depression?","options":["Neurotic depression","Reactive depression","Psychotic (autogenous) depression","Situational depression"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Endogenous depression is also called psychotic/autogenous; exogenous is neurotic/reactive.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are alternate names for exogenous (neurotic, sometimes reactive) depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Regarding diurnal variation of mood, endogenous depression classically shows the patient is:","options":["More sad during evening","More sad during morning","Equally sad all day","Happier in the morning only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In endogenous depression, patients are more sad during morning; in exogenous, more sad during evening.  Why A wrong: That is exogenous depression.  Why C/D wrong: Not per the table."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The insomnia pattern characteristic of endogenous depression is:","options":["Early or initial (difficulty falling asleep)","Late or terminal (early morning awakening)","Hypersomnia","No sleep disturbance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endogenous depression = usually late or terminal insomnia (early morning awakening); exogenous = early/initial (difficulty falling asleep).  Why A wrong: That is exogenous.  Why C/D wrong: Not per the table."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Regarding effect of company, a patient with endogenous depression typically:","options":["Feels better when in a group","Feels better when alone","Feels worse when alone","Is unaffected by company"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In endogenous depression, the patient feels better when alone; in exogenous, feels better when in a group.  Why A wrong: That is exogenous.  Why C/D wrong: Not per the table."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the differences table, relapses are described as ______ in endogenous depression and ______ in exogenous depression.","options":["Uncommon; Common","Common; Uncommon","Common; Common","Uncommon; Uncommon"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Relapses are common in endogenous and uncommon in exogenous depression.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the table values."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The preferred management for endogenous depression according to the table is:","options":["Psychotherapy alone","ECT + antidepressants","Anti-anxiety drugs only","Lifestyle changes only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of endogenous depression = ECT + antidepressants; exogenous = psychotherapy (antidepressants also).  Why A wrong: Psychotherapy is emphasized for exogenous depression.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated management."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which of the following is a symptom of MANIA rather than depression? (SATA)","options":["Inflated self-esteem","Decreased need for sleep","Flight of ideas with grandiose delusions","Low self-esteem and confidence","Euphoric with intense feelings of well-being"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Mania features include inflated self-esteem, decreased need for sleep, flight of ideas with grandiose/persecutory delusions, and euphoria with intense well-being (extroverted personality).  Why D wrong: Low self-esteem and confidence is a depression feature (introvert personality), not mania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the source, the personality type associated with depression versus mania is:","options":["Depression – extroverted; Mania – introvert","Depression – introvert; Mania – extroverted","Both introvert","Both extroverted"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists introvert personality under depression and extroverted personality under mania.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the table."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which muscles, per \"Also Remember,\" are involved in making the sad expression of the face and are considered the grief muscles of the body?","options":["Frontalis and nasalis","Corrugator muscle and depressor anguli oris muscles","Orbicularis oris and buccinator","Masseter and mentalis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The corrugator muscle and depressor anguli oris muscles make the sad expression and are considered the grief muscles of the body.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the muscles named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Because endogenous depression is caused by genetic and biological factors, it is also referred to as:","options":["\"Reactive\" depression","\"Biologically based\" depression","\"Situational\" depression","\"Neurotic\" depression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Endogenous depression is caused by genetic and biological factors, so it is referred to as \"biologically based\" depression.  Why A/C/D wrong: These terms are used for exogenous/reactive depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HRSD/HAM-D) is best described as: (PYQ: NORCET-2022)","options":["A scale to assess handedness/laterality","A multiple-item questionnaire used to assess the severity of depression; the most common rating scale for depression","A cognitive screening tool for dementia","A self-scored anxiety inventory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: HRSD (also HDRS or HAM-D) is a multiple-item questionnaire used to assess severity of depression and is the most common rating scale for depression; purpose is to determine presence and severity.  Why A wrong: Handedness/laterality is the Edinburgh inventory.  Why C wrong: HAM-D is not a dementia screen.  Why D wrong: HAM-D is clinician-rated, not a self-scored anxiety inventory."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Approximately how long does the Hamilton depression scale assessment take?","options":["About 5 minutes","About 10 minutes","About 20 minutes","About 45 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states assessment time is about 20 minutes.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the stated duration."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Hamilton's original depression scale had how many items, and the extended version (HRSD-29) had up to how many items?","options":["Original 10 items; up to 20","Original 17 items; up to 29 items","Original 21 items; up to 30 items","Original 7 items; up to 13 items"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hamilton's original scale had 17 items; other versions included up to 29 items (HRSD-29).  Why A/C/D wrong: These item counts do not match the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"On the Hamilton classification of depression, a score of 0–7 indicates:","options":["Not depressed","Mild (sub-threshold)","Moderate (mild)","Very severe"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Classification: Not depressed 0–7; Mild (sub-threshold) 8–13; Moderate (mild) 14–18; Severe (moderate) 19–22; Very severe (severe) >23.  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to higher score bands."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"On the Hamilton depression classification, which score range indicates \"Severe (moderate)\"?","options":["8–13","14–18","19–22",">23"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe (moderate) = 19–22.  Why A wrong: 8–13 is mild (sub-threshold).  Why B wrong: 14–18 is moderate (mild).  Why D wrong: >23 is very severe (severe)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the source, the second most common rating scale for depression is:","options":["Beck Depression Inventory (BDI)","Montgomery-Asberg Depression Rating Scale (MADRS)","Zung Self-Rating Depression Scale","Raskin Depression Rating Scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MADRS is described as the second most common rating scale for depression.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are other rating scales listed but not identified as the second most common."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which scale CANNOT be used to assess a subject for a mood disorder? (Practice Question)","options":["Beck inventory","Edinburgh inventory","Wakefield questionnaire","Hamilton rating scale"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Edinburgh inventory measures dominance of a person's right or left hand (laterality/handedness), not mood.  Why A wrong: Beck inventory is a self-scored depression inventory.  Why C wrong: The Wakefield questionnaire measures frequency/intensity of symptoms associated with depression.  Why D wrong: The Hamilton rating scale is the most widely used clinician-rated scale for depression severity.  Note: \"Edinburgh inventory\" here means the Edinburgh Handedness Inventory (laterality). Do not confuse it with the Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale (EPDS), which IS a validated mood/depression screening tool for postnatal depression."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which depression inventory is noted as being self-scored, with the scoring scale at the end of the questionnaire?","options":["Hamilton rating scale","Beck inventory","Edinburgh inventory","Wakefield questionnaire"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Beck inventory depression inventory can be self-scored; the scoring scale is at the end of the questionnaire.  Why A wrong: Hamilton is clinician-rated.  Why C wrong: Edinburgh assesses handedness.  Why D wrong: Wakefield measures frequency/intensity of depression-associated symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Anxiety is best described as:","options":["A false fixed belief","A normal response against the stimulus / a feeling of worry, nervousness, or unease about something with an uncertain outcome","A perception without an external stimulus","An irresistible urge to steal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anxiety is the normal response against the stimulus and a feeling of worry, nervousness, or unease about something with an uncertain outcome.  Why A wrong: That describes a delusion.  Why C wrong: That describes a hallucination.  Why D wrong: That describes kleptomania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which of the following is/are subtypes of anxiety disorder shown in the source diagram? (SATA)","options":["Social Anxiety Disorder","Generalised Anxiety Disorder","Panic Disorder with/without agoraphobia","Obsessive Compulsive Disorder","Post Traumatic Stress Disorder"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The anxiety disorder diagram lists Social Anxiety Disorder, Generalised Anxiety Disorder, Specific Phobia, Panic Disorder w/without agoraphobia, Obsessive Compulsive Disorder, and Post Traumatic Stress Disorder.  Why not others: All listed options appear in the diagram (Specific Phobia is also included but not offered here)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In MILD anxiety, which of the following is characteristic?","options":["Attention focused on a small part of a specific area","Able to recognize anxiety, with enhanced learning and performance","Feelings of unreality and loss of control","Complete immobility and hallucination"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Mild anxiety — able to recognize anxiety, increased alertness, enhances learning and performance (a helpful anxiety).  Why A wrong: That is severe anxiety.  Why C/D wrong: These are panic anxiety features."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In MODERATE anxiety, the client typically:","options":["Loses all ability to focus and hallucinates","Is able to sustain attention on a focal point, may talk faster, vital signs increase, with narrowed perception and decreased problem-solving ability","Has enhanced learning and performance","Uses violence toward self or others"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Moderate anxiety — able to sustain attention on a focal point, may talk faster, vital signs increase, agitation, muscles tighten, narrow perception, decreased attention span and problem-solving.  Why A/D wrong: These are panic anxiety features.  Why C wrong: Enhanced learning is a mild anxiety feature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"A patient whose attention is focused on a small part of a specific area, may be unaware of anxiety, shows increased vitals, flight of ideas, and is unable to function with ritualistic behaviour is experiencing which level of anxiety?","options":["Mild anxiety","Moderate anxiety","Severe anxiety","Panic anxiety"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe anxiety — attention focused on a small part of a specific area, may be unaware of anxiety, vitals increase, severe physical symptoms, flight of ideas, unable to function, unresponsive, ritualistic behaviour.  Why A/B wrong: These are milder levels.  Why D wrong: Panic includes loss of control, terror, hallucination, and violence."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which level of anxiety is considered a psychiatric emergency, with feelings of unreality, terror, loss of control, distorted perception/hallucination, and possible violence toward self or others?","options":["Mild anxiety","Moderate anxiety","Severe anxiety","Panic anxiety"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Panic anxiety = feelings of unreality, confusion, terror, self-absorption, possible violence, loss of control, exhaustion; it is a psychiatric emergency with distorted perception/hallucination and being out of control.  Why A/B/C wrong: Only panic is explicitly called a psychiatric emergency."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the Common Anxiety Disorders table, which disorder is defined as a persistent, irrational fear of a specific object, activity, or situation that the individual often recognizes as irrational?","options":["Panic disorder","Phobia","Social anxiety disorder","PTSD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phobia = persistent, irrational fear of a specific object/activity/situation leading to a desire for or actual avoidance; the individual often recognizes the reactions are irrational.  Why A wrong: Panic disorder involves sudden onset of extreme apprehension/fear.  Why C wrong: Social anxiety is fear provoked by social situations.  Why D wrong: PTSD is caused by past traumatic events."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Panic disorder is characterized by:","options":["Anxiety about physical appearance","Panic attacks with sudden onset of extreme apprehension or fear, where normal functioning can be suspended and misinterpretation of reality can occur","Fear of specific social situations only","Anxiety developing during pregnancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Panic disorder — panic attacks occur as a result of a sudden onset of extreme apprehension or fear; normal functioning can be suspended and misinterpretation of reality can occur.  Why A wrong: That describes body dysmorphic disorder.  Why C wrong: That describes social anxiety disorder.  Why D wrong: That describes perinatal anxiety."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which defence mechanisms are used by clients in OCD? (TRICK: IUD Reaction) (SATA)","options":["Undoing","Isolation","Reaction formation","Displacement","Sublimation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Defence mechanisms used by OCD clients — Undoing, Isolation, Reaction formation, Displacement (TRICK: IUD Reaction).  Why E wrong: Sublimation is not listed among the OCD defence mechanisms in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Body Dysmorphic Disorder is characterized by obsessions and compulsions related to:","options":["Contamination and germs","An individual's own physical appearance","Past traumatic events","Fear of open spaces"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Body dysmorphic disorder = obsessions and compulsions related to an individual's own physical appearance.  Why A wrong: That is contamination OCD.  Why C wrong: That describes PTSD.  Why D wrong: That describes agoraphobia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Perinatal anxiety (perinatal OCD) refers to anxiety problems that develop in some women:","options":["Only before puberty","During pregnancy or in the first year after giving birth","Only during menopause","Only during lactation beyond 2 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Perinatal anxiety/OCD develops in some women during pregnancy or in the first year after giving birth.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the timeframe defined in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which class of drugs is recommended as anxiolytics in the management of phobias?","options":["Antipsychotics such as haloperidol","Benzodiazepines such as alprazolam and clonazepam","Lithium","Methylphenidate"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Management of phobias includes anxiolytics such as benzodiazepines (alprazolam and clonazepam).  Why A wrong: Antipsychotics are not listed as the anxiolytic for phobia.  Why C wrong: Lithium is a mood stabilizer.  Why D wrong: Methylphenidate is the ADHD drug of choice."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which psychotherapy techniques are recommended in the management of phobias? (SATA)","options":["Systematic desensitization","Flooding therapy","Electroconvulsive therapy","Aversion therapy only","Antidepressant and antihypertensive medication"],"correct":"A,B,E","rationale":"Why correct: Management of phobias includes psychotherapy — systematic desensitization and flooding therapy — plus antidepressants and antihypertensives.  Why C wrong: ECT is not listed for phobia management.  Why D wrong: Aversion therapy is not the psychotherapy listed for phobias here."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which specific phobia is correctly matched with its fear? (SATA)","options":["Acrophobia – fear of heights","Algophobia – fear of pain","Hematophobia – fear of blood","Zoophobia – fear of animals","Claustrophobia – fear of open spaces"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Acrophobia = fear of heights; Algophobia = fear of pain; Hematophobia = fear of blood; Zoophobia = fear of animals.  Why E wrong: Claustrophobia is fear of closed spaces (not open spaces)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Match the phobia to the correct fear: Gamophobia, Bibliophobia, Iatrophobia. (SATA)","options":["Gamophobia – fear of marriage","Bibliophobia – fear of books","Iatrophobia – fear of doctors","Gamophobia – fear of games","Iatrophobia – fear of insects"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Gamophobia = fear of marriage; Bibliophobia = fear of books; Iatrophobia = fear of doctors.  Why D wrong: Gamophobia is marriage, not games.  Why E wrong: Iatrophobia is doctors; fear of insects is entomophobia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Philophobia is the fear of:","options":["Falling in love","Fire","Philosophy","Flowers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Philophobia = fear of falling in love.  Why B wrong: Fear of fire is pyrophobia.  Why C/D wrong: Not listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Ophidiophobia and Entomophobia refer respectively to fear of:","options":["Snakes; insects","Insects; snakes","Birds; animals","Crowds; strangers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ophidiophobia = fear of snakes; Entomophobia = fear of insects.  Why B wrong: Reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Birds = ornithophobia, crowds = enochlophobia, strangers = xenophobia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Match the phobia: Hydrophobia, Mysophobia, Ornithophobia. (SATA)","options":["Hydrophobia – fear of water","Mysophobia – fear of dirt or germs","Ornithophobia – fear of birds","Hydrophobia – fear of heights","Ornithophobia – fear of fire"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Hydrophobia = fear of water; Mysophobia = fear of dirt/germs; Ornithophobia = fear of birds.  Why D wrong: Fear of heights is acrophobia.  Why E wrong: Fear of fire is pyrophobia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Androphobia and Gynophobia refer respectively to fear of:","options":["Men; women","Women; men","Marriage; strangers","Doctors; needles"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Androphobia = fear of man (men); Gynophobia = fear of women.  Why B wrong: Reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not matching the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Glossophobia is the fear of:","options":["Speaking in public","Glass","Tongues","Books"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Glossophobia = fear of speaking in public.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the source definition (books = bibliophobia)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Belenophobia and Pyrophobia refer respectively to fear of:","options":["Needles; fire","Fire; needles","Birds; germs","Being alone; cooking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Belenophobia = fear of needles; Pyrophobia = fear of fire.  Why B wrong: Reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not matching the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Autophobia and Nosocomephobia refer respectively to fear of:","options":["Being alone; hospital","Cars; germs","Self; cooking","Heights; dentists"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Autophobia = fear of being alone; Nosocomephobia = fear of hospital.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not matching the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Mageirocophobia and Dentophobia refer respectively to fear of:","options":["Cooking; dentists","Dentists; cooking","Germs; heights","Magic; teeth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Mageirocophobia = fear of cooking; Dentophobia = fear of dentists.  Why B wrong: Reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not matching the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Dissociate Conversion Disorder (DCD) was previously known as:","options":["Hypochondriasis","Hysteria","Malingering","Ganser syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DCD was previously known as hysteria.  Why A wrong: Hypochondriasis is a different disorder (fear of serious illness).  Why C wrong: Malingering is intentional feigning for gain.  Why D wrong: Ganser syndrome is a separate dissociative disorder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Conversion disorder is best described as a mental condition in which a person has:","options":["Two or more distinct personalities","Pseudo blindness, paralysis, or other nervous system related problems","Excessive worry about having a serious illness","An irresistible urge to set fires"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Conversion disorder = a mental condition in which a person has pseudo blindness, paralysis, or other nervous system related problems; unexpressed feelings/anxiety convert into physical symptoms which are not real.  Why A wrong: That describes dissociative identity disorder.  Why C wrong: That describes hypochondriasis.  Why D wrong: That describes pyromania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"\"La belle indifference,\" most commonly associated with conversion disorder, refers to:","options":["Extreme concern about symptoms","An apparent lack of concern shown by some patients toward their symptoms","Complete memory loss","Repeated hand washing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: La belle indifference = an apparent lack of concern shown by some patients toward their symptoms, most commonly associated with conversion disorder (CD).  Why A wrong: It is a lack, not excess, of concern.  Why C/D wrong: These describe amnesia and OCD respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In conversion disorders, \"primary gain\" and \"secondary gain\" are respectively defined as:","options":["Primary: to get sympathy; Secondary: to resolve own anxiety","Primary: to resolve own anxiety; Secondary: to get sympathy from family members or others","Primary: financial reward; Secondary: avoiding work","Primary: attention; Secondary: revenge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Primary gain = to resolve own anxiety; Secondary gain = to get sympathy from family members or others.  Why A wrong: Reversed.  Why C/D wrong: Not the source definitions."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Treatment approaches for conversion/dissociative conversion disorder include: (SATA)","options":["Behaviour modification","Aversion stimuli","Placebo theory","Lithium therapy","ECT as first line"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Treatment includes behaviour modification, aversion stimuli, and placebo theory.  Why D/E wrong: Lithium and ECT as first line are not listed as treatments for DCD in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Dissociative Identity Disorder (DSM-5) is characterized by:","options":["Inability to remember personal information of a traumatic nature","The presence of two or more well-defined personality states with recurrent memory slips of daily events, personal information, and/or traumatic events","Persistent depersonalization/derealization","Nonsensical or wrong answers to questions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dissociative identity disorder = identity distress due to presence of two or more well-defined personality states and recurrent memory slips of daily events, important personal information, and/or traumatic events which are not usually forgotten.  Why A wrong: That describes dissociative amnesia.  Why C wrong: That describes depersonalization/derealization disorder.  Why D wrong: That describes Ganser syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Dissociative Amnesia is defined as:","options":["Two or more personality states","Inability to remember personal information, generally of a traumatic or stressful nature, which is not usually forgotten","Experiences of unreality about surroundings","Excessive worry about illness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dissociative amnesia = inability to remember personal information, generally of a traumatic or stressful nature, which is not usually forgotten.  Why A wrong: That is dissociative identity disorder.  Why C wrong: That is derealization.  Why D wrong: That is hypochondriasis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In depersonalization/derealization disorder, \"derealization\" refers to:","options":["Experiences of unreality, distancing, or being an observer of one's own thoughts, feelings, sensations, body or actions","Experience of unreality or distancing from one's surroundings, with the sense of reality kept","Loss of personal identity","Two or more personality states"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Derealization = experience of unreality or distancing from your surroundings; in both depersonalization and derealization the sense of reality is kept.  Why A wrong: That describes depersonalization (observer of one's own thoughts/body).  Why C/D wrong: Not the definitions given."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Ganser syndrome is best described as:","options":["A common anxiety disorder treated with benzodiazepines","A rare dissociative disorder (previously classified as factitious) characterized by nonsensical or wrong answers to questions or doing things incorrectly, with symptoms such as fugue, amnesia or conversion, visual pseudo hallucinations, and decreased consciousness","A personality disorder of Cluster A","Fear of germs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ganser syndrome is a rare dissociative disorder previously classified as a factitious disorder, characterized by nonsensical/wrong answers or doing things incorrectly, other dissociative symptoms (fugue, amnesia, conversion), often with visual pseudo hallucinations and decreased consciousness.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not describe Ganser syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Ganser syndrome is also known as:","options":["Kanner's syndrome","Nonsense syndrome or balderdash syndrome","Tourette syndrome","Rett syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Ganser syndrome is also known as nonsense syndrome or balderdash syndrome.  Why A wrong: Kanner's syndrome = autism spectrum disorder.  Why C wrong: Tourette syndrome = chronic tics.  Why D wrong: Rett syndrome is a genetic brain disorder affecting females."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Hypochondriasis is best described as a condition in which a person is:","options":["Excessively and unduly worried about having a serious illness, with obsession about having a serious but undiagnosed medical condition","Unable to remember personal information","Experiencing two or more personalities","Compulsively drinking alcohol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypochondriasis = excessive and undue worry about having a serious illness; obsession with having a serious but undiagnosed medical condition, often misinterpreting typical body functions as signs of illness.  Why B wrong: That is dissociative amnesia.  Why C wrong: That is dissociative identity disorder.  Why D wrong: That is dipsomania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Match the taste disorders correctly: Ageusia, Dysgeusia, Hypogeusia. (SATA)","options":["Ageusia – complete taste loss","Dysgeusia – distorted taste perception (triggered by a taste stimulus)","Hypogeusia – reduced ability to taste","Ageusia – gustatory hallucination","Hypogeusia – complete loss of taste"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Ageusia = complete taste loss; Dysgeusia = distorted taste perception (triggered by taste stimulus); Hypogeusia = reduced ability to taste.  Why D wrong: Gustatory hallucination is phantogeusia.  Why E wrong: Complete loss is ageusia, not hypogeusia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Phantogeusia is defined as:","options":["Reduced ability to taste","Gustatory hallucination (occurs without a stimulus)","Distorted taste triggered by a stimulus","Complete taste loss"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Phantogeusia = gustatory hallucination that occurs without a stimulus.  Why A wrong: That is hypogeusia.  Why C wrong: That is dysgeusia.  Why D wrong: That is ageusia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Match the smell (olfactory) disorders correctly: Anosmia, Hyposmia, Parosmia, Phantosmia. (SATA)","options":["Anosmia – complete loss of ability to smell","Hyposmia – reduced ability to smell","Parosmia – distorted odour perception (triggered by a stimulus)","Phantosmia – olfactory hallucination (occurs without a stimulus)","Anosmia – olfactory hallucination"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Anosmia = complete loss of ability to smell; Hyposmia = reduced ability to smell; Parosmia = distorted odour perception (triggered by a stimulus); Phantosmia = olfactory hallucination (occurs without a stimulus).  Why E wrong: Anosmia is loss of smell, not a hallucination (that is phantosmia)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is a psychiatric disorder in which a person experiences:","options":["Only physical symptoms with no mental cause","Uncontrollable and recurring thoughts (obsessions), engages in repetitive behaviours (compulsions), or both","Two or more personality states","Complete loss of smell"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OCD = uncontrollable, recurring thoughts (obsessions), repetitive behaviours (compulsions), or both; these can take up many hours and interfere with family/social relationships.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe conversion disorder, dissociative identity disorder, and anosmia respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Pharmacological treatment of OCD includes SSRIs such as: (SATA)","options":["Clomipramine","Fluvoxamine","Fluoxetine","Sertraline","Methylphenidate"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Pharmacological treatment of OCD includes antidepressants including SSRIs — Clomipramine, Fluvoxamine, Fluoxetine, Sertraline. (Note: clomipramine is technically a tricyclic used in OCD, but the source lists it here.)  Why E wrong: Methylphenidate is the drug of choice for ADHD, not OCD."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the source, the drug of choice for OCD is:","options":["Sertraline","Fluoxetine (an SSRI antidepressant)","Clomipramine","Lithium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for OCD is fluoxetine, an SSRI antidepressant.  Why A/C wrong: These are also used in OCD but not named as the drug of choice.  Why D wrong: Lithium is a mood stabilizer, not the OCD drug of choice."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In OCD, the \"contamination\" obsession most commonly leads to which compulsion?","options":["Checking","Washing & cleaning","Ordering and rearranging","Cancelling or undoing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Contamination (fear of germs/dust/chemicals) leads to washing & cleaning — repeated hand washing, excessive showering, cleaning household items to remove germs/contaminants.  Why A wrong: Checking follows fear of causing harm/mistakes.  Why C/D wrong: These are \"other compulsions.\""},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The OCD obsession of \"losing control\" includes which of the following fears? (SATA)","options":["Fear of acting on an impulse to harm others/oneself","Fear of insults or stealing things","Fear of germs and contamination","Fear of losing things"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Losing control\" obsessions = fear of acting on an impulse to harm others/oneself and fear of insults or stealing things.  Why C wrong: Fear of germs/contamination is the contamination obsession.  Why D wrong: Fear of losing things is listed under \"other obsessions.\""},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"other obsessions\" in OCD? (SATA)","options":["Being superstitious about lucky & unlucky colours or numbers","Getting over-concerned about catching certain disease like cancer","Concerned about being perfect or performing tasks perfectly","Fear of losing things","Repeated hand washing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other obsessions = superstition about lucky/unlucky colours or numbers, over-concern about catching disease like cancer, concern about being perfect/performing tasks perfectly, and fear of losing things.  Why E wrong: Repeated hand washing is a compulsion (washing & cleaning), not an obsession."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"other compulsions\" in OCD? (SATA)","options":["Repeating routine activities or body movements","Ordering and rearranging things repeatedly","Cancelling or undoing","Avoiding situations which trigger obsessions","Fear of insults"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Other compulsions = repeating routine activities/body movements, ordering and rearranging things repeatedly, cancelling or undoing, and avoiding situations which trigger obsessions.  Why E wrong: Fear of insults is an obsession (losing control), not a compulsion."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which habitual/impulsive disorder is defined as \"compulsive drinking\"?","options":["Pyromania","Kleptomania","Dipsomania","Trichotillomania"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Dipsomania = compulsive drinking.  Why A wrong: Pyromania = pathological fire setting.  Why B wrong: Kleptomania = irresistible urge to steal.  Why D wrong: Trichotillomania = pulling of hair."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Pyromania is defined as:","options":["Compulsive drinking","Pathological fire setting without any profit","Irresistible urge to steal things","Extraction of nasal mucus by own finger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pyromania = pathological fire setting without any profit.  Why A wrong: That is dipsomania.  Why C wrong: That is kleptomania.  Why D wrong: That is rhinotillexomania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Kleptomania is defined as:","options":["Pathological fire setting","Irresistible urge to steal things","Compulsive drinking","Uncontrollable urge to keep gambling"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kleptomania = irresistible urge to steal things.  Why A wrong: That is pyromania.  Why C wrong: That is dipsomania.  Why D wrong: That is gambling."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Rhinotillexomania is defined as:","options":["Pulling of hair","Extraction of nasal mucus by one's own finger","Nail biting","Compulsive drinking"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rhinotillexomania = extraction of nasal mucus by own finger.  Why A wrong: That is trichotillomania.  Why C wrong: Nail biting is a separate entry.  Why D wrong: That is dipsomania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Trichotillomania is defined as: (PYQ: AIIMS BPL, NORCET-2022)","options":["Uncontrollable urge to gamble","Pulling of hair and biting, mostly scalp hair","Nail biting due to anxiety","Compulsive fire setting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trichotillomania = pulling of hair and biting, mostly scalp hair (AIIMS BPL, NORCET-2022).  Why A wrong: That is gambling disorder.  Why C wrong: Nail biting/thumb sucking is a separate entry (due to anxiety).  Why D wrong: That is pyromania."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the impulsive disorders list, nail biting and thumb sucking are attributed to:","options":["Anxiety","Fire setting urge","Genetic mutation","Nutritional deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nail biting & thumb sucking are described as due to anxiety.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the cause stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Gambling (as an impulsive disorder) is defined as:","options":["Irresistible urge to steal","Uncontrollable urge to keep gambling, e.g., ludo game, video game","Compulsive drinking","Pathological fire setting"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Gambling = uncontrollable urge to keep gambling, e.g., ludo game, video game.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are kleptomania, dipsomania, and pyromania respectively."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Autism spectrum disorder is also known as childhood neuro-developmental disorder or:","options":["Asperger syndrome","Kanner's syndrome","Rett syndrome","Tourette syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ASD is also known as childhood neuro-developmental disorder or Kanner's syndrome.  Why A wrong: Asperger is a separate (milder) ASD.  Why C wrong: Rett is a genetic brain disorder in females.  Why D wrong: Tourette = chronic tics."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Autism spectrum disorder is characterised by which features? (SATA)","options":["Impaired social communication, social relationship, and delayed developmental milestones","Lack of social smile and eye-to-eye contact","Lack of awareness about surroundings","Cannot perceive danger","Superior intelligence in all cases"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Autism is characterised by impaired social communication, social relationship, and delayed development milestone; lack of social smile and eye-to-eye contact; lack of awareness about surroundings; and inability to perceive danger.  Why E wrong: Superior intelligence in all cases is not a defining feature of autism."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Asperger syndrome differs from other autism spectrum disorders in that it involves:","options":["Severe intellectual disability","Relatively normal language and intelligence, with difficulties in social interaction and nonverbal communication and restricted, repetitive patterns of behaviour","Complete absence of language","Onset only in adulthood"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Asperger is a neurodevelopmental disorder with significant difficulties in social interaction and nonverbal communication plus restricted/repetitive behaviour; as a milder ASD it differs by relatively normal language and intelligence.  Why A/C wrong: Language and intelligence are relatively normal.  Why D wrong: It is a childhood neurodevelopmental disorder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Rett syndrome is a genetic brain disorder that typically becomes apparent after what age and mainly affects which sex?","options":["After 6 to 18 months of age; mainly females","After 5 years of age; mainly males","At birth; both sexes equally","After puberty; mainly females"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Rett syndrome typically becomes apparent after 6 to 18 months of age and affects mainly females.  Why B/C/D wrong: These age/sex combinations contradict the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Complications of Rett syndrome can include: (SATA)","options":["Seizures","Scoliosis","Sleeping problems","Smaller head size and problems walking","Superior intelligence"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Rett syndrome features slower growth, problems walking, smaller head size; complications include seizures, scoliosis, and sleeping problems; symptoms include problems with language, coordination, and repetitive movements.  Why E wrong: Superior intelligence is not associated with Rett syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Involuntary repetitive sounds such as grunting, sniffing, or throat clearing are called:","options":["Motor tics","Vocal tics","Stereotypy","Echolalia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Involuntary repetitive sounds (grunting, sniffing, throat clearing) are vocal tics; movements of limbs/body parts are motor tics.  Why A wrong: Motor tics are movements of limbs/body parts.  Why C/D wrong: Not the terms used in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Chronic tics are known as: (PYQ: AIIMS-Nagpur-2020 context)","options":["Kanner's syndrome","Tourette syndrome","Rett syndrome","Ganser syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Chronic tics are known as Tourette syndrome.  Why A wrong: Kanner's = autism.  Why C wrong: Rett is a genetic brain disorder in females.  Why D wrong: Ganser = nonsense syndrome."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which of the following are examples of tics given in the source? (SATA)","options":["Eye blinking","Shrugging shoulder","Grimacing","Repeated vocalisation","Fire setting"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of tic include eye blinking, shrugging shoulder, grimacing, and repeated vocalisation.  Why E wrong: Fire setting is pyromania, not a tic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Mental retardation is now known as:","options":["Learning disability","Intellectual disability","Developmental delay","Cognitive impairment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states mental retardation is now known as intellectual disability.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the term specified in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the source, the normal IQ in a human being is:","options":["70–90","90–110","110–130","50–70"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal IQ in human being is 90–110.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the source's stated normal IQ."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The term \"intelligence\" was coined by:","options":["Binet","Steered Bennet (Stern/Bennet as printed)","Wechsler","Kraepelin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the intelligence word was coined by \"Steered bennet\" (as printed). Key per source text.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not credited in the source (note: printed name appears garbled)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The most common congenital cause of mental retardation (intellectual disability) is:","options":["Lead poisoning","Down syndrome","PKU","Oxygen deprivation during labour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Causes include congenital causes with Down syndrome being most common; others are lead poisoning, PKU, trauma & infection during/after pregnancy, oxygen deprivation during/after labour, nutritional deficiencies.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are listed causes but Down syndrome is named as most common congenital cause."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The formula for IQ is:","options":["IQ = CA/MA × 100","IQ = MA/CA × 100","IQ = MA × CA / 100","IQ = (MA + CA)/2"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Formula for IQ = MA/CA × 100, where MA = mental age and CA = chronological age.  Why A wrong: The ratio is reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These are not the correct formula."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the formula IQ = MA/CA × 100, \"MA\" and \"CA\" stand for:","options":["Maximum age and Current age","Mental age and Chronological age","Median age and Calendar age","Mean age and Comparative age"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MA = Mental age (printed as \"Metal age\"), CA = Chronological age.  Why A/C/D wrong: These expansions do not match the source (which defines IQ = Intelligence quotient)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the IQ range table for levels of MR, an IQ of 70–50 corresponds to which level and educational classification?","options":["Mild MR – Educable","Moderate MR – Trainable","Severe MR – Dependent","Profound MR – Custodial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: IQ 70–50 = Mild MR, educational classification Educable.  Why B wrong: 50–35 = Moderate MR (Trainable).  Why C wrong: 35–20 = Severe MR (Dependent).  Why D wrong: <20 = Profound MR (Custodial).  Note: Source uses IQ-only cut-offs (≈ICD-10: mild 50–69, moderate 35–49, severe 20–34, profound <20). DSM-5/DSM-5-TR now grades intellectual disability by adaptive functioning (conceptual, social, practical), not IQ alone."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the level-of-MR table, \"Moderate MR\" corresponds to which IQ range and educational classification?","options":["70–50; Educable","50–35; Trainable","35–20; Dependent","<20; Custodial"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 50–35 = Moderate MR, classified as Trainable.  Why A wrong: 70–50 = Mild MR (Educable).  Why C wrong: 35–20 = Severe MR (Dependent).  Why D wrong: <20 = Profound MR (Custodial)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the level-of-MR table, an IQ below 20 corresponds to:","options":["Mild MR – Educable","Moderate MR – Trainable","Severe MR – Dependent","Profound MR – Custodial"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: IQ <20 = Profound MR, educational classification Custodial.  Why A/B/C wrong: These correspond to higher IQ bands (Mild, Moderate, Severe)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In the \"Level of Intelligence\" table, an IQ of 0–24 corresponds to:","options":["Imbecile","Idiot","Moron","Borderline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: IQ 0–24 = Idiot (as printed in source's dated terminology).  Why A wrong: Imbecile = 25–49.  Why C wrong: Moron = 50–69.  Why D wrong: Borderline = 70–79."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Match the IQ level to intelligence category: Imbecile, Moron, Borderline. (SATA)","options":["Imbecile – 25–49","Moron – 50–69","Borderline – 70–79","Imbecile – 0–24","Moron – 80–89"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Imbecile = 25–49; Moron = 50–69; Borderline = 70–79.  Why D wrong: 0–24 = Idiot.  Why E wrong: 80–89 = Low normal."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the Level of Intelligence table, the \"Normal/average\" IQ range is: (PYQ: ESIC-2024)","options":["80–89","90–109","110–119","120–139"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Normal/average = 90–109 (ESIC-2024).  Why A wrong: 80–89 = Low normal.  Why C wrong: 110–119 = Superior.  Why D wrong: 120–139 = Very Genius."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"According to the Level of Intelligence table, an IQ of >140 corresponds to:","options":["Superior","Very Genius","Near Genius","Low normal"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: IQ >140 = Near Genius; Superior = 110–119; Very Genius = 120–139.  Why A/B wrong: These are lower ranges.  Why D wrong: Low normal = 80–89."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"\"Functional enuresis\" in child psychiatry is defined as:","options":["Involuntary passage of stool in one's clothing after 5 years of age","Involuntary passage of urine in one's clothing after 5 years of age","Eating of non-nutritive substances","Impaired reading ability"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Functional enuresis = involuntary passage of urine in one's clothing after 5 years of age.  Why A wrong: That is functional encopresis.  Why C wrong: That is pica.  Why D wrong: That is dyslexia."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"\"Functional encopresis\" is defined as:","options":["Involuntary passage of urine in one's clothing after 5 years of age","Involuntary passage of stool in one's clothing after 5 years of age","Eating chalk, sand, and coal","Abnormal pronunciation of words"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Functional encopresis = involuntary passage of stool in one's clothing after 5 years of age.  Why A wrong: That is enuresis.  Why C wrong: That is pica.  Why D wrong: That is developmental phonological disorder."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Pica is defined as eating of non-eatable or non-nutritive substances (like chalk, sand & coal) and may put the child at risk of: (PYQ: NHM, AIIMS, DSSSB)","options":["Vitamin D toxicity","Iron deficiency anaemia (nutritional deficiency)","Diabetes","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pica = eating non-eatable/non-nutritive substances like chalk, sand & coal; due to eating of non-nutritive substances the child may risk nutritional deficiency like iron deficiency anaemia (NHM, AIIMS, DSSSB).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the risk stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Developmental reading disorder is also known as:","options":["Dyscalculia","Dyslexia","Dysphasia","Dyspraxia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Developmental reading disorder (dyslexia) = impaired reading & reading ability.  Why A wrong: Dyscalculia is arithmetic disorder.  Why C/D wrong: Not named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Developmental arithmetic disorder (dyscalculia) is defined as:","options":["Impaired reading ability","Inability to learn mathematical symbols & formula","Abnormal pronunciation of words","Involuntary passage of urine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dyscalculia = inability to learn mathematical symbols & formula.  Why A wrong: That is dyslexia.  Why C wrong: That is developmental phonological disorder.  Why D wrong: That is enuresis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Developmental phonological disorder is characterized by abnormal pronunciation of particular words, for example:","options":["\"Rabbit\" pronounced as \"wabbit\"","Inability to read","Eating chalk","Involuntary passage of stool"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Developmental phonological disorder = abnormal pronunciation of a particular word, like Rabbit pronounced as wabbit.  Why B wrong: That is dyslexia.  Why C wrong: That is pica.  Why D wrong: That is encopresis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The drug of choice for ADHD is:","options":["Fluoxetine","Methylphenidate","Lithium","Haloperidol"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drug of choice for ADHD is methylphenidate.  Why A wrong: Fluoxetine is the drug of choice for OCD.  Why C wrong: Lithium is a mood stabilizer.  Why D wrong: Haloperidol is an antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"A personality disorder is best defined as a type of mental disorder in which a person has:","options":["Two or more distinct identities","A rigid and unhealthy pattern of thinking, functioning, and behaving","Recurrent panic attacks","Involuntary urine passage after age 5"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A personality disorder = a type of mental disorder with a rigid and unhealthy pattern of thinking, functioning, and behaving.  Why A wrong: That describes dissociative identity disorder.  Why C wrong: That describes panic disorder.  Why D wrong: That describes enuresis."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which personality disorders belong to DSM-5 Cluster A? (SATA)","options":["Paranoid","Schizoid","Schizotypal","Antisocial","Borderline"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Cluster A = Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal.  Why D/E wrong: Antisocial and Borderline are Cluster B."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In DSM-5 Cluster A, the \"Paranoid\" personality is characterized by being:","options":["Suspicious, distrustful, hypervigilant, hostile, irritable","A loner, detached, unemotional, passive","Manipulative with abandonment fears","Submissive and clingy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paranoid = suspicious, distrustful, hypervigilant, hostile, irritable.  Why B wrong: That describes schizoid.  Why C wrong: That describes borderline (Cluster B).  Why D wrong: That describes dependent (Cluster C)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Cluster A \"Schizoid\" personality is characterized by:","options":["Preferring to be a loner, detached, unemotional, passive","Unusual thoughts, perceptions and bizarre behaviour","Grandiosity and lack of empathy","Attention-seeking and dramatic behaviour"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Schizoid = prefers to be a loner, detached, unemotional, passive.  Why B wrong: That describes schizotypal.  Why C wrong: That describes narcissistic (Cluster B).  Why D wrong: That describes histrionic (Cluster B)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Cluster A \"Schizotypal\" personality is characterized by:","options":["Suspicious and hostile behaviour","Unusual thoughts, perceptions, and bizarre behaviour","Fear of criticism and rejection","Rigid, controlling perfectionism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Schizotypal = unusual thoughts, perceptions and bizarre behaviour.  Why A wrong: That describes paranoid.  Why C wrong: That describes avoidant (Cluster C).  Why D wrong: That describes obsessive-compulsive (Cluster C)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which personality disorders belong to DSM-5 Cluster B? (SATA)","options":["Antisocial","Borderline","Histrionic","Narcissistic","Avoidant"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Cluster B = Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, Narcissistic.  Why E wrong: Avoidant is Cluster C."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In Cluster B, the personality characterized by \"disregard and violation of the rights of others\" is:","options":["Antisocial","Borderline","Histrionic","Narcissistic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antisocial = disregard and violation of the rights of others.  Why B wrong: Borderline = manipulative, abandonment fears, labile mood, impulsivity, inner emptiness, self-harm.  Why C wrong: Histrionic = superficial, theatrical, attention-seeking, dramatic.  Why D wrong: Narcissistic = grandiosity, lack of empathy, egocentric."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which Cluster B personality is characterized by being manipulative, with abandonment fears, labile mood, impulsivity, inner emptiness, and self-harm?","options":["Antisocial","Borderline","Histrionic","Narcissistic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Borderline = manipulative, abandonment fears, labile mood, impulsivity, inner emptiness, self-harm.  Why A wrong: Antisocial = violation of others' rights.  Why C wrong: Histrionic = theatrical, attention-seeking.  Why D wrong: Narcissistic = grandiosity, lack of empathy."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Cluster B \"Histrionic\" and \"Narcissistic\" personalities are respectively characterized by:","options":["Histrionic – superficial, theatrical, attention-seeking, dramatic; Narcissistic – grandiosity, lack of empathy, egocentric","Histrionic – grandiosity; Narcissistic – attention-seeking","Both by fear of criticism","Both by submissiveness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Histrionic = superficial, theatrical, attention-seeking, dramatic; Narcissistic = grandiosity, lack of empathy, egocentric.  Why B wrong: Reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These describe Cluster C traits."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which personality disorders belong to DSM-5 Cluster C? (SATA)","options":["Avoidant","Dependent","Obsessive-compulsive","Schizoid","Borderline"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Cluster C = Avoidant, Dependent, Obsessive-compulsive.  Why D wrong: Schizoid is Cluster A.  Why E wrong: Borderline is Cluster B."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"In DSM-5 Cluster C, which personality is characterized by \"avoidance due to fear of criticism and rejection\"?","options":["Avoidant","Dependent","Obsessive-compulsive","Paranoid"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avoidant = avoidance due to fear of criticism and rejection.  Why B wrong: Dependent = submissive, clingy, needs to be taken care of.  Why C wrong: Obsessive-compulsive = rigid, controlling, perfectionist.  Why D wrong: Paranoid is Cluster A."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"The Cluster C \"Dependent\" and \"Obsessive-compulsive\" personalities are respectively characterized by:","options":["Dependent – submissive, clingy, needs to be taken care of; Obsessive-compulsive – rigid, controlling, perfectionist","Dependent – perfectionist; Obsessive-compulsive – clingy","Both by grandiosity","Both by suspiciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dependent = submissive, clingy, needs to be taken care of; Obsessive-compulsive = rigid, controlling, perfectionist.  Why B wrong: Reversed.  Why C wrong: Grandiosity = narcissistic (Cluster B).  Why D wrong: Suspiciousness = paranoid (Cluster A)."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which of the following are listed as signs/symptoms of depression in the Depression column? (SATA)","options":["Loss of interest in pleasurable activities","Low self-esteem & confidence","Suicidal ideations/tendencies","Difficulty and inability in making decisions","Inflated self-esteem"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: The depression column lists sadness of mood, loss of interest in pleasurable activities, low self-esteem & confidence, easily fatigued, changes in appetite, sleep disturbance, feelings of guilt/lack of energy, reduced attention/concentration, suicidal ideations/tendencies, difficulty and inability in making decisions, poor concentration, and introvert personality.  Why E wrong: Inflated self-esteem is a manic feature."},{"section":"Batch 005 · Types & clinical features of depression…","question":"Which OCD compulsion is described as \"checking repeatedly that you didn't or will not harm others/yourself or made a terrible mistake\"?","options":["Washing & cleaning","Checking","Ordering","Undoing"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Checking = checking repeatedly that you didn't or will not harm others/yourself or made a terrible mistake.  Why A wrong: Washing & cleaning follows contamination obsessions.  Why C/D wrong: These are \"other compulsions.\""},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Sleep is basically divided into which two phases?","options":["Alpha sleep and Beta sleep","Non-REM (NREM) sleep and REM sleep (REMS)","Light sleep and Deep sleep only","Orthodox sleep and Delta sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states sleep is basically divided into two phases — Non-REM (NREM) and REM sleep (REMS).  Why A wrong: Alpha and beta are EEG wave types, not the two overall sleep phases.  Why C wrong: Light/deep are descriptive substages, not the primary two-phase division.  Why D wrong: Orthodox and delta are alternate names/features within NREM, not the two main phases."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"NREM sleep is called Non-REM sleep because on the electro-oculogram there is:","options":["Rapid eye movement","No rapid eye movement; rather slow or quiescent eye movement","Complete absence of any eye activity","Fluttering of the eyelids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NREM is named Non-REM because no rapid eye movement is seen on the electro-oculogram; rather there is slow or quiescent eye movement.  Why A wrong: Rapid eye movement characterizes REM sleep, not NREM.  Why C wrong: There is slow/quiescent movement, not complete absence.  Why D wrong: Eyelid fluttering is not described in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"NREM sleep occupies approximately what percentage of total sleep time?","options":["20–30%","40–50%","60–70%","80–90%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NREM occupies 60–70% of total sleep time.  Why A wrong: 20–30% is the proportion for REM sleep.  Why B wrong: 40–50% is the proportion of Stage II within NREM, not total NREM.  Why D wrong: Not stated; overstates NREM proportion."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which neurotransmitter mediates NREM sleep?","options":["Noradrenaline","Dopamine","Serotonin","Acetylcholine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NREM is mediated by serotonin.  Why A wrong: Noradrenaline mediates REM sleep.  Why B wrong: Dopamine is not identified as the NREM mediator in the source.  Why D wrong: Acetylcholine is not the mediator named for NREM in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"NREM sleep is also referred to as all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["S-Sleep (synchronized sleep)","Quiet sleep","Orthodox sleep","Paradoxical sleep"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Paradoxical sleep is another name for REM sleep, NOT NREM.  Why A wrong: NREM is referred to as S-Sleep (synchronized sleep).  Why B wrong: NREM is also called Quiet sleep.  Why C wrong: NREM is also called Orthodox sleep."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Stage I of NREM sleep is characterized by disappearance of which wave activity and appearance of theta activity?","options":["Delta waves","Alpha waves","Beta waves","Sigma waves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage I is the transition from wakefulness to sleep, characterized by disappearance of α-Alpha wave activity and appearance of θ-Theta activity, with slow eye movements.  Why A wrong: Delta waves appear in stages III & IV, not stage I.  Why C wrong: Beta waves are associated with REM (low-voltage activity), not stage I disappearance.  Why D wrong: Sigma/sleep spindle waves characterize stage II."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Sleep spindles seen in Stage II of NREM sleep are 14 Hz waves also known as:","options":["Delta waves","Sigma waves","K-complex waves","Theta waves"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Stage II is characterized by sleep spindles, which are 14 Hz waves also known as Sigma waves (along with K-complex).  Why A wrong: Delta waves are seen in stages III/IV.  Why C wrong: K-complex is a separate feature of stage II, not the name for the 14 Hz spindle.  Why D wrong: Theta waves appear in stage I."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In Stage II of NREM sleep, the vital signs:","options":["Rise sharply","Start to decline","Become irregular and fluctuate","Remain completely unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In stage II of NREM sleep, vital signs start to decline.  Why A wrong: Vital signs increase in REM sleep, not stage II NREM.  Why C wrong: Fluctuation of vitals is characteristic of REM sleep.  Why D wrong: The source specifically states they start to decline."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Stages III and IV of NREM sleep (deep sleep) are also called slow wave sleep because they are characterized by which waves?","options":["Alpha waves","Theta waves","Delta waves","Sigma waves"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Stages III and IV are deep/slow wave sleep, characterized by Δ-Slow-Delta-waves; delta wave appearance starts in stage 3 and dominates in stage 4.  Why A wrong: Alpha waves disappear at sleep onset (stage I).  Why B wrong: Theta waves characterize stage I.  Why D wrong: Sigma waves characterize stage II."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In which NREM stage does delta wave appearance start, and in which stage does it dominate?","options":["Starts in stage 1, dominates in stage 2","Starts in stage 2, dominates in stage 3","Starts in stage 3, dominates in stage 4","Starts in stage 4, dominates in REM"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Delta waves appearance starts in stage 3 and is dominated in stage 4.  Why A wrong: Stage 1 shows theta, not delta.  Why B wrong: Stage 2 shows sleep spindles/K-complex, not delta onset.  Why D wrong: Delta is a NREM feature; REM shows low-voltage beta activity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following commonly occur in the NREM phase of sleep? (SATA)","options":["Night terrors","Sleepwalking","Bruxism","Bed wetting (enuresis)","Sleep talking"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists night terrors, sleepwalking, confusional arousals, bruxism, bed wetting (enuresis), sleep starts, sleep talking, and head-banging as commonly occurring in the NREM phase.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: All five are explicitly listed as NREM-phase phenomena."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"REM sleep is also known as paradoxical sleep because:","options":["It occurs only in children","There is a paradoxical elevation of brain activity, metabolism, and physiological activity","It lacks any dreaming","Vital signs remain completely stable"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: REM is called paradoxical sleep because there is a paradoxical elevation of brain activity, metabolism, and physiological activity.  Why A wrong: Not related to age in the source.  Why C wrong: Vivid dreams occur in REM; it is not dreamless.  Why D wrong: In REM, vitals fluctuate (increased HR, BP, respiration), not stable."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In REM sleep, the high amplitude slow waves seen in stages 3 & 4 of NREM are replaced by:","options":["Rapid, low voltage activity (β-beta waves)","Delta waves","Sleep spindles","Theta waves"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In REM, high amplitude slow waves of NREM are replaced by rapid, low voltage activity (β-beta waves).  Why B wrong: Delta waves are the high-amplitude NREM waves being replaced.  Why C wrong: Sleep spindles belong to stage II NREM.  Why D wrong: Theta waves belong to stage I NREM."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which neurotransmitter mediates REM sleep?","options":["Serotonin","Noradrenaline","GABA","Dopamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: REM is mediated by noradrenaline.  Why A wrong: Serotonin mediates NREM sleep.  Why C wrong: GABA is not named as the REM mediator here.  Why D wrong: Dopamine is not identified as the REM mediator."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following occur in the REM phase of sleep? (SATA)","options":["REM sleep behaviour disorder (RBD)","Sleep paralysis","Nightmares","Early morning nocturnal penile erection and vivid dreams","Fluctuating vitals (increased HR, BP, respiration)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: The source states RBD, sleep paralysis, and nightmares occur in REM; all vitals fluctuate (increased HR, BP, respiration, irregular breathing, intense brain activity); and early morning nocturnal penile erection with vivid dreams occurs in REM.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed as a REM-phase feature."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the \"Also Remember\" box, somnambulism (sleep walking) and night terrors may occur in which stage of sleep? (ESIC-2024)","options":["NREM Stage I","NREM Stage II","NREM Stage III & IV","REM stage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Somnambulism and night terrors may occur in NREM-III & IV stage of sleep (ESIC-2024).  Why A wrong: Not stage I per source.  Why B wrong: Bruxism occurs in NREM-II, not somnambulism/night terrors.  Why D wrong: These occur in deep NREM, not REM."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Bruxism (teeth grinding) may occur in which stage of sleep?","options":["NREM-I","NREM-II","NREM-III & IV","REM"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states bruxism (teeth grinding) may occur in NREM-II stage of sleep.  Why A wrong: Not stage I per source.  Why C wrong: NREM-III & IV is where somnambulism/night terrors occur.  Why D wrong: Bruxism is a NREM-II phenomenon."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Somniloquy (sleep talking) may occur in which stage(s) of sleep?","options":["Only NREM-II","All stages of NREM sleep","Only REM sleep","Only NREM-III & IV"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Somniloquy (sleep talking) may occur in all stages of NREM sleep.  Why A wrong: It is not limited to stage II.  Why C wrong: It is a NREM, not REM, phenomenon.  Why D wrong: It occurs in all NREM stages, not only deep stages."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Early morning nocturnal penile erection is a normal phenomenon occurring in the REM stage and lasts for approximately how long per night?","options":["20–40 minutes","40–60 minutes","80–120 minutes","120–180 minutes"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Early morning nocturnal penile erection occurs in REM stage, is a normal phenomenon, and occurs for 80–120 minutes per night.  Why A/B/D wrong: These durations are not stated; the source specifies 80–120 minutes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Nightmares or fearful/vivid dreams occur in the REM stage usually at what time?","options":["Just after falling asleep","Around midnight","At morning time usually after 4 am","Only during afternoon naps"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Nightmares/fearful/vivid dreams occur in REM usually at morning time after 4 am.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not stated; source specifies morning after 4 am."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"The rate of all vital signs (HR, BP, RR, and temperature) shows what change in the REM stage of sleep?","options":["Decreased","Increased","Unchanged","Only HR increases"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rate of all vital signs (HR, BP, RR, and temp.) are increased in REM stage of sleep.  Why A wrong: They decrease/decline in NREM, not increase.  Why C wrong: Source specifies increase.  Why D wrong: All vital signs increase, not only HR."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Polysomnography is defined as:","options":["A study of brain tumors","A study of sleep","A test for anemia","A cardiac stress test"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source defines polysomnography as a study of sleep.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are unrelated diagnostic tests not matching the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Under sleep disorders classified as dyssomnia, hypersomnia and insomnia respectively refer to:","options":["Excessive sleep; lack of sleep","Lack of sleep; excessive sleep","Sleep walking; teeth grinding","Excessive sleepiness; talking in sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Under dyssomnia, hypersomnia = excessive sleep and insomnia = lack of sleep.  Why B wrong: Reverses the definitions.  Why C wrong: These describe parasomnias (somnambulism/bruxism).  Why D wrong: These describe somnolence/somniloquy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Match the parasomnia to its definition — \"excessive sleepiness\" is termed:","options":["Somnambulism","Somnolence","Somniloquy","Bruxism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Somnolence is defined as excessive sleepiness.  Why A wrong: Somnambulism = walking in sleep.  Why C wrong: Somniloquy = talking during sleeping.  Why D wrong: Bruxism = teeth grinding."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Night terror is best described as:","options":["Un-pleasurable dreams in the early morning","Episodes of screaming, intense fear, and flailing while still asleep, often paired with sleepwalking","Talking during sleep","Grinding of teeth during sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Night terror = episodes of screaming, intense fear and flailing while still asleep, often paired with sleepwalking.  Why A wrong: That describes nightmares/vivid dreams.  Why C wrong: That is somniloquy.  Why D wrong: That is bruxism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Narcolepsy is characterized by daytime sleep & drowsiness and sleep apnoea usually lasting for approximately:","options":["5 seconds","10 seconds","30 seconds","60 seconds"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In narcolepsy, sleep apnoea usually lasts for 10 seconds.  Why A/C/D wrong: Source specifies 10 seconds."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Kleine-Levin syndrome is characterized by:","options":["Recurrent episodes of excessive sleep with cognitive & behavioural changes","Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion","Hallucinations while falling asleep","Inability to move on waking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kleine-Levin syndrome = recurrent episode of excessive sleep with cognitive & behavioural changes.  Why B wrong: That is cataplexy.  Why C wrong: That is hypnagogic hallucination.  Why D wrong: That describes sleep paralysis/hypno-paralysis."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Cataplexy is defined as:","options":["Recurrent excessive sleep episodes","Sudden brief loss of voluntary muscle tone triggered by strong emotions like laughter","Talking during sleep","Eye remaining open but patient unable to move"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cataplexy = sudden brief loss of voluntary muscle tone triggered by strong emotions like laughter.  Why A wrong: That is Kleine-Levin syndrome.  Why C wrong: That is somniloquy.  Why D wrong: That is hypno-paralysis."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Hallucinations that occur while falling asleep are called ______, whereas those occurring while waking up from sleep are called ______.","options":["Hypnopompic; hypnagogic","Hypnagogic; hypnopompic","Hypno-paralysis; cataplexy","Cataplexy; hypnopompic"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hypnagogic hallucinations occur while falling asleep; hypnopompic hallucinations occur while waking up from sleep.  Why A wrong: Reverses the two terms.  Why C wrong: Neither describes falling asleep/waking hallucinations correctly.  Why D wrong: Cataplexy is a muscle-tone loss, not a hallucination while falling asleep."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Hypno-paralysis is described as:","options":["Eye remaining open but the patient not able to move","Loss of muscle tone with emotion","Excessive daytime sleep","Teeth grinding at night"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypno-paralysis = eye remains open but patient is not able to move.  Why B wrong: That is cataplexy.  Why C wrong: That is hypersomnia/narcolepsy.  Why D wrong: That is bruxism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"A mental health crisis is best defined as:","options":["A chronic long-standing mental illness","Any situation in which a person's behaviour puts them at risk of hurting themselves/others, arising from sudden onset of a stressful situation","A planned therapeutic intervention","A normal developmental milestone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A mental health crisis is any situation in which a person's behaviour puts them at risk of hurting; sudden onset of any stressful situation or event in life is known as crisis.  Why A wrong: Crisis is acute/sudden, not chronic.  Why C wrong: A crisis is the emergency, not the intervention.  Why D wrong: A normal milestone is not itself a crisis (though maturational crises relate to development)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Maturational crisis (also known as developmental crisis) is best exemplified by:","options":["Loss of job","Mass disaster or war","Adolescence, marriage, pregnancy, parenthood","Rape or airline crash"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Maturational/developmental crisis occurs by normal life events; examples include adolescence, marriage, pregnancy, parenthood.  Why B wrong: Mass disaster/war are adventitious crises.  Why D wrong: Rape/airline crash are adventitious crises.  Why A wrong: Loss of job is a situational crisis example."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which type of crisis arises from an external rather than internal source, is the MOST COMMON type of crisis, and has a sudden unexpected onset?","options":["Maturational crisis","Situational crisis","Adventitious crisis","Developmental crisis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Situational crisis arises from external rather than internal sources, is the most common type of crisis, and has sudden unexpected onset (e.g., loss of job, loss of loved one, sexual assault, divorce, RTA).  Why A/D wrong: Maturational/developmental crisis arises from normal life events.  Why C wrong: Adventitious crisis is accidental/unplanned (mass disaster), not the most common type."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Adventitious crisis is characterized by which of the following? (SATA)","options":["It is not a part of everyday life","It is accidental & unplanned","It can be natural or human-made","Examples include mass disaster, violence, fire, flood, tornadoes, war","It arises from normal developmental stages"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Adventitious crisis is not part of everyday life, is accidental & unplanned, natural or human-made, with examples like mass disaster, violence, fire, flood, tornadoes, war, airline crash, rape.  Why E wrong: Normal developmental stages describe maturational crisis, not adventitious."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as tasks for crisis intervention? (SATA)","options":["Counselling","Psycho analysis","Abreaction theory","Pharmacological treatment","Follow the SAFER R model"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Tasks for crisis intervention include counselling, psycho analysis, abreaction theory, psychological support, pharmacological treatment, and following the SAFER R model.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed among the tasks."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"A crisis intervention is best described as:","options":["A long-term rehabilitation program","An immediate and short-term emergency response to mental, emotional, physical, and behavioural distress","A purely pharmacological treatment","A preventive vaccination program"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A crisis intervention is an immediate and short-term emergency response to mental, emotional, physical, and behavioural distress; it helps restore an individual's equilibrium and minimize potential for long-term trauma.  Why A wrong: It is short-term, not long-term rehabilitation.  Why C wrong: Pharmacology is only one task, not the whole definition.  Why D wrong: Not a vaccination program."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the SAFER-R model of crisis intervention, the steps in order are:","options":["Stabilise, Acknowledge, Facilitate understanding, Encourage adaptive coping, Restore functioning, Refer as needed","Support, Assess, Follow, Educate, Rest, Refer","Screen, Analyze, Fix, Evaluate, Report, Review","Stabilise, Assess, Facilitate, Encourage, Restrain, Refer"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: SAFER-R = Stabilise the client, Acknowledge the client, Facilitate understanding, Encourage adaptive coping, Restore functioning, Refer as needed.  Why B/C/D wrong: These misstate one or more of the six steps."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Match the sexual disorder — \"uncomfortableness with one's anatomical sex/gender\" is termed:","options":["Transvestism","Dual role","Transsexualism","Fetishism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transvestism = un-comfortableness with one's anatomical sex/gender.  Why B wrong: Dual role = wearing opposite sex clothing for temporary enjoyment.  Why C wrong: Transsexualism = sexual arousal by wearing opposite sex clothing.  Why D wrong: Fetishism = arousal with non-living objects.  Note: The source's labels for transvestism and transsexualism are swapped relative to standard DSM-5-TR/ICD-11 terminology (discomfort with one's anatomical sex/gender is properly gender dysphoria/transsexualism; sexual arousal from cross-dressing is properly transvestism/transvestic disorder). Answer keyed to the source, as the exam follows this book."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Fetishism is defined as sexual arousal or enjoyment with:","options":["An animal","Non-living objects like bras, gloves, panty, and shoes","A pre-puberty age child","Showing one's genitalia to a stranger"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fetishism = sexual arousal or enjoyment with non-living objects like bras, gloves, panty, and shoes.  Why A wrong: Arousal with an animal is zoophilia.  Why C wrong: Arousal with a pre-puberty child is paedophilia.  Why D wrong: Showing genitalia to a stranger is exhibitionism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Match these paraphilias correctly. (SATA — select all correctly matched pairs)","options":["Zoophilia — sexual arousal/enjoyment with an animal","Paedophilia — sexual arousal with a pre-puberty age child","Exhibitionism — showing one's genitalia to a stranger","Frotteurism — touching or rubbing of another's genital without consent","Voyeurism — sexual arousal by watching others naked or in a sexual situation"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five pairings match the source definitions exactly (zoophilia = animal; paedophilia = pre-puberty child; exhibitionism = showing genitalia to a stranger; frotteurism = touching/rubbing others' genital without consent; voyeurism = watching others naked/in sexual enjoyment situation like watching porn videos).  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is an accurate match to the text."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Sexual masochism is defined as:","options":["Sexual arousal by wearing opposite sex clothing","Sexual arousal through injury, pain, or violence on self by another","Female hyper sexuality","Male hyper sexuality"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sexual masochism = sexual arousal through injury, pain, or violence on self by another.  Why A wrong: That describes transsexualism.  Why C wrong: Female hyper sexuality = nymphomania.  Why D wrong: Male hyper sexuality = satyriasis."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Nymphomania refers to ______ and satyriasis refers to ______.","options":["Male hyper sexuality; female hyper sexuality","Female hyper sexuality; male hyper sexuality","Arousal with objects; arousal with animals","Watching others; showing genitalia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Nymphomania = female hyper sexuality; satyriasis = male hyper sexuality.  Why A wrong: Reverses the definitions.  Why C/D wrong: Describe fetishism/zoophilia and voyeurism/exhibitionism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Homosexuality between males is called ______ and between females is known as ______.","options":["Lesbians; gay","Gay; lesbians","Dual role; transvestism","Satyriasis; nymphomania"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Male-to-male enjoyment is called gay; female-to-female enjoyment is known as lesbians.  Why A wrong: Reverses the terms.  Why C/D wrong: Unrelated terms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the sexual dysfunction concept map, which of the following is listed under \"Lifestyle factors\"? (SATA)","options":["Obesity","Smoking","Alcohol","Diabetes","Bisphenol A"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Lifestyle factors listed are obesity, smoking, and alcohol.  Why D wrong: Diabetes is under disease factors.  Why E wrong: Bisphenol A is an environmental factor."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"Disease factors\" contributing to sexual dysfunction? (SATA)","options":["Diabetes","Cardiovascular disease","Chronic kidney disease","Cancer","Heavy metals"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Disease factors include diabetes, cardiovascular disease, chronic kidney disease, and cancer.  Why E wrong: Heavy metals are listed under environmental factors, not disease factors."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"Drug factors\" causing sexual dysfunction? (SATA)","options":["Antidepressants","Cardiovascular drugs","Antibacterials","Anticholinergic drugs","Phytoestrogens"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Drug factors include antidepressants, cardiovascular drugs, antibacterials, and anticholinergic drugs.  Why E wrong: Phytoestrogens are listed under environmental factors, not drug factors."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as \"Environmental factors\" for sexual dysfunction? (SATA)","options":["Bisphenol A","Polychlorinated biphenyl","Phytoestrogens","Heavy metals","Obesity"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Environmental factors include Bisphenol A, polychlorinated biphenyl, phytoestrogens, and heavy metals.  Why E wrong: Obesity is a lifestyle factor."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"A psychoactive substance is defined as:","options":["Any substance that interferes with mood, awareness, thoughts, feelings, or behaviour","A substance used only for anesthesia","A substance that has no effect on the CNS","A nutritional supplement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Psychoactive substance = any substance that interferes with mood, awareness, thoughts and feelings or behaviour.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the definition (psychoactive substances specifically alter mental function)."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the PASA glossary, \"intoxication\" and \"withdrawal\" respectively refer to:","options":["Symptoms after excessive intake; symptoms after partial or total abstinence","Symptoms after abstinence; symptoms after excessive intake","Physical dependence; psychological dependence","Tolerance; addiction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intoxication = group of symptoms appearing after excessive intake of a substance; withdrawal = group of symptoms appearing after partial or total abstinence of substance.  Why B wrong: Reverses the two definitions.  Why C/D wrong: Describe different glossary terms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the glossary, \"drug misuse\" differs from \"drug use\" in that drug misuse involves using a substance:","options":["For a non-intended purpose entirely","For its intended purpose but NOT in appropriate amount, frequency, strength, and manner","In a self-decided manner deviating from social/medical patterns","In the exactly appropriate amount and manner"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drug misuse = use of substance/drug for its intended purpose but NOT in appropriate amount, frequency, strength, and manner.  Why C wrong: That describes drug abuse (self-decided, deviating from approved patterns).  Why D wrong: That is drug use (appropriate amount, frequency, strength, manner).  Why A wrong: Misuse is still for the intended purpose, just improper amount/manner."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Addiction, as defined in the glossary, is:","options":["A bio-psychosocial disorder characterized by persistent use of drugs (including alcohol) despite substantial harm and adverse consequences","A temporary group of symptoms after abstinence","Reduced reaction to a drug at a given dose","The escalating effect of a drug at a given dose"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Addiction = a bio-psychosocial disorder characterized by persistent use of drugs (including alcohol) despite substantial harm and adverse consequences.  Why B wrong: That is withdrawal.  Why C wrong: That is tolerance.  Why D wrong: That is drug sensitization/reverse tolerance."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Drug sensitization (reverse tolerance) is defined as:","options":["Reduced reactions to a drug from repeated administration at a given dose","The escalating effect of a drug resulting from repeated administration at a given dose","Complete physical dependence","Group of symptoms after abstinence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drug sensitization or reverse tolerance = the escalating effect of a drug resulting from repeated administration at a given dose.  Why A wrong: That describes tolerance (the opposite).  Why C wrong: That is physical dependence.  Why D wrong: That is withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Tolerance is defined as:","options":["Escalating effect of a drug at a given dose","Reduced reactions to a drug resulting from repeated administration at a given dose","Persistent use despite harm","Symptoms after excessive intake"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tolerance = reduced reactions to a drug resulting from repeated administration at a given dose.  Why A wrong: That is drug sensitization/reverse tolerance.  Why C wrong: That is addiction.  Why D wrong: That is intoxication."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which substance is noted as the MOST COMMON type of substance use in India?","options":["Opioids","Cannabis","Alcohol","Cocaine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Alcohol (most common in India)\" among types of substance use.  Why A wrong: Among opioids, heroin is most common, but opioids overall are not the most common in India.  Why B/D wrong: Cannabis and cocaine are listed but not as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Among opioids, which is noted as the most common?","options":["Morphine","Codeine","Heroin","Methadone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states \"Opioids: heroin is most common.\"  Why A/B/D wrong: Morphine, codeine, and methadone are opioids but heroin is specified as most common."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as hallucinogens in the types of substance use? (SATA)","options":["LSD","Phencyclidine","Nicotine","Cannabis","Cocaine"],"correct":"A,B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists \"Hallucinogens - LSD, phencyclidine.\"  Why C wrong: Nicotine is a separate listed category.  Why D wrong: Cannabis is listed separately, not as a hallucinogen here.  Why E wrong: Cocaine is listed separately."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Alcoholism is best described as:","options":["Occasional social drinking","The most severe form of alcohol abuse, involving the inability to manage drinking habits","A single episode of intoxication","A form of drug misuse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alcoholism is the most severe form of alcohol abuse and involves the inability to manage drinking habits; also commonly referred to as alcohol use disorder.  Why A wrong: Social drinking is not alcoholism.  Why C wrong: A single intoxication episode is not alcoholism.  Why D wrong: It is described as the most severe alcohol abuse, not simple misuse."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Alcohol use disorder is organized into which three categories?","options":["Acute, subacute, chronic","Mild, moderate, and severe","Type I, Type II, Type III","Primary, secondary, tertiary"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohol use disorder is organized into three categories: mild, moderate, and severe.  Why A/C/D wrong: These classification schemes are not stated in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"One standard drink (1 peg) is equal to how much in India?","options":["15 ml","30 ml","45 ml","60 ml"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: One standard drink like 1 peg equals 30 ml in India.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifies 30 ml."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Regarding alcohol absorption and metabolism, the source states that:","options":["50% is absorbed from the stomach and 50% metabolised in the kidney","90% is absorbed from the small intestines and 90% metabolised in the liver by oxidation","100% is absorbed from the large intestine","It is not metabolised but excreted unchanged"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 90% alcohol is absorbed from small intestines and 90% metabolised in the liver by oxidation, so alcohol is a leading cause of liver disorders.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the stated 90%/90% figures and liver metabolism."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In alcohol metabolism, alcohol is metabolised by which enzyme, and then acetic acid is excreted by which organ?","options":["Aldehyde dehydrogenase; kidneys","Alcohol dehydrogenase; lungs","Cytochrome P450; skin","Monoamine oxidase; liver"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states alcohol is metabolised in/by aldehyde dehydrogenase, then acetic acid is excreted by kidneys.  Why B/C/D wrong: These enzyme/organ combinations are not what the source states for this step."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Disulfiram inhibits aldehyde dehydrogenase, leading to accumulation of aldehyde and an unpleasant reaction to alcohol intake known as:","options":["Antabuse reaction","Disulfiram Ethanol Reaction (DER)","Wernicke encephalopathy","Korsakoff syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aldehyde dehydrogenase is inhibited by disulfiram, leading to accumulation of aldehyde and an unpleasant reaction to alcohol intake known as Disulfiram Ethanol Reaction (DER).  Why A wrong: Antabuse is a brand name for disulfiram, not the reaction name.  Why C/D wrong: These are thiamine-deficiency syndromes, not the drug reaction."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following tools are used to assess alcoholic clients? (SATA) (NORCET, CNCI-2022)","options":["CAGE Questionnaire","Drug Abuse Screening Test (DAST)","Alcohol Use Disorder Identification Test (AUDIT)","Michigan Alcohol Screening Test (MAST)","Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CAGE Questionnaire, DAST, AUDIT, and MAST are the tools listed to assess alcoholic clients (NORCET, CNCI-2022).  Why E wrong: GCS assesses level of consciousness, not alcoholism, and is not listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the Blood Alcohol Concentration (BAC) table, a serum ethanol level of <25 mg/dl produces which symptom?","options":["Coma","Warmth & well-being","Cerebellar dysfunction","Respiratory depression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: BAC <25 mg/dl produces warmth & well-being.  Why A wrong: Coma occurs at 250–400 mg/dl.  Why C wrong: Cerebellar dysfunction occurs at 100–250 mg/dl.  Why D wrong: Respiratory depression/death occurs at >400 mg/dl."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the BAC table, euphoria, feeling of happiness, and relaxation occur at which serum ethanol level?","options":["<25 mg/dl","25–50 mg/dl","100–250 mg/dl",">400 mg/dl"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 25–50 mg/dl produces euphoria, feeling of happiness, relaxation.  Why A wrong: <25 mg/dl = warmth & well-being.  Why C wrong: 100–250 mg/dl = cerebellar dysfunction/ataxia/black out.  Why D wrong: >400 mg/dl = respiratory depression/death."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"At which BAC level does incoordination, decreased reaction time/reflexes, impaired judgement & driving ability, and unsteadiness occur?","options":["<25 mg/dl","25–50 mg/dl","50–100 mg/dl","250–400 mg/dl"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 50–100 mg/dl produces incoordination, decreased reaction time/reflexes, impaired judgement & driving ability, unsteadiness.  Why A/B wrong: These lower levels cause warmth and euphoria.  Why D wrong: 250–400 mg/dl causes coma."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"At a BAC of 100–250 mg/dl, the expected features include cerebellar dysfunction like ataxia, slurred speech, nystagmus, and:","options":["Black out","Coma","Death","Warmth"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: 100–250 mg/dl produces cerebellar dysfunction like ataxia, slurred speech, nystagmus, and black out.  Why B wrong: Coma is at 250–400 mg/dl.  Why C wrong: Death is at >400 mg/dl.  Why D wrong: Warmth is at <25 mg/dl."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the BAC table, coma occurs at which serum ethanol level, and respiratory depression/loss of protective reflexes & death at which?","options":["Coma at 250–400 mg/dl; death at >400 mg/dl","Coma at 100–250 mg/dl; death at 250–400 mg/dl","Coma at >400 mg/dl; death at 250–400 mg/dl","Coma at 50–100 mg/dl; death at 100–250 mg/dl"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Coma occurs at 250–400 mg/dl; respiratory depression, loss of protective reflexes & death occurs at >400 mg/dl.  Why B/C/D wrong: Misassign the coma/death thresholds."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Alcohol dependency may cause which syndrome and which vitamin deficiency?","options":["Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome and thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency","Guillain-Barré syndrome and vitamin B12 deficiency","Down syndrome and folate deficiency","Cushing syndrome and vitamin D deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohol dependency may cause Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (WKS) and thiamine vitamin deficiency.  Why B/C/D wrong: These syndromes/deficiencies are not linked to alcohol dependency in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the DSM-IV criteria for alcohol dependence, how many of the following criteria must be manifest during a 12-month period?","options":["At least 2 out of 5","At least 3 out of 7","At least 4 out of 6","At least 5 out of 8"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: According to DSM-IV criteria, at least 3 out of 7 criteria must be manifest during a 12-month period.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ratios do not match the DSM-IV criteria stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are among the 7 DSM-IV criteria for alcohol dependence? (SATA)","options":["Tolerance","Withdrawal symptoms or clinically defined alcohol withdrawal syndrome","Use in larger amounts or for longer periods than intended","Persistent desire or unsuccessful efforts to cut down on alcohol use","Use is continued despite knowledge of alcohol-related harm"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All five are among the 7 DSM-IV criteria (tolerance; withdrawal; larger amounts/longer periods; persistent desire/unsuccessful cut-down efforts; time spent obtaining/recovering; social/occupational/recreational pursuits given up; continued use despite harm).  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Korsakoff syndrome (KS) is an amnestic disorder caused by deficiency of which vitamin, typically associated with prolonged use of alcohol?","options":["Vitamin B12","Thiamine (vitamin B1)","Vitamin C","Folic acid"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Korsakoff syndrome is an amnestic disorder caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, typically associated with prolonged use of alcohol.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source specifically names thiamine (B1) deficiency."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Clinical features of Korsakoff syndrome include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Anterograde amnesia","Retrograde amnesia","Fixation amnesia","Confabulation","Lack of insight and apathy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical features of KS include anterograde, retrograde and fixation amnesia, confabulation, lack of insight, and apathy.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In Wernicke's encephalopathy, thiamine deficiency causes accumulation of which substance in the brain, causing excitatory damage to CNS neurons?","options":["GABA","Glutamate","Serotonin","Dopamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Due to thiamine deficiency, glutamate is accumulated in brain, which causes excitatory damage to CNS neurons.  Why A wrong: GABA is inhibitory; the source names glutamate accumulation.  Why C/D wrong: Not named as the accumulated substance in WE."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Clinical features of Wernicke's encephalopathy include which of the following? (SATA)","options":["Amnesia and memory impairment","Depression","Retinal haemorrhage","Pupillary changes","Impaired vision & hearing"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: WE clinical features include amnesia, memory impairment, depression, retinal haemorrhage, pupillary changes, and impaired vision & hearing.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome (AWS), symptoms start at around what time after the last drink?","options":["1 hour","3 hours","6 hours","12 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: AWS symptoms start at around 6 hours after the last drink.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies ~6 hours."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In AWS, peak incidence of alcohol withdrawal seizures occurs at what time, and peak incidence of delirium tremens at what time?","options":["Seizures at 6–12 hours; DT at 24–36 hours","Seizures at 24–36 hours; DT at 48–72 hours","Seizures at 48–72 hours; DT at 24–36 hours","Seizures at 12–24 hours; DT at 48 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Peak incidence of alcohol withdrawal seizures occurs at 24–36 hours and peak incidence of delirium tremens at 48–72 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: Misassign the timing of seizures versus DT."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which assessment scale/tool is used specifically for alcohol withdrawal syndrome?","options":["CIWA-Ar (Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for Alcohol)","MMSE","Glasgow Coma Scale","AUDIT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for Alcohol scale (CIWA-Ar) is used for alcohol withdrawal syndrome.  Why B/C wrong: MMSE and GCS assess cognition/consciousness, not withdrawal.  Why D wrong: AUDIT screens for alcohol use disorder, not withdrawal severity."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"The MOST COMMON and successful drug for the treatment of AWS is:","options":["Chlordiazepoxide (or diazepam group of benzodiazepines)","Disulfiram","Naltrexone","Haloperidol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common and successful drug for treatment of AWS is chlordiazepoxide or diazepam group of benzodiazepines; the drug of choice for alcohol withdrawal syndrome is chlordiazepoxide (NHM, AIIMS, DSSSB-2019).  Why B wrong: Disulfiram is used for aversion/deterrent therapy, not withdrawal.  Why C/D wrong: Not named as the AWS treatment of choice in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"AWS leads to an imbalance between which two neurotransmitters?","options":["Serotonin and dopamine","Excitatory glutamate and inhibitory GABA","Noradrenaline and acetylcholine","Histamine and glycine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: AWS leads to an imbalance between excitatory glutamate and inhibitory GABA neurotransmitters.  Why A/C/D wrong: Not the neurotransmitter pair named in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are severe and life-threatening symptoms of AWS? (SATA)","options":["Alcoholic hallucinosis","Withdrawal seizures","Delirium tremens","Mild insomnia","Trembling and palpitations"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Severe and life-threatening symptoms of AWS include alcoholic hallucinosis, withdrawal seizures, and delirium tremens.  Why D/E wrong: Insomnia, trembling, palpitations, and anxiety are listed as MILD symptoms of AWS, not severe."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the Expected Clinical Features of AWS table, anorexia, diaphoresis, GI upset, insomnia, headache (hangover), mild anxiety, palpitations, and tremulousness appear at what time after cessation?","options":["6–12 hours","12–24 hours","48 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: These early symptoms appear 6–12 hours after cessation.  Why B wrong: 12–24 hours is for alcoholic hallucinosis.  Why C wrong: 48 hours is for withdrawal seizures.  Why D wrong: 48–72 hours is for delirium tremens."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the AWS table, alcoholic hallucinosis (auditory, tactile, or visual hallucinations) appears at what time after cessation?","options":["6–12 hours","12–24 hours","48 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Alcoholic hallucinosis (auditory, tactile, or visual hallucinations) appears at 12–24 hours.  Why A wrong: 6–12 hours is for early symptoms.  Why C wrong: 48 hours is for withdrawal seizures.  Why D wrong: 48–72 hours is for delirium tremens."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the AWS table, withdrawal seizures (Rum fits) presenting as generalized tonic-clonic seizures appear at what time?","options":["6–12 hours","12–24 hours","48 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Withdrawal seizures (Rum fits), generalized tonic clonic seizures, appear at 48 hours.  Why A/B wrong: These are for earlier symptoms/hallucinosis.  Why D wrong: 48–72 hours is for delirium tremens."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) with agitation, diaphoresis, disorientation, visual hallucinations, hypertension, low-grade fever, and tachycardia appears at what time after cessation?","options":["6–12 hours","12–24 hours","48 hours","48–72 hours"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohol withdrawal delirium (delirium tremens) appears at 48–72 hours.  Why A/B/C wrong: These earlier windows correspond to other clinical features."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, the legally accepted blood alcohol concentration for driving is 0.08% (80 mg/dl) in the US and what value in India? (NORCET-2022, NORCET-5 Prelims-2023)","options":["0.08% (80 mg/dl)","0.05% (50 mg/dl)","0.03% (30 mg/dl)","0.16% (160 mg/dl)"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: 80 mg/dl (0.08%) is legally accepted for driving in the US, and 30 mg/dl (0.03%) is legally accepted for driving in India.  Why A wrong: 0.08% is the US limit, not India's.  Why B/D wrong: Not the stated Indian limit."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, blackouts from large quantities of rapidly consumed alcohol tend to occur when the BAC reaches about:","options":["0.03 percent","0.08 percent","0.16 percent","0.25 percent"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Blackouts tend to occur when BAC reaches about 0.16 percent.  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies about 0.16 percent."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the management of alcohol abuse disorders, the drug of choice for detoxification (alcohol withdrawal symptoms) is Chlordiazepoxide, with the second choice being:","options":["IV Lorazepam","Disulfiram","Naltrexone","Haloperidol"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chlordiazepoxide is the drug of choice for alcohol withdrawal symptoms; second choice is IV Lorazepam.  Why B wrong: Disulfiram is the deterrent/aversion agent.  Why C/D wrong: Not named as the second choice."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the management of alcohol abuse, the fluid of choice for an alcoholic patient is:","options":["Normal saline alone","Ringer's lactate","D5% with Vitamin B1 (thiamine)","D10% alone"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Fluid of choice for alcoholic patient is D5% with Vitamin B1 (thiamine); fluid replacement corrects electrolyte imbalance and dehydration.  Why A/B/D wrong: Not the specified fluid of choice."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"The deterrent agent (aversion classical conditioning therapy) and drug of choice for aversion therapy in alcohol abuse is:","options":["Chlordiazepoxide","Disulfiram (also known as antabuse)","Lorazepam","Thiamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The DOC for aversion therapy is Disulfiram, also known as antabuse.  Why A/C wrong: These are used for withdrawal/detoxification.  Why D wrong: Thiamine treats deficiency, not aversion therapy."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the disulfiram-ethanol reaction flow chart, ethanol is converted to acetaldehyde by alcohol dehydrogenase; acetaldehyde is then converted to harmless acetate by which enzyme (which disulfiram blocks)?","options":["Alcohol dehydrogenase","Acetaldehyde dehydrogenase","Monoamine oxidase","Cytochrome P450"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acetaldehyde is converted to acetate (harmless) by acetaldehyde dehydrogenase; disulfiram blocks this enzyme, causing excess acetaldehyde and hangover.  Why A wrong: Alcohol dehydrogenase converts ethanol to acetaldehyde (earlier step).  Why C/D wrong: Not the enzyme in this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In opioid terminology, what is the difference between an opiate and an opioid?","options":["Opiate is synthetic; opioid is natural","Opiate is extracted from the exudates of poppy; opioid is a natural or synthetic drug that binds to opioid receptors producing an agonist effect","They are exactly the same","Opiate binds receptors; opioid does not"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Opiate = drug extracted from the exudates of poppy; opioid = natural or synthetic drug that binds to opioid receptors producing agonist effect.  Why A wrong: Reverses natural/synthetic incorrectly.  Why C wrong: They are distinguished in the source.  Why D wrong: Both act on receptors, but the definitions differ as stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Opium is an extract from which plant?","options":["Cannabis sativa","Papaver somniferum (poppy seeds)","Erythroxylum coca","Datura stramonium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Opium = extract from poppy seeds 'Papaver somniferum'.  Why A wrong: Cannabis sativa is the source of cannabis, not opium.  Why C wrong: Coca is the source of cocaine.  Why D wrong: Datura is not the opium source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are naturally occurring opioids? (SATA)","options":["Morphine","Codeine","Thebaine","Heroin","Methadone"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Naturally occurring opioids are morphine, codeine, and thebaine.  Why D wrong: Heroin is a semisynthetic opioid.  Why E wrong: Methadone is a synthetic opioid."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Heroin (Smack, diacetylmorphine), dihydromorphine, and dihydrocodeine are classified as which type of opioid?","options":["Naturally occurring opioids","Semisynthetic opioids","Synthetic opioids","Endogenous opioid peptides"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Semisynthetic opioids include heroin (Smack, diacetylmorphine), dihydromorphine, and dihydrocodeine.  Why A wrong: Naturally opioids = morphine, codeine, thebaine.  Why C wrong: Synthetic opioids = pethidine, methadone, etc.  Why D wrong: Endogenous peptides = endorphins, enkephalins."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are synthetic opioids? (SATA)","options":["Pethidine","Methadone","Buprenorphine","Pentazocine","Dextropropoxyphene"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Synthetic opioids include pethidine, methadone, buprenorphine, pentazocine, and dextropropoxyphene.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are endogenous (natural) opioid peptides present in the body? (SATA)","options":["Endorphins","Leu-enkephalins","Met-enkephalin","Morphine","Heroin"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Endogenous/natural opioid peptides present in the body are endorphins, leu-enkephalins, and met-enkephalin.  Why D/E wrong: Morphine and heroin are exogenous opioids, not endogenous peptides."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"The most commonly abused opioid is:","options":["Morphine","Codeine","Heroin (Diacetylmorphine, Smack)","Pethidine"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly abused opioid is heroin (diacetylmorphine, smack).  Why A/B/D wrong: These are opioids but not identified as most commonly abused."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"An eating disorder is best defined as:","options":["A neurological movement disorder","A mental disorder defined by abnormal eating behaviors that negatively affect a person's physical or mental health","A gastrointestinal infection","A metabolic thyroid condition"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: An eating disorder is a mental disorder defined by abnormal eating behaviors that negatively affect physical or mental health.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the source definition."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"A patient who eats a large amount of food in a short period of time (binging) then tries to rid themselves of the food (purging) has which eating disorder?","options":["Binge eating disorder","Anorexia nervosa","Bulimia nervosa","Pica"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bulimia nervosa = the patient eats a large quantity (binging) then tries to rid themselves of the food (purging).  Why A wrong: Binge eating disorder is eating a large amount in a short time WITHOUT the described purging.  Why B wrong: Anorexia nervosa involves intense fear of weight gain and food restriction.  Why D wrong: Pica is eating non-food items."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Match the eating disorder — a patient who has an intense fear of gaining weight and restricts food or over-exercises has:","options":["Binge eating disorder","Anorexia nervosa","Bulimia nervosa","Rumination syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Anorexia nervosa = the patient has an intense fear of gaining weight and restricts food or over-exercises to manage this fear.  Why A wrong: Binge eating is eating large amounts in a short time.  Why C wrong: Bulimia involves binging then purging.  Why D wrong: Rumination is regurgitation of undigested food."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Pica and rumination syndrome are respectively defined as:","options":["Eating non-food items; regurgitating undigested or minimally digested food","Regurgitating food; eating non-food items","Binging then purging; restricting food","Fear of weight gain; feelings of guilt"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Pica = the patient eats non-food items; rumination syndrome = the patient regurgitates undigested or minimally digested food.  Why B wrong: Reverses the two definitions.  Why C/D wrong: Describe bulimia/anorexia features."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which psychiatric condition is noted to be commonly present in a client with an eating disorder?","options":["Schizophrenia","OCD (Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder)","Bipolar mania","Dementia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: OCD is commonly present in a client with eating disorder; anxiety disorders, depression, and substance abuse are also common among people with eating disorders.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not named as commonly present with eating disorders in the source."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Russell's sign, a nondescript dorsal scar (calluses, abrasions) on the knuckles/back of the hands caused by repeated contact of the incisor teeth during self-induced vomiting, is associated with which condition?","options":["Anorexia nervosa","Bulimia nervosa","Pica","Binge eating disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Russell's sign is caused by repeated contact of the incisor teeth during self-induced vomiting, associated with bulimia nervosa.  Why A wrong: Anorexia involves restriction, not the self-induced vomiting pattern producing Russell's sign.  Why C/D wrong: Not associated with Russell's sign."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the Anorexia vs Bulimia comparison, weight in anorexia nervosa versus bulimia nervosa is respectively:","options":["Significantly underweight; normal weight or overweight","Normal weight; underweight","Overweight; underweight","Both significantly underweight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In anorexia nervosa the patient is significantly underweight; in bulimia nervosa the patient is normal weight or overweight.  Why B/C/D wrong: Misstate the weight profiles."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following symptoms are listed specifically for anorexia nervosa? (SATA)","options":["Menstrual disturbances (amenorrhoea)","Lanugo (fine hair all over body)","Low blood pressure","Hair loss or dry hair","Weakness and nail biting"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Anorexia nervosa symptoms listed include menstrual disturbances (amenorrhoea), weakness and nail biting, nutritional deficiencies, low blood pressure, heart and kidney problem, hair loss/dry hair, and lanugo (fine hair all over body).  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following symptoms are listed for bulimia nervosa? (SATA)","options":["Weakness, fatigue","Dehydration","Mouth and throat problems such as sore throat, sores in mouth, ulcers, dental problems","Lanugo","Amenorrhoea"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Bulimia nervosa symptoms listed are weakness/fatigue, dehydration, and mouth/throat problems (sore throat, sores, ulcers, dental problems).  Why D wrong: Lanugo is an anorexia nervosa symptom.  Why E wrong: Amenorrhoea is listed under anorexia nervosa."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"In the Anorexia vs Bulimia comparison, which emotional symptom is specific to bulimia nervosa (and not listed for anorexia)?","options":["Anxiety and depression","Fear of gaining weight","Feelings of guilt / self-destructive behaviour","Obsessive-compulsive behaviour"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bulimia nervosa emotional symptoms include anxiety and depression, feelings of guilt, and self-destructive behaviour; feelings of guilt/self-destructive behaviour are the ones distinct from the anorexia list.  Why A wrong: Anxiety and depression appear in both columns.  Why B/D wrong: Fear of gaining weight and obsessive-compulsive behaviour are anorexia nervosa emotional symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Regarding relationships, anorexia nervosa patients tend to be withdrawn and may avoid others when eating, whereas bulimia nervosa patients:","options":["Are always completely isolated","May be withdrawn but may be able to develop relationships with others","Never form any relationships","Only interact during meals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In bulimia, the patient may be withdrawn but may be able to develop relationship with others; in anorexia, withdrawn and may avoid others when eating.  Why A/C/D wrong: Overstate isolation beyond what the source describes."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"A psychiatric emergency is best defined as a condition where the patient's disturbances of thought, affect, and psychomotor activity lead to:","options":["A threat to their own existence (suicide) or a threat to people in the environment","Only mild anxiety","A gradual chronic decline over years","Improved insight and judgement"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A psychiatric emergency is a condition where disturbances of thought, affect and psychomotor activity lead to a threat to the patient's existence (suicide), or threat to people in the environment; it presents in acute form needing immediate attention.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the acute, threatening nature of a psychiatric emergency."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as objectives of managing psychiatric emergencies? (SATA)","options":["To safeguard the life of the patient","To bring down the anxiety of family members","To enhance emotional security of others in the environment","To permanently cure the underlying disorder in one visit","To ignore other patients"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Objectives are to safeguard the life of the patient, bring down family members' anxiety, and enhance emotional security of others in the environment.  Why D/E wrong: Not listed as objectives; the source instead emphasizes protecting other patients and calm management."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are types of psychiatric emergencies? (SATA)","options":["Suicide or deliberate self harm","Catatonic stupor and panic attacks","Withdrawal symptoms of drug dependence","Alcohol or drug overdose","Severe depression (suicidal/homicidal tendencies, agitation or stupor)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Types of psychiatric emergencies include suicide/deliberate self harm, violence/excitement, catatonic stupor and panic attacks, withdrawal symptoms of drug dependence, alcohol/drug overdose, hysterical attacks, epilepsy/status epilepticus, and severe depression.  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as general guidelines to manage psychiatric emergencies? (SATA)","options":["Handle with utmost tact and speech so well-being of other patients is not affected","Act in a calm and coordinated manner to prevent other clients from getting anxious","Shift the client as early as possible to a room where they can be safeguarded against injury","Ensure other clients are reassured and routine activities proceed normally","Allow the disturbance to escalate freely"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: These four guidelines are explicitly listed, along with familiarity with both psychiatric and medical emergencies.  Why E wrong: Contradicts the calm, safe management approach described."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Suicide is defined as:","options":["The act of intentionally causing one's own death","Accidental death from illness","Deliberate harm to another person","A normal grief reaction"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Suicide is the act of intentionally causing one's own death.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not match the definition of suicide."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"The MOST COMMON method of suicide in India is ______, and the second most common is ______.","options":["Poisoning; hanging","Hanging; poisoning of pesticides","Firearms; drowning","Drug overdose; hanging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Most common method of suicide in India is hanging; second most common is poisoning of pesticides.  Why A wrong: Reverses hanging and poisoning.  Why C/D wrong: Not the methods stated."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"The MOST COMMON cause of suicide is:","options":["Financial loss","Depression","Schizophrenia","Substance abuse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of suicide is depression.  Why A/C/D wrong: While risk factors exist, depression is specifically named as the most common cause."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which therapies are noted as successful management therapies for depression (in the context of suicide)? (SATA)","options":["ECT (electroconvulsive therapy)","Anti-depressants","Psychotherapy","Aversion therapy","Insulin coma therapy"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: ECT, anti-depressants, and psychotherapy are the successful management therapies for depression.  Why D wrong: Aversion therapy is used for alcohol abuse, not depression here.  Why E wrong: Insulin coma therapy is not mentioned."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Regarding suicide risk, the source states that those who have previously attempted suicide are:","options":["At lower risk for future attempts","At a higher risk for future attempts","At no different risk than the general population","Immune from future attempts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Those who have previously attempted suicide are at a higher risk for future attempts.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the stated increased risk."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are listed as risk factors for suicide? (SATA)","options":["Depression and bipolar disorder","Autism spectrum disorders and schizophrenia","Personality disorders and anxiety disorders","Substance abuse including alcoholism and benzodiazepine use/withdrawal","Chronic fatigue syndrome"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Risk factors include mental disorders (depression, bipolar disorder, autism spectrum disorders, schizophrenia, personality disorders, anxiety disorders), physical disorders such as chronic fatigue syndrome, and substance abuse (alcoholism and use/withdrawal from benzodiazepines).  Why A/B/C/D/E all correct: Each is explicitly listed as a risk factor."},{"section":"Batch 006 · Sleep stages, sleep disorders…","question":"Which of the following are appropriate nursing interventions/suicidal prevention strategies for a client at high risk of suicidal ideation? (SATA)","options":["Initiate and maintain close observation of the client","Use therapeutic techniques like open-ended questions, non-judgemental approach, and restating","Place the client's room near the nurse's station and within view of the staff","Leave the client alone in an isolated room far from staff","Allow unrestricted access to potentially harmful objects"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Close observation should be initiated for high-risk clients; maintain close observation; use therapeutic techniques (open-ended questions, non-judgemental, restating); the client's room should be near the nurse's station and within view of staff.  Why D/E wrong: Directly contradict the safe-observation strategy — isolation and access to harmful objects increase risk."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Where should the bed of a high-risk suicidal client be allotted in the ward?","options":["In a private room away from staff","Near to the exit of the ward or near the door, elevators and stairs","In the farthest corner from the nursing station","In an isolated single room with the door locked"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the client's bed should be allotted near to the exit of the ward or near to the door, elevators and stairs, allowing close observation.  Why A wrong: Private rooms must NOT be assigned to high-risk clients.  Why C wrong: A far corner reduces observation, increasing suicide risk.  Why D wrong: Locking the door isolates the client and prevents rapid intervention."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A nurse is caring for a high-risk suicidal client. Which action is contraindicated?","options":["Making frequent, irregular rounds especially at night","Ensuring oral medications are actually ingested","Assigning the client to a private room","Keeping the environment free of dangerous or harmful equipment"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source explicitly says \"Do not assign private room to the high-risk clients.\"  Why A wrong: Frequent, irregular rounds (especially at night) are recommended.  Why B wrong: Close observation to ensure oral medications are ingested is recommended.  Why D wrong: The room/environment should be free from dangerous or harmful equipment or objects."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"When observing a suicidal client who takes oral medication, the nurse must specifically ensure that:","options":["The client crushes all tablets","The medication is actually ingested and not hidden by the client","The client takes medication only at night","Family members administer the drugs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Close observation is needed to confirm that oral medication given is ingested and not concealed by the client.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated precaution; the concern is confirming ingestion to prevent hoarding for overdose."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"If a suicidal client's suicidal tendency appears to have decreased, within what time frame may the client re-attempt suicide, requiring continued close observation?","options":["Next 6–12 hours","Next 24–48 hours","Next 72–96 hours","Next 1 week"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source states that if suicidal tendency decreases, observe the client closely because the client may re-attempt suicide within the next 24–48 hours.  Why A/C/D wrong: These time frames are not what the source specifies."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which stimulating environment is recommended by the source for a suicidal client?","options":["Complete silence and isolation","Music, play and dancing therapy","Dim lighting and restricted movement","Prolonged bed rest"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source recommends providing a stimulant environment like music, play and dancing therapy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not recommended; isolation and restriction increase risk."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The SAD PERSONS scale is primarily used for:","options":["Assessing dementia severity","Suicide risk assessment and depression in clinical settings","Measuring alcohol withdrawal","Screening for schizophrenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The SAD PERSONS scale is used for suicide risk assessment and depression in a clinical setting.  Why A/C/D wrong: Those are assessed by other scales (e.g., GDS, CIWA-Ar, PANSS)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Who first developed the SAD PERSONS scale?","options":["Patterson","Gerald A. Juhnke","Ivan Pavlov","B.F. Skinner"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The SAD PERSONS scale was first developed by Patterson.  Why B wrong: Gerald A. Juhnke developed the Adapted-SAD PERSONS Scale.  Why C/D wrong: Pavlov and Skinner are associated with conditioning theories, not this scale."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Adapted-SAD PERSONS Scale was developed by:","options":["Patterson","Gerald A. Juhnke","J. Meduna","Bini & Cerletti"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Adapted-SAD PERSONS Scale was developed by Gerald A. Juhnke.  Why A wrong: Patterson developed the original SAD PERSONS scale.  Why C/D wrong: These are associated with convulsive therapy/ECT, not the scale."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In the SAD PERSONS scale, the letter \"S\" (Sex) scores 1 point for which gender?","options":["Female","Male","Both equally","Transgender only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In the scale, \"S – SEX\" scores 1 point for Male.  Why A/C/D wrong: The criterion assigns the point to male sex."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In the SAD PERSONS scale, which age ranges score 1 point under \"A\" (Age)?","options":["20–45 years","<20 years and >45 years","Only >65 years","Only <10 years"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Age criterion scores 1 point for <20 years and >45 years.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ranges do not match the scale criterion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In the SAD PERSONS scale, which items each carry a score of 2 (the highest weight)?","options":["Sex, Age, Excessive drug use","Depression (major), Rationally loss (psychosis/severe depression), Organised plan (determined suicide plan)","Separated, No supports, Sickness","Psychiatry history, Previous attempts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Depression (major, e.g. depressed mood) = 2, Rationally loss (psychosis: severe depression) = 2, and Organised plan (determined suicide plan) = 2.  Why A/C/D wrong: Sex, Age, Excessive drug use, Separated, No supports, Sickness, Psychiatry history, and Previous attempts each score 1."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In the SAD PERSONS scale, \"P\" stands for Psychiatry history and scores 1 point for:","options":["Chronic illness","Previous attempts","Loss of spouse","Alcohol abuse"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"P – Psychiatry history\" scores 1 for previous attempts.  Why A wrong: Chronic illness is the \"S – Sickness\" criterion.  Why C wrong: Loss of spouse is the \"S – Separated\" criterion.  Why D wrong: Alcohol/drug abuse is the \"E – Excessive drug use\" criterion."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In the SAD PERSONS scale, \"N – No supports\" (score 1) refers to a client who:","options":["Has a determined suicide plan","Has no community back-up and is generally isolated","Is separated from spouse","Has severe depression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"N – No supports\" describes no community back-up, generally isolated (score 1).  Why A wrong: Determined plan is \"O – Organised plan.\"  Why C wrong: Separation is \"S – Separated.\"  Why D wrong: Severe depression relates to \"R – Rationally loss.\""},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following are listed as risk factors of suicide? (Select all that apply)","options":["Previous suicide attempt","Serious physical illness / chronic pain","History of childhood trauma","Inflexible thinking","Family history of mental illness and suicide"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All listed items appear in the \"Risk Factors of Suicide\" box, along with male gender, mental illness, substance abuse, shame/despair, aggression/impulsivity, triggering life events, suicide exposure, access to lethal means, and genes.  Why none wrong: Every option is directly from the source list."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following is listed as a warning sign of suicide?","options":["Talking about being a burden","Increased appetite and weight gain","Improved sleep pattern","Decreased substance use"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: \"Talking about being a burden\" is listed among warning signs of suicide.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source lists sleeping too little or too much and increased substance use as warning signs; the distractors are the opposite."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following are warning signs of suicide per the source? (Select all that apply)","options":["Being isolated","Looking for a way to access lethal means","Expressing hopelessness","Extreme mood swings","Talking or posting about wanting to die"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: All are listed warning signs, along with increased anxiety, feeling trapped/unbearable pain, increased substance use, increased anger or rage, abnormal sleep, and making plans for suicide.  Why none wrong: Every option is from the source list."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to the NCRB Report-2021, how many people commit suicide every year in India?","options":["More than 50,000","More than 1,00,000","More than 5,00,000","Exactly 10,000"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Every year, more than 1,00,000 people commit suicide in the country.  Why A/C/D wrong: These figures do not match the report."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"As per the NCRB 2021 report, what was the male-to-female ratio of suicide victims?","options":["50 : 50","72.5 : 27.4","60 : 40","45 : 55"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In 2021 the male-to-female ratio of suicide victims was 72.5 : 27.4.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ratios do not match the NCRB data."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In 2021, of the 1,64,033 persons who died by suicide, how many were men and how many were women?","options":["45,026 men and 1,18,970 women","1,18,970 men and 45,026 women","1,00,000 men and 64,033 women","82,000 men and 82,033 women"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 1,18,970 were men and 45,026 were women in 2021.  Why A wrong: The figures are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These breakdowns are not from the report."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB 2021, the total of 1,64,033 suicides in India showed what percentage increase compared to 2020?","options":["3.3%","6.2%","7.2%","12.0%"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The total showed an increase of 7.2% in comparison to 2020.  Why B wrong: The rate of suicides increased by 6.2% during 2021 over 2020 (a separate figure).  Why A wrong: 3.3% is the 2022 rate rise (August 2022 report).  Why D wrong: 12.0 was the all-India suicide rate in 2021, not the percentage increase."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"How is the suicide mortality rate defined in the NCRB report?","options":["Number of suicide deaths in a year per 1,000 population","Number of suicide deaths in a year per 100,000 population","Percentage of total deaths due to suicide","Number of attempts per 10,000 population"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suicide mortality rate is the number of suicide deaths in a year per 100,000 populations.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not match the defined denominator."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The all-India suicide rate in 2021 was 12 per one lakh people. This was described as:","options":["The lowest rate since 1967","The highest rate of suicide-related deaths since 1967 in India","Equal to the 2020 rate","A rate unchanged since 1990"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The rate of 12 in 2021 was the highest rate of suicide-related deaths since 1967 in India.  Why A/C/D wrong: The source describes it as the highest since 1967, not the lowest or unchanged."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB 2021, which four metropolitan cities reported the higher number of suicides in India?","options":["Delhi, Chennai, Bengaluru and Mumbai","Delhi, Kolkata, Pune and Hyderabad","Mumbai, Kolkata, Chennai and Ahmedabad","Delhi, Jaipur, Lucknow and Patna"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The four metros were Delhi City (2,760), Chennai (2,699), Bengaluru (2,292) and Mumbai (1,436).  Why B/C/D wrong: These city groupings do not match the report."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"As per NCRB 2021, which was the major (leading) cause of suicide?","options":["Illness","Family Problems / domestic violence (other than marriage-related issues)","Unemployment","Bankruptcy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: 'Family Problems/domestic violence (other than marriage-related issues)' was the major cause of suicide, accounting for 33.2% of total suicides.  Why A wrong: Illness was the second cause, accounting for 18.6%.  Why C/D wrong: These were contributing causes but not the leading one."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB 2021, Family Problems/domestic violence and Illness accounted for what percentages of total suicides, respectively?","options":["18.6% and 33.2%","33.2% and 18.6%","24.4% and 12.0%","45.7% and 39.7%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Family Problems/domestic violence accounted for 33.2% and Illness 18.6% of total suicides in 2021.  Why A wrong: The values are reversed.  Why C/D wrong: These percentages relate to other statistics (cybercrime rise; crimes against children)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB 2021, which Union Territory (most populous UT) reported the highest number of suicides, and how many?","options":["Puducherry (504)","Delhi (2,840)","A & N Islands (39.7)","Chandigarh (1,436)"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delhi, the most-populous UT, reported the highest number of suicides (2,840), followed by Puducherry (504).  Why A wrong: Puducherry (504) was second.  Why C wrong: 39.7 was the highest rate (A & N Islands), not a count.  Why D wrong: Not stated for Chandigarh."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB 2021, which region reported the highest RATE of suicide (39.7)?","options":["Delhi","A & N Islands","Maharashtra","Puducherry"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A & N Islands reported the highest rate of suicide (39.7) in 2021, while the all-India rate was 12.0.  Why A/C/D wrong: Delhi had the highest UT count; Maharashtra had the highest absolute number; Puducherry was second among UTs by count."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB 2021, which state reported the majority (highest number) of suicides?","options":["Tamil Nadu (18,925)","Maharashtra (22,207)","Madhya Pradesh (14,965)","Karnataka"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Maharashtra reported the majority of suicides (22,207), followed by Tamil Nadu (18,925) and Madhya Pradesh (14,965).  Why A/C wrong: Tamil Nadu and Madhya Pradesh were second and third.  Why D wrong: A specific figure for Karnataka is not given here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In which year was the National Crime Records Bureau (NCRB) established?","options":["1971","1986","1990","2001"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NCRB was established in 1986.  Why A/C/D wrong: These years are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The NCRB is part of which ministry and is headquartered where?","options":["Ministry of Health; Mumbai","Ministry of Home Affairs; New Delhi","Ministry of Law; Kolkata","Ministry of Defence; New Delhi"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NCRB is part of the Ministry of Home & Affairs and is headquartered in New Delhi.  Why A/C/D wrong: These ministries/locations are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"How many divisions does the NCRB have?","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: NCRB has four divisions: Crime and Criminal Tracking Network and Systems (CCTNS), Crime Statistics, Finger Prints, and Training.  Why A/B/D wrong: The number is four."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to the NCRB August 2022 report, which city emerged as the safest city in India for the third consecutive year?","options":["Pune","Hyderabad","Kolkata","Mumbai"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Kolkata emerged as the safest city in India for the third consecutive year, with the least cognisable offences per lakh population.  Why A/B wrong: Pune (Maharashtra) and Hyderabad (Telangana) secured second and third positions.  Why D wrong: Mumbai is not listed as safest here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Per the NCRB August 2022 report, the crime rate per 1,00,000 population dropped from 445.9 in 2021 to what value in 2022?","options":["422.2","400.0","465.9","12.0"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The crime rate per 1,00,000 population dropped from 445.9 in 2021 to 422.2 in 2022.  Why B/C/D wrong: These values are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB August 2022, cybercrime reporting surged by what percentage, totalling 65,893 cases?","options":["8.7%","24.4%","4.2%","16.7%"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Cybercrime reporting surged by 24.4%, totalling 65,893 cases (up from 52,974 in 2021).  Why A wrong: 8.7% was the rise in crimes against children.  Why C wrong: 4.2% was the surge in suicides in 2022.  Why D wrong: 16.7% was the rise in animal/reptile/insect bite cases."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"As per NCRB August 2022, India witnessed a significant surge in suicides in 2022, totaling over how many cases, and the suicide rate rose by:","options":["Over 1.7 lakh; rose by 3.3%","Over 1.0 lakh; rose by 7.2%","Over 2.0 lakh; rose by 4.2%","Over 50,000; rose by 6.2%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In 2022 India witnessed a surge in suicides totaling over 1.7 lakh cases (a 4.2% increase over 2021), and the suicide rate rose by 3.3%.  Why B/C/D wrong: These figures mix up other statistics from the report."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to NCRB August 2022, which profession-wise group constituted the largest portion (26.4%) of total suicide victims and remained the largest group?","options":["Students","Unemployed persons","Daily wage earners","Agricultural workers"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Daily wage earners constituted 26.4% of total suicides and remained the largest profession-wise group among suicide victims.  Why A wrong: Over 12,000 of the suicides were students.  Why B wrong: Unemployed persons comprised 9.2%.  Why D wrong: Agricultural workers and farmers reported the highest, but daily wage earners were the largest single group (26.4%)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Per NCRB August 2022, which state has the highest number/rate of crimes under the NDPS Act?","options":["Maharashtra","Punjab","Rajasthan","Kerala"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Punjab has the highest number of crimes rate under the NDPS Act.  Why A/C/D wrong: These states are not identified as highest under NDPS Act."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"When managing a psychiatric emergency in a suicidal client, which nursing action is appropriate?","options":["Leave the drug tray within reach of the patient","Allow the patient to bolt the door from inside","Ensure the patient is never left alone and is accompanied to the bathroom","Take suicidal threats lightly if the client seems calm"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the patient should never be left alone and someone should accompany them to the bathroom.  Why A wrong: Do not leave the drug tray within reach of the patient.  Why B wrong: Do not allow the patient to bolt the door from inside.  Why D wrong: Take all suicidal threats or attempts seriously."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"During intervention for a psychiatric emergency, the nurse should listen attentively with open-ended questions EXCEPT for clients with which condition?","options":["Depression","Mania","Anxiety","PTSD"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source says to listen attentively with open-ended questions except in a client of mania (open-ended questions can escalate a manic client).  Why A/C/D wrong: The exception noted is specifically mania."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which communication techniques are recommended during a psychiatric emergency intervention? (Select all that apply)","options":["Do not interrupt","Acknowledge understanding of the pain and distress","Use silence and restating","Ask the client to stop crying","Provide accurate and responsible information"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Do not interrupt, acknowledge pain/distress, use silence and restating, and provide accurate/responsible (non-judgemental) information are all recommended.  Why D wrong: The source specifically says \"Do not ask them to stop crying.\""},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"For treatment of the life-threatening physical problem in a psychiatric emergency, which medication class is used to reduce anxiety?","options":["Antipsychotics","Benzodiazepines","SSRIs","Beta-blockers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The source lists benzodiazepines to reduce anxiety (along with group therapy & counselling, and ECT & other).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the class named for acute anxiety reduction here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"ECT (Electro-Convulsive Therapy) is also known as:","options":["Electric shock therapy (EST)","Deep brain stimulation","Transcranial magnetic therapy","Insulin coma therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ECT is also known as electric shock therapy (EST).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different neuromodulation/historical therapies."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"ECT therapy was brought (introduced) by whom, and in which year?","options":["J. Meduna, 1930","Bini & Cerletti, 1938","Ivan Pavlov, 1890","B.F. Skinner, 1948"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This therapy was brought by Bini & Cerletti in 1938.  Why A wrong: J. Meduna is the \"father of convulsive therapy,\" not the introducer of ECT in 1938.  Why C/D wrong: Pavlov and Skinner are associated with conditioning theories."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Who is regarded as the \"Father of convulsive therapy\"?","options":["J. Meduna","Cerletti","Patterson","Gerald Juhnke"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The father of convulsive therapy is J. Meduna.  Why B wrong: Cerletti (with Bini) introduced ECT itself.  Why C/D wrong: Associated with the SAD PERSONS scale, not convulsive therapy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"After ECT, what type of seizure is produced?","options":["Absence (petit mal) seizure","Tonic-clonic (grand mal) type seizure","Myoclonic seizure only","Focal seizure without loss of consciousness"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: After ECT it produces tonic-clonic or grand mal type seizures.  Why A/C/D wrong: These seizure types are not what ECT induces."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In indirect ECT, seizure activity is measured by which method, described as the most reliable?","options":["Observational method","EEG (electroencephalogram)","ECG","Blood pressure monitoring"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In indirect ECT, seizure activity is measured by EEG (most reliable), whereas in direct ECT it is measured by observational method.  Why A wrong: Observation is used in direct ECT.  Why C/D wrong: ECG and BP do not measure seizure activity."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"What is the voltage range used in ECT (with the average)?","options":["50–90 volts (average 70)","70–130 volts (average 110 volts)","130–200 volts (average 150)","200–1600 volts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Voltage of ECT is 70–130 volts (average 110 volt); current is 200–1600 amp.  Why A/C wrong: These voltage ranges are incorrect.  Why D wrong: 200–1600 refers to the amperage, not voltage."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The duration of voltage applied in ECT is:","options":["0 to 1.0 second (average 0.6 sec)","1 to 2 seconds (average 1.5 sec)","5 to 10 seconds","0.1 second fixed"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Duration of voltage is 0 to 1.0 second (0.6 sec average).  Why B/C/D wrong: These durations are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"What is the usual frequency of ECT?","options":["Once a month","2–3 times in a week or as per indication","Daily","Once every three months"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Frequency of ECT is 2–3 times in a week or as per indication.  Why A/C/D wrong: These frequencies are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to the source, how many ECT sessions are indicated for depressive episodes?","options":["1–4 sessions","6–12 sessions","8–20 sessions",">15 sessions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Depressive episodes require 6–12 sessions.  Why A wrong: Catatonia & delirium: 1–4 sessions.  Why C wrong: Manic episodes: 8–20 sessions.  Why D wrong: Schizophrenia: >15 sessions."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Match the ECT sessions correctly: catatonia & delirium, manic episodes, and schizophrenia respectively require:","options":["6–12, 8–20, >15 sessions","1–4, 8–20, >15 sessions","1–4, 6–12, 8–20 sessions","8–20, 1–4, 6–12 sessions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catatonia & delirium = 1–4 sessions, manic episodes = 8–20 sessions, schizophrenia = >15 sessions.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pairings mismatch the source values (depressive episodes = 6–12)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which is the MOST common indication for ECT?","options":["Generalized anxiety disorder","Severe depression, other catatonic schizophrenia, and OCD","Substance use disorder","Personality disorders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common indication of ECT is severe depression, other catatonic schizophrenia, OCD, etc.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not listed as the most common ECT indications."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In UNILATERAL ECT electrode placement, where are the electrodes positioned?","options":["Both sides of the temporal region","One electrode on the non-dominant temporal region and the second one about 10 cm above (from) the first electrode","On the occipital region bilaterally","On the frontal midline"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In unilateral ECT, one electrode is placed on the non-dominant temporal region and the second about 10 cm from the first electrode.  Why A wrong: That describes bilateral ECT.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described placements."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which statement about unilateral versus bilateral ECT is correct?","options":["Unilateral ECT is more effective than bilateral","Unilateral ECT is less effective compared to bilateral ECT","Bilateral ECT causes fewer cognitive side effects","Bilateral ECT has a slower therapeutic response"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Unilateral ECT is less effective compared to bilateral ECT; bilateral is commonly used and more effective.  Why A wrong: Bilateral is more effective.  Why C wrong: Bilateral causes MORE cognitive side effects.  Why D wrong: Bilateral has a MORE rapid therapeutic response."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Compared to unilateral ECT, bilateral ECT involves which of the following?","options":["More superficial brain structures and less cognitive side effect","More deep brain structures, more rapid therapeutic response, and more cognitive side effect","Less rapid therapeutic response","No therapeutic effect"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Bilateral ECT involves more deep brain structures, more rapid therapeutic response, and more cognitive side effect.  Why A/C wrong: These describe unilateral ECT (superficial structures, less rapid response, less cognitive side effect).  Why D wrong: Bilateral is more effective."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In bilateral ECT, the electrodes are placed how far above the midpoint of an imaginary line drawn between the outer canthus of the eye and the tragus?","options":["1.5 cm","2.5 cm","5 cm","10 cm"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In bilateral ECT, electrodes are placed 2.5 cm above the midpoint of the imaginary line drawn between the outer canthus of the eye up to the tragus.  Why A/C/D wrong: These distances are incorrect for bilateral placement."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (MHA-2017), which form of ECT is banned in India?","options":["Indirect ECT","Modified ECT","Direct ECT (without anaesthetic or muscle relaxants)","Bilateral ECT"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Direct ECT is given without anaesthetic or muscle relaxants; according to MHA-2017, direct ECT is banned in India.  Why A/B wrong: Indirect ECT (also known as modified ECT) is permitted; it uses premedication.  Why D wrong: Bilateral refers to electrode placement, not the banned technique."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Indirect ECT is also known as:","options":["Direct ECT","Modified ECT (used with pre-medication)","Unmodified ECT","Aversion therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Indirect ECT is also known as modified ECT and is used with pre-medication.  Why A/C wrong: Direct/unmodified ECT is the banned form without medication.  Why D wrong: Aversion therapy is a separate behavioural technique."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Before ECT, why does the nurse need to find out the client's dominant hand?","options":["To decide the anaesthetic dose","To recognise the non-dominant temporal region for electrode placement","To determine seizure threshold","To assess cognitive function"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Finding the dominant hand helps recognise the non-dominant temporal region (used for unilateral electrode placement).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are not the stated reason."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Regarding pre-ECT preparation, which nursing action is correct?","options":["Withhold sedatives before 24 hours and maintain NPO/NBM at least 4–6 hrs before","Shave the client's hair completely before procedure","Give a heavy meal 1 hour before ECT","Keep all jewellery and prosthesis in place during ECT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Withhold sedatives before 24 hours and maintain NPO/NBM at least 4–6 hrs before ECT.  Why B wrong: Assess vital signs and hair care, but there is no need to shave the hair.  Why C wrong: The client must be NPO, not fed.  Why D wrong: Loosen clothes and remove all jewellery and artificial prosthesis, handing them to relatives."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Before ECT, atropine sulphate is administered IM for what purpose?","options":["To induce seizures","To minimise oral secretions","To relax skeletal muscles","To provide sedation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer atropine sulphate IM to minimise oral secretions (anticholinergic action).  Why A wrong: ECT itself induces seizures.  Why C wrong: Succinylcholine is the muscle relaxant.  Why D wrong: Methohexital/thiopental provide sedation."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"During ECT, in which position should the client be placed, and how is a pillow used?","options":["Prone position; pillow under the head only","Supine position during therapy (side-lateral after procedure); place a pillow beneath the cervical and lumbar region to prevent injury during seizures","Sitting upright; no pillow","Trendelenburg position; pillow under the knees"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Place the client supine during therapy and side-lateral after the procedure; place a pillow beneath the cervical and lumbar region to prevent injury while seizures are active.  Why A/C/D wrong: These positions/pillow placements are not what the source specifies."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"During ECT, which drugs does the nurse administer as premedication (anaesthetic and muscle relaxant)?","options":["Atropine and adrenaline","Methohexital or thiopental and succinylcholine","Lithium and haloperidol","Disulfiram and metronidazole"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Administer methohexital or thiopental (anaesthetic) and succinylcholine (muscle relaxant) during ECT.  Why A wrong: Atropine is given before, to reduce secretions; adrenaline is not used.  Why C/D wrong: These are unrelated psychiatric/deterrent drugs."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"During ECT, what is applied on the electrodes to increase conductivity?","options":["Petroleum jelly","Water-soluble jelly","Alcohol swab","Dry gauze"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Use water-soluble jelly on the electrodes to increase conductivity.  Why A/C/D wrong: These do not enhance electrode conductivity as specified."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"After ECT, before offering liquids to the client, the nurse should first check the:","options":["Blood pressure","Gag reflex","Pupillary reflex","Deep tendon reflex"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Offer liquid to the client after checking the gag reflex (to prevent aspiration).  Why A/C/D wrong: These reflexes are not the specified pre-feeding check."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which is the MOST common complication of ECT?","options":["Fracture","Retrograde amnesia","Death","Headache"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Retrograde amnesia is the most common complication of ECT.  Why A/C/D wrong: Fracture, confusion, headache, disorientation and death are possible complications but retrograde amnesia is the most common."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"What is the ABSOLUTE contraindication for ECT?","options":["High-risk pregnancy","Increased intracranial pressure (increased ICP)","Recent MI","Hypertension"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The absolute contraindication for ECT is increased ICP.  Why A/C/D wrong: High-risk pregnancy, recent MI, and HTN are relative contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following are RELATIVE contraindications of ECT? (Select all that apply)","options":["High-risk pregnancy","Recent MI","Retinal detachment","Increased ICP","Severe pulmonary diseases and fracture"],"correct":"A,B,C,E","rationale":"Why correct: Relative contraindications include high-risk pregnancy, recent MI, retinal detachment, HTN, severe pulmonary diseases and fracture.  Why D wrong: Increased ICP is the ABSOLUTE contraindication, not relative."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In the medications used in ECT, atropine sulphate belongs to which drug class and serves what purpose?","options":["Anaesthetic; to induce sedation","Anticholinergic; to minimize secretion, prevent aspiration risk, and decrease vagal stimulation","Muscle relaxant; to minimize amnesia","Stimulant; to induce seizures"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine sulphate is an anticholinergic used to minimize secretion, prevent risk of aspiration, and decrease vagal stimulation.  Why A wrong: Methohexital/thiopental sodium are the anaesthetic agents.  Why C wrong: Succinylcholine is the muscle relaxant.  Why D wrong: No stimulant is used; ECT itself induces the seizure."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In ECT medications, methohexital or thiopental sodium is used as an anaesthetic agent for what purpose?","options":["To induce vomiting","For procedural sedation and to minimize fear and anxiety","To relax muscles","To reduce secretions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methohexital/thiopental sodium (anaesthetic agent) is used for procedural sedation and to minimize fear and anxiety.  Why A wrong: It is not an emetic.  Why C wrong: Succinylcholine relaxes muscles.  Why D wrong: Atropine reduces secretions."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Succinylcholine used in ECT is a muscle relaxant that helps minimize which effects (NORCET-2022)?","options":["Amnesia, headache, confusion, disorientation","Seizure activity itself","Secretions and vagal stimulation","Fear and anxiety"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Succinylcholine (muscle relaxant) is used to minimize amnesia, headache, confusion and disorientation (by preventing violent motor convulsions/injury).  Why B wrong: It does not stop the therapeutic seizure.  Why C wrong: That is atropine's role.  Why D wrong: That is the anaesthetic agent's role."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to the TRICK mnemonic for ECT medications (\"AMS\"), in what order are the medications administered?","options":["In descending order of dose","In ascending order (Atropine, Methohexital, Succinylcholine)","All simultaneously","Random order"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Trick — AMS (Atropine, Methohexital/anaesthetic, Succinylcholine) means administer medications in ascending order.  Why A/C/D wrong: The mnemonic specifies ascending order, not descending, simultaneous, or random."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In aversion therapy, the noxious stimulus is presented at what point relative to the undesired behaviour?","options":["Before the behaviour","Immediately after exhibiting the undesired behaviour","Several hours after the behaviour","Only when the client requests it"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In aversion therapy, punishment or noxious stimuli is presented immediately after exhibiting undesired behaviour.  Why A/C/D wrong: The timing is immediately after the behaviour."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following can serve as a noxious stimulus in aversion therapy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Electric shock","A substance that induces vomiting","Social disapproval","Corporeal punishment","Positive praise"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Noxious stimuli can be electric shock, a substance that induces vomiting, social disapproval, and corporeal punishment.  Why E wrong: Positive praise is a reinforcer, not a noxious/aversive stimulus."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"What are the indications for aversion therapy?","options":["Depression and anxiety","Alcoholism and impulsive behaviour","Dementia and delirium","Phobic disorders only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Aversion therapy is indicated for alcoholism and impulsive behaviour.  Why A/C wrong: Not the indications given.  Why D wrong: Systematic desensitisation and flooding are indicated for phobic disorders."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following is classified as an INDIVIDUAL psychotherapy?","options":["Family therapy","Psychoanalysis","Therapeutic community","Token economy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Individual psychotherapy includes psychoanalysis, hypnosis, abreaction, reality therapy, and uncovering.  Why A wrong: Family therapy is an interpersonal psychotherapy.  Why C wrong: Therapeutic community is an other psycho-social therapy.  Why D wrong: Token economy is a behavioural psychotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Marital therapy, family therapy, and transactional therapy are examples of which category of psychotherapy?","options":["Individual psychotherapy","Interpersonal psychotherapy","Behavioral psychotherapy","Group psychotherapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Interpersonal psychotherapy includes marital therapy, family therapy, and transactional therapy.  Why A/C/D wrong: These belong to individual, behavioural, and other categories."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following are behavioural psychotherapy techniques? (Select all that apply)","options":["Systematic desensitization","Aversion therapy","Assertiveness training","Implosive (flooding) therapy","Token economy"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Behavioral psychotherapy includes behavior modification, systematic desensitization, aversion therapy, assertiveness training, cognitive behavior therapy, implosive (flooding) therapy, positive reinforcement, response shaping, modelling, and token economy.  Why none wrong: All options are listed under behavioural psychotherapy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Occupational therapy, recreational therapy, music therapy, and dance therapy are grouped under which category?","options":["Individual psychotherapy","Interpersonal psychotherapy","Other psycho-social therapy","Behavioral psychotherapy"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Other psycho-social therapy includes therapeutic community, milieu therapy, occupational therapy, play therapy, recreational therapy, music therapy, and dance therapy.  Why A/B/D wrong: These are separate categories."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Disulfiram is a medicine primarily used in patients with which condition?","options":["Depression","Alcohol misuse","Schizophrenia","Opioid dependence"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disulfiram is a medicine used in a patient with alcohol misuse.  Why A/C/D wrong: It is not used for these conditions."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Disulfiram is also known as deterrent therapy because it inhibits alcohol metabolism by blocking which enzyme?","options":["Alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH)","Acetaldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH)","Monoamine oxidase","Cytochrome P450 only"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Disulfiram (deterrent therapy) inhibits alcohol metabolism by blocking acetaldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH).  Why A wrong: ADH converts ethanol to acetaldehyde; disulfiram blocks the next step (ALDH).  Why C/D wrong: These are not the enzyme blocked by disulfiram."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In alcohol metabolism, which enzyme converts ethanol to acetaldehyde, and which converts acetaldehyde to acetate?","options":["ALDH converts ethanol; ADH converts acetaldehyde","ADH (alcohol dehydrogenase) converts ethanol to acetaldehyde; ALDH (aldehyde dehydrogenase) converts acetaldehyde to acetate","Both steps use ADH","Both steps use ALDH"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolism of alcohol: Ethanol → (ADH) → Acetaldehyde → (ALDH) → Acetate. Disulfiram inhibits ALDH.  Why A/C/D wrong: These mis-assign the enzymes to the wrong steps."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The accumulation of which toxic product (due to disulfiram) causes flushing, tachycardia, diaphoresis, nausea and headache?","options":["Ethanol","Acetaldehyde","Acetate","Ammonia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acetaldehyde is a toxic product which causes flushing, tachycardia, diaphoresis, nausea and headache when it accumulates.  Why A wrong: Ethanol is the starting alcohol.  Why C wrong: Acetate is the harmless end product.  Why D wrong: Ammonia is not part of this pathway."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which drug may cause a disulfiram-like reaction when combined with alcohol?","options":["Paracetamol","Metronidazole","Aspirin","Amoxicillin"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metronidazole and alcohol may cause a disulfiram-like reaction.  Why A/C/D wrong: These drugs are not associated with the disulfiram-like reaction here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following are contraindications of disulfiram therapy? (Select all that apply)","options":["Children","Pregnant women","Recent MI","Liver cirrhosis and acute hepatitis","Psychosis and major depression"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Contraindications of disulfiram therapy include children, pregnant women, recent MI, liver cirrhosis & acute hepatitis, psychosis, and major depression.  Why none wrong: All options are listed contraindications."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"What is the goal of milieu therapy?","options":["To administer punishment for undesired behaviour","To manipulate the surrounding environment to make it therapeutic","To induce seizures for depression","To block acetaldehyde dehydrogenase"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The goal of milieu therapy is to manipulate the surrounding environment to make it therapeutic; the patient learns acceptable behaviour, decision-making, and leadership skills.  Why A/C/D wrong: These describe aversion therapy, ECT, and disulfiram, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Milieu therapy may also be called:","options":["Deterrent therapy","Therapeutic community or therapeutic environment","Convulsive therapy","Aversion therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Therapy involving the milieu or environment may be called milieu therapy, therapeutic community, or therapeutic environment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are different therapies."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, systematic desensitisation therapy and flooding therapy are indicated for which disorders?","options":["Phobic disorders","Personality disorders","Marital disharmony","Alcoholism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Systematic desensitisation therapy and flooding therapy are indicated for phobic disorders.  Why B wrong: Cognitive therapy is indicated for personality disorders.  Why C wrong: Individual psychotherapy is indicated for marital disharmony.  Why D wrong: Aversion therapy (and individual psychotherapy) applies to alcoholism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"According to the \"Should Remember\" box, behavioural therapy is indicated for which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Social phobia","Eating disorders","OCD","PTSD","Depression and schizophrenia"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Behavioural therapy is indicated for social phobia, eating disorders, OCD, PTSD, depression and schizophrenia.  Why none wrong: All are listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, individual psychotherapy is indicated for:","options":["Phobic disorders","Marital disharmony, stress, sexual disorders, and alcoholics","Schizophrenia only","Dementia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Individual psychotherapy is indicated for marital disharmony, stress and sexual disorders, and alcoholics.  Why A wrong: Phobic disorders → systematic desensitisation/flooding.  Why C/D wrong: Not the stated indications."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Per the \"Should Remember\" box, cognitive therapy is indicated for which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Personality disorders","Anxiety","Eating disorders","Suicidal thoughts or attempts","Sexual disorders"],"correct":"A,B,C,D,E","rationale":"Why correct: Cognitive therapy is indicated for personality disorders, anxiety, eating disorders, suicidal thoughts or attempts, and sexual disorders.  Why none wrong: All are listed in the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Operant conditioning theory (also called instrumental conditioning) was proposed by:","options":["Ivan Pavlov","B.F. (S.K.) Skinner","J. Meduna","Sigmund Freud"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Operant conditioning theory was proposed by S.K. (B.F.) Skinner; it is also called instrumental conditioning.  Why A wrong: Pavlov proposed classical conditioning.  Why C/D wrong: Meduna and Freud are linked to convulsive therapy and psychoanalysis."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In operant conditioning, \"positive reinforcement\" means:","options":["Removing an undesirable stimulus to increase a behaviour","Adding a desirable stimulus to increase the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur","Adding an undesirable (aversive) stimulus to decrease a behaviour","Removing a desirable stimulus to decrease a behaviour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive reinforcement = adding a desirable stimulus to increase the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur.  Why A wrong: That is negative reinforcement.  Why C wrong: That is positive punishment.  Why D wrong: That is negative punishment."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In operant conditioning, \"positive punishment\" is defined as:","options":["Adding a desirable stimulus to increase a behaviour","Adding an undesirable (aversive) stimulus to decrease the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur","Removing an undesirable stimulus to increase a behaviour","Removing a desirable stimulus to decrease a behaviour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Positive punishment = adding (\"positive\") an aversive/undesirable stimulus to decrease the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur.  Why A wrong: That is positive reinforcement.  Why C wrong: That is negative reinforcement.  Why D wrong: That is negative punishment.  Note: Some printings of the source loosely word this as \"adding a desirable stimulus\"; the factually correct definition of positive punishment is adding an aversive/undesirable stimulus (e.g., a scolding or electric shock) to reduce a behaviour."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"\"Removing an undesirable stimulus to increase the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur\" describes which operant conditioning concept?","options":["Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Negative reinforcement = removing an undesirable stimulus to increase the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur (e.g., a child cleans their room to avoid nagging parents).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are defined differently in the source."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"\"Removing a desirable stimulus to decrease the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur\" describes which concept (e.g., a child losing iPad privileges after refusing to eat vegetables)?","options":["Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Negative punishment = removing (\"negative\") a desirable stimulus to decrease the likelihood of a behaviour to reoccur; the example (losing the desirable iPad privileges) confirms a desirable stimulus is taken away.  Why A/B/C wrong: These are the other three operant conditioning quadrants."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Classical conditioning theory was proposed by whom, and in what year?","options":["B.F. Skinner, 1948","Ivan Pavlov, 1890","Bini & Cerletti, 1938","J. Meduna, 1934"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Classical conditioning theory was proposed by Ivan Pavlov in 1890.  Why A wrong: Skinner proposed operant conditioning.  Why C/D wrong: These relate to ECT/convulsive therapy."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Classical conditioning is also known as:","options":["Instrumental conditioning","Pavlovian or respondent conditioning","Operant conditioning","Aversion conditioning"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Classical conditioning is also called Pavlovian conditioning or respondent conditioning; it involves a neutral stimulus becoming associated with a stimulus that naturally produces a behaviour.  Why A/C wrong: Instrumental/operant conditioning is Skinner's theory.  Why D wrong: Not a term used for classical conditioning here."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"In Pavlov's classical conditioning experiment, what did he pair with feeding the dogs?","options":["An electric shock","Ringing a bell","A flashing light","A whistle"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pavlov was experimenting with dogs, ringing a bell whenever they were fed.  Why A/C/D wrong: The stimulus described in the source is the bell."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The GAF (Global Assessment of Functioning) scale is used to assess:","options":["Overall functioning of mental health","Alcohol withdrawal","Eating patterns","Seizure severity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The GAF scale assesses overall functioning of mental health.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by CIWA-Ar, the Eating Attitudes Test, and EEG respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The MMSE (Mini-Mental Status Examination) assesses which of the following?","options":["Orientation, registration, recall, calculation, language, and motor skills","Only blood pressure","Alcohol dependence","Mania severity"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MMSE assesses orientation, registration, recall, calculation, language, and motor skills.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not what the MMSE assesses."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Cognitive Capacity Screening Examination (CCS Scale) assesses:","options":["Orientation, memory, calculation, and language","Personality traits","Suicide risk","Alcohol withdrawal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The CCS scale assesses orientation, memory, calculation, and language.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other listed scales."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Global Deterioration Scale is used to assess which condition?","options":["Depression","Dementia (primary degenerative dementia based on orientation, memory, and neurologic function)","Eating disorders","Bipolar disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Global Deterioration Scale assesses dementia — primary degenerative dementia based on orientation, memory, and neurologic function.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are assessed by depression scales, the Eating Attitudes Test, and BISS respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Functional Dementia Scale measures which of the following?","options":["Orientation, affect, and the ability to perform activities of daily living","Alcohol use severity","Positive and negative symptoms of schizophrenia","Mania over a week"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Functional Dementia Scale measures orientation, affect, and the ability to perform activities of daily living (dementia).  Why B/C/D wrong: These correspond to AUDIT, PANSS, and ASRM respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Beck Depression Inventory is used to:","options":["Assess eating patterns","Determine the severity of depression and monitor the client's response during treatment","Screen for alcoholism","Assess personality traits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Beck Depression Inventory assesses depression — determines its severity and monitors the client's response during treatment.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are addressed by the Eating Attitudes Test, CAGE/DAST/AUDIT/MAST, and MMPI/BFI respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Eating Attitudes Test is used to assess:","options":["Patterns of eating (eating disorders)","Depression severity","Suicide risk","Schizophrenia symptoms"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Eating Attitudes Test assesses eating disorders — patterns of eating.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by depression, SAD PERSONS, and PANSS/BPRS scales respectively."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The MMPI (Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory) is used to assess:","options":["Personality traits and ego function in adults and adolescents","Depression severity","Alcohol withdrawal","Dementia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The MMPI assesses personality traits — personality traits and ego function in adults and adolescents.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other scales."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which is described as the MOST common assessment scale for depression (NORCET-2022)?","options":["Montgomery Asberg Depression Rating Scale (MADRS)","The Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HRSD) / Hamilton Depression Rating Scale (HDRS)","Quick Inventory of Depressive Symptomatology (QIDS)","Beck Depression Inventory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The Hamilton Rating Scale for Depression (HRSD/HDRS) is the most common assessment scale for depression, a multiple-item questionnaire used to assess severity of depression (NORCET-2022).  Why A wrong: MADRS is the second most common scale for depression.  Why C wrong: QIDS is used to assess severity but is not described as most common.  Why D wrong: Beck is used to determine severity but not labelled most common."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Montgomery Asberg Depression Rating Scale (MADRS) is described as:","options":["The most common scale for depression","The second most common scale for depression","A scale for schizophrenia","A scale for mania"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MADRS is the second most common scale for depression.  Why A wrong: HRSD is the most common.  Why C/D wrong: MADRS assesses depression, not schizophrenia or mania."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The CIWA-Ar scale (Clinical Institute Withdrawal Assessment for Alcohol) is used to assess:","options":["Depression","Alcohol withdrawal syndrome (AWS)","Dementia","Personality traits"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: CIWA-Ar is an assessment scale/tool used for alcohol withdrawal syndrome (AWS).  Why A/C/D wrong: These are assessed by different scales."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"Which of the following scales are used to assess ALCOHOLISM / alcoholic clients? (Select all that apply)","options":["CAGE Questionnaires","DAST (Drug Abuse Screening Test)","AUDIT (Alcohol Use Disorder Identification Test)","MAST (Michigan Alcohol Screening Test)","BFI (The Big Five Inventory)"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: CAGE, DAST, AUDIT and MAST are all used to assess alcoholism / alcoholic clients.  Why E wrong: BFI (Big Five Inventory) measures personality traits, not alcoholism."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The BFI (The Big Five Inventory) measures which five personality traits?","options":["Extraversion, agreeableness, conscientiousness, neuroticism, and openness","Mood, sleep, speech, confidence, and activity","Orientation, memory, recall, language, and calculation","Depression, anxiety, mania, psychosis, and dementia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Big Five Inventory measures the big five personality traits: extraversion, agreeableness, conscientiousness, neuroticism, and openness.  Why B wrong: Those are the domains of the ASRM (mania) scale.  Why C wrong: Those relate to cognitive scales.  Why D wrong: These are different disorders, not the Big Five traits."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Altman Self-Rating Mania Scale (ASRM) assesses mania based on which parameters over a week?","options":["Mood, self-confidence, sleep disturbance, speech, and activity level","Orientation and memory","Alcohol craving","Eating patterns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ASRM assesses mania — mood, self-confidence, sleep disturbance, speech, and activity level over a week.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other scales."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS) is used to measure symptoms of which disorder?","options":["Depression","Schizophrenia (measures symptoms such as depression, anxiety, hallucinations and unusual behaviour)","Eating disorders","Bipolar disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The BPRS measures psychiatric symptoms of schizophrenia such as depression, anxiety, hallucinations and unusual behaviour.  Why A/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other scales (HRSD, Eating Attitudes Test, BISS)."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The PANSS (Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale) and BCASS (Brief Clinical Assessment Scale for Schizophrenia) are used for which disorder?","options":["Schizophrenia (measuring symptom severity)","Depression","Mania","Dementia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: PANSS measures symptom severity of schizophrenia; BCASS is also used for schizophrenia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These scales are specific to schizophrenia, not the other disorders."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"The BISS (Bipolar Inventory of Symptoms Scale) is used for which disorder?","options":["Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia","Depression only","Dementia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The BISS is used for bipolar disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other listed scales."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A DECREASED level of dopamine is associated with which conditions?","options":["Mania and schizophrenia","Depression and Parkinson's disease","Alzheimer's disease","Anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased dopamine is associated with depression and Parkinson's disease.  Why A wrong: Increased dopamine is linked to mania and schizophrenia.  Why C/D wrong: These relate to acetylcholine and serotonin imbalances."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"An INCREASED level of dopamine is associated with:","options":["Depression","Parkinson's disease","Mania and schizophrenia","Alzheimer's disease"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased dopamine is associated with mania and schizophrenia.  Why A/B wrong: Decreased dopamine causes depression and Parkinson's disease.  Why D wrong: Alzheimer's disease is linked to decreased acetylcholine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A DECREASED level of acetylcholine is associated with which disease?","options":["Depression","Alzheimer's disease","Mania","Anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased acetylcholine is associated with Alzheimer's disease.  Why A wrong: Increased acetylcholine is linked to depression.  Why C/D wrong: These correspond to other neurotransmitter changes."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"An increased level of acetylcholine is associated with:","options":["Alzheimer's disease","Depression","Schizophrenia","Anxiety"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Increased acetylcholine is associated with depression.  Why A wrong: Decreased acetylcholine is linked to Alzheimer's disease.  Why C/D wrong: Not the imbalance stated for acetylcholine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A DECREASED level of norepinephrine is associated with which condition?","options":["Depression","Mania","Anxiety","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased norepinephrine is associated with depression.  Why B/C/D wrong: Increased norepinephrine is linked to mania, anxiety, and schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"An INCREASED level of norepinephrine is associated with which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mania","Anxiety","Schizophrenia","Parkinson's disease","Depression"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Increased norepinephrine is associated with mania, anxiety, and schizophrenia.  Why D wrong: Parkinson's disease relates to decreased dopamine.  Why E wrong: Depression is linked to DECREASED norepinephrine."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A DECREASED level of serotonin is associated with depression, while an INCREASED level is associated with:","options":["Anxiety","Mania","Parkinson's disease","Alzheimer's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased serotonin → depression; increased serotonin → anxiety.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are associated with other neurotransmitter imbalances."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A decreased level of histamine is associated with which condition?","options":["Depression","Mania","Schizophrenia","Parkinson's disease"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased histamine is associated with depression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not linked to decreased histamine in the table."},{"section":"Batch 007 · Suicide precautions & nursing care, SAD PERSONS assessment scale…","question":"A DECREASED level of GABA (gamma-amino butyric acid) is associated with which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Anxiety disorder","Schizophrenia","Seizure disorders","Parkinson's disease","Alzheimer's disease"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Decreased GABA is associated with anxiety disorder, schizophrenia, and seizure disorders.  Why D wrong: Parkinson's disease is linked to decreased dopamine.  Why E wrong: Alzheimer's disease is linked to decreased acetylcholine."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"According to the \"List of Popular Theories\" table, who proposed the Moral Development Theory (1960) that outlined a total of 6 stages of human development?","options":["Jean Piaget","Lawrence Kohlberg","Sigmund Freud","Erik Erikson"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Kohlberg's moral development theory (1960) is a theory for personality development and proposed a total of 6 stages for human development.  Why A wrong: Piaget proposed 4 stages of development.  Why C wrong: Freud proposed the psychosexual/psychoanalytical theories, not the moral development theory.  Why D wrong: Erikson proposed 8 psychosocial stages."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Jean Piaget's Theory of Development (1936) proposed how many stages for human personality development?","options":["3 stages","4 stages","5 stages","6 stages"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Piaget proposed a total of 4 stages for human personality development.  Why A wrong: No 3-stage model is attributed to Piaget in the table.  Why C wrong: 5 stages is Freud's psychosexual theory.  Why D wrong: 6 stages is Kohlberg's model."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In Piaget's theory, which stage corresponds to the age range 7–11 years old?","options":["Sensorimotor stage","Preoperational stage","Concrete operational stage","Formal operational stage"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The Concrete operational stage covers 7–11 years old.  Why A wrong: Sensorimotor is 0–2 years old.  Why B wrong: Preoperational is 2–7 years old.  Why D wrong: Formal operational is 11 years through adulthood."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Piaget's Sensorimotor stage covers which age range?","options":["0–2 years old","2–7 years old","7–11 years old","11 years through adulthood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Sensorimotor stage is 0–2 years old.  Why B wrong: 2–7 years is Preoperational.  Why C wrong: 7–11 years is Concrete operational.  Why D wrong: 11+ years is Formal operational."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which Sigmund Freud book, published in 1901, examines the psychological basis for the forgetting of names and words and the misuse of words in speech and writing?","options":["The Interpretation of Dreams","Psychopathology of Everyday Life","Theory of Infantile Sexuality","Psychosexual Theory"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Psychopathology of Everyday Life (1901) examines the psychological basis for forgetting names/words and the misuse of words.  Why A wrong: The Interpretation of Dreams (1899) is about why we dream.  Why C wrong: Infantile Sexuality is Freud's essay on childhood sexual urges.  Why D wrong: Psychosexual theory (1905) covers the 5 stages of development."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In which year was Sigmund Freud's book \"The Interpretation of Dreams\" published?","options":["1899","1901","1905","1936"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Interpretation of Dreams was published in 1899.  Why B wrong: 1901 is Psychopathology of Everyday Life.  Why C wrong: 1905 is the psychosexual theory.  Why D wrong: 1936 is Piaget's Theory of Development."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"According to Freud's Psycho-analytical theory (1901), the structure of personality consists of which three components?","options":["Conscious, preconscious, unconscious","Id, Ego, Superego","Oral, anal, phallic","Sensorimotor, preoperational, formal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The structure of personality is Id, Ego, Superego.  Why A wrong: Conscious/preconscious/unconscious is the topographical (level of consciousness) model.  Why C wrong: Oral/anal/phallic are psychosexual stages.  Why D wrong: These are Piaget's cognitive stages."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Freud's levels of consciousness (topographical model) include which of the following?","options":["Unconscious, semiconscious and conscious","Id, Ego, Superego","Sensory, motor, cognitive","Oral, latency, genital"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The table lists levels of consciousness as unconscious, semiconscious and conscious.  Why B wrong: These are structural personality components.  Why C/D wrong: Not levels of consciousness."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Sigmund Freud is renowned as the \"father of\" which of the following?","options":["Psychoanalysis","Intelligence testing","Psychiatry","Behaviourism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Freud is renowned as the father of psychoanalysis.  Why B wrong: Father of Intelligence Test is Alfred Binet (p.973).  Why C wrong: The term \"Psychiatry\" was coined by Christian Reil.  Why D wrong: Behaviourism is associated with Watson/Skinner, not Freud."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Freud's Psychosexual theory (1905) proposed 5 stages of human development. Which is the correct sequence?","options":["Oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital","Anal, oral, latency, phallic, genital","Phallic, oral, anal, genital, latency","Latency, genital, oral, anal, phallic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The 5 psychosexual stages are oral, anal, phallic, latency, and genital.  Why B/C/D wrong: These reorder the stages incorrectly."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Match the theorist to their theory: Who is credited with the Classical conditioning theory (1897)?","options":["B.F. Skinner","Ivan Pavlov","Albert Bandura","Edward Lee Thorndike"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Classical conditioning theory (1897) — learning when a neutral stimulus becomes associated with a stimulus that naturally produces a behaviour — was given by Ivan Pavlov.  Why A wrong: Skinner gave operant conditioning theory.  Why C wrong: Bandura gave social learning theory.  Why D wrong: Thorndike gave trial-and-error learning theory."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Operant conditioning theory (1948), in which the motivation for a behavior happens after the behavior is demonstrated (via reinforcer or punisher), is attributed to which theorist?","options":["Pavlov","Skinner","Vygotsky","Sullivan"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Operant conditioning theory (1948) is attributed to Skinner (B.F. Skinner).  Why A wrong: Pavlov gave classical conditioning.  Why C wrong: Vygotsky gave socio-cultural theory.  Why D wrong: Sullivan gave inter-personal theory."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Social learning theory, which studied children to understand how they learn from others, was proposed by:","options":["Albert Bandura","Jean Piaget","Erik Erikson","Harry Stack Sullivan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Albert Bandura proposed the Social learning theory and studied children to understand how they learn from others.  Why B/C/D wrong: Piaget (cognitive stages), Erikson (psychosocial), Sullivan (interpersonal) are different theorists."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Socio-cultural theory, holding that human development and learning originate in social and cultural interaction, was given by:","options":["Urie Bronfenbrenner","Lev Vygotsky","Charles Spearman","Kathryn Barnard"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Vygotsky's socio-cultural theory is a theory for cognitive development, believing development/learning originate in social and cultural interaction.  Why A wrong: Bronfenbrenner gave the ecological systems theory.  Why C wrong: Spearman gave the two-factor theory of intelligence.  Why D wrong: Barnard gave the Parent Child Interaction Model."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Erikson's stages of psychosocial development (1950) identify how many stages a healthy developing individual should pass through from infancy to late adulthood?","options":["4 stages","5 stages","6 stages","8 stages"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Erikson's psychosocial development theory identifies a series of 8 stages.  Why A wrong: 4 stages is Piaget.  Why B wrong: 5 stages is Freud's psychosexual.  Why C wrong: 6 stages is Kohlberg."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Ecological systems theory, describing how different environments influence our behavior across the lifespan, was proposed by:","options":["Urie Bronfenbrenner","Edward Lee Thorndike","Cannon and Bard","Stern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Urie Bronfenbrenner proposed the Ecological systems theory (environment/ecological system).  Why B wrong: Thorndike gave trial-and-error learning.  Why C wrong: Cannon and Bard gave a theory for emotions.  Why D wrong: Stern gave the Attitude-Behavior-Context (ABC) theory."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In Thorndike's Trial and error learning theory (cat experiment), what served as the motivation/drive for learning?","options":["Fear of punishment","The cat's hunger (with food as the stimulus/goal)","Social imitation","Reinforcement schedules"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per Thorndike, actions are determined by motivation/drive and a stimulus/goal; in the cat experiment the motivation was the cat's hunger and the food was the stimulus for behaviour change.  Why A wrong: The source cites hunger, not fear of punishment.  Why C wrong: Imitation relates to Bandura's social learning.  Why D wrong: Reinforcement schedules relate to Skinner's operant conditioning."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Inter-personal theory, holding that personality development and behaviour disorders are related to and determined by relationships between persons, was given by:","options":["Hildegard Peplau","Harry Stack Sullivan","Charles Spearman","Albert Bandura"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Harry Stack Sullivan's inter-personal theory (a theory for behaviour) states personality development and behaviour disorders are determined by relationships between persons.  Why A wrong: Peplau gave the Interpersonal Relations Theory for nursing.  Why C wrong: Spearman gave the two-factor theory of intelligence.  Why D wrong: Bandura gave social learning theory."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Interpersonal Relations Theory, focusing on the therapeutic nurse-patient relationship using problem-solving techniques, was developed by:","options":["Hildegard Peplau","Harry Stack Sullivan","Kathryn Barnard","Erik Erikson"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hildegard Peplau's Interpersonal Relations Theory and the Nursing Process focus on the therapeutic relationship using problem-solving to collaborate and meet the patient's needs.  Why B wrong: Sullivan gave the (general) inter-personal theory of behaviour.  Why C wrong: Barnard gave the Parent Child Interaction Model.  Why D wrong: Erikson gave psychosocial development stages."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Spearman's Two Factor Theory was proposed as a theory for which construct?","options":["Emotions","Intelligence","Personality","Behaviour"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Charles Spearman proposed the two-factor theory for intelligence.  Why A wrong: Emotions theory is Cannon and Bard.  Why C wrong: Personality development is Freud/Bandura etc.  Why D wrong: Behaviour is covered by conditioning theories."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Cannon and Bard theory is a theory for:","options":["Emotions","Intelligence","Cognitive development","Personality"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Cannon and Bard theory is a theory for emotions.  Why B wrong: Intelligence is Spearman.  Why C wrong: Cognitive development is Vygotsky/Piaget.  Why D wrong: Personality is Freud/Bandura."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Attitude-Behavior-Context (ABC) theory, stating that behavior is an interactive product of personal attitudinal variables and contextual factors, is attributed to:","options":["Stern","Kathryn Barnard","Vygotsky","Bronfenbrenner"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ABC theory (a theory for behaviour) is attributed to Stern.  Why B wrong: Barnard gave the Parent Child Interaction Model.  Why C/D wrong: Different theorists (cognitive development / ecological systems)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Parent Child Interaction Model (PCIT), positing that a strong secure attachment relationship is the foundation for effective limit setting and discipline, was proposed by:","options":["Kathryn Barnard","Hildegard Peplau","Erik Erikson","Harry Stack Sullivan"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kathryn Barnard's Parent Child Interaction Model posits a strong, secure attachment relationship is necessary for effective limit setting and consistency in discipline, improving mental health for parent and child.  Why B/C/D wrong: Peplau, Erikson, and Sullivan proposed other theories."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A \"sudden, repetitive, stereotyped motor movement or verbal speech\" is best described by which term?","options":["Tic","Waxy flexibility","Echolalia","Perseveration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A tic is a sudden, repetitive, stereotyped motor movement or verbal speech.  Why B wrong: Waxy flexibility is passive yielding of body parts to placement.  Why C wrong: Echolalia is repetition of heard/spoken words.  Why D wrong: Perseveration is inappropriate repetition of the same thoughts."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A patient with schizophrenia passively yields all movable parts of the body to any efforts made at placing them in certain positions. This is termed:","options":["Catatonia","Waxy flexibility","Stupor","Akathisia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Waxy flexibility is the condition in which the individual with schizophrenia passively yields all movable parts to positioning efforts.  Why A wrong: Catatonia is broader psychomotor immobility triggered by emotions.  Why C wrong: Stupor is diminished consciousness, mute and still with eyes open.  Why D wrong: Akathisia is motor restlessness/inability to sit still."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Who coined the term \"Psychiatry\"?","options":["Sigmund Freud","Christian Reil","Ivan Pavlov","Alfred Binet"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The term \"Psychiatry\" was coined by Christian Reil.  Why A wrong: Freud is father of psychoanalysis.  Why C wrong: Pavlov is classical conditioning.  Why D wrong: Binet is father of the Intelligence Test."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Cognitive model suggests that psychological disorders result from what?","options":["Repressed childhood sexual conflicts","Acquiring dysfunctional ways of thinking and acting","Genetic neurotransmitter imbalance only","Faulty attachment in infancy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The cognitive model suggests psychological disorders result from acquiring dysfunctional ways of thinking and acting.  Why A wrong: That reflects psychoanalytic theory, not the cognitive model.  Why C wrong: The source describes cognitive (thinking) causes, not solely genetics.  Why D wrong: Attachment theory is separate."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In which year was the Indian Lunacy Act passed?","options":["1912","1987","1997","2017"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Indian Lunacy Act was passed in the year 1912.  Why B wrong: 1987 is the Indian Mental Health Act (IMHA), replaced by MHA 2017.  Why C wrong: 1997 is cited for the Indian Mental Health Act (per source).  Why D wrong: 2017 is the Mental Healthcare Act."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Per the source, the Indian Mental Health Act was passed in which year?","options":["1912","1987","1997","2017"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The source states the Indian Mental Health Act was passed in the year 1997.  Why A wrong: 1912 is the Indian Lunacy Act.  Why B wrong: The IMHA (referenced as being replaced) is dated 1987 elsewhere; the source explicitly states 1997 for the Indian Mental Health Act — key the printed value.  Why D wrong: 2017 is the Mental Healthcare Act."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (MHA 2017) replaced which earlier act?","options":["Indian Lunacy Act 1912","IMHA-1987","Persons with Disabilities Act","National Trust Act"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MHA 2017, passed in 2017, replaced the IMHA-1987.  Why A wrong: The Lunacy Act was earlier (1912) and not the one replaced by MHA 2017 per source.  Why C/D wrong: Not mentioned as replaced acts."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A key provision of the Mental Health Act 2017 is that it:","options":["Mandates indirect ECT for all patients","Decriminalizes suicide attempt by a mentally ill person","Bans all antipsychotic drugs","Prohibits advance directives"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MHA 2017 decriminalizes suicide attempt by a mentally ill person.  Why A wrong: MHA 2017 does NOT recommend ECT without muscle relaxants (indirect ECT).  Why C wrong: It does not ban antipsychotics.  Why D wrong: It empowers, not prohibits, advance directives."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"As per MHA 2017, which statement about ECT is correct?","options":["Indirect ECT (without muscle relaxants) is recommended for all","ECT without use of muscle relaxants (indirect ECT) is not recommended, and ECT is not recommended for minors","ECT is recommended for minors only","ECT is banned entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Per MHA 2017, ECT therapy without muscle relaxants (indirect ECT) is not recommended, and ECT is not recommended for minors.  Why A wrong: Indirect ECT is specifically NOT recommended.  Why C wrong: ECT is not recommended for minors.  Why D wrong: ECT is restricted, not banned outright."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Mental Health Act (MHA 2017) empowers a mentally ill person to have which right (NORCET-5 Prelims 2023)?","options":["Right to refuse hospitalization always","Right to make an advance directive towards how he/she wants to be treated","Right to prescribe own medication","Right to unrestricted discharge"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MHA 2017 empowers a mentally ill person to make an advance directive towards the way he/she wants to be treated.  Why A/C/D wrong: These specific rights are not what the source cites."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The two most common eating disorders that put a client at risk for severe cardiovascular and GI complications and can result in death are:","options":["Pica and rumination disorder","Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa","Binge eating disorder and night eating syndrome","Anorexia nervosa and pica"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The two most common eating disorders are anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa, risking severe cardiovascular and GI complications and death.  Why A/C/D wrong: These pairings are not the two most common cited."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In anorexia nervosa, the client characteristically:","options":["Deliberately starves herself or engages in binge eating and purging, wanting to become as thin as possible","Wants to gain weight rapidly","Has no concern about body weight","Only over-exercises without dietary change"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In anorexia nervosa the client deliberately starves herself or engages in binge eating and purging and wants to become as thin as possible, refusing to maintain an appropriate weight.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the defining features of anorexia nervosa."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Bulimia nervosa is characterized by episodic binge eating followed by purging. Which method(s) may the client also use?","options":["Laxatives, enemas, diuretics, or syrup of ipecac","Only fasting","Only excessive exercise","Insulin omission only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In bulimia nervosa, purging is by vomiting, and the client may also use laxatives, enemas, diuretics, or syrup of ipecac.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the purging methods listed for bulimia nervosa."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Russell's sign (bruised knuckles due to repeated self-induced vomiting) is most characteristically observed in which disorder (AIIMS)?","options":["Anorexia nervosa (restricting type)","Bulimia nervosa","Major depression","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Russell's sign (bruised knuckles from repeated self-induced vomiting) is observed in bulimia nervosa.  Why A wrong: Restricting-type anorexia does not typically involve vomiting.  Why C/D wrong: Not associated with Russell's sign."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In bulimia nervosa, which metabolic disturbance may occur from diarrhoea caused by enemas and excessive laxative use?","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic acidosis may occur from diarrhoea caused by enemas and excessive laxative use.  Why B wrong: Metabolic alkalosis occurs from frequent vomiting (loss of gastric acid).  Why C/D wrong: These are respiratory, not the described metabolic disturbances."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is described as the most common metabolic complication in bulimia nervosa, occurring from frequent vomiting?","options":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Hyperkalemia","Hypernatremia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Metabolic alkalosis (the most common metabolic complication) may occur from frequent vomiting.  Why A wrong: Metabolic acidosis is from laxative/enema-induced diarrhoea.  Why C/D wrong: Not cited as the most common metabolic complication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Constant binging and purging behaviours can result in which severe complications? (Select all that apply)","options":["Severe electrolyte imbalances","Erosion of tooth enamel","Oesophageal tears and gastric rupture","Menstrual irregularities","Improved bone density"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Constant binging/purging can cause severe electrolyte imbalances, tooth enamel erosion from gastric acid exposure, menstrual irregularities, oesophageal tears, and in severe cases gastric rupture.  Why E wrong: Improved bone density is not a complication; eating disorders harm bone health."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A client with anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa is at risk for electrolyte imbalance or fluid volume deficit. What should be periodically monitored?","options":["Serum electrolyte level","Blood glucose only","Liver enzymes only","Thyroid panel only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because of the risk of electrolyte imbalance/fluid volume deficit, serum electrolyte level should be periodically monitored.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies serum electrolytes as the periodic monitoring priority."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Perfectionism is most commonly seen in clients with which type of disorder?","options":["Eating disorders","Substance use disorders","Dementia","Bipolar disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Perfectionism is more commonly seen in clients with eating disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the association given in the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF) scale allows evaluation of the client's overall functioning in which domains?","options":["Psychological, social, and occupational functioning","Motor and sensory function only","Financial status only","Family history only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The GAF scale allows evaluation of the client's overall psychological, social, and occupational functioning.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not what GAF assesses."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which behaviour modification technique is commonly used successfully to treat phobias?","options":["Systematic desensitization","Aversion therapy","Milieu therapy","Catharsis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Systematic desensitization is a common behaviour modification technique used successfully to treat phobias.  Why B wrong: Aversion therapy is used for alcoholism, impulsive behaviour, and paraphilias.  Why C wrong: Milieu therapy manipulates the environment therapeutically.  Why D wrong: Catharsis is releasing strong emotions."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Sundowners syndrome — an increase in agitation occurring in the evening accompanied by confusion — is commonly seen in clients with which condition (AIIMS-Manglagiri-2018)?","options":["Dementia","Bulimia nervosa","Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sundowners syndrome is an increase in evening agitation with confusion, commonly seen in clients with dementia.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not associated with sundowning in the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Clients who experience posttraumatic stress disorder (PTSD) are at high risk for what?","options":["Suicide and other forms of violent behaviours","Improved trust","Weight gain only","Enhanced concentration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients with PTSD are at high risk for suicide and other forms of violent behaviours.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not risks cited for PTSD."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"An effective communication strategy for a nurse to use with a client with PTSD is:","options":["Listening attentively and staying with the client","Leaving the client alone to process","Challenging the client's memories","Encouraging the client to relive the trauma repeatedly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The source states listening attentively and staying with the client is an effective communication strategy for PTSD.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the recommended strategy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Hypnosis is described as one of the main therapies for clients with which type of disorder?","options":["Dissociate (dissociative) disorders","Eating disorders","Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypnosis is one of the main therapies for clients who dissociate (dissociative disorders).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the disorders cited for hypnosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"To decrease stimulation in a client with mania, the nurse should:","options":["Redirect and focus the client's communication and not allow the client to talk about different topics","Encourage rapid topic changes","Use only open-ended questions","Increase group activities"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: To decrease stimulation in mania, the nurse should redirect and focus the client's communication and not allow talking about different topics.  Why B wrong: Rapid topic change increases stimulation.  Why C wrong: Open-ended questions may enable endless talking (closed questions are best).  Why D wrong: Increasing activity heightens stimulation."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"For a manic client, which questioning approach is best, and why?","options":["Open-ended questions, because they build rapport","Closed questions, because open-ended questions may enable the client to talk endlessly and feel out of control","No questions at all","Only written questions"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: For a manic client it is best to ask closed questions because open-ended questions may enable endless talking and contribute to feeling out of control.  Why A/C/D wrong: Contradict the source's guidance."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What must the nurse check before allowing a client to have a drink after an electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) procedure?","options":["The client's gag reflex","The client's blood pressure only","The client's pupil size only","The client's grip strength"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The nurse must check the client's gag reflex before allowing a drink after ECT (to prevent aspiration).  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the specific pre-drinking check cited."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which of the following are symptoms suggestive of mania in a client with a history of bipolar disorder?","options":["Headache, agitation, and indigestion","Bradycardia and hypersomnia","Constipation and sedation","Diplopia and ptosis"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Headache, agitation, and indigestion are symptoms suggestive of mania in a client with a history of bipolar disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the manic symptoms cited."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Thoughtless or reckless spending is a common symptom of which episode?","options":["Manic episode","Depressive episode","Catatonic episode","Panic attack"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thoughtless or reckless spending is a common symptom of a manic episode.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not characteristic of these states."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A client with a personality disorder uses maladaptive behaviour primarily to:","options":["Relate to others and fulfill basic emotional needs","Improve cognition","Enhance memory","Avoid all social contact permanently"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A client with a personality disorder uses maladaptive behaviour to relate to others and fulfill basic emotional needs.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the stated purpose."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Antisocial personality disorder leads the client to have:","options":["A total disregard for the rights of others","Extreme perfectionism","Fear of public places","Excessive attachment to others"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antisocial personality disorder leads the client to have a total disregard for the rights of others.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other conditions."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Borderline personality disorder results in a pattern of instability in which areas? (Select all that apply)","options":["Mood","Interpersonal relationships","Self-esteem and self-identity","Behaviour and cognition","Bone metabolism"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Borderline personality disorder results in a pattern of instability in a person's mood, interpersonal relationships, self-esteem, self-identity, behaviour, and cognition.  Why E wrong: Bone metabolism is not part of the described instability."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Impulsiveness is the most prominent characteristic of which personality disorder?","options":["Borderline personality disorder","Paranoid personality disorder","Antisocial personality disorder","Schizoid personality disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Impulsiveness is the most prominent characteristic of borderline personality disorder.  Why B wrong: Paranoid PD is characterized by extreme distrust of others.  Why C wrong: Antisocial PD is disregard for others' rights.  Why D wrong: Not the disorder cited for impulsiveness."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Paranoid personality disorder is characterized by:","options":["Extreme distrust of others","Perfectionism","Fear of public places","Reckless spending"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Paranoid personality disorder is characterized by extreme distrust of others.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other conditions/symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Agoraphobia is characterized by fear of what?","options":["Public places","Heights","Enclosed spaces","Water"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Agoraphobia is characterized by fear of public places.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other specific phobias."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A speech pattern characterized by rapid transition from topic to topic, typically without finishing one idea, common in mania, is called:","options":["Flight of ideas","Clang associations","Echolalia","Neologism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flight of ideas is rapid transition from topic to topic without finishing one idea, common in mania.  Why B wrong: Clang associations are the use of rhyming.  Why C wrong: Echolalia is repetition of heard words.  Why D wrong: Neologism is invented/new words."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Clang associations refer to the use of what in speech?","options":["Rhyming","Invented words","Repeated heard words","Obscene words"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clang associations are the use of rhyming.  Why B wrong: Invented words are neologism.  Why C wrong: Repetition of heard words is echolalia.  Why D wrong: Obscene verbal utterance is coprolalia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Dysthymic disorder is best described as a mood disorder that:","options":["Is similar to major depression but remains mild to moderate in severity","Ranges from moderate depression to hypomania","Causes psychotic delusions","Always requires hospitalization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dysthymic disorder is a mood disorder similar to major depression but remains mild to moderate in severity.  Why B wrong: That describes cyclothymic disorder.  Why C/D wrong: Not features of dysthymia in the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Cyclothymic disorder is characterized by a mood range from:","options":["Moderate depression to hypomania","Severe depression to full mania","Euthymia to psychosis","Mild to moderate depression only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cyclothymic disorder is characterized by a mood range from moderate depression to hypomania.  Why B wrong: Full mania with severe depression describes bipolar I, not cyclothymia.  Why C/D wrong: Not the described range."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Major mood disorders include which of the following?","options":["Bipolar disorder and major depression","Generalized anxiety and OCD","Schizophrenia and delirium","Phobias and PTSD"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Major mood disorders include bipolar disorder and major depression.  Why B/D wrong: These are anxiety disorders.  Why C wrong: These are psychotic/cognitive disorders."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Major anxiety disorders include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Generalized anxiety","Obsessive-compulsive disorder","Panic disorder","Phobias and posttraumatic stress disorder","Bipolar disorder"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Major anxiety disorders include generalized anxiety, OCD, panic disorder, phobias, and PTSD.  Why E wrong: Bipolar disorder is a mood disorder, not an anxiety disorder."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"According to the DSM-IV-TR, the most common cognitive disorders described are:","options":["Delirium, dementia, and amnestic disorders","Anxiety, depression, and mania","Schizophrenia, phobia, and OCD","Anorexia, bulimia, and pica"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cognitive disorders in DSM-IV-TR are delirium, dementia, and amnestic disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the DSM-IV-TR cognitive disorders category."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Symptoms of cognitive disorders include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Cognitive impairment","Behavioural dysfunction","Personality changes","Increased muscle strength"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Symptoms of cognitive disorders include cognitive impairment, behavioural dysfunction, and personality changes.  Why D wrong: Increased muscle strength is not a symptom of cognitive disorders."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which schizophrenia symptoms are examples of POSITIVE symptoms?","options":["Delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, grossly disorganized/catatonic behaviour","Flat affect and alogia","Avolition and anhedonia","Poverty of speech only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Positive symptoms of schizophrenia are delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, and grossly disorganized or catatonic behaviour.  Why B/C/D wrong: Flat affect, alogia, avolition, anhedonia are negative symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which set represents NEGATIVE symptoms of schizophrenia?","options":["Flat affect, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition, and anhedonia","Delusions and hallucinations","Disorganized speech and catatonia","Flight of ideas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Negative symptoms include flat affect, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (lack of self-initiated behaviours), and anhedonia (minimal enjoyment of activities).  Why B/C wrong: These are positive symptoms.  Why D wrong: Flight of ideas is a manic speech pattern."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Schizophrenia is characterized by disturbances lasting for at least how long?","options":["1 month","3 months","6 months","12 months"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Schizophrenia is characterized by disturbances for at least 6 months (in thought content/form, perception, affect, sense of self, volition, relationships, work/self-care, and psychomotor behaviour).  Why A/B/D wrong: The source specifies at least 6 months."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Delusions are best defined as:","options":["False, fixed beliefs not shared by other members of the client's social, cultural, or religious background","False sensory perceptions","Repetition of heard words","Invented new words"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delusions are false, fixed beliefs that aren't shared by other members of the client's social, cultural, or religious background.  Why B wrong: That defines hallucinations.  Why C wrong: That is echolalia.  Why D wrong: That is neologism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A client who believes his personal thoughts are broadcast to the external world is experiencing which form of delusion?","options":["Thought broadcasting","Persecutory delusion","Erotic delusion","Nihilistic delusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Thought broadcasting is the delusion that one's personal thoughts are broadcast to the external world.  Why B wrong: Persecutory delusion = being followed/tricked/tormented.  Why C wrong: Erotic delusion = idealized relationship with someone of higher status.  Why D wrong: Nihilistic delusion = belief that oneself/body/world does not exist."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The idea that one is being followed, tricked, tormented, or made the subject of ridicule is an example of which delusion?","options":["Persecutory delusion","Erotic delusion","Grandiose delusion","Somatic delusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A persecutory delusion is the idea that one is being followed, tricked, tormented, or made the subject of ridicule.  Why B wrong: Erotic delusion involves an idealized relationship.  Why C/D wrong: Not described here."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A client with erotic delusions falsely believes he shares an idealized relationship with another person, usually someone of:","options":["Higher status such as a celebrity","His immediate family","The same profession","Lower socioeconomic status"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In erotic delusions, the client falsely believes he shares an idealized relationship with another person, usually someone of higher status such as a celebrity.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source specifies higher-status person/celebrity."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The most commonly experienced type of hallucination in schizophrenics is:","options":["Auditory hallucination","Visual hallucination","Tactile hallucination","Olfactory hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly seen hallucination in schizophrenics is auditory hallucination.  Why B wrong: Visual hallucination is most common in alcoholism clients.  Why C/D wrong: Not the most common in schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The most common hallucination seen in an alcoholism client is:","options":["Auditory","Visual","Gustatory","Tactile"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The most common hallucination in an alcoholism client is visual hallucination.  Why A wrong: Auditory is most common in schizophrenia.  Why C/D wrong: Not cited as most common in alcoholism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A client with catatonic schizophrenia may exhibit which features? (Select all that apply)","options":["Bizarre postures","Waxy flexibility (posture held in odd fixed positions)","Resistance to being moved","Flight of ideas"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Catatonic schizophrenia may show bizarre postures, waxy flexibility (postures held in odd/unusual fixed positions), and resistance to being moved.  Why D wrong: Flight of ideas is a manic speech pattern, not a catatonic feature."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Clients with paranoid schizophrenia characteristically have:","options":["Delusions or frequent auditory hallucinations unrelated to reality","Flat affect only","Waxy flexibility only","Complete mutism only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients with paranoid schizophrenia have delusions or frequent auditory hallucinations unrelated to reality.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other subtypes/features."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Common paraphilias include which of the following?","options":["Exhibitionism, fetishism, and frotteurism","Anorexia, bulimia, and pica","Mania, depression, and dysthymia","Delirium, dementia, and amnesia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Common paraphilias include exhibitionism, fetishism, and frotteurism.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are eating, mood, and cognitive disorders, not paraphilias."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Exhibitionism is defined as:","options":["Exposing one's genitals and occasionally masturbating in a public place","Rubbing one's genitals on a non-consenting person","Using an object to become sexually aroused","Fear of sexual contact"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Exhibitionism is exposing one's genitals and occasionally masturbating in a public place.  Why B wrong: That is frotteurism.  Why C wrong: That is fetishism.  Why D wrong: Not a paraphilia definition given."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Fetishism is best defined as:","options":["Use of an object to become sexually aroused","Exposing genitals in public","Rubbing genitals on a non-consenting person","Fear of intimacy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Fetishism is the use of an object to become sexually aroused.  Why B wrong: That is exhibitionism.  Why C wrong: That is frotteurism.  Why D wrong: Not a definition given."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Frotteurism refers to:","options":["Rubbing one's genitals on another non-consenting person to become aroused","Exposing one's genitals in public","Using an object for arousal","Preoccupation with illness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Frotteurism is rubbing one's genitals on another non-consenting person to become aroused.  Why B wrong: That is exhibitionism.  Why C wrong: That is fetishism.  Why D wrong: That is hypochondriasis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Premature ejaculation is marked by:","options":["Persistent and recurrent onset of orgasm and ejaculation with minimal sexual stimulation","Inability to attain an erection","Delayed orgasm","Painful coitus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Premature ejaculation is marked by persistent and recurrent onset of orgasm and ejaculation with minimal sexual stimulation.  Why B wrong: That is male erectile disorder.  Why C wrong: That describes orgasmic disorder.  Why D wrong: Painful coitus is dyspareunia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In male erectile disorder, the client has:","options":["Persistent or recurrent inability to attain or maintain an adequate erection until completion of sexual activity","Rapid ejaculation","Painful intercourse","Loss of sexual desire only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In male erectile disorder the client has a persistent or recurrent inability to attain or maintain an adequate erection until completion of sexual activity.  Why B wrong: That is premature ejaculation.  Why C wrong: That is dyspareunia/vaginismus.  Why D wrong: That describes hypoactive desire, not erectile disorder."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Sexual pain disorders include which two conditions?","options":["Dyspareunia (painful coitus) and vaginismus (painful vaginal contraction)","Premature ejaculation and erectile disorder","Exhibitionism and fetishism","Anorgasmia and hypoactive desire"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sexual pain disorders include dyspareunia (painful coitus) and vaginismus (painful vaginal contraction).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other categories of dysfunction/paraphilia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Vaginismus is defined as:","options":["Recurrent or persistent involuntary contraction of the perineal muscles surrounding the outer third of the vagina when vaginal penetration is attempted","Painful bruising of knuckles","Absence of orgasm","Loss of libido"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vaginismus is recurrent/persistent involuntary contraction of the perineal muscles surrounding the outer third of the vagina when penetration is attempted.  Why B wrong: Knuckle bruising is Russell's sign.  Why C/D wrong: Not vaginismus."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Primary sleep disorders may be categorized as:","options":["Dyssomnias or parasomnias","Acute or chronic only","REM or NREM only","Organic or functional only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Primary sleep disorders may be categorized as dyssomnias or parasomnias.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the primary categorization given."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Dyssomnias involve which of the following?","options":["Excessive sleep or difficulty initiating and maintaining sleep","Nightmares only","Sleepwalking only","Sleep terror only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dyssomnias involve excessive sleep or difficulty initiating and maintaining sleep.  Why B/C/D wrong: Nightmares, sleepwalking, and sleep terror are parasomnias."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which of the following are examples of dyssomnias? (Select all that apply)","options":["Primary insomnia","Circadian rhythm sleep disorder","Obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome","Primary hypersomnia and narcolepsy","Sleepwalking disorder"],"correct":"A,B,C,D","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of dyssomnias include primary insomnia, circadian rhythm sleep disorder, obstructive sleep apnoea syndrome, primary hypersomnia, and narcolepsy.  Why E wrong: Sleepwalking disorder is a parasomnia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Examples of parasomnias include which of the following?","options":["Nightmare disorder, sleep terror disorder, and sleepwalking disorder","Narcolepsy and primary insomnia","Obstructive sleep apnoea","Circadian rhythm sleep disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Examples of parasomnias include nightmare disorder, sleep terror disorder, and sleepwalking disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are dyssomnias."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Sleep can be broken down into which two major phases that alternate throughout the sleep period?","options":["Rapid-eye-movement (REM) and non-REM sleep","Light and deep only","Stage 1 and Stage 2 only","Dream and dreamless only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sleep has two major phases — rapid-eye-movement (REM) sleep and non-REM sleep — which alternate throughout the sleep period.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the two major phases described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Stage 1 NREM sleep is characterized by low-voltage mixed-frequency EEG and slow eye movement and occupies about what percentage of time asleep in healthy adults (DAMAN DIU-2018)?","options":["About 5%","About 50%","About 20%","About 10%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 1 NREM occupies about 5% of time spent asleep in healthy adults.  Why B wrong: 50% is Stage 2.  Why C wrong: 20–25% is REM.  Why D wrong: 10–20% is stages 3 and 4."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Stage 2 NREM sleep is characterized by which specific EEG waveforms and occupies about what percentage of sleep?","options":["Sleep spindles and K complexes; about 50%","Delta waves; about 10%","Slow eye movement; about 5%","Beta waves; about 25%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stage 2 NREM shows sleep spindles and K complexes and occupies about 50% of time asleep.  Why B wrong: Delta waves/10% describe stages 3-4.  Why C wrong: That describes stage 1.  Why D wrong: Incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Stages 3 and 4 NREM (slow-wave or delta sleep) are the deepest levels of sleep and occupy about what percentage of sleep time, declining to what in the elderly?","options":["About 10%–20%, declining to 0% in the elderly","About 50%, declining to 25%","About 5%, declining to 2%","About 25%, declining to 10%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stages 3 and 4 NREM (slow-wave/delta sleep) occupy about 10%-20% of sleep time, declining to 0% in the elderly.  Why B/C/D wrong: These percentages are incorrect for slow-wave sleep."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"REM sleep, during which the majority of typical story-like dreams occur, occupies about what percentage of total sleep?","options":["About 20–25%","About 5%","About 50%","About 10%"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: REM sleep occupies about 20-25% of total sleep and is when the majority of story-like dreams occur.  Why B wrong: 5% is stage 1.  Why C wrong: 50% is stage 2.  Why D wrong: 10% is minimum of slow-wave sleep."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"REM sleep periods increase in duration during which part of the night?","options":["The last half of the night, toward the morning","The first hour of sleep","Only at midnight","They remain constant all night"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: REM sleep periods increase in duration during the last half of the night, toward the morning.  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In hypochondriasis, the client is preoccupied by fear of a serious illness despite medical assurance of good health. This is also known as:","options":["Doctor shopping","Malingering","Ganser syndrome","Somnambulism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypochondriasis (preoccupation with fear of serious illness despite reassurance) is also known as Doctor shopping.  Why B wrong: Malingering is intentional feigning for gain.  Why C wrong: Ganser syndrome is a factitious disorder.  Why D wrong: Somnambulism is sleepwalking."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In circadian rhythm sleep disorder, there is a mismatch between what (GMCH-2016)?","options":["The internal sleep-wake circadian rhythm and the timing/duration of sleep","REM and NREM sleep","Melatonin and cortisol only","Body temperature and heart rate"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In circadian rhythm sleep disorder there is a mismatch between the internal sleep-wake circadian rhythm and the timing and duration of sleep.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described mismatch."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In narcolepsy, the client:","options":["Develops an overwhelming urge to sleep at any time of day regardless of previous sleep, and may fall asleep two to six times a day at inappropriate times","Cannot sleep at all","Only has nightmares","Sleepwalks nightly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In narcolepsy the client develops an overwhelming urge to sleep at any time regardless of previous sleep, and may fall asleep two to six times a day at inappropriate times (e.g. driving, attending class).  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe insomnia, nightmare disorder, and sleepwalking."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Nightmare disorder is characterized by:","options":["Recurrence of frightening dreams that cause the client to awaken from sleep","Sitting up screaming with intense anxiety","Walking during sleep","Overwhelming daytime sleepiness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Nightmare disorder is characterized by recurrence of frightening dreams that cause the client to awaken from sleep.  Why B wrong: That describes sleep terror disorder.  Why C wrong: That is sleepwalking/somnambulism.  Why D wrong: That describes narcolepsy/hypersomnia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In sleep terror disorder, the client may:","options":["Sit up in bed screaming or crying with a frightened expression and signs of intense anxiety","Have recurrent frightening dreams causing awakening","Fall asleep during the day repeatedly","Walk during sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In sleep terror disorder, the client may sit up in bed screaming or crying with a frightened expression and signs of intense anxiety.  Why B wrong: That is nightmare disorder.  Why C wrong: That is narcolepsy.  Why D wrong: That is sleepwalking."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Alcohol is classified as which type of substance, and what is a consequence of excessive intake?","options":["A sedative that creates euphoria; excessive intake causes respiratory depression and coma","A stimulant causing hyperactivity","A hallucinogen causing visual distortions only","An antipsychotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alcohol is a sedative that creates a feeling of euphoria; sedation increases with the amount ingested and respiratory depression and coma can occur with excessive intake.  Why B/C/D wrong: Alcohol is a sedative (CNS depressant), not a stimulant, hallucinogen, or antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Shifting of an emotion from its original object to a substitute is a coping mechanism called:","options":["Displacement","Denial","Projection","Regression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Shifting an emotion from its original object to a substitute is displacement.  Why B wrong: Denial is avoiding awareness of truth/reality.  Why C wrong: Projection is attributing unacceptable feelings to others.  Why D wrong: Regression is returning to earlier behaviour."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Avoiding the awareness of truth or reality is a coping mechanism called:","options":["Denial","Displacement","Rationalization","Sublimation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Avoiding awareness of truth or reality is denial.  Why B wrong: Displacement shifts emotion to a substitute.  Why C wrong: Rationalization substitutes acceptable reasons.  Why D wrong: Sublimation is not the definition given."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Creation of unrealistic or improbable images to escape from daily pressures and responsibilities is a coping mechanism called:","options":["Fantasy","Identification","Projection","Regression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Creation of unrealistic/improbable images to escape daily pressures is fantasy.  Why B wrong: Identification is adopting another person's characteristics.  Why C wrong: Projection is displacing negative feelings onto another.  Why D wrong: Regression is reverting to earlier behaviour."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Unconscious adoption of the personality characteristics, attitudes, values, and behaviour of another person is called:","options":["Identification","Fantasy","Rationalization","Repression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Unconscious adoption of another person's personality characteristics/attitudes/values/behaviour is identification.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe fantasy, giving excuses, and excluding thoughts from consciousness respectively."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Displacement of negative feelings onto another person is a coping mechanism called:","options":["Projection","Identification","Regression","Repression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Displacement of negative feelings onto another person is projection (also described as attributing unacceptable feelings to others).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Substitution of acceptable reasons for the real or actual reasons motivating behaviour is a coping mechanism called:","options":["Rationalization","Regression","Repression","Projection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Substitution of acceptable reasons for the real reasons motivating behaviour is rationalization (an adaptive mechanism of giving excuses when a person cannot solve a problem).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Returning to the behaviour of an earlier, less worrisome time in life (like a child) is a coping mechanism called:","options":["Regression","Repression","Fantasy","Identification"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Returning to the behaviour of an earlier/childlike, less worrisome time is regression.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Exclusion of unacceptable thoughts and feelings from the conscious mind, leaving the client to operate in the subconscious, is called:","options":["Repression","Denial","Displacement","Rationalization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Repression is the exclusion of unacceptable thoughts and feelings from the conscious mind, leaving the client to operate in the subconscious (placing uncomfortable thoughts in relatively inaccessible areas of the subconscious).  Why B wrong: Denial avoids awareness of reality.  Why C wrong: Displacement shifts emotion to a substitute.  Why D wrong: Rationalization substitutes acceptable reasons."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Attributing unacceptable feelings to others is a mechanism known as:","options":["Projection","Introjection","Sublimation","Isolation"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Attributing unacceptable feelings to others is projection.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different mechanisms; isolation separates thought from feeling."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Intellectualization is best described as:","options":["Avoiding expressing actual emotions in a stressful situation by using logic, reasoning, and analysis (focusing on facts)","Returning to childlike behaviour","Adopting another's personality","Excluding thoughts from consciousness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Intellectualization is an attempt to avoid expressing actual emotions associated with a stressful situation by using the intellectual process of logic, reasoning, and analysis; it is a defence mechanism in which the client avoids dealing with emotions by focusing on facts.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe regression, identification, and repression."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Separation of a thought or memory from its associated feeling or emotion is known as which defence mechanism?","options":["Isolation","Displacement","Projection","Denial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Separation of a thought or memory from its associated feeling or emotion is isolation.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct mechanisms."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Splitting, a defence mechanism commonly seen in clients with personality disorders, involves:","options":["Perceiving the world as all good or all bad","Attributing feelings to others","Returning to childlike behaviour","Focusing on facts to avoid emotions"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Splitting is a defence mechanism common in personality disorders in which the world is perceived as all good or all bad.  Why B wrong: That is projection.  Why C wrong: That is regression.  Why D wrong: That is intellectualization."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Transference is best defined as:","options":["The unconscious assignment of negative or positive feelings evoked by a significant person in the client's past to another person","Separation of thought from feeling","The nurse's feelings toward the client","Perceiving all good or all bad"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Transference is the unconscious assignment of negative or positive feelings, evoked by a significant person in the client's past, to another person.  Why B wrong: That is isolation.  Why C wrong: That describes countertransference.  Why D wrong: That is splitting."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Ego Defense mechanism is a mechanism designed to:","options":["Reduce stress and conflict caused by specific experiences","Enhance cognition","Improve memory storage","Increase emotional expression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ego defense mechanism is designed to reduce stress and conflict caused by specific experiences.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the purpose described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The most commonly used defensive mechanism by a physically abusive individual is:","options":["Displacement","Projection","Regression","Denial"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most commonly used defensive mechanism by a physically abusive individual is displacement.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other mechanisms not cited for the physical abuser."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Auditory hallucination is the most common type of hallucination experienced by patients with:","options":["Mental illness","Diabetes","Hypertension","Renal failure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Auditory hallucination is the most common type of hallucination experienced by patients with mental illness.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not associated with auditory hallucination as the primary type."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A fixed false belief of a person which cannot be changed by logical reasoning is known as:","options":["Delusion","Hallucination","Illusion","Obsession"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A fixed false belief that cannot be changed by logical reasoning is a delusion.  Why B wrong: Hallucination is a false sensory perception.  Why C wrong: An illusion is a misperception of a real stimulus.  Why D wrong: An obsession is an unwanted intrusive thought."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The belief that oneself, a part of one's body, or the real world does not exist is known as:","options":["Nihilistic delusion","Persecutory delusion","Grandiose delusion","Erotic delusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The belief that oneself, a part of one's body, or the real world does not exist is a nihilistic delusion.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other delusion types."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Catharsis is best defined as:","options":["The process of releasing strong emotions through a particular activity or experience (open expression of previously suppressed feelings)","A false fixed belief","Repetition of heard words","Coining of new words"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catharsis is the process of releasing strong emotions through a particular activity/experience — an open expression of previously suppressed feelings.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe delusion, echolalia, and neologism."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Confabulation is best described as:","options":["A symptom of various memory disorders in which made-up stories fill in gaps in memory","A fixed false belief","Inability to sit still","Loss of pleasure"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Confabulation is a symptom of various memory disorders in which made-up stories fill in gaps in memory.  Why B wrong: That is delusion.  Why C wrong: That is akathisia.  Why D wrong: That is anhedonia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Catatonia is defined as:","options":["A state of psychomotor immobility and behavioural abnormality manifested by stupor, triggered by emotions such as laughing/crying","Motor restlessness","Repetition of movements","Fear of public places"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catatonia is a state of psychomotor immobility and behavioural abnormality manifested by stupor triggered by emotions such as laughing, crying, etc.  Why B wrong: That is akathisia.  Why C wrong: That is echopraxia.  Why D wrong: That is agoraphobia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A state of diminished consciousness in which the patient remains mute and still with the eyes open is called:","options":["Stupor","Coma","Catalepsy","Delirium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Stupor is a state of diminished consciousness in which the patient remains mute and still with the eyes open.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are different altered states."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is the most common cause of dementia?","options":["Alzheimer's disease","Vascular dementia","Parkinson's disease","Traumatic brain injury"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common cause of dementia is Alzheimer's disease.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are less common causes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which test is considered the best test for diagnosis of organic mental disorder?","options":["Bender gestalt test","Rorschach test","Thematic Apperception Test","Beck Depression Inventory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The best test for diagnosis of organic mental disorder is the Bender gestalt test.  Why B/C wrong: Rorschach and TAT are projective personality tests.  Why D wrong: BDI diagnoses depression severity."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Rorschach Test and Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) are what type of tests, used to assess personality structure and mental disorders?","options":["Projective tests","Objective IQ tests","Neuroimaging tests","Blood tests"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Rorschach Test and TAT are projective tests that assess personality structure, pattern of thoughts, emotional problems, and mental disorders; the Rorschach projective test is designed to measure unconscious intentions.  Why B/C/D wrong: They are not objective IQ, imaging, or blood tests."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The extreme feeling of happiness is known as:","options":["Euphoria","Anhedonia","Apathy","Dysphoria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The extreme feeling of happiness is known as euphoria.  Why B wrong: Anhedonia is inability to experience pleasure.  Why C wrong: Apathy is lack of emotional feeling.  Why D wrong: Dysphoria is a state of unease/dissatisfaction."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"When a patient is not aware of his/her own mental disorder, it is called:","options":["Lack of insight","Denial","Confabulation","Anosognosia only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: When a patient is not aware of his/her mental disorder, it is called lack of insight.  Why B wrong: Denial is a defence mechanism avoiding reality.  Why C wrong: Confabulation is filling memory gaps.  Why D wrong: The source term is \"lack of insight.\""},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A person who is aloof, isolated, and does not socialize is considered to be:","options":["Withdrawn","Manic","Euphoric","Impulsive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A person who is aloof, isolated, and does not socialize is considered withdrawn.  Why B/C/D wrong: These describe other states."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Verbal utterance that is often obscene is known as:","options":["Coprolalia","Echolalia","Neologism","Perseveration"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Verbal utterance that is often obscene is coprolalia.  Why B wrong: Echolalia is repetition of words spoken by others.  Why C wrong: Neologism is coining new meaningless words.  Why D wrong: Perseveration is inappropriate repetition of the same thoughts."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Echolalia is defined as:","options":["The repetition of the words spoken by others","The repetition of movements performed by others","The coining of new words","Obscene verbal utterance"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Echolalia is the repetition of the words spoken by others (repetition of heard words).  Why B wrong: That is echopraxia.  Why C wrong: That is neologism.  Why D wrong: That is coprolalia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Echopraxia is defined as:","options":["The repetition of movements performed by others","The repetition of heard words","Invented new words","Obscene speech"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Echopraxia is the repetition of movements performed by others.  Why B wrong: That is echolalia.  Why C wrong: That is neologism.  Why D wrong: That is coprolalia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Neologism is defined as:","options":["The coining of new words without any meaning (invented or new words)","Repetition of heard words","Fear of public places","Fixed false belief"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Neologism is the coining of new words without any meaning (invented or new words).  Why B wrong: That is echolalia.  Why C wrong: That is agoraphobia.  Why D wrong: That is delusion."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Persistent and inappropriate repetition of the same thoughts is known as:","options":["Perseveration","Echolalia","Confabulation","Flight of ideas"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Persistent and inappropriate repetition of the same thoughts is perseveration.  Why B wrong: Echolalia is repetition of heard words.  Why C wrong: Confabulation fills memory gaps.  Why D wrong: Flight of ideas is rapid topic changes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"An unwanted thought that a person finds intrusive and distressing is called:","options":["Obsession","Compulsion","Delusion","Hallucination"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: An unwanted thought a person finds intrusive and distressing is an obsession.  Why B wrong: A compulsion is a behaviour one feels compelled to perform.  Why C/D wrong: These are a fixed false belief and a false perception."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A behaviour that a person feels compelled to perform is defined as:","options":["Compulsion","Obsession","Tic","Mannerism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A behaviour a person feels compelled to perform is a compulsion.  Why B wrong: An obsession is an intrusive thought.  Why C/D wrong: These are different phenomena."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which test, also called a sleep study, is used to diagnose sleep disorders?","options":["Polysomnography","Electroencephalography alone","Bender gestalt test","Rorschach test"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Polysomnography, also called a sleep study, is used to diagnose sleep disorders.  Why B wrong: EEG alone is a component but not the full sleep study named.  Why C/D wrong: These assess organic mental disorder and personality, respectively."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Most vivid dreams occur during which sleep phase?","options":["REM sleep","Stage 1 NREM","Stage 2 NREM","Slow-wave (delta) sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most vivid dreams occur during REM sleep.  Why B/C/D wrong: Vivid, story-like dreams are characteristic of REM, not NREM stages."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Penile erection or clitoris engorgement occurs during which phase of sleep?","options":["REM sleep","Stage 1 NREM","Slow-wave sleep","Stage 2 NREM"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Penile erection or clitoris engorgement occurs during REM sleep.  Why B/C/D wrong: These NREM stages are not when this occurs."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"NREM sleep is also known by which other name(s)?","options":["Orthodox sleep or Synchronized ('S') sleep","Paradoxical sleep","Desynchronized sleep","Dream sleep"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NREM sleep is also known as Orthodox sleep or Synchronized ('S') sleep.  Why B/C/D wrong: Paradoxical/desynchronized/dream sleep are terms for REM."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Restless leg syndrome (Ekbom's syndrome) is a painful condition that leads to:","options":["Severe insomnia and periodic limb movement during sleep","Daytime sleep attacks","Sleepwalking","Nightmares"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Restless leg syndrome (Ekbom's syndrome) is a painful condition leading to severe insomnia and periodic limb movement during sleep.  Why B wrong: That is narcolepsy.  Why C/D wrong: These are parasomnias, not RLS."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Willis Ekbom Syndrome is also known as (BPL AIIMS-2018):","options":["Restless legs syndrome","Kleine-Levin syndrome","Ganser syndrome","Sundowners syndrome"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Willis Ekbom Syndrome is also known as restless legs syndrome.  Why B wrong: Kleine-Levin is a hypersomnolence disorder in teenage boys.  Why C wrong: Ganser is a factitious disorder.  Why D wrong: Sundowners is evening agitation in dementia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Sleep walking is otherwise called:","options":["Somnambulism","Somniloquy","Bruxism","Cataplexy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sleep walking is otherwise called somnambulism.  Why B wrong: Somniloquy is sleep talking.  Why C wrong: Bruxism is teeth grinding.  Why D wrong: Cataplexy is sudden loss of muscle tone."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Systematic desensitization was developed by whom, and is useful for which conditions?","options":["Joseph Wolpe; phobic anxiety disorder, OCD, and certain sexual disorders","Ivan Pavlov; alcoholism only","Sigmund Freud; depression only","B.F. Skinner; mania only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe and is useful for phobic anxiety disorder, OCD, and certain sexual disorders.  Why B/C/D wrong: These attributions and indications are incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Flooding (implosion) is the treatment of choice for which condition?","options":["Phobia","Bipolar disorder","Schizophrenia","Dementia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Flooding or implosion is a useful therapy for phobia and is the choice of therapy for phobia (the patient is suddenly exposed to a direct and maximum fear-producing stimulus).  Why B/C/D wrong: Flooding is not indicated for these."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Extinction is a behavioural therapy technique especially useful in treating:","options":["Temper tantrum","Phobia","Alcoholism","Depression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extinction is a behavioural therapy technique useful in temper tantrum.  Why B wrong: Flooding is for phobia.  Why C wrong: Aversion therapy is for alcoholism.  Why D wrong: Not the indication cited."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Repetitive, uncontrollable thoughts and acts are termed as:","options":["Obsessive-compulsive disorders (OCDs)","Phobias","Delusions","Manic episodes"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Repetitive, uncontrollable thoughts and acts are termed obsessive-compulsive disorders (OCDs).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct conditions."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Aversion therapy is useful for which conditions? (Select all that apply)","options":["Alcoholism","Impulsive behaviour","Paraphilias","Dementia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Aversion therapy is useful for alcoholism, impulsive behaviour, and paraphilias.  Why D wrong: Dementia is not an indication for aversion therapy."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Aversive conditioning is defined as the eradication of unwanted habits by:","options":["Associating an unpleasant condition with the habit","Rewarding the habit","Ignoring the habit","Exposing to maximum fear"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Eradication of unwanted habits by associating an unpleasant condition with it is aversive conditioning.  Why B/C wrong: These would not eradicate the habit.  Why D wrong: That describes flooding."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which antipsychotic was discovered in 1950 and was the first antipsychotic?","options":["Chlorpromazine (typical antipsychotic)","Haloperidol","Clozapine","Fluphenazine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Chlorpromazine (a typical antipsychotic) was discovered in 1950 and was the first antipsychotic.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are later antipsychotics.  Note: Chlorpromazine was synthesized in 1950 (Charpentier) and first introduced into psychiatric/antipsychotic clinical use in 1952 (Delay & Deniker); \"discovered in 1950\" per the source refers to the synthesis year."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS) are most common with which drugs?","options":["Fluphenazine and Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine and Clozapine","Lithium and Valproate","Benztropine and Bromocriptine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Extrapyramidal symptoms are most common with Fluphenazine and Haloperidol.  Why B wrong: Those are associated more with orthostatic hypotension/agranulocytosis.  Why C wrong: Mood stabilizers, not primary EPS causes here.  Why D wrong: These treat EPS/NMS rather than cause EPS."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Orthostatic hypotension is most common with which antipsychotics? (Select all that apply)","options":["Chlorpromazine","Thioridazine","Clozapine","Haloperidol"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Orthostatic hypotension is most common with Chlorpromazine, Thioridazine, and Clozapine.  Why D wrong: Haloperidol is cited for EPS, not primarily orthostatic hypotension."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Agranulocytosis is more common with typical than atypical antipsychotics, with which notable exception?","options":["Clozapine","Haloperidol","Fluphenazine","Chlorpromazine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Agranulocytosis is more common with typical than atypical antipsychotics except Clozapine (an atypical) whose major side effect is agranulocytosis.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited exception."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The major side effect of Clozapine is agranulocytosis. Therefore, what monitoring is suggested?","options":["Periodical complete blood count (CBC)","Serum lithium levels","Liver function tests only","Blood glucose only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Because Clozapine's major side effect is agranulocytosis, periodical complete blood count is suggested (blood screening for CBC required — AIIMS Jodhpur-2017).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the specific monitoring for agranulocytosis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is the treatment of choice for Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome (NMS)?","options":["Bromocriptine or dantrolene","Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Lithium"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Bromocriptine or dantrolene is the treatment of choice for NMS.  Why B/C wrong: Antipsychotics cause NMS, not treat it.  Why D wrong: Lithium treats bipolar mania."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which drug class (e.g. Benztropine) is used to treat extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS)?","options":["Anticholinergics","Beta-blockers","Antidepressants","Mood stabilizers"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Anticholinergics (e.g. Benztropine) are used to treat extrapyramidal symptoms (EPS).  Why B/C/D wrong: These classes are not cited for EPS treatment."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is the drug of choice for treatment and management of bipolar mania?","options":["Lithium carbonate","Haloperidol","Chlorpromazine","Clozapine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The drug of choice for treatment and management of bipolar mania is lithium carbonate.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are antipsychotics, not the drug of choice for bipolar mania per source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A patient on lithium therapy should be advised to drink how much water daily and follow which diet?","options":["6 to 8 large glasses of water daily and a high sodium diet","2 glasses of water daily and a low sodium diet","Restrict all fluids and follow a low sodium diet","Unlimited caffeine and a low sodium diet"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A patient on lithium therapy should drink 6 to 8 large glasses of water each day, avoid excessive caffeine (coffee, tea, colas) which promotes increased urine output, and maintain adequate sodium intake (the source's \"high sodium diet\").  Why B/C/D wrong: Contradict the lithium precautions — low sodium increases lithium toxicity (lithium and sodium compete for renal reabsorption, so sodium depletion raises lithium levels), and fluids/caffeine are misstated.  Note: The precise, evidence-based teaching is to maintain a NORMAL, CONSISTENT (adequate) sodium and fluid intake rather than a deliberately high-sodium diet; a sudden increase in sodium can push lithium below the therapeutic level, while a drop in sodium (or dehydration) precipitates toxicity. Option A is keyed because it is the only choice that does not recommend the dangerous \"low sodium diet.\""},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is the therapeutic serum level of lithium?","options":["0.8–1.2 mEq/L","0.4–0.6 mEq/L","1.5–2.0 mEq/L","2.0–2.5 mEq/L"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The therapeutic serum level of lithium is 0.8–1.2 mEq/L.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are outside the stated therapeutic range (higher levels risk toxicity)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The goal of Milieu therapy is to:","options":["Manipulate the environment so that all aspects of the client's hospital experience are considered therapeutic","Release suppressed emotions","Expose the client to maximum fear","Associate a habit with an unpleasant stimulus"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The goal of milieu therapy is to manipulate the environment so all aspects of the client's hospital experience are considered therapeutic (the nurse utilizes all aspects of the hospital environment therapeutically).  Why B wrong: That is catharsis.  Why C wrong: That is flooding.  Why D wrong: That is aversive conditioning."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The type of loss in which the client is aware that a loss will occur is known as:","options":["Anticipatory loss","Actual loss","Perceived loss","Maturational loss"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The type of loss in which the client is aware that a loss will occur is anticipatory loss.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are other categories of loss."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Kleine-Levin syndrome is a rare sleep disorder mainly affecting which group, with which main feature?","options":["Teenage boys; intermittent hypersomnolence","Elderly women; insomnia","Infants; night terrors","Middle-aged men; narcolepsy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Kleine-Levin syndrome is a rare sleep disorder mainly affecting teenage boys, with the main feature being intermittent hypersomnolence.  Why B/C/D wrong: Incorrect population/features."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Ganser syndrome is a type of which disorder?","options":["Factitious disorder, in which a person deliberately and consciously acts as if he/she has a physical or mental illness when not really sick","Mood disorder","Psychotic disorder","Cognitive disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Ganser syndrome is a type of factitious disorder in which a person deliberately and consciously acts as if they have a physical or mental illness when not really sick.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is a factitious disorder, not these categories."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The immediate nursing intervention for a patient who has just awakened from an electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) treatment is to:","options":["Orient the patient","Offer a drink immediately","Ambulate the patient at once","Administer a sedative"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The immediate nursing intervention for a patient who has just awakened from ECT is to orient the patient.  Why B wrong: Gag reflex must be checked before giving a drink.  Why C/D wrong: Not the immediate priority."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Freud's topographical model of the mind consists of which three levels?","options":["Conscious, preconscious, unconscious","Id, ego, superego","Oral, anal, genital","Sensory, motor, cognitive"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Freud's topographical model of mind consists of conscious, preconscious, and unconscious.  Why B wrong: Id/ego/superego is the structural model.  Why C/D wrong: These are stages/domains, not the topographical model."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Who is regarded as the \"Father of Intelligence Test\"?","options":["Alfred Binet","Charles Spearman","Sigmund Freud","Christian Reil"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Father of Intelligence Test is Alfred Binet.  Why B wrong: Spearman proposed the two-factor theory of intelligence.  Why C wrong: Freud is father of psychoanalysis.  Why D wrong: Reil coined the term \"psychiatry.\""},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Which examination measures orientation, registration, recall, calculation, language, and motor skills?","options":["The Mini-Mental Status Examination (MMSE)","The Cognitive Capacity Screening Examination","The Global Deterioration Scale","The Beck Depression Inventory"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Mini-Mental Status Examination measures orientation, registration, recall, calculation, language, and motor skills.  Why B wrong: The Cognitive Capacity Screening Examination measures orientation, memory, calculation, and language (no registration/motor skills).  Why C wrong: The GDS stages degenerative dementia.  Why D wrong: BDI assesses depression."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Cognitive Capacity Screening Examination measures which of the following?","options":["Orientation, memory, calculation, and language","Orientation, registration, recall, calculation, language, and motor skills","Only motor skills","Only affect"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Cognitive Capacity Screening Examination measures orientation, memory, calculation, and language.  Why B wrong: That is the MMSE.  Why C/D wrong: Incomplete/incorrect."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Global Deterioration Scale assesses and stages primary degenerative dementia based on which parameters?","options":["Orientation, memory, and neurologic function","Affect and activities of daily living","Personality traits and ego function","Eating patterns"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Global Deterioration Scale assesses and stages primary degenerative dementia based on orientation, memory, and neurologic function.  Why B wrong: That describes the Functional Dementia Scale.  Why C wrong: That describes the MMPI.  Why D wrong: Eating patterns relate to the Eating Attitudes Test."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Beck Depression Inventory helps to do which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Diagnose depression","Determine its severity","Monitor the client's response during treatment","Stage degenerative dementia"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The Beck Depression Inventory helps diagnose depression, determine its severity, and monitor the client's response during treatment.  Why D wrong: Staging degenerative dementia is done by the Global Deterioration Scale."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Eating Attitudes Test is used to detect patterns that suggest which condition?","options":["An eating disorder","Depression","Dementia","A sleep disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Eating Attitudes Test detects patterns that suggest an eating disorder.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are assessed by other tools."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) helps assess what, in adults and adolescents?","options":["Personality traits and ego function","Depression severity only","Sleep architecture","Motor skills only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory helps assess personality traits and ego function in adults and adolescents.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are measured by other tools."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"In conversion disorder, anxiety is expressed through:","options":["Physical symptoms","Verbal aggression","Delusions","Hallucinations"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: In conversion disorder, anxiety is expressed through physical symptoms.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the mode of expression in conversion disorder."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The ability of a person to readily recover from stressful situations is called:","options":["Resilience","Rationalization","Regression","Repression"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ability to readily recover from stressful situations is resilience.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are defence mechanisms, not recovery ability."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The ideal size for group therapy is:","options":["8 to 10 members","2 to 4 members","15 to 20 members","25 to 30 members"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The ideal size for group therapy is 8 to 10 members.  Why B/C/D wrong: These fall outside the stated ideal range."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is the most common symptom of alcohol withdrawal?","options":["Tremor","Seizure","Hallucination","Coma"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common symptom of alcohol withdrawal is tremor.  Why B/C/D wrong: These may occur but are not cited as the most common."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Korsakoff's psychosis, a long-term effect complication of alcoholism, occurs due to deficiency of which vitamin?","options":["Vitamin Thiamine (B1)","Vitamin B12","Vitamin C","Vitamin D"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Korsakoff's psychosis is a long-term complication of alcoholism occurring due to deficiency of Vitamin Thiamine (B1).  Why B/C/D wrong: These deficiencies are not the cited cause."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is described as the only effective treatment for alcoholism, particularly in short-term alcoholic addicts?","options":["Total abstinence","Gradual reduction","Substitution therapy","Aversion therapy alone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Total abstinence is the only effective treatment for alcoholism, particularly in short-term alcoholic addicts.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not cited as the only effective treatment."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Patients who are on haloperidol are advised not to drive or operate machines due to which side effects?","options":["Dizziness and drowsiness","Hypertension and tachycardia","Diarrhoea and polyuria","Weight loss and insomnia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Patients on haloperidol are advised not to drive or operate machines due to the side effects of dizziness and drowsiness.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the cited side effects for this precaution."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"What is the antidote for methanol poisoning?","options":["Ethanol (drinking alcohol)","Naloxone","Flumazenil","Atropine"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The antidote for methanol is ethanol (drinking alcohol).  Why B wrong: Naloxone is the antidote for opioids.  Why C wrong: Flumazenil is the antidote for benzodiazepines.  Why D wrong: Atropine is used for organophosphate poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Atypical antipsychotics, compared to typical antipsychotics, have:","options":["A lower incidence of extrapyramidal effects","A higher incidence of extrapyramidal effects","No side effects at all","Higher risk of tardive dyskinesia always"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Atypical antipsychotics have a lower incidence of extrapyramidal effects than the typical antipsychotics.  Why B/D wrong: EPS incidence is lower with atypicals.  Why C wrong: Atypicals still have side effects (e.g. clozapine's agranulocytosis)."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Antipsychotic drugs are also known by which names (ESIC-2019)?","options":["Neuroleptic drugs / tranquilizers","Anxiolytics","Mood stabilizers","Antidepressants"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Antipsychotic drugs are also known as neuroleptic drugs or tranquilizers.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are distinct drug classes."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Tardive dyskinesia is a side effect of what?","options":["Long-term use of certain antipsychotics","Short-term lithium use","Antidepressant overdose","Alcohol withdrawal"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Tardive dyskinesia is a side effect of long-term use of certain antipsychotics.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the cause of tardive dyskinesia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) is a life-threatening neurological disorder most often caused by:","options":["An adverse reaction to antipsychotic drugs (e.g. haloperidol), associated with rapid rise in temperature","Lithium toxicity","Alcohol withdrawal","Vitamin deficiency"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: NMS is a life-threatening neurological disorder most often caused by an adverse reaction to antipsychotic drugs such as haloperidol and is associated with a rapid increase in temperature.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cause of NMS."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is most commonly used to treat which condition, especially in clients unresponsive to antidepressants (AIIMS Raipur 2018)?","options":["Major depression","Schizophrenia","Dementia","Mania only"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ECT is most commonly used to treat major depression in clients who haven't responded to antidepressants or who have medical problems that contraindicate antidepressants.  Why B/C/D wrong: Major depression is the primary cited indication."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Analysis of free association is characteristic of which approach?","options":["Freudian psychoanalysis","Classical conditioning","Milieu therapy","Aversion therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Analysis of free association is characteristic of Freudian psychoanalysis.  Why B wrong: Classical conditioning is a pure behavioural intervention.  Why C/D wrong: These are other therapies."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Classical conditioning is characteristic of what type of intervention?","options":["A pure behavioural intervention","A psychoanalytic intervention","A cognitive intervention","A pharmacological intervention"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Classical conditioning is characteristic of a pure behavioural intervention.  Why B/C/D wrong: It is behavioural, not psychoanalytic, cognitive, or pharmacological."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Nonverbal clues to suicidal ideation include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Giving away personal possessions","A sudden calmness","Risk-taking behaviours","Increased appetite"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Nonverbal clues to suicidal ideation include giving away personal possessions, a sudden calmness, and risk-taking behaviours.  Why D wrong: Increased appetite is not listed as a suicidal clue."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Clients with major depression generally don't exhibit suicidal behaviour until:","options":["Their outlook on their problems begins to improve","They are discharged","They stop medication","They begin ECT"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients with major depression generally don't exhibit suicidal behaviour until their outlook on their problems begins to improve (they regain energy to act).  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited timing."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Common causes of delirium include which of the following? (Select all that apply)","options":["Postoperative conditions or metabolic disorders","Withdrawal from alcohol and drugs","Exposure to toxic substances","Normal aging alone"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: Common causes of delirium include postoperative conditions or metabolic disorders, withdrawal from alcohol and drugs, and exposure to toxic substances.  Why D wrong: Normal aging alone is not a cited cause of delirium."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"A client with Alzheimer's-type dementia suffers from a global impairment of which functions?","options":["Cognitive functioning, memory, and personality","Motor and sensory function only","Cardiovascular function","Renal function"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A client with Alzheimer's-type dementia suffers from a global impairment of cognitive functioning, memory, and personality.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the impairments described."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The dementia in Alzheimer's-type dementia occurs in what manner?","options":["Gradually but with continuous decline","Suddenly and reversibly","Only after age 90","In a stepwise, fully reversible pattern"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Alzheimer's-type dementia occurs gradually but with continuous decline.  Why B wrong: Sudden/reversible describes delirium.  Why C/D wrong: Not accurate to the source."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Damage from Alzheimer's-type dementia is (Daman & Diu-2018, IGIMS-2018):","options":["Irreversible","Fully reversible","Reversible with vitamins","Reversible with abstinence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Damage from Alzheimer's-type dementia is irreversible.  Why B/C/D wrong: The damage cannot be reversed."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Most health care providers categorize how many stages of Alzheimer's-type dementia?","options":["Three stages: mild, moderate, and severe","Two stages: early and late","Four stages","Five stages"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Most health care providers categorize three stages of Alzheimer's-type dementia: mild, moderate, and severe.  Why B/C/D wrong: The source cites three stages."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Causes of Alzheimer's-type dementia include alterations in which neurotransmitter level (AIIMS Delhi-2017)?","options":["Acetylcholine","Dopamine","Serotonin","GABA"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Causes of Alzheimer's-type dementia include alterations in acetylcholine levels.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are not the cited neurotransmitter alteration."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Delirium is characterized by:","options":["An acute onset lasting from several hours to several days; potentially reversible but can be life-threatening if not treated","A gradual, irreversible decline","Permanent memory loss with no reversibility","Onset only in childhood"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium is characterized by an acute onset that may last from several hours to several days, is potentially reversible, but can be life-threatening if not treated.  Why B/C wrong: Those describe dementia.  Why D wrong: Not accurate."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Vascular dementia is caused by:","options":["An irreversible alteration in brain function that damages or destroys brain tissue","A reversible metabolic disturbance","Thiamine deficiency","Antipsychotic drugs"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Vascular dementia is caused by an irreversible alteration in brain function that damages or destroys brain tissue.  Why B wrong: It is irreversible, not a reversible disturbance.  Why C wrong: Thiamine deficiency causes Korsakoff's psychosis.  Why D wrong: Antipsychotics cause tardive dyskinesia/NMS, not vascular dementia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Akathisia is best defined as:","options":["Motor restlessness and inability to sit still","Inability to experience pleasure","Lack of emotional feeling","Inability to recall past experience"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Akathisia is motor restlessness and inability to sit still.  Why B wrong: That is anhedonia.  Why C wrong: That is apathy.  Why D wrong: That is amnesia."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Amnesia is best defined as:","options":["Partial or total inability to recall past experience, which may be organic or emotional","Loss of pleasure","Motor restlessness","Lack of insight"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Amnesia is the partial or total inability to recall past experience, which may be organic or emotional.  Why B wrong: That is anhedonia.  Why C wrong: That is akathisia.  Why D wrong: That is lack of insight."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Apathy is defined as:","options":["Lack of emotional feeling","Inability to sit still","Inability to recall past events","Extreme happiness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apathy is lack of emotional feeling.  Why B wrong: That is akathisia.  Why C wrong: That is amnesia.  Why D wrong: That is euphoria."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Cognitive impairment is typically associated with which conditions?","options":["Delirium or dementia","Mania or hypomania","Phobia or panic disorder","Anorexia or bulimia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Cognitive impairment is typically associated with delirium or dementia.  Why B/C/D wrong: These are mood, anxiety, and eating disorders, not primarily cognitive."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Persistent drug use can result in which two phenomena?","options":["Tolerance and withdrawal","Euphoria and insight","Resilience and abstinence","Catharsis and transference"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Persistent drug use can result in tolerance and withdrawal.  Why B/C/D wrong: These do not describe consequences of persistent drug use."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Verbalizing the relationship between stress and behaviours indicates that the client:","options":["Understands the disease process","Is in denial","Is experiencing a manic episode","Has cognitive impairment"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Verbalizing the relationship between stress and behaviours indicates the client understands the disease process.  Why B/C/D wrong: These contradict the insight demonstrated."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"The client with a sleep disorder commonly suffers from which of the following?","options":["Excessive daytime sleepiness and impaired ability to perform daily tasks safely","Improved concentration","Weight gain only","Hypervigilance and euphoria"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The client with a sleep disorder commonly suffers from excessive daytime sleepiness and impaired ability to perform daily tasks safely or properly.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the cited consequences."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Sexual dysfunctions are characterized by:","options":["A deficiency or loss of desire for sexual activity or a disturbance in the sexual response cycle","Excessive libido always","Exposing genitals in public","Fear of illness"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sexual dysfunctions are characterized by a deficiency or loss of desire for sexual activity or a disturbance in the sexual response cycle.  Why B wrong: Not the definition.  Why C wrong: That is exhibitionism (a paraphilia).  Why D wrong: That is hypochondriasis."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Clients with gender identity disorders characteristically:","options":["Want to become or be like the opposite sex and are extremely uncomfortable with their assigned gender roles","Have no concern about gender","Experience visual hallucinations","Fear public places"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clients with gender identity disorders want to become or be like the opposite sex and are extremely uncomfortable with their assigned gender roles.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the described features."},{"section":"Batch 008 · Psychology/psychiatry theories & theorists, MHA 2017 & lunacy acts…","question":"Dyspareunia (painful coitus) most commonly occurs during which activity, though it may also occur before or after?","options":["Intercourse","Sleep","Eating","Exercise"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dyspareunia is most commonly experienced during intercourse but may also occur before or after intercourse.  Why B/C/D wrong: Not the associated activity."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which of the following is considered the foundation for developing a nurse-client relationship in psychiatric nursing?","options":["Pharmacotherapy","Therapeutic communication","Electroconvulsive therapy","Family therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The page states that therapeutic communication is the foundation for developing a nurse-client relationship.  Why A wrong: Pharmacotherapy treats symptoms but is not the basis of the relationship.  Why C wrong: ECT is a biological treatment, not a relationship foundation.  Why D wrong: Family therapy is a treatment modality, not the basis of the nurse-client relationship."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Biological therapy in psychiatric nursing includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Psychoactive drugs","Electroconvulsive therapy","Non-convulsive electrical stimulation","Group psychotherapy"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Group psychotherapy is a psychosocial therapy, not a biological therapy.  Why A wrong: Psychoactive drugs are listed as biological therapy.  Why B wrong: ECT is listed as biological therapy.  Why C wrong: Non-convulsive electrical stimulation is listed as biological therapy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The CAGE Questionnaire consists of how many questions and is used to screen for which condition? (NIMHANS-2019)","options":["Two questions; depression","Four questions; alcoholism","Six questions; anxiety","Ten questions; dementia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The CAGE Questionnaire includes four questions, in which two or three positive responses indicate alcoholism.  Why A wrong: It has four questions, not two, and screens for alcoholism.  Why C wrong: It has four questions and does not screen for anxiety.  Why D wrong: It is a four-item tool, not a ten-item dementia scale."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"In the CAGE Questionnaire, how many positive responses are required to indicate alcoholism?","options":["One positive response","Two or three positive responses","All four positive responses only","Zero positive responses"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Two or three positive responses on the CAGE indicate alcoholism.  Why A wrong: A single positive response is not the stated threshold.  Why C wrong: All four are not required; two or three suffice.  Why D wrong: Zero responses indicate no problem."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which therapy involves the entire family to improve family function?","options":["Group therapy","Family therapy","Milieu therapy","Cognitive therapy"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Family therapy involves the entire family to improve family function.  Why A wrong: Group therapy involves unrelated members to increase self-awareness.  Why C wrong: Milieu therapy uses the environment as treatment.  Why D wrong: Cognitive therapy targets thought patterns of an individual."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Group therapy aims to achieve all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["Increase self-awareness","Change maladaptive behaviours","Improve interpersonal relationships","Replace the need for individual medication"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: Replacing medication is not a stated aim of group therapy.  Why A wrong: Increasing self-awareness is a stated aim.  Why B wrong: Changing maladaptive behaviours is a stated aim.  Why C wrong: Improving interpersonal relationships is a stated aim."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"In which therapeutic communication technique does the nurse attempt to describe the client's feelings and then seek corrective feedback from the client?","options":["Reflecting","Sharing impressions","Silence","Confrontation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: In \"sharing impressions\" the nurse describes the client's feelings and seeks corrective feedback from the client.  Why A wrong: Reflecting directs the question back to the client, not describing feelings for feedback.  Why C wrong: Silence provides time without describing feelings.  Why D wrong: Confrontation points out discrepancies, not sharing perceived feelings for verification."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"During a physical examination of a psychiatric client, the nurse should assess all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["General appearance and behaviour","Mood and thought processes","Cognitive function and coping mechanisms","Family's financial income status"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists appearance, behaviour, mood, thought processes, cognitive function, coping mechanisms, and potential for self-destructive behaviour — not financial income.  Why A wrong: General appearance and behaviour are assessed.  Why B wrong: Mood and thought processes are assessed.  Why C wrong: Cognitive function and coping mechanisms are assessed."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"What does the abbreviation DSM stand for in psychiatry?","options":["Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders","Descriptive Symptom Measurement","Diagnostic Standard for Mental Health","Disease Screening Module"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DSM stands for the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, which describes diagnostic criteria for each diagnosis.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the recognized expansion of DSM."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which DSM-IV-TR axis describes personality disorders and mental retardation?","options":["Axis I","Axis II","Axis IV","Axis V"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: DSM-IV-TR Axis-II describes personality disorders and mental retardation.  Why A wrong: Axis I covers clinical disorders (not specified here).  Why C wrong: Axis IV covers psychosocial and environmental problems.  Why D wrong: Axis V is the global assessment of functioning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Psychosocial and environmental problems are described under which DSM-IV-TR axis?","options":["Axis II","Axis III","Axis IV","Axis V"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: DSM-IV-TR Axis-IV describes psychosocial and environmental problems.  Why A wrong: Axis II covers personality disorders and mental retardation.  Why B wrong: Axis III (not the answer per this page) covers general medical conditions.  Why D wrong: Axis V is global assessment of functioning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The DSM-IV-TR Axis V represents a global assessment of functioning based on a scale of:","options":["1 to 10","1 to 50","1 to 100","0 to 30"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Axis V is a global assessment of functioning (GAF) based on a scale of 1 to 100.  Why A, B, D wrong: These do not match the stated 1–100 GAF scale."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Flumazenil is the antidote for which class of drugs? (FAQ's)","options":["Opioids","Benzodiazepines","Tricyclic antidepressants","Beta-blockers"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Flumazenil is an antidote of benzodiazepines.  Why A wrong: Naloxone is the antidote for opioids.  Why C wrong: TCA toxicity is managed with sodium bicarbonate, not flumazenil.  Why D wrong: Glucagon is used for beta-blocker overdose."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Naloxone is used as an antidote for poisoning by: (FAQ's)","options":["Benzodiazepines","Opioids","Alcohol","Organophosphates"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Naloxone is an antidote of opioids.  Why A wrong: Flumazenil is the benzodiazepine antidote.  Why C wrong: Alcohol has no single specific antidote of this type.  Why D wrong: Atropine/pralidoxime treat organophosphate poisoning."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Naltrexone is best described as a/an:","options":["Opioid antagonist","Opioid agonist","Benzodiazepine agonist","Antipsychotic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist (used in relapse prevention).  Why B wrong: It blocks rather than activates opioid receptors.  Why C wrong: It has no benzodiazepine agonist action.  Why D wrong: It is not an antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which two features are the most important characteristics experienced by a patient with depression?","options":["Elevated mood and grandiosity","Depressed mood and loss of pleasure or interest","Hallucinations and delusions","Flight of ideas and pressured speech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Depressed mood and loss of pleasure or interest (anhedonia) are the most important characteristics of depression.  Why A wrong: Elevated mood and grandiosity suggest mania.  Why C wrong: Hallucinations/delusions are psychotic features, not core depression features.  Why D wrong: Flight of ideas and pressured speech suggest mania."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Deficiency of which neurotransmitter is found in depression?","options":["Dopamine","Serotonin (5-HT / 5-hydroxytryptamine)","Acetylcholine","GABA"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Deficiency of serotonin (5-HT, 5-hydroxytryptamine) is found in depression.  Why A wrong: Dopamine excess is linked to psychosis, not the cited depression deficiency.  Why C wrong: Acetylcholine deficiency is linked to Alzheimer's.  Why D wrong: GABA is not the neurotransmitter cited for depression here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The common risk factor in a patient with severe depression is:","options":["Weight gain","Suicidal tendency","Hypertension","Improved concentration"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The common risk factor in a patient with severe depression is suicidal tendency.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not identified as the key risk factor of severe depression on this page."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"According to the National Crime Records Bureau 2014, the most common method of suicide in India is:","options":["Poisoning","Hanging","Drowning","Self-immolation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Hanging is the most common method of suicide in India per NCRB 2014.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are other methods but not the most common per the cited source."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Aphonia is defined as:","options":["Inability to produce voiced sound","Loss of ability to read","Loss of memory","Inability to swallow"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Aphonia is the inability to produce voiced sound.  Why B wrong: Loss of reading is alexia/dyslexia.  Why C wrong: Memory loss is amnesia.  Why D wrong: Inability to swallow is dysphagia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"A state of indifference or the suppression of emotions such as anxiety, excitement, motivation and passion is called:","options":["Apathy","Euphoria","Ambivalence","Anhedonia"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Apathy is a state of indifference or suppression of emotions such as anxiety, excitement, motivation and passion.  Why B wrong: Euphoria is exaggerated well-being.  Why C wrong: Ambivalence is coexisting opposite feelings.  Why D wrong: Anhedonia is specifically loss of pleasure."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Suspiciousness and mistrust of others (paranoia) is a characteristic feature of which personality disorder?","options":["Histrionic personality disorder","Schizotypal personality disorder","Dependent personality disorder","Narcissistic personality disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Suspiciousness and mistrust of others (paranoia) is a characteristic feature of schizotypal personality disorder per this page.  Why A wrong: Histrionic disorder features attention-seeking and emotionality.  Why C wrong: Dependent disorder features excessive need to be cared for.  Why D wrong: Narcissistic disorder features grandiosity."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"A patient with schizotypal personality disorder characteristically has great difficulty in:","options":["Following medication schedules","Improving and achieving close relationships with others","Maintaining physical hygiene","Sleeping through the night"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Patients with schizotypal personality have great difficulty improving and achieving close relationships with others.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not the specific difficulty highlighted for schizotypal personality on this page."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Sheldon's classification of personality (body types) includes which of the following?","options":["Ectomorphs, Endomorphs, and Mesomorphs","Type A, Type B, and Type C","Id, Ego, and Superego","Sanguine, Choleric, and Phlegmatic"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Sheldon's classification of personality includes Ectomorphs, Endomorphs, and Mesomorphs.  Why B wrong: Type A/B/C is a behavioural pattern classification, not Sheldon's.  Why C wrong: Id/ego/superego is Freud's structural model.  Why D wrong: These are the four classical temperaments, not Sheldon's somatotypes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The release of feelings that takes place by talking about emotionally charged areas is known as:","options":["Sublimation","Abreaction","Regression","Projection"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abreaction is the release of feelings by talking about emotionally charged areas.  Why A wrong: Sublimation channels impulses into acceptable activity.  Why C wrong: Regression is reverting to earlier behaviour.  Why D wrong: Projection attributes one's feelings to others."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Grief is best defined as:","options":["A permanent psychotic state","A reaction of an individual to a significant loss, which may be real or anticipated","An irrational fear of open spaces","A type of learning disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Grief is a reaction of an individual to a significant loss; the loss may be real or anticipated.  Why A wrong: Grief is a normal reaction, not a psychotic state.  Why C wrong: That defines agoraphobia.  Why D wrong: Learning disorders (e.g., dyslexia) are unrelated to grief."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The most common psychotherapy used to help a person with Obsessive Compulsive Disorder (OCD) is:","options":["Behaviour modification","Psychoanalysis","Electroconvulsive therapy","Family therapy"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common psychotherapy used for OCD is behaviour modification.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not identified as the most common psychotherapy for OCD on this page."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which drug is most useful in the treatment of Obsessive Compulsive Disorder (OCD)?","options":["Haloperidol","Fluoxetine (SSRI)","Diazepam","Lithium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fluoxetine, an SSRI, is the drug most useful in treating OCD.  Why A wrong: Haloperidol is an antipsychotic (drug of choice for Tourette's, not OCD).  Why C wrong: Diazepam is a benzodiazepine anxiolytic.  Why D wrong: Lithium is a mood stabilizer for bipolar disorder."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Trichotillomania is a disorder that involves: (BPL AIIMS-2013, NORCET-2022)","options":["Recurrent irresistible urge to pull out hair","Recurrent stealing of objects","Setting fires deliberately","Compulsive hand washing"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Trichotillomania is a recurrent irresistible urge to pull out hair.  Why B wrong: Recurrent stealing is kleptomania.  Why C wrong: Deliberate fire-setting is pyromania.  Why D wrong: Compulsive hand washing is an OCD symptom."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which drug is the first-line drug of choice for the treatment of ADHD? (FAQ's)","options":["Haloperidol","Methylphenidate","Fluoxetine","Lithium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Methylphenidate is the first-line drug of choice for ADHD.  Why A wrong: Haloperidol is the drug of choice for Tourette's disorder.  Why C wrong: Fluoxetine is used for OCD/depression.  Why D wrong: Lithium is used for bipolar disorder."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Dyslexia is a learning disorder that involves difficulty in:","options":["Reading","Making arithmetical calculations","Writing letters only","Speaking"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dyslexia is a learning disorder that involves difficulty reading.  Why B wrong: Difficulty with arithmetic is dyscalculia.  Why C wrong: Difficulty writing is dysgraphia, not dyslexia.  Why D wrong: Speaking difficulty is not the definition of dyslexia given here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Severe difficulty in making arithmetical calculations is referred to as:","options":["Dyslexia","Dyscalculia","Dysgraphia","Dysphasia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dyscalculia refers to severe difficulty in making arithmetical calculations.  Why A wrong: Dyslexia is difficulty reading.  Why C wrong: Dysgraphia is difficulty writing.  Why D wrong: Dysphasia is a language/speech impairment."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"A tic is best described as an abnormal involuntary movement that is:","options":["Slow, sustained and writhing","Sudden, repetitive and purposeless","Rhythmic and voluntary","Present only during sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A tic is an abnormal involuntary movement which occurs suddenly, repetitively and purposelessly.  Why A wrong: Slow writhing movements describe athetosis.  Why C wrong: Tics are involuntary, not voluntary/rhythmic.  Why D wrong: The page does not restrict tics to sleep."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Tourette syndrome is a disorder of:","options":["Mood","Tic","Memory","Sleep"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tourette syndrome is a disorder of tic.  Why A, C, D wrong: Tourette syndrome is specifically a tic disorder, not primarily a mood, memory, or sleep disorder."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Magical thinking is usually seen in which age group?","options":["Toddlers and preschoolers","Adolescents","Middle-aged adults","The elderly"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Magical thinking is usually seen in toddlers and preschoolers.  Why B, C, D wrong: The page specifies toddlers and preschoolers, not these older groups."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which drug is the drug of choice to treat Tourette's disorder?","options":["Methylphenidate","Haloperidol","Fluoxetine","Naltrexone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Haloperidol is the drug of choice to treat Tourette's disorder.  Why A wrong: Methylphenidate is first-line for ADHD (and can worsen tics).  Why C wrong: Fluoxetine treats OCD/depression.  Why D wrong: Naltrexone is an opioid antagonist."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Patients taking monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) should avoid food rich in:","options":["Potassium","Tyramine","Iron","Calcium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MAOI patients should avoid tyramine-rich food to prevent hypertensive crisis.  Why A, C, D wrong: Potassium, iron, and calcium are not the substances contraindicated with MAOIs here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which of the following are examples of tyramine-rich foods to be avoided by MAOI patients? (Select all that apply)","options":["Aged cheeses","Alcoholic beverages","Cured meats","Fresh boiled rice","Plain drinking water"],"correct":"A,B,C","rationale":"Why correct: The page lists aged cheeses, alcoholic beverages, and cured meats as tyramine-rich foods.  Why D wrong: Fresh boiled rice is not listed as tyramine-rich.  Why E wrong: Plain water contains no tyramine."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The most common indication for electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is: (FAQ's)","options":["Major depression","Generalized anxiety disorder","Dyslexia","Alcohol dependence"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: The most common indication of ECT is major depression.  Why B, C, D wrong: These are not the most common indication for ECT per this page."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which type of schizophrenia responds best to ECT? (FAQ's)","options":["Paranoid schizophrenia","Catatonic schizophrenia","Disorganized schizophrenia","Residual schizophrenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Catatonic schizophrenia is the type that best responds to ECT.  Why A, C, D wrong: The page specifically names catatonic schizophrenia, not these subtypes."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The voltage needed to produce seizures while giving ECT is:","options":["30 to 50 volts","70 to 130 volts","150 to 200 volts","220 to 240 volts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The voltage needed to produce seizures during ECT is 70 to 130 volts.  Why A, C, D wrong: These do not match the stated 70–130 volt range."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Which drug is administered during ECT to prevent injuries to the patient during the seizure? (NORCET-2022)","options":["Succinylcholine","Atropine","Flumazenil","Naloxone"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Succinylcholine (a muscle relaxant) is given during ECT to prevent injuries during the seizure. Note: succinylcholine is actually a skeletal (neuromuscular) muscle relaxant; the source's phrase \"smooth muscle relaxant\" is a misprint, but the intended answer remains succinylcholine.  Why B wrong: Atropine is given to minimize secretions and prevent bradyarrhythmia, not to relax muscles.  Why C wrong: Flumazenil is a benzodiazepine antidote.  Why D wrong: Naloxone is an opioid antidote."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Atropine is given to a patient before ECT primarily to:","options":["Induce the seizure","Minimize secretions and prevent transient postictal bradyarrhythmia due to excessive vagal tone","Relax skeletal muscles","Reverse benzodiazepine sedation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atropine is given before ECT to minimize secretions and to prevent transient postictal bradyarrhythmia due to excessive vagal tone.  Why A wrong: The electrical stimulus, not atropine, induces the seizure.  Why C wrong: Succinylcholine relaxes muscles.  Why D wrong: Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The most common side effect of ECT is:","options":["Permanent paralysis","Temporary memory loss","Hypertensive crisis","Hepatic failure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Temporary memory loss is the most common side effect of ECT.  Why A, C, D wrong: These are not identified as the most common ECT side effect on this page."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Agoraphobia is the fear of open spaces and is described on this page as:","options":["The rarest phobia","The most common type of phobia seen in clinical practice","A fear seen only in children","A fear of animals"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Agoraphobia is fear of open spaces and is the most common type of phobia seen in clinical practice.  Why A wrong: It is the most common, not rarest.  Why C wrong: It is not limited to children.  Why D wrong: Fear of animals is zoophobia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"An irrational fear related to closed spaces is referred to as:","options":["Agoraphobia","Claustrophobia","Acrophobia","Hypochondriasis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Irrational fear of closed spaces is claustrophobia.  Why A wrong: Agoraphobia is fear of open spaces.  Why C wrong: Acrophobia is fear of high places.  Why D wrong: Hypochondriasis is fear of having serious illness."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Fear of high places is called:","options":["Claustrophobia","Acrophobia","Agoraphobia","Xenophobia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Fear of high places is called acrophobia.  Why A wrong: Claustrophobia is fear of closed spaces.  Why C wrong: Agoraphobia is fear of open spaces.  Why D wrong: Xenophobia is fear of strangers/foreigners."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Preoccupation with a fear of having a serious physical illness, not based on real or organic pathology, is termed: (FAQ's)","options":["Hypochondriasis","Agoraphobia","Delusion","Depersonalization"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Hypochondriasis is a preoccupation with fear of having a serious physical illness not based on real or organic pathology.  Why B wrong: Agoraphobia is fear of open spaces.  Why C wrong: A delusion is a false fixed belief, broader than health fears.  Why D wrong: Depersonalization is feeling detached from oneself."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The part of the mind that distinguishes right from wrong and acts as the censor of behaviour is the:","options":["Id","Ego","Superego","Unconscious"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The superego distinguishes right from wrong and acts as the censor of behaviour.  Why A wrong: The id is the pleasure-driven instinctual part.  Why B wrong: The ego mediates between id and reality.  Why D wrong: The unconscious is a level of awareness, not the moral censor."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Who is considered the First Psychiatric Nurse?","options":["Florence Nightingale","Linda Richards","Dorothea Dix","Hildegard Peplau"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Linda Richards is considered the First Psychiatric Nurse.  Why A wrong: Florence Nightingale is the founder of modern nursing.  Why C wrong: Dorothea Dix was a mental health reformer, not the first psychiatric nurse per this page.  Why D wrong: Hildegard Peplau is known for interpersonal theory in nursing."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Who is regarded as the father of Modern Psychiatry? (FAQ's)","options":["Emil Kraepelin","Sigmund Freud","Carl Jung","Philippe Pinel"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Sigmund Freud is the father of Modern Psychiatry per this page.  Why A wrong: Kraepelin is known for psychiatric classification.  Why C wrong: Jung founded analytical psychology.  Why D wrong: Pinel is associated with humane treatment/moral therapy."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Freud's model of development comprising the id, ego and superego is called the:","options":["Topographical model","Structural model","Interpersonal model","Behavioural model"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Freud's model comprising id, ego and superego is called the Structural Model.  Why A wrong: The topographical model concerns conscious/preconscious/unconscious levels.  Why C wrong: The interpersonal model is Peplau/Sullivan's, not Freud's structural model.  Why D wrong: The behavioural model is not Freud's structural model."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Blunt affect refers to:","options":["An exaggeration of emotional experience","A reduction in emotional experience","Rapidly shifting emotions","Complete absence of any facial expression"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Blunt affect is a reduction in emotional experience.  Why A wrong: That would be an exaggerated, not reduced, affect.  Why C wrong: Rapidly shifting emotion is labile affect.  Why D wrong: Complete absence of expression is flat affect, a more severe reduction than \"blunt\" per this definition."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"A pattern of communication that includes many irrelevant and unnecessary trivial details before the person is able to come to the point is called:","options":["Circumstantiality","Tangentiality","Perseveration","Neologism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Circumstantiality is a communication pattern with many irrelevant/unnecessary trivial details before reaching the point (the person eventually reaches the point).  Why B wrong: In tangentiality the person never returns to the point.  Why C wrong: Perseveration is repetition of the same response.  Why D wrong: A neologism is a made-up word."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Idea of reference is best defined as:","options":["A false belief of having a serious illness","An incorrect belief that the statements or actions of others are related to oneself","A fear of open spaces","A pathological repetition of another's speech"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Idea of reference is the incorrect belief that the statements or actions of others are related to oneself.  Why A wrong: That describes hypochondriasis.  Why C wrong: That describes agoraphobia.  Why D wrong: That describes echolalia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The phenomenon in which an unfamiliar place suddenly becomes totally familiar ('already seen') is called:","options":["Jamais vu","Déjà vu","Amnesia","Confabulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Déjà vu ('already seen') is when an unfamiliar place becomes totally familiar.  Why A wrong: Jamais vu is the opposite — a familiar place becomes unfamiliar.  Why C wrong: Amnesia is memory loss.  Why D wrong: Confabulation is filling memory gaps with fabricated details."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"The phenomenon in which a familiar place becomes totally unfamiliar ('never seen') is called:","options":["Déjà vu","Jamais vu","Depersonalization","Derealization"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Jamais vu ('never seen') is when a familiar place becomes totally unfamiliar.  Why A wrong: Déjà vu is the opposite — unfamiliar becomes familiar.  Why C wrong: Depersonalization is feeling detached from oneself.  Why D wrong: Derealization, while related, is not the specific term defined here."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"A delusion is best defined as:","options":["A false perception without an external stimulus","A false, fixed and unshakeable belief that is not amenable to reasoning and is not in keeping with the patient's socio-cultural and educational background","An irresistible urge to pull out hair","A reduction in emotional experience"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A delusion is a false, fixed, unshakeable belief not amenable to reasoning and not in keeping with the patient's socio-cultural and educational background.  Why A wrong: A false perception without external stimulus is a hallucination.  Why C wrong: That describes trichotillomania.  Why D wrong: That describes blunt affect."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Dystonia is best described as:","options":["Muscle rigidity that affects posture, gait, eye and movement","Loss of ability to read","Fear of high places","Inability to produce voiced sound"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Dystonia is muscle rigidity that affects posture, gait, eye and movement.  Why B wrong: Loss of reading ability is dyslexia/alexia.  Why C wrong: Fear of high places is acrophobia.  Why D wrong: Inability to produce voiced sound is aphonia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"Echolalia refers to:","options":["Pathological repetition by imitation of the speech of another","A false fixed belief","Making up new words","Loss of pleasure or interest"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Echolalia is the pathological repetition by imitation of the speech of another.  Why B wrong: A false fixed belief is a delusion.  Why C wrong: Making up new words is a neologism.  Why D wrong: Loss of pleasure/interest is anhedonia."},{"section":"Batch 009 · therapeutic communication, biological/family/group therapy…","question":"A key diagnostic feature of anorexia nervosa described at the top of the page includes all of the following EXCEPT:","options":["An intense fear of becoming fat","Maintenance of body weight below the expected level","Preoccupation with weight","An irresistible urge to pull out hair"],"correct":"D","rationale":"Why correct: An irresistible urge to pull out hair is trichotillomania, not a feature of anorexia nervosa.  Why A wrong: Intense fear of becoming fat is a feature described.  Why B wrong: Maintenance of body weight below expected level is described.  Why C wrong: Preoccupation with weight is described."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on the psychiatric unit tells the nurse, \"I won't be a burden much longer — I've given away my guitar and my savings.\" What is the nurse's PRIORITY action?","options":["Document the statement and inform the physician at the next round","Ask the client directly whether he has a plan to harm or kill himself","Reassure the client that his family cares about him","Restrict the client to his room to prevent elopement"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Giving away prized possessions is a behavioural warning sign of imminent suicide; the immediate priority is a direct risk assessment — asking about suicidal ideation, plan, means and intent. Asking about suicide does NOT increase risk.  Why wrong: Delaying documentation (A) ignores urgency; false reassurance (C) blocks communication and dismisses feelings; isolating the client (D) increases risk and is not a substitute for assessment.  High-yield takeaway: Direct questioning about suicidal intent is always the FIRST step; a client with a specific plan and available means is at highest risk."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which client on suicide watch is at the HIGHEST immediate risk and needs one-to-one continuous observation?","options":["A client who says life is hopeless but has no plan","A client who suddenly appears calm and cheerful after days of severe depression","A client verbalizing anger about being admitted","A client with vague thoughts that others would be \"better off\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A sudden, unexplained lift in mood in a severely depressed client is an ominous sign — it often means the person has decided on suicide and feels relief, and energy has returned to act on the plan.  Why wrong: Hopelessness without a plan (A) and passive ideation (D) are concerning but lower acuity; anger about admission (C) is not itself a suicide predictor.  High-yield takeaway: \"Sudden calm\" in a formerly agitated depressed patient = red flag; also, risk is highest in early recovery when energy returns before mood fully lifts."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Under the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, a person who attempts suicide shall, unless proved otherwise, be presumed to be suffering from severe stress and shall NOT be punished. This provision is contained in:","options":["Section 20","Section 94","Section 115","Section 309 IPC"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Section 115 of the MHCA 2017 — \"Presumption of severe stress in case of attempt to commit suicide\" — effectively decriminalizes attempted suicide and mandates care/rehabilitation instead of prosecution.  Why wrong: Sec 20 is the right to community living; Sec 94 is emergency treatment; Section 309 of the IPC is the OLD provision that criminalized attempted suicide (now overridden).  High-yield takeaway: MHCA §115 decriminalizes attempted suicide; the client gets treatment, not punishment."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which provision is PROHIBITED for a person with mental illness under Section 95 of the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017?","options":["Electro-convulsive therapy with muscle relaxants and anaesthesia","Electro-convulsive therapy without muscle relaxants and anaesthesia (unmodified ECT)","Voluntary admission of an adult","Making an advance directive"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Section 95 prohibits unmodified ECT (ECT without muscle relaxants and anaesthesia), ECT for minors (except with Board approval), sterilisation as a treatment for mental illness, and chaining.  Why wrong: Modified ECT (A) is permitted; voluntary admission (C) and advance directives (D) are patient rights protected by the Act.  High-yield takeaway: MHCA §95 bans UNMODIFIED ECT; ECT in minors needs Mental Health Review Board approval and guardian consent."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A newly admitted competent adult wants to specify, in writing, how she wishes to be treated (and NOT treated) should she become unable to make decisions in future. Under the Mental Healthcare Act 2017, this document is a/an:","options":["Nominated representative form (Section 14)","Advance directive (Section 5)","Emergency treatment order (Section 94)","Involuntary admission request"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Section 5 gives every person the right to make an advance directive stating how they wish to be cared for/treated and how they wish NOT to be treated, and to appoint a nominated representative.  Why wrong: The nominated representative (A, §14) is a person, not the directive itself; §94 is emergency treatment; involuntary admission is a different pathway.  High-yield takeaway: Advance directive = §5; Nominated representative = §14. The advance directive does NOT apply during emergency treatment (§9)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 defines the threshold of mental illness capacity around the person's ability to understand information and make decisions. The Act shifted India's approach primarily toward a:","options":["Custodial, institution-centred model","Rights-based, patient-autonomy model","Purely pharmacological model","Family-consent-only model"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: MHCA 2017 replaced the custodial Mental Health Act 1987 with a rights-based framework — right to access care, community living, confidentiality, protection from cruelty, and to make treatment choices.  Why wrong: A describes the old 1987 Act; C and D ignore the Act's autonomy and rights emphasis.  High-yield takeaway: MHCA 2017 = rights-based & recovery-oriented; aligns India with the UN Convention on Rights of Persons with Disabilities."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on lithium presents with coarse tremors, vomiting, diarrhoea, slurred speech and ataxia. His last serum lithium level was 2.4 mEq/L. What is the nurse's PRIORITY action?","options":["Administer the next scheduled dose of lithium","Withhold lithium and notify the physician immediately","Encourage a low-sodium diet","Restrict all fluids"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A level of 2.4 mEq/L with these signs indicates moderate-to-severe lithium toxicity; the drug must be held and the prescriber notified. Treatment is supportive; haemodialysis for severe toxicity (>2.5–3.0 or symptomatic).  Why wrong: Giving more lithium (A) worsens toxicity; LOW sodium (C) and fluid restriction (D) REDUCE lithium excretion and increase levels — the opposite of what is needed.  High-yield takeaway: Therapeutic lithium = 0.6–1.2 mEq/L; toxicity >1.5; life-threatening >2.0. Low sodium and dehydration raise lithium levels."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which teaching point is MOST important for a client starting lithium therapy?","options":["\"Reduce your salt and water intake to prevent weight gain.\"","\"Maintain a consistent salt and fluid intake and stay well hydrated.\"","\"Stop the drug as soon as your mood improves.\"","\"Take an antacid whenever you feel nauseated.\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Because lithium is handled by the kidney like sodium, a stable salt/fluid intake keeps levels steady; sodium loss or dehydration (sweating, diarrhoea, diuretics) raises lithium to toxic levels.  Why wrong: Cutting salt/water (A) causes toxicity; stopping abruptly (C) risks relapse; routine antacids (D) are irrelevant and self-medication is discouraged.  High-yield takeaway: \"Consistent sodium + hydration\" is the cardinal lithium teaching; thiazide diuretics and NSAIDs raise lithium levels."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on haloperidol suddenly develops a stiff, twisted neck (torticollis), upward-deviated eyes (oculogyric crisis) and jaw spasm 8 hours after the first dose. This is acute dystonia. What is the nurse's PRIORITY intervention?","options":["Administer IM/IV benztropine or diphenhydramine as prescribed","Reassure the client it will resolve on its own","Hold all future doses permanently and document","Apply physical restraints"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Acute dystonia is a distressing, potentially airway-threatening EPS that responds rapidly to anticholinergics — IM/IV benztropine or diphenhydramine is the emergency treatment.  Why wrong: It rarely self-resolves fast enough and is frightening (B); permanent discontinuation (C) is a physician decision, not the immediate action; restraints (D) worsen distress and do not treat the muscle spasm.  High-yield takeaway: Acute dystonia occurs EARLY (hours–days), especially with high-potency typicals (haloperidol); treat with anticholinergics. Laryngeal dystonia is a medical emergency."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Match the extrapyramidal symptom to its description — a client on antipsychotics who paces constantly, cannot sit still, and feels an inner restlessness is experiencing:","options":["Acute dystonia","Akathisia","Tardive dyskinesia","Pseudoparkinsonism"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Akathisia = subjective inner restlessness with an inability to remain still (pacing, rocking). It is often mistaken for worsening anxiety/agitation. Managed with beta-blockers (propranolol), benzodiazepines, or dose reduction.  Why wrong: Dystonia (A) = sustained muscle spasms; tardive dyskinesia (C) = late, involuntary orofacial movements; pseudoparkinsonism (D) = tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, shuffling gait.  High-yield takeaway: Akathisia = \"cannot sit still\" and is a NORCET trap because it mimics anxiety — treat with propranolol, NOT more antipsychotic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on long-term antipsychotics develops involuntary lip-smacking, tongue protrusion and chewing movements. The nurse recognizes tardive dyskinesia. Which statement is TRUE?","options":["It appears within hours of the first dose","It is often irreversible and worsened by anticholinergics","It responds rapidly to benztropine","It only occurs with atypical antipsychotics"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tardive dyskinesia is a LATE EPS (months–years) that may be irreversible; anticholinergics (benztropine) can WORSEN it. Newer VMAT2 inhibitors (valbenazine, deutetrabenazine) are used to treat it.  Why wrong: It is late, not early (A); anticholinergics worsen rather than treat it (C); it is more common with typical (older) antipsychotics (D).  High-yield takeaway: TD = LATE and potentially permanent; do NOT give anticholinergics (they help dystonia/parkinsonism but worsen TD). Use AIMS scale to screen."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on antipsychotics develops high fever (40°C), lead-pipe muscle rigidity, altered consciousness, autonomic instability and a markedly raised creatine kinase (CK). The nurse suspects:","options":["Serotonin syndrome","Neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS)","Malignant hyperthermia","Anticholinergic delirium"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NMS = hyperthermia + LEAD-PIPE rigidity + altered mental status + autonomic dysfunction + elevated CK, developing over days after a dopamine antagonist. Stop the antipsychotic; give dantrolene and/or bromocriptine, cooling and supportive care.  Why wrong: Serotonin syndrome (A) features HYPERreflexia, clonus, myoclonus and develops within hours of serotonergic drugs; malignant hyperthermia (C) is triggered by volatile anaesthetics/succinylcholine intra-op; anticholinergic delirium (D) lacks rigidity/raised CK.  High-yield takeaway: NMS = \"lead-pipe rigidity + HYPOreflexia + slow onset (days)\"; Serotonin syndrome = \"clonus + HYPERreflexia + fast onset (hours).\""},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client started on an SSRI 2 days ago, who also takes tramadol, presents with agitation, diaphoresis, hyperreflexia, inducible clonus, and shivering. The MOST likely diagnosis is:","options":["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","Serotonin syndrome","Lithium toxicity","Alcohol withdrawal"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Serotonin syndrome results from excess serotonergic activity (SSRI + tramadol) and classically presents with the triad of neuromuscular excitability (clonus, hyperreflexia, tremor), autonomic instability, and altered mental status, with rapid (hours) onset. Treat by stopping serotonergic drugs; cyproheptadine is the antidote.  Why wrong: NMS (A) causes rigidity and hyporeflexia over days; lithium toxicity (C) gives coarse tremor/GI signs, not clonus; alcohol withdrawal (D) follows cessation, not drug initiation.  High-yield takeaway: Clonus + hyperreflexia + recent serotonergic drug = serotonin syndrome; antidote = cyproheptadine. Watch tramadol, MAOIs, linezolid, triptans as culprits."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on clozapine reports a sore throat, fever and mouth ulcers. Which laboratory value MOST urgently requires clozapine to be stopped?","options":["Absolute neutrophil count (ANC) below 500/mm³","Serum sodium of 138 mEq/L","Fasting glucose of 90 mg/dL","Haemoglobin of 13 g/dL"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Clozapine can cause life-threatening agranulocytosis; an ANC below 500/mm³ = severe neutropenia, mandating immediate discontinuation and haematology referral. Sore throat/fever are classic warning signs of infection from neutropenia.  Why wrong: Sodium 138 (B), glucose 90 (C) and Hb 13 (D) are all normal values.  High-yield takeaway: Clozapine needs regular ANC monitoring (weekly x6 months, then every 2 weeks x6 months, then monthly); ANC <500 = STOP. Also watch for myocarditis and seizures."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which feature BEST distinguishes delirium from dementia?","options":["Delirium has an insidious onset over months","Delirium has an acute onset with fluctuating consciousness and is often reversible","Dementia typically fluctuates hour to hour","Delirium never involves hallucinations"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium = ACUTE onset, clouded/fluctuating consciousness, disturbed attention, often reversible with treatment of the underlying cause. Dementia = gradual, progressive, clear consciousness (until late), largely irreversible.  Why wrong: Insidious months-long onset (A) describes dementia; fluctuation is a delirium feature not dementia (C); delirium commonly includes visual hallucinations (D).  High-yield takeaway: Delirium = acute + fluctuating + impaired consciousness + REVERSIBLE; treat the cause (infection, drugs, metabolic). It is a medical emergency."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"An elderly client with recent onset of poor concentration and memory complaints answers \"I don't know\" to most questions, has a history of depression, and improves with antidepressant treatment. This picture is MOST consistent with:","options":["Alzheimer's dementia","Pseudodementia (depression-related cognitive impairment)","Delirium","Vascular dementia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Pseudodementia is cognitive impairment caused by depression in the elderly; clients highlight their deficits, give \"I don't know\" answers, have relatively acute onset, and improve when the depression is treated.  Why wrong: In true dementia (A, D) patients often conceal deficits and confabulate, with gradual decline; delirium (C) has clouded consciousness and acute medical cause.  High-yield takeaway: Pseudodementia = depression mimicking dementia; the patient EMPHASIZES memory loss and improves with antidepressants — a classic NORCET differentiation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client looks at a coiled rope on the floor at dusk and screams that it is a snake. When the light is switched on he realizes it is a rope. This is an example of:","options":["Delusion","Hallucination","Illusion","Confabulation"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An illusion is a MISinterpretation of a REAL external stimulus (rope perceived as snake). It corrects when the stimulus is clarified — common in delirium and normal in poor lighting/anxiety.  Why wrong: A delusion (A) is a fixed false belief; a hallucination (B) is a perception WITHOUT any external stimulus; confabulation (D) is filling memory gaps with fabricated content.  High-yield takeaway: Illusion = misperceived REAL stimulus; Hallucination = perception with NO stimulus; Delusion = false fixed BELIEF (thought, not perception)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client firmly believes the newsreader on TV is sending him secret personal messages, despite clear evidence otherwise. This is a:","options":["Delusion of reference","Delusion of grandeur","Nihilistic delusion","Somatic delusion"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: A delusion of reference is the false belief that neutral external events (TV, newspaper, gestures) carry special personal meaning directed at oneself.  Why wrong: Grandeur (B) = inflated self-importance/power; nihilistic (C) = belief that oneself/part of body/world does not exist; somatic (D) = false belief about the body.  High-yield takeaway: Reference = \"the media/people are talking about ME\"; distinguish from thought broadcasting (thoughts are known to others)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which is a hallmark difference between typical (first-generation) and atypical (second-generation) antipsychotics?","options":["Typicals cause fewer extrapyramidal symptoms than atypicals","Atypicals have a higher risk of metabolic syndrome (weight gain, diabetes, dyslipidaemia)","Atypicals have no effect on negative symptoms","Typicals are the drugs of choice for treatment-resistant schizophrenia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Atypicals (olanzapine, clozapine, quetiapine, risperidone) act on D2 and 5-HT2 receptors, cause FEWER EPS but MORE metabolic side effects (weight gain, dyslipidaemia, type 2 diabetes) requiring metabolic monitoring.  Why wrong: Typicals cause MORE EPS, not fewer (A); atypicals improve negative symptoms better (C); clozapine (an atypical) is the drug of choice for treatment-resistant schizophrenia (D).  High-yield takeaway: Typicals → more EPS/hyperprolactinaemia; Atypicals → more metabolic syndrome; clozapine = gold standard for treatment-resistant schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"ICD-11 came into effect globally on:","options":["1 January 2019","28 May 2019","1 January 2022","18 March 2022"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: ICD-11 was adopted at the 72nd World Health Assembly (28 May 2019) but came into effect for reporting on 1 January 2022.  Why wrong: 1 Jan 2019 (A) is incorrect; 28 May 2019 (B) is the WHA adoption date, not the effective date; 18 March 2022 (D) is the DSM-5-TR publication date.  High-yield takeaway: ICD-11 effective 1 Jan 2022; adopted May 2019. DSM-5-TR published 18 March 2022."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which condition was newly recognized as a diagnosis in ICD-11 under \"Disorders due to addictive behaviours\"?","options":["Gaming disorder","Kleptomania","Pyromania","Trichotillomania"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: ICD-11 introduced Gaming disorder (6C51) alongside gambling disorder under disorders due to addictive behaviours — characterized by impaired control over gaming, priority given to gaming over other activities, and continuation despite negative consequences.  Why wrong: Kleptomania (B), pyromania (C) and trichotillomania (D) are impulse-control/body-focused disorders that existed previously and are not \"addictive behaviour\" additions.  High-yield takeaway: Gaming disorder is a NEW ICD-11 diagnosis; also new: complex PTSD, prolonged grief disorder, body integrity dysphoria."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"In ICD-11, the classical subtypes of schizophrenia (paranoid, hebephrenic, catatonic, etc.) have been:","options":["Retained exactly as in ICD-10","Removed in favour of a dimensional symptom-specifier approach","Expanded to include ten new subtypes","Merged into a single \"psychosis NOS\" code"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ICD-11 abolished the traditional schizophrenia subtypes and instead uses symptom specifiers/dimensions (positive, negative, depressive, manic, psychomotor, cognitive symptoms) because subtypes had poor reliability and predictive value — mirroring DSM-5, which also dropped subtypes.  Why wrong: They were not retained (A) or expanded (C); it is not a single NOS code (D) — a multidimensional rating is used.  High-yield takeaway: Both ICD-11 and DSM-5/5-TR DROPPED schizophrenia subtypes in favour of dimensional specifiers."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which new diagnosis was added in DSM-5-TR (2022) that was not a separate disorder in DSM-5 (2013)?","options":["Prolonged grief disorder","Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder","Hoarding disorder","Premenstrual dysphoric disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DSM-5-TR (published 18 March 2022) added Prolonged grief disorder as a formal diagnosis (grief symptoms persisting and impairing function ≥12 months in adults).  Why wrong: Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder (B), hoarding disorder (C), and premenstrual dysphoric disorder (D) were all already introduced in DSM-5 (2013).  High-yield takeaway: DSM-5-TR's headline addition = Prolonged grief disorder; DSM-5-TR = 2022 text revision, not a new edition."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"The National Mental Health Programme (NMHP) of India was launched in the year:","options":["1975","1982","1996","2017"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NMHP was launched in 1982 by the Government of India to ensure availability and accessibility of minimum mental healthcare, integrate mental health into general health services, and promote community participation.  Why wrong: 1975 (A) is incorrect; 1996 (C) is when the District Mental Health Programme (DMHP) began under the NMHP; 2017 (D) is the MHCA year.  High-yield takeaway: NMHP = 1982; DMHP = 1996 (based on the Bellary model, Karnataka)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"The District Mental Health Programme (DMHP) in India was launched in 1996 based on which pioneering model?","options":["Chandigarh model","Bellary model (Karnataka)","Ranchi model","Vellore model"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The DMHP was launched in 1996 based on the Bellary model developed at NIMHANS, Bangalore — decentralizing mental health care to the district and primary-care level.  Why wrong: Chandigarh (A), Ranchi (C) and Vellore (D) are important psychiatric centres but are not the basis of the DMHP.  High-yield takeaway: DMHP (1996) = Bellary/NIMHANS model; provides community-based mental health services at the district level under the NMHP."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016, the number of recognized disabilities was increased from 7 to:","options":["10","15","21","25"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: The RPwD Act 2016 expanded the list of disabilities from 7 (under the 1995 Act) to 21, explicitly including mental illness, intellectual disability, autism spectrum disorder, and specific learning disabilities.  Why wrong: 10 (A), 15 (B) and 25 (D) are incorrect counts.  High-yield takeaway: RPwD Act 2016 = 21 disabilities (up from 7); mental illness is now a recognized disability entitling reservation and benefits."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse must place a highly agitated client in seclusion. Which action reflects safe and legal practice under a rights-based framework?","options":["Use seclusion as the first-line response to any noncompliance","Use it only as a last resort, with a time-limited order, continuous monitoring and documentation","Leave the client unobserved to allow him to calm down","Combine seclusion with unmodified restraints indefinitely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Seclusion/restraint (MHCA §97 addresses restraint & seclusion) is a last resort to prevent imminent harm, requires a time-limited physician order, least-restrictive means, continuous monitoring of vitals/circulation/hydration, and thorough documentation.  Why wrong: First-line use for noncompliance (A) is unethical and illegal; leaving the client unobserved (C) is dangerous; indefinite restraint (D) violates the client's rights.  High-yield takeaway: Restraint/seclusion = LAST resort, LEAST restrictive, TIME-LIMITED, with continuous monitoring and a physician's order; never for staff convenience or punishment."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Before modified ECT, which is the MOST important immediate nursing preparation?","options":["Give a heavy breakfast to prevent hypoglycaemia","Keep the client nil by mouth (NPO) for about 6–8 hours and obtain informed consent","Withhold all pre-medication","Encourage the client to void only after the procedure"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECT is performed under general anaesthesia, so the client must be NPO (~6–8 hours) to prevent aspiration; valid informed consent, baseline vitals, removal of dentures/jewellery, and an emptied bladder are essential.  Why wrong: A heavy breakfast (A) risks aspiration; pre-medication such as atropine/glycopyrrolate is often given, not withheld (C); the bladder should be emptied BEFORE the procedure (D).  High-yield takeaway: ECT pre-care = NPO 6–8 h, informed consent, empty bladder, remove dentures/jewellery, baseline vitals; post-ECT priority = airway and orientation (confusion is common)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"The MOST common adverse effect a client experiences immediately after a course of ECT is:","options":["Permanent personality change","Transient confusion and short-term memory loss","Chronic seizure disorder","Irreversible paralysis"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The commonest ECT adverse effects are transient post-ictal confusion and short-term (anterograde/retrograde) memory disturbance, which usually resolve over days to weeks. Headache and muscle soreness also occur.  Why wrong: Permanent personality change (A), a chronic seizure disorder (C) and paralysis (D) are NOT recognized effects of properly administered modified ECT.  High-yield takeaway: ECT's main side effects are transient confusion + memory loss; reorient the client and ensure safety after each session."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with alcohol dependence stops drinking abruptly and, 2–3 days later, develops disorientation, visual hallucinations (seeing insects), tremors, tachycardia and fever. The nurse recognizes delirium tremens. Which is the PRIORITY pharmacological management?","options":["Haloperidol as first-line","Benzodiazepines (e.g., chlordiazepoxide/diazepam) with thiamine","Naltrexone","Disulfiram"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Delirium tremens is a life-threatening alcohol withdrawal emergency; benzodiazepines are first-line to control autonomic hyperactivity and prevent seizures, plus PARENTERAL thiamine to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy.  Why wrong: Antipsychotics (A) lower the seizure threshold and are only adjuncts; naltrexone (C) and disulfiram (D) are for relapse prevention/aversion in maintenance, not acute withdrawal.  High-yield takeaway: DT (24–72 h after last drink) = medical emergency → benzodiazepines + thiamine; give thiamine BEFORE glucose to avoid precipitating Wernicke's."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with anorexia nervosa is admitted at 30% below ideal body weight and refeeding is started. On day 3 she develops confusion, weakness, oedema and a serum phosphate of 1.0 mg/dL. The nurse recognizes refeeding syndrome. The PRIORITY is to:","options":["Rapidly increase caloric intake to restore weight","Slow refeeding and correct electrolytes (especially phosphate, potassium, magnesium)","Restrict all fluids","Withhold thiamine"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Refeeding syndrome is driven by an insulin surge causing intracellular shifts of phosphate, potassium and magnesium; management is SLOW caloric increase with close electrolyte monitoring/replacement and thiamine supplementation to prevent cardiac and neurological collapse.  Why wrong: Rapid refeeding (A) worsens the syndrome; fluid restriction (C) is not the core issue; thiamine should be GIVEN, not withheld (D).  High-yield takeaway: Refeeding syndrome = HYPOphosphataemia + hypokalaemia + hypomagnesaemia → \"start low, go slow\" and replace electrolytes; the danger is fatal arrhythmia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse is caring for a client in an acute manic episode who has not eaten or slept for two days. Which nursing intervention is the PRIORITY?","options":["Provide detailed insight-oriented psychotherapy","Offer high-calorie finger foods and provide a low-stimulation environment","Encourage participation in competitive group games","Confront the client about grandiose beliefs"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: A manic client cannot sit to eat, so portable high-calorie finger foods meet nutritional needs, and a calm, low-stimulation environment reduces escalation. Physical exhaustion and dehydration are real dangers.  Why wrong: Insight therapy (A) is ineffective during acute mania; competitive/stimulating activities (C) increase agitation; confrontation (D) provokes hostility.  High-yield takeaway: Acute mania priorities = meet physiological needs (nutrition/hydration/rest), reduce stimulation, ensure safety; save insight work for the euthymic phase."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with paranoid schizophrenia refuses meals, stating the food is poisoned. What is the BEST nursing response?","options":["\"The food is perfectly safe; stop being paranoid.\"","Allow the client to select sealed/packaged foods or eat from communal dishes he sees others eating","Force-feed the client to prevent malnutrition","Insist he explain why he thinks the food is poisoned before he eats"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Offering sealed foods or letting the client eat from a shared serving he watches being distributed reduces the perceived threat while still meeting nutritional needs — a practical, non-confrontational strategy.  Why wrong: Denying the delusion (A) increases mistrust; force-feeding (C) is a rights violation and traumatic; demanding justification (D) reinforces focus on the delusion.  High-yield takeaway: Never argue with or reinforce a delusion; meet the underlying need (nutrition) with practical, trust-building measures."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client experiencing command hallucinations tells the nurse, \"The voices are telling me to hurt the man in the next bed.\" What should the nurse do FIRST?","options":["Ask what the voices are specifically commanding and ensure the safety of the client and others","Tell the client the voices are not real","Leave the client alone until the voices stop","Document the statement for the next shift"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Command hallucinations to harm are a safety emergency; the nurse must assess exactly what the voices command and take immediate steps to protect the client and potential victim (increased observation, separation, notify team).  Why wrong: Disputing reality (B) is not the priority action and does not ensure safety; leaving the client (C) is dangerous; delaying to document (D) ignores imminent risk.  High-yield takeaway: Command hallucinations to harm self/others = assess content + ensure safety FIRST; do not leave the client unattended."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"During a panic attack, a client is hyperventilating, trembling and says he feels he is \"going to die.\" What is the nurse's PRIORITY intervention?","options":["Leave the client alone to regain control","Stay with the client, remain calm, and use short, simple directions to guide slow breathing","Explain the neurobiology of panic in detail","Encourage the client to describe childhood trauma"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: During peak panic, the priority is a calm, reassuring presence with brief, clear directions and help to slow breathing; the client's attention and processing are markedly narrowed, so simplicity and safety come first.  Why wrong: Leaving the client (A) heightens fear; detailed teaching (C) cannot be processed during panic; exploring trauma (D) is inappropriate during acute panic.  High-yield takeaway: In acute panic, DON'T leave the client alone; stay, stay calm, keep instructions short and concrete — teaching is for AFTER the attack subsides."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with obsessive-compulsive disorder performs elaborate hand-washing rituals for hours. Early in treatment, the BEST nursing approach is to:","options":["Abruptly prevent the client from performing the ritual","Allow time for the ritual while gradually setting limits, and ensure basic needs are met","Ridicule the ritual to discourage it","Ignore the behaviour completely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Rituals reduce overwhelming anxiety; abruptly blocking them early causes panic. The nurse initially allows time for the ritual, meets basic needs, builds trust, then gradually sets limits as anxiety is managed (exposure and response prevention is introduced progressively).  Why wrong: Abrupt prevention (A) precipitates severe anxiety; ridicule (C) is harmful; complete ignoring (D) neglects the client's distress and safety (e.g., skin breakdown).  High-yield takeaway: Don't stop OCD rituals abruptly; allow time initially, then set gradual limits; the treatment of choice is ERP (exposure and response prevention) + SSRIs."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client uses the defence mechanism of \"reaction formation.\" Which behaviour is the BEST example?","options":["A man who is angry at his boss shouts at his wife at home","A woman with unconscious hostility toward her child becomes overly protective and doting","A student who fails an exam says the test was unfair","A person forgets a painful traumatic memory entirely"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reaction formation = expressing the OPPOSITE of an unacceptable impulse (unconscious hostility expressed as exaggerated affection/overprotection).  Why wrong: A illustrates displacement; C is rationalization; D is repression.  High-yield takeaway: Reaction formation = doing/feeling the OPPOSITE of the true impulse; distinguish from displacement (redirect to safer target) and rationalization (logical excuse)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client attributes his own unacceptable feelings of jealousy to his partner, accusing her of being jealous. This defence mechanism is:","options":["Projection","Sublimation","Regression","Introjection"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Projection = attributing one's own unacceptable thoughts/feelings to another person (\"I'm not jealous — SHE is\").  Why wrong: Sublimation (B) channels impulses into socially acceptable activity; regression (C) reverts to earlier developmental behaviour; introjection (D) incorporates another's values as one's own.  High-yield takeaway: Projection = \"it's not me, it's YOU\"; sublimation is the most mature/adaptive defence."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A therapeutic communication technique in which the nurse restates the client's message to check understanding and encourage elaboration is:","options":["Reassurance","Reflection/restating","Giving advice","Challenging"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Reflection/restating repeats the main idea/feeling the client expressed to convey listening, verify meaning and invite the client to continue — a core therapeutic technique.  Why wrong: Reassurance (A), giving advice (C) and challenging (D) are NON-therapeutic blocks that shut down exploration and impose the nurse's views.  High-yield takeaway: Therapeutic = active listening, open-ended questions, reflection, silence; non-therapeutic = advice, false reassurance, \"why\" questions, judging."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which of the following is a NON-therapeutic communication response the nurse should AVOID?","options":["\"Tell me more about how you're feeling.\"","\"Don't worry, everything will be fine.\"","Using silence to allow the client to reflect","\"You seem anxious today.\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: \"Don't worry, everything will be fine\" is false reassurance — it dismisses the client's feelings, blocks communication and is non-therapeutic.  Why wrong: Open-ended invitation (A), silence (C), and observation-based reflection (D) are all therapeutic techniques.  High-yield takeaway: False reassurance, giving approval/advice, and \"why\" questions are communication blocks; NORCET loves asking which response is NON-therapeutic."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"According to Erikson's psychosocial stages, an adolescent (12–18 years) is primarily resolving the conflict of:","options":["Trust vs mistrust","Autonomy vs shame and doubt","Identity vs role confusion","Integrity vs despair"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Adolescence corresponds to Identity vs role confusion — the task of forming a stable personal and vocational identity.  Why wrong: Trust vs mistrust (A) = infancy; autonomy vs shame (B) = toddler; integrity vs despair (D) = old age.  High-yield takeaway: Erikson adolescence = Identity vs role confusion; young adult = intimacy vs isolation; middle age = generativity vs stagnation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with antisocial personality disorder repeatedly manipulates staff by \"splitting\" — praising one nurse while degrading another. The MOST effective team strategy is to:","options":["Allow each nurse to set individual, differing rules","Maintain consistent limits and communicate as a team","Assign only one favourite nurse to the client","Avoid all limit-setting to prevent conflict"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Manipulation and splitting are countered by a UNIFIED team approach — consistent limits, clear communication among staff, and shared documentation so the client cannot play staff against one another.  Why wrong: Differing rules (A) enable manipulation; a single favoured nurse (C) fosters dependency/manipulation; no limits (D) reinforces maladaptive behaviour.  High-yield takeaway: Splitting/manipulation → consistency + team communication + firm, non-punitive limits."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with borderline personality disorder shows self-harm scratches and says, \"You're the only nurse who understands me — the others hate me.\" The MOST therapeutic response is to:","options":["Agree that the other nurses are unkind","Acknowledge feelings, avoid taking sides, and reinforce consistent expectations","Promise to be the only nurse who cares for her","Discharge her for manipulation"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: This is splitting (idealization/devaluation) typical of BPD; the nurse acknowledges the client's feelings without colluding, avoids special allegiances, and maintains consistent boundaries and team communication. DBT is the evidence-based therapy.  Why wrong: Agreeing (A) and promising exclusivity (C) reinforce splitting and dependency; punitive discharge (D) is inappropriate and abandoning.  High-yield takeaway: BPD = fear of abandonment + splitting + self-harm; manage with consistency, boundaries and DBT (dialectical behaviour therapy)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which finding in a client on an MAOI (e.g., phenelzine) who ate aged cheese and cured meats requires IMMEDIATE action?","options":["Mild drowsiness","Severe throbbing headache, neck stiffness and BP 210/120 mmHg","Dry mouth","Increased appetite"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Tyramine-rich foods with an MAOI cause a hypertensive crisis — sudden severe occipital headache, neck stiffness, palpitations and dangerously high BP; it is a medical emergency requiring immediate treatment (e.g., phentolamine) and holding the drug.  Why wrong: Drowsiness (A), dry mouth (C) and increased appetite (D) are minor/expected effects, not emergencies.  High-yield takeaway: MAOI + tyramine (aged cheese, cured/smoked meats, fermented foods, red wine) = hypertensive crisis; strict dietary teaching is essential."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client abruptly stops a long-term benzodiazepine. Which symptom indicates dangerous withdrawal requiring urgent intervention?","options":["Mild rebound insomnia only","Generalized tonic-clonic seizures","Increased appetite","Transient euphoria"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Abrupt cessation of high-dose/long-term benzodiazepines can cause a withdrawal syndrome including seizures, autonomic instability and delirium — potentially life-threatening; benzodiazepines must be TAPERED.  Why wrong: Mild rebound insomnia (A) is expected and not dangerous; increased appetite (C) and euphoria (D) are not features of dangerous withdrawal.  High-yield takeaway: Never stop benzodiazepines abruptly — taper to avoid seizures; withdrawal resembles alcohol withdrawal."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A postpartum woman on day 10 believes her baby is possessed and hears voices telling her the infant must \"be saved.\" She has not slept and is agitated. This is MOST likely:","options":["Postpartum blues","Postpartum (puerperal) psychosis","Normal maternal fatigue","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Postpartum psychosis is a psychiatric EMERGENCY (delusions, hallucinations, disorganization, high risk of infanticide/suicide) usually within the first 2 weeks; it requires urgent hospitalization and never leaving mother and baby unsupervised.  Why wrong: Postpartum blues (A) is mild, transient tearfulness resolving in ~2 weeks without psychosis; normal fatigue (C) and GAD (D) do not include delusions/hallucinations or infanticidal risk.  High-yield takeaway: Postpartum psychosis = emergency with delusions/hallucinations + infanticide risk → immediate admission and safety; distinguish from benign postpartum blues and postpartum depression."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A child is brought with persistent deficits in social communication and restricted, repetitive behaviours since early childhood. In DSM-5/DSM-5-TR, the previously separate categories (autistic disorder, Asperger's, PDD-NOS) are now unified under:","options":["Autism spectrum disorder","Rett syndrome","Childhood disintegrative disorder","Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: DSM-5 merged the older subtypes into a single dimensional diagnosis — Autism spectrum disorder — with severity levels; DSM-5-TR retained and refined this.  Why wrong: Rett syndrome (B) and childhood disintegrative disorder (C) were removed as separate DSM categories; ADHD (D) is a distinct neurodevelopmental disorder.  High-yield takeaway: DSM-5/5-TR = single \"Autism Spectrum Disorder\" (severity-graded), abolishing Asperger's as a separate label."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"The Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE) has a maximum score of 30. A score in which range generally suggests cognitive impairment/dementia?","options":["27–30","24 and above","Below 24 (commonly ≤23)","Exactly 30 only"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: An MMSE score below 24 (commonly ≤23) suggests cognitive impairment; scores of 24–30 are generally considered normal (adjusting for education/age).  Why wrong: 27–30 (A) and ≥24 (B) are broadly normal ranges; requiring a perfect 30 (D) is incorrect.  High-yield takeaway: MMSE max = 30; <24 raises concern for dementia; useful for tracking cognitive decline over time."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which client statement about lithium indicates the need for FURTHER teaching (i.e., an INCORRECT understanding)?","options":["\"I will have my blood lithium level checked regularly.\"","\"I'll drink plenty of fluids, especially in hot weather or if I have diarrhoea.\"","\"I'll start a strict low-salt diet to lose weight.\"","\"I'll report tremors, vomiting or confusion to my doctor.\""],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: A strict low-salt diet is DANGEROUS with lithium — sodium restriction increases lithium reabsorption and can precipitate toxicity; this statement shows a misunderstanding needing correction.  Why wrong: Regular level monitoring (A), maintaining hydration (B) and reporting toxicity signs (D) are all CORRECT lithium self-care.  High-yield takeaway: On lithium: keep salt/fluid intake CONSISTENT — never suddenly restrict sodium; NORCET negatively-worded item (\"needs further teaching\")."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with major depression, currently on an antidepressant, begins to show increased energy and psychomotor activity while still expressing hopelessness. Why is this a HIGH-RISK period for suicide?","options":["Antidepressants immediately eliminate suicidal thoughts","Returning energy may enable the client to act on persistent suicidal thoughts before mood fully improves","The client is now completely recovered","Suicidal risk always ends once medication starts"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Antidepressants can restore energy/psychomotor drive before the depressive mood/hopelessness lifts; a previously immobilized client now has the energy to act on suicidal thoughts — hence risk PEAKS in early treatment.  Why wrong: Antidepressants do not immediately abolish suicidality (A); the client is not recovered (C); risk does not automatically end with medication (D).  High-yield takeaway: Suicide risk is highest in EARLY recovery (energy returns before mood lifts) — maintain vigilant precautions during this window."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on risperidone develops galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea and breast tenderness. The nurse recognizes this is due to:","options":["Serotonin syndrome","Hyperprolactinaemia from D2 blockade","Anticholinergic effect","Metabolic syndrome"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Dopamine (D2) blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin; risperidone is notable among atypicals for hyperprolactinaemia, causing galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, gynaecomastia and sexual dysfunction.  Why wrong: Serotonin syndrome (A) features clonus/hyperreflexia; anticholinergic effects (C) = dry mouth/constipation/urinary retention; metabolic syndrome (D) = weight gain/dyslipidaemia/glucose intolerance.  High-yield takeaway: Risperidone (and typicals) → hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, gynaecomastia); aripiprazole is prolactin-sparing."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with generalized anxiety disorder is prescribed buspirone. Which teaching point is correct?","options":["\"It works within 30 minutes for acute panic.\"","\"It may take 2–4 weeks for the full effect and is non-addictive.\"","\"You can drink alcohol freely with it.\"","\"Stop it abruptly once you feel calmer.\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Buspirone is a non-benzodiazepine anxiolytic with delayed onset (2–4 weeks), no dependence/sedation and no immediate anti-panic effect; it is taken regularly, not as needed.  Why wrong: It does NOT work acutely (A); alcohol should be avoided with CNS agents (C); it is taken continuously for effect, not stopped when feeling better (D).  High-yield takeaway: Buspirone = SLOW onset (2–4 wks), non-addictive, NOT for acute anxiety/panic — a key NORCET distinction from benzodiazepines."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse is assessing a client for Wernicke's encephalopathy secondary to chronic alcoholism. Which classic triad supports this diagnosis?","options":["Confusion, ophthalmoplegia and ataxia","Fever, rigidity and raised CK","Clonus, hyperreflexia and diaphoresis","Tremor, vomiting and diarrhoea"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Wernicke's encephalopathy (thiamine/B1 deficiency) presents with the triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia (nystagmus/eye-movement palsy) and ataxia; it is reversible with prompt IV thiamine, but untreated may progress to irreversible Korsakoff psychosis.  Why wrong: B = NMS; C = serotonin syndrome; D = lithium toxicity.  High-yield takeaway: Wernicke's triad = \"Confusion + Ophthalmoplegia + Ataxia\"; give thiamine BEFORE glucose. Korsakoff = irreversible memory loss + confabulation."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"During the acute crisis phase of crisis intervention, the nurse's PRIMARY goal is to:","options":["Explore the client's early childhood conflicts","Help the client return to at least the pre-crisis level of functioning","Provide long-term personality reconstruction","Prescribe definitive psychotherapy for underlying disorders"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Crisis intervention is short-term and reality-focused; the goal is to restore the client to at least the PRE-crisis level of functioning using active, directive support and problem-solving.  Why wrong: Exploring childhood conflict (A) and personality reconstruction (C) are long-term therapies, not crisis work; definitive psychotherapy (D) belongs to later phases.  High-yield takeaway: Crisis intervention = short-term, present-focused, aims to restore pre-crisis functioning; a crisis typically resolves in 4–6 weeks."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on an antipsychotic is found with a temperature of 39.5°C, muscle rigidity and confusion. Along with stopping the drug and cooling, which medication is used specifically to treat NMS?","options":["Cyproheptadine","Dantrolene (with or without bromocriptine)","Flumazenil","Naloxone"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: NMS is treated by stopping the offending antipsychotic and giving dantrolene (muscle relaxant) and/or bromocriptine (dopamine agonist), plus aggressive cooling and supportive care.  Why wrong: Cyproheptadine (A) is the antidote for serotonin syndrome; flumazenil (C) reverses benzodiazepines; naloxone (D) reverses opioids.  High-yield takeaway: NMS antidote = dantrolene ± bromocriptine; Serotonin syndrome antidote = cyproheptadine — a favourite NORCET pairing to confuse."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with schizophrenia states, \"The government has planted a microchip in my brain to control my thoughts and broadcast them to everyone.\" The belief that others can hear one's thoughts is termed:","options":["Thought insertion","Thought broadcasting","Thought withdrawal","Delusion of control"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Thought broadcasting = the belief that one's thoughts are transmitted so others can hear them. These are first-rank (Schneiderian) symptoms of schizophrenia.  Why wrong: Thought insertion (A) = thoughts placed into one's mind by others; thought withdrawal (C) = thoughts removed; delusion of control/passivity (D) = belief that one's actions are controlled by an external force.  High-yield takeaway: Broadcasting = thoughts go OUT (others hear them); insertion = thoughts come IN; withdrawal = thoughts taken away — classic Schneiderian first-rank symptoms."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse notes that a client repeats every word said to him (echolalia), imitates the nurse's movements (echopraxia), and maintains bizarre postures for long periods. These features suggest:","options":["Catatonia","Mania","Akathisia","Panic disorder"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Catatonia is characterized by psychomotor disturbance — echolalia, echopraxia, waxy flexibility, posturing, mutism, negativism or stupor/excitement. In ICD-11 it is recognized as a distinct syndrome.  Why wrong: Mania (B) = elevated mood/overactivity; akathisia (C) = drug-induced restlessness; panic disorder (D) = discrete anxiety attacks.  High-yield takeaway: Catatonia = echolalia + echopraxia + waxy flexibility + posturing; may respond to benzodiazepines (lorazepam) and ECT; lethal catatonia is an emergency."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client says, \"I feel blue, the sky is blue, blue jeans, jeans are denim...\" rapidly shifting topics based on sound rather than meaning. This thought disorder is:","options":["Flight of ideas / clang association","Circumstantiality","Perseveration","Neologism"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Linking words by SOUND rather than meaning is clang association (a form of flight of ideas), typical of acute mania.  Why wrong: Circumstantiality (B) = over-inclusive detail but eventually reaches the point; perseveration (C) = repeating the same response; neologism (D) = inventing new words.  High-yield takeaway: Clang association = rhyming/sound-based links; flight of ideas is classic in mania; neologisms and word salad point to schizophrenia."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client is diagnosed with conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) presenting with sudden \"paralysis\" of the legs after a traumatic argument, yet shows surprisingly little concern about the deficit. This lack of concern is called:","options":["Anhedonia","La belle indifférence","Alexithymia","Anosognosia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: La belle indifférence = a characteristic (though not universal) lack of appropriate distress about a serious functional symptom, described in conversion disorder.  Why wrong: Anhedonia (A) = inability to feel pleasure; alexithymia (C) = difficulty identifying/describing emotions; anosognosia (D) = inability to recognize one's own illness (e.g., after stroke).  High-yield takeaway: La belle indifférence = calm indifference to a dramatic neurological deficit in conversion disorder; symptoms are not intentionally produced (unlike factitious/malingering)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Under the Mental Healthcare Act 2017, emergency treatment (Section 94) permits a registered medical practitioner to provide treatment to prevent harm. However, this section specifically does NOT allow the use of:","options":["Life-saving medication","Electro-convulsive therapy","First aid","Transport to a mental health establishment"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Section 94 explicitly states that emergency treatment shall NOT include electro-convulsive therapy; emergency care covers essential/life-saving measures to prevent harm but not ECT.  Why wrong: Essential medication (A), first aid (C) and transport (D) fall within permissible emergency treatment.  High-yield takeaway: MHCA §94 emergency treatment EXCLUDES ECT; emergency treatment is limited (generally up to 72 hours) until a proper assessment is done."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client on an SSRI for 3 weeks reports no mood improvement and is frustrated. The BEST nursing response is:","options":["\"The medication isn't working; you should stop it today.\"","\"Antidepressants often take 2–4 weeks or longer to show full effect; let's continue and monitor.\"","\"You must be doing something wrong.\"","\"Double your dose to speed things up.\""],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: SSRIs typically require 2–4 weeks (sometimes 6) for a therapeutic mood response; the nurse should educate, encourage adherence and continued monitoring rather than premature discontinuation.  Why wrong: Abrupt stopping (A) risks relapse/discontinuation syndrome; blaming (C) is non-therapeutic; self-adjusting the dose (D) is unsafe.  High-yield takeaway: SSRIs take 2–4+ weeks for mood effect; monitor for early activation and increased suicide risk in young adults during this lag."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"According to Kübler-Ross, a terminally ill client who says, \"If I can just live to see my daughter's wedding, I'll never miss my medication again,\" is in which stage of grief?","options":["Denial","Anger","Bargaining","Acceptance"],"correct":"C","rationale":"Why correct: Bargaining involves attempts to negotiate/postpone the inevitable, often with promises (\"if I do X, then let me have Y\").  Why wrong: Denial (A) = refusal to accept reality; anger (B) = \"why me?\"; acceptance (D) = calm coming to terms with the outcome.  High-yield takeaway: Kübler-Ross stages = Denial, Anger, Bargaining, Depression, Acceptance (DABDA); they are not strictly linear."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client experiencing a flashback of a past traumatic event with hypervigilance, nightmares and avoidance, persisting for 4 months after a road accident, is MOST consistent with:","options":["Acute stress disorder","Post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD)","Adjustment disorder","Generalized anxiety disorder"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: PTSD is diagnosed when trauma-related symptoms (re-experiencing/flashbacks, avoidance, hyperarousal, negative cognitions) persist beyond 1 month.  Why wrong: Acute stress disorder (A) lasts 3 days to 1 month; adjustment disorder (C) is a milder stress reaction without the full trauma triad; GAD (D) is pervasive worry not tied to a specific trauma.  High-yield takeaway: <1 month = acute stress disorder; >1 month = PTSD. ICD-11 also adds Complex PTSD (with disturbances in self-organization)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse plans care for a client with severe negative symptoms of schizophrenia (avolition, flat affect, social withdrawal). Which intervention is MOST appropriate?","options":["Insist on immediate participation in large, stimulating group activities","Begin with brief, simple one-to-one interactions and gradually increase social engagement","Leave the client alone until motivation returns","Interpret the withdrawal as deliberate defiance"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Negative symptoms respond to a gradual, low-demand approach — short, simple one-to-one contacts building trust, then slowly increasing socialization and structured activity.  Why wrong: Large stimulating groups (A) overwhelm the client; isolation (C) worsens withdrawal; labelling it defiance (D) is inaccurate and non-therapeutic.  High-yield takeaway: Negative symptoms (5 A's: Affective flattening, Alogia, Avolition, Anhedonia, Asociality) → start small, build gradually; they respond less to typical antipsychotics."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with major depressive disorder is prescribed ECT after failing multiple medication trials. The MOST important reason ECT may be chosen as first-line in some situations is:","options":["It is cheaper than medication","In severe depression with high suicide risk or refusal to eat/drink, ECT can produce a rapid response","It cures depression permanently in one session","It avoids the need for consent"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: ECT is highly effective and acts faster than antidepressants, making it valuable when a rapid response is critical — severe depression with active suicidality, catatonia, or life-threatening refusal of food/fluids.  Why wrong: Cost (A) is not the rationale; a single session is not curative (C); informed consent is REQUIRED (and legally protected under MHCA) — ECT never bypasses consent (D).  High-yield takeaway: ECT is indicated for severe/refractory depression, high suicide risk, catatonia, and severe mania — chosen for its RAPID response; informed consent is mandatory."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A community health nurse under the DMHP is educating villagers to reduce stigma. Which action BEST reflects the rights-based, community-oriented goal of the National Mental Health Programme?","options":["Advising families to keep mentally ill relatives isolated at home","Integrating mental health care into primary health centres and promoting community awareness","Referring every client only to distant tertiary institutions","Discouraging clients from working or studying"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: The NMHP/DMHP aim to integrate mental health into general/primary health care, ensure accessibility at the community level, and reduce stigma through awareness — aligning with the rights-based MHCA 2017 and community-living principle.  Why wrong: Isolation (A), sole reliance on distant tertiary care (C) and discouraging participation (D) contradict community integration and the right to community living.  High-yield takeaway: NMHP objective = accessible, community-based, integrated mental health care + de-stigmatization; DMHP delivers this at the district level."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A client with opioid overdose is unresponsive with pinpoint pupils and a respiratory rate of 6/min. Which is the PRIORITY intervention?","options":["Administer naloxone and support airway/breathing","Give flumazenil","Administer activated charcoal orally","Encourage oral fluids"],"correct":"A","rationale":"Why correct: Opioid overdose (pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression + decreased consciousness) is managed with airway/breathing support and naloxone, the specific opioid antagonist.  Why wrong: Flumazenil (B) reverses benzodiazepines, not opioids; oral charcoal (C) is unsafe in an unresponsive client (aspiration risk); oral fluids (D) are contraindicated in an unresponsive patient.  High-yield takeaway: Opioid toxidrome = pinpoint pupils + respiratory depression + coma → naloxone; benzodiazepine antidote = flumazenil (used cautiously)."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"Which statement about the \"nominated representative\" under the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 is CORRECT?","options":["Only a spouse can be a nominated representative","The person with mental illness can appoint a nominated representative to support decision-making, and if none is appointed a default order applies","The nominated representative can override the client's advance directive at will","A nominated representative is only for minors"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Section 14 lets a person appoint a nominated representative to assist with care decisions and safeguard rights; if none is appointed, the Act specifies a default hierarchy (e.g., relative, caregiver) and the Board can appoint one.  Why wrong: It is not limited to a spouse (A) or to minors (D); the representative supports the person's expressed wishes and cannot arbitrarily override a valid advance directive (C).  High-yield takeaway: Nominated representative (§14) = supported decision-making, not a substitute decision-maker who overrides the client's autonomy."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse observes a client on high-dose typical antipsychotics develop a shuffling gait, resting tremor, mask-like face and cogwheel rigidity. The nurse documents this as:","options":["Tardive dyskinesia","Drug-induced parkinsonism (pseudoparkinsonism)","Akathisia","Acute dystonia"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Drug-induced (pseudo)parkinsonism from D2 blockade mimics Parkinson's disease — tremor, rigidity (cogwheel), bradykinesia, shuffling gait, mask-like facies; treated with anticholinergics or dose reduction.  Why wrong: Tardive dyskinesia (A) = involuntary orofacial movements; akathisia (C) = restlessness; acute dystonia (D) = sustained spasms/posturing.  High-yield takeaway: Pseudoparkinsonism = tremor + rigidity + bradykinesia (like Parkinson's), treated with anticholinergics — unlike tardive dyskinesia, which anticholinergics worsen."},{"section":"Batch 010 · Priority nursing actions & case-based reasoning, patient safety…","question":"A nurse is triaging psychiatric emergencies. Which situation requires the MOST immediate (first) intervention?","options":["A client with chronic auditory hallucinations he has managed for years","A client who has swallowed an unknown quantity of pills 20 minutes ago and is drowsy","A client complaining of dry mouth on an antipsychotic","A stable depressed client requesting to talk about his feelings"],"correct":"B","rationale":"Why correct: Acute overdose with declining consciousness is an immediate life threat (airway, breathing, circulation, poison management) — physiological emergencies take priority over psychiatric/psychosocial concerns.  Why wrong: Long-standing managed hallucinations (A), a minor anticholinergic effect (C), and a stable client wanting to talk (D) are lower acuity.  High-yield takeaway: In triage, ABC/physiological life threats (overdose) outrank chronic or psychosocial issues — apply Maslow: physiological/safety needs first."}];
